/* Generated by build_study.js: modules, glossary, mini exam banks and the prof's review questions. */
window.STUDY={"modules":[{"id":"m1","n":1,"file":"module-1.html","title":"Introduction to Cell Biology and the Cell Theory","short":"The cell theory","progressKey":"biol130-m1-progress-v1","blurb":"Where cell biology started: the first microscopes, the cell theory, and what does and doesn't count as a cell.","topics":["Hooke, Leeuwenhoek and the first microscopes","The three tenets of the cell theory","Viruses, viroids and prions","Prokaryotic vs eukaryotic, plant vs animal cells","Model organisms"],"ready":true,"outcomes":["Explain why the cell is the fundamental unit of life and explain the three tenets of the cell theory.","Explain the basic properties of cells and the close relationship between structure and function in biological systems.","Compare and contrast various features of prokaryotic cells and eukaryotic cells.","Describe how model organisms are used to study and understand biological processes."],"concepts":["The Cell Theory","Comparison of prokaryotes and eukaryotes","The Central Dogma","Metabolism & role of ATP","Eukaryotic features","Role of model organisms"],"chapters":[{"id":"seeing","title":"Seeing cells","pages":"p. 4","blurb":"What a cell is, what Hooke and Leeuwenhoek saw, and the three kinds of microscopy.","summary":"Cells are the basic unit of life, and almost all are too small to see without a microscope. Hooke saw empty walls; Leeuwenhoek saw living cells. Next: how those observations became a theory.","terms":["cell","hooke","leeuwenhoek","light-microscopy","electron-microscopy","fluorescence-microscopy"]},{"id":"theory","title":"The cell theory","pages":"p. 5","blurb":"Schleiden, Schwann and Virchow, and the three tenets of the cell theory.","summary":"All living things are made of cells, the cell is the unit of life, and cells come only from other cells. Next: the things that break the rule.","terms":["cell-theory","schleiden","schwann","virchow","unicellular"]},{"id":"exceptions","title":"Not quite alive","pages":"p. 5","blurb":"Viruses, viroids and prions: the exceptions to the cell theory.","summary":"Viruses are nucleic acid in a protein coat; viroids are bare circles of RNA; prions are protein only. None can reproduce without a living cell. Next: what every real cell has.","terms":["exception","virus","capsid","viral-genome","viroid","prion","host-cell"]},{"id":"shared","title":"What every cell has","pages":"p. 6","blurb":"The basic properties of cells, cytoplasm vs cytosol, the central dogma and ATP.","summary":"Every cell has a membrane, a genetic program read through the central dogma, and runs on energy carried by ATP. Next: the two kinds of cells.","terms":["cell-properties","plasma-membrane","cytoplasm","cytosol","gene","central-dogma","transcription","translation","bioenergetics","metabolism","atp","self-regulation"]},{"id":"prokaryotes","title":"Prokaryotes and the tree of life","pages":"p. 7","blurb":"Three domains, two kinds of cell, and what a bacterium is made of.","summary":"Prokaryotes (Bacteria and Archaea) have no nucleus and no membrane-bound organelles, a single circular chromosome, and divide by binary fission. Next: the eukaryotes.","terms":["prokaryote","eukaryote","tree-of-life","bacteria","archaea","eukarya","nucleoid","plasmid","cell-wall","flagellum","ribosome","binary-fission","bacterial-shapes"]},{"id":"eukaryotes","title":"The eukaryotes","pages":"pp. 8–9","blurb":"Protists, fungi, plants and animals, and what sets plant cells apart.","summary":"Protists are diverse and mostly single-celled; fungi have chitin walls and absorb food; plants have walls, vacuoles, chloroplasts and plasmodesmata; animals have no walls. Next: the organelles inside.","terms":["eukaryote","protists","fungi","chitin","heterotroph","autotroph","plants","animals","vacuole","plasmodesmata","chloroplast"]},{"id":"organelles","title":"Inside a eukaryotic cell","pages":"p. 9","blurb":"The endomembrane system, the organelles outside it, and what each one does.","summary":"The endomembrane system builds, modifies and ships materials; mitochondria, chloroplasts, peroxisomes and ribosomes work outside it. Next: the organisms scientists use to study all this.","terms":["organelle","nucleus","endomembrane","non-endomembrane","er","golgi","lysosome","vesicle","mitochondria","peroxisome","cytoskeleton","ribosome","nucleolus"]},{"id":"models","title":"Model organisms","pages":"p. 10","blurb":"Why biologists study bacteria, yeast, cress, flies, worms and mice.","summary":"Model organisms are non-human species that are easy to study and tell us about biology in general. Last: build four cells.","terms":["model-organism","ecoli","yeast","arabidopsis","drosophila","celegans","mouse"]},{"id":"build","title":"Build a cell","pages":"pp. 3–11","blurb":"The finale: assemble a bacterium, an animal cell, a plant cell and a yeast cell.","summary":"You can now build any of the four cell types from their parts, and explain why each part belongs. Try the \\","terms":["prokaryote","eukaryote","nucleoid","nucleus","cell-wall","chloroplast","vacuole","plasmodesmata","mitochondria","lysosome"]}]},{"id":"m2","n":2,"file":"module-2.html","title":"Molecules of Life","short":"Molecules of life","progressKey":"biol130-m2-progress-v1","blurb":"The chemistry of life, from atoms and bonds to water, carbon and the four kinds of macromolecules.","topics":["Atoms, valence electrons and bonds","Water, polarity and pH","Carbon and functional groups","Condensation and hydrolysis","Carbohydrates, lipids, proteins, nucleic acids"],"ready":true,"outcomes":["Name and describe the components of an atom and how bonds form between atoms to form molecules.","Explain the properties of water and its role in life.","Describe the structures and functions of proteins, nucleic acids, carbohydrates, and lipids."],"concepts":["Properties of atoms","Molecules and the types of chemical bonds","Polar versus nonpolar molecules","Chemical reactions","Properties of water molecules","pH of a solution","Properties of carbon","Common functional groups on biological molecules","Structure of organic macromolecules - proteins, nucleic acids, carbohydrates, and lipids"],"chapters":[{"id":"atoms","title":"Atoms","pages":"p. 13","blurb":"Elements, protons, neutrons, electrons, orbitals and valence electrons.","summary":"An atom is a nucleus of protons and neutrons with electrons around it. The valence electrons in the outer shell decide how many bonds it makes. Next: the bonds themselves.","terms":["element","atom","nucleus-atom","proton","neutron","electron","orbital","valence","molecule"]},{"id":"bonds","title":"Bonds","pages":"pp. 14–15","blurb":"Covalent bonds, polar and nonpolar, electronegativity, and ionic bonds.","summary":"Equal sharing gives a nonpolar covalent bond, unequal sharing a polar one, and stealing an electron gives ions held by an ionic bond. Next: water, the molecule that makes polarity matter.","terms":["covalent-bond","electronegativity","polar-covalent","nonpolar-covalent","polar","nonpolar","ionic-bond","cation","anion"]},{"id":"water","title":"Water","pages":"pp. 15–16","blurb":"Chemical reactions, hydrogen bonds, dissolving salt, and hydrophilic vs hydrophobic.","summary":"Water is polar, so its molecules hydrogen-bond to each other, pull ions apart, and push nonpolar molecules together. Next: how acidic or basic a solution is.","terms":["chemical-reaction","reactants","products","solvent","hydrogen-bond","hydrophilic","hydrophobic","amphipathic"]},{"id":"ph","title":"pH","pages":"p. 17","blurb":"Hydrogen ions, the pH scale, acids and bases.","summary":"pH measures H⁺: below 7 is acidic, above 7 basic, and each step is tenfold. Next: carbon, the backbone of organic molecules.","terms":["ph","acid","base","hydrogen-ion","hydroxide"]},{"id":"carbon","title":"Carbon and functional groups","pages":"pp. 18, 20","blurb":"Why carbon is the backbone of life, and the functional groups that give molecules their character.","summary":"Carbon makes four bonds, forming chains and rings. Functional groups with N, O, P or S are polar; methyl is nonpolar. Next: how small molecules become macromolecules.","terms":["organic-molecule","carbon","functional-group","fg-amino","fg-carboxyl","fg-hydroxyl","fg-carbonyl","fg-sulfhydryl","fg-methyl","fg-phosphate","fg-amide"]},{"id":"macro","title":"Building macromolecules","pages":"p. 19","blurb":"The four kinds of biological molecule, monomers and polymers, condensation and hydrolysis.","summary":"Condensation links monomers and releases water; hydrolysis uses water to break them apart. Next: the first of the four, proteins.","terms":["macromolecule","protein","nucleic-acid","carbohydrate","lipid","polymer","monomer","condensation","hydrolysis"]},{"id":"proteins","title":"Proteins","pages":"pp. 21–22","blurb":"What proteins do, amino acid structure, zwitterions and peptide bonds.","summary":"Amino acids share a backbone and differ only in their R groups; peptide bonds join them into polypeptides. Next: nucleic acids.","terms":["protein","protein-functions","amino-acid","alpha-carbon","r-group","polypeptide","zwitterion","peptide-bond"]},{"id":"nucleic","title":"Nucleic acids","pages":"pp. 23–25","blurb":"Nucleotides and nucleosides, purines and pyrimidines, DNA vs RNA, phosphodiester bonds and ATP.","summary":"A nucleotide is a base, a sugar and a phosphate; phosphodiester bonds link them into strands that grow 5′ → 3′. Nucleotides also carry energy (ATP), signal (cAMP) and help enzymes (NAD). Next: carbohydrates.","terms":["nucleic-acid","dna","rna","nucleotide","nucleoside","nitrogenous-base","pyrimidine","purine","deoxyribose","ribose","nucleotide-functions","atp","phosphodiester","five-three"]},{"id":"carbs","title":"Carbohydrates","pages":"pp. 26–28","blurb":"Monosaccharides and isomers, ring forms, glycosidic bonds, and di-, oligo- and polysaccharides.","summary":"Monosaccharides close into rings, link by glycosidic bonds, and build up into disaccharides, oligosaccharides and polysaccharides for energy or structure. Next: lipids.","terms":["carbohydrate","monosaccharide","isomer","glycosidic","disaccharide","oligosaccharide","polysaccharide","glycogen-starch"]},{"id":"lipids","title":"Lipids","pages":"pp. 29–30","blurb":"Triacylglycerols, saturated and unsaturated fatty acids, steroids and phospholipids.","summary":"Lipids are hydrophobic and not polymers: triacylglycerols store energy, steroids like cholesterol sit in membranes and make hormones, and amphipathic phospholipids build membranes. Last: the finale.","terms":["lipid","triacylglycerol","fatty-acid","glycerol","saturated","unsaturated","steroid","cholesterol","phospholipid","amphipathic"]},{"id":"finale","title":"Name that molecule","pages":"pp. 12–31","blurb":"The finale: ten mystery molecules. Read the clues and name them.","summary":"From atoms to macromolecules: you can now read a molecule's makeup and say what kind it is. Try the chemistry desk for endless practice, or take the mini exam.","terms":["protein","nucleic-acid","carbohydrate","lipid"]}]},{"id":"m3","n":3,"file":"module-3.html","title":"Cell Border Control: Cells and Membranes","short":"Cells and membranes","progressKey":"cbc-progress-v1","blurb":"Follow glucose from the gut into the blood through one intestinal cell.","topics":["Organelles and the endomembrane system","Mitochondria, chloroplasts and endosymbiosis","Membrane structure and fluidity","Osmosis and tonicity","Passive, active and bulk transport"],"ready":true,"outcomes":["Describe the composition of cell membranes.","Explain the relationship between the structure of the eukaryotic endomembrane system and the function of each of the membrane-bound compartments.","Describe the structure, function, and origin of mitochondria and chloroplasts.","Describe the mechanism behind the selective permeability of membranes and the reasons why certain substances can pass through them while others cannot."],"concepts":["Structure of cell membranes (phospholipid bilayer) and the fluid mosaic model","Selectively permeable membrane",{"text":"Membrane transport including","sub":["Passive transport, simple diffusion and facilitated diffusion,","Active transport – primary and secondary"]},"Role of the Na+/K+-ATPase","Electrochemical gradient","Function of cell wall and the cytoskeleton","Endomembrane system compartmentalization of cells","Role of various structures in the endomembrane system","Structure and function of mitochondria and chloroplasts"],"chapters":[{"id":"cell","title":"Your cell","pages":"pp. 37–40","blurb":"The parts of an animal cell, and how it ships a new protein to its membrane.","summary":"You know your cell's parts and how a new membrane protein travels from the nucleus, through the ER and Golgi, to the plasma membrane. Next you will look closely at the membrane itself.","terms":["lumen","apical","basolateral","tight-junction","interstitial-fluid","cytoplasm","cytosol","nucleus","nucleolus","nuclear-pores","er","golgi","lysosome","vesicle","endomembrane","mitochondria","cytoskeleton","vacuole","ribosome"]},{"id":"power","title":"Power plants","pages":"pp. 41–42","blurb":"Mitochondria and chloroplasts: how their structure fits their job, and the endosymbiont theory of where they came from.","summary":"Mitochondria make ATP and chloroplasts capture light energy. Both have two membranes, their own DNA and ribosomes, and divide on their own: leftovers of the bacteria they once were. Next: the border itself.","terms":["mitochondria","chloroplast","outer-membrane","cristae","matrix","intermembrane-space","stroma","thylakoid","granum","mito-dna","endosymbiosis"]},{"id":"border","title":"The border","pages":"pp. 33–34, 36","blurb":"The phospholipid bilayer, its proteins, and what can cross it unaided.","summary":"Your border is a hydrophobic core with protein gates set into it. Small nonpolar molecules cross on their own; almost everything else needs a protein. Next: what water does.","terms":["phospholipid","hydrophilic-head","fatty-acid-tails","bilayer","fluid-mosaic","selective-permeability","cholesterol","integral-protein","transmembrane-protein","peripheral-protein","glycoprotein","channel","carrier","simple-diffusion"]},{"id":"chem","title":"Membrane chemistry","pages":"pp. 33–36","blurb":"Why phospholipids form bilayers on their own, what controls fluidity, and what membrane proteins and carbohydrates do.","summary":"Phospholipids assemble into self-sealing bilayers because water excludes their tails. Tail length, double bonds, cholesterol and temperature set how fluid the membrane is. Next: what water does.","terms":["amphipathic","micelle","liposome","bilayer","fluid-mosaic","cholesterol","lipid-raft","flip-flop","membrane-functions","membrane-protein-functions","glycoprotein","glycolipid"]},{"id":"water","title":"Water","pages":"p. 45","blurb":"Osmosis, osmotic pressure, and what isotonic, hypotonic and hypertonic do to a cell.","summary":"Water follows solute. Compare the outside to the cell, name the solution, and you can predict what the cell does. Next: moving solutes down their gradients.","terms":["osmosis","osmotic-pressure","isotonic","hypotonic","hypertonic","crenation","plasmolysis","lysis","turgor","aquaporin","cell-wall"]},{"id":"downhill","title":"Downhill","pages":"pp. 43–44","blurb":"Passive transport: simple diffusion, channels and carriers.","summary":"Passive transport runs downhill. The bilayer handles small nonpolar molecules, channels handle ions and water, and carriers handle bigger polar molecules like glucose. Next: going uphill.","terms":["diffusion","simple-diffusion","facilitated-diffusion","passive-transport","channel","carrier","glut","concentration-gradient"]},{"id":"pump","title":"The pump","pages":"pp. 46–47","blurb":"Primary active transport, the Na⁺/K⁺ pump and electrochemical gradients.","summary":"The Na⁺/K⁺ pump spends ATP to keep Na⁺ low inside the cell. That stored gradient is the battery for the next chapter.","terms":["active-transport","primary-active","nak-pump","atp","electrochemical-gradient","membrane-potential","concentration-gradient","importer","exporter"]},{"id":"glucose","title":"The glucose run","pages":"pp. 47–48","blurb":"Secondary active transport, symport and antiport, and the full route from gut to blood.","summary":"You built the whole route. Na⁺ flowing in pays for glucose to come in uphill; glucose leaves downhill; the pump resets the Na⁺ gradient with ATP. That is the core of this unit.","terms":["secondary-active","cotransport","uniporter","symporter","antiporter","sglt","glut","apical","basolateral","interstitial-fluid","tight-junction"]},{"id":"bulk","title":"Bulk traffic","pages":"pp. 49–50","blurb":"Exocytosis, endocytosis, phagocytosis, pinocytosis and receptor-mediated endocytosis.","summary":"Some cargo is too big or too plentiful for any transporter. It travels in vesicles instead, and that costs energy.","terms":["bulk-transport","exocytosis","endocytosis","phagocytosis","pinocytosis","rme","endosome","lysozyme"]},{"id":"meal","title":"Full meal","pages":"pp. 32–51","blurb":"The finale. Every system runs at once and each call counts.","summary":"You ran the border through a whole meal. Replay any chapter to push your first-try score up, or head to Checkpoint for endless practice.","terms":["simple-diffusion","atp","hypertonic","secondary-active","glut","rme","lysosome","exocytosis","channel","nak-pump"]}]},{"id":"m4","n":4,"file":"module-4.html","title":"Nucleic Acids and Information Flow","short":"Nucleic acids","progressKey":"biol130-m4-progress-v1","blurb":"Take a gene from its enhancer to a finished mRNA.","topics":["Griffith and Avery: DNA as the genetic material","Nucleotides and the double helix","The central dogma","Transcription in prokaryotes and eukaryotes","RNA processing and alternative splicing"],"ready":true,"outcomes":["Describe the chemical composition and structure of DNA and analyze how it stores and transmits information.","Explain the Central Dogma and how genetic information flows in the process of transcription.","Explain the significance of RNA processing of a primary transcript to messenger RNA."],"concepts":["Chemical composition and structure of DNA","DNA structure and function","Transcription","RNA processing"],"chapters":[{"id":"material","title":"The genetic material","pages":"pp. 53–54","blurb":"How Griffith, then Avery, MacLeod and McCarty showed that DNA carries genetic information.","summary":"Griffith showed that something in dead cells could transform living ones. Avery's group showed that something was DNA. Next: what DNA is made of.","terms":["dna","griffith","transformation","avery","nuclease","replication"]},{"id":"blocks","title":"Nucleotides and phosphodiester bonds","pages":"p. 55","blurb":"The three parts of a nucleotide, the four bases, and how a strand gets its 5′ and 3′ ends.","summary":"A strand is a chain of nucleotides with a 5′ phosphate at one end and a 3′–OH at the other, and it only grows from the 3′ end. Next: two strands together.","terms":["nucleotide","nucleoside","deoxyribose","carbons","purines","pyrimidines","phosphate","phosphodiester","polarity","five-three"]},{"id":"helix","title":"The double helix","pages":"pp. 53, 56–57","blurb":"Antiparallel strands, complementary base pairs, hydrogen bonds, base stacking and packing into chromosomes.","summary":"Two antiparallel strands, held by weak hydrogen bonds and base stacking, wound into a right-handed helix and packed by histones. Next: how the cell reads it.","terms":["double-helix","watson-crick","chargaff","antiparallel","complementary","grooves","hydrogen-bonding","base-stacking","chromatin"]},{"id":"dogma","title":"Gene expression and the central dogma","pages":"pp. 58–59","blurb":"Genomes, genes, gene expression, the central dogma and where transcription happens.","summary":"Genes are transcribed into RNA and translated into protein, in the cytoplasm for prokaryotes and across two compartments for eukaryotes. Next: RNA itself.","terms":["genome","gene","gene-expression","central-dogma","transcription","translation","reverse-transcription","ribosome"]},{"id":"rna","title":"RNA","pages":"p. 60","blurb":"How RNA differs from DNA: sugar, base, 5′ end, size and shape.","summary":"RNA is a less stable, usually single-stranded cousin of DNA, with ribose and uracil. Next: how RNA is made.","terms":["rna","ribose","uracil","mrna"]},{"id":"initiate","title":"Starting transcription","pages":"pp. 61–63","blurb":"What transcription needs, promoters and the TATA box, sigma factor, general transcription factors, enhancers and the mediator.","summary":"Transcription starts at a promoter. Bacteria use a sigma factor; eukaryotes assemble general transcription factors, activators, mediator and Pol II through DNA looping. Next: elongation.","terms":["rna-polymerase","template","ntp","promoter","tata","terminator","sigma","gtf","activator","enhancer","mediator","pol2","bubble","stages"]},{"id":"elongate","title":"Elongation","pages":"pp. 61, 63–64","blurb":"Driving RNA polymerase along the template, and the chemistry of adding each nucleotide.","summary":"The template is read 3′→5′, the RNA grows 5′→3′, and every nucleotide is paid for by its own triphosphate. Next: how prokaryotes and eukaryotes differ.","terms":["template","rna-transcript","bubble","channels","ntp","pyrophosphate","five-three"]},{"id":"proeuk","title":"Prokaryotic vs eukaryotic transcription","pages":"p. 65","blurb":"Coupled transcription and translation, polycistronic mRNA, and why eukaryotes need processing.","summary":"Prokaryotes translate as they transcribe, and one mRNA can carry several genes. Eukaryotes separate the two with a nucleus, so the transcript has to be processed. Next: processing.","terms":["primary-transcript","coupled","polycistronic"]},{"id":"process","title":"RNA processing and alternative splicing","pages":"pp. 65–66","blurb":"The 5′ cap, the poly(A) tail, RNA splicing, and one gene making more than one protein.","summary":"A capped, spliced, polyadenylated mRNA is ready to leave the nucleus, and alternative splicing lets one gene make several proteins. Last: the finale.","terms":["rna-processing","cap","polya","exonuclease","splicing","introns-exons","alt-splicing"]},{"id":"finale","title":"Express the gene","pages":"pp. 52–67","blurb":"The finale: take one gene from enhancer to ribosome, one call at a time.","summary":"You took a gene from its enhancer to the ribosome. Replay any chapter to raise your first-try score, or practise at the sequence desk.","terms":["enhancer","gtf","template","pyrophosphate","cap","splicing","polya","coupled","alt-splicing"]}]},{"id":"m5","n":5,"file":"module-5.html","title":"Protein Structure, Function and Synthesis","short":"Proteins","progressKey":"biol130-m5-progress-v1","blurb":"Amino acids to delivered proteins: folding, the genetic code, translation and sorting.","topics":["Amino acids and their R groups","The four levels of protein structure","Codons, anticodons and the genetic code","Translation: initiation, elongation, termination","Regulation and protein sorting"],"ready":true,"outcomes":["Describe the properties of amino acids and how linear polymers of amino acids form three-dimensional structures of proteins with specific functions.","Describe the process of translation to create a newly synthesized peptide in prokaryotes and eukaryotes.","Explain the Central Dogma of molecular biology, the role of mRNA and tRNA to the process of translation.","Explain how protein synthesis relates to protein sorting in the cell."],"concepts":["Amino acid structure and properties of amino acids","Molecular structure of proteins","Protein synthesis","Regulation of protein synthesis and sorting"],"chapters":[{"id":"aa","title":"Amino acids","pages":"pp. 69–70","blurb":"The four parts of an amino acid, and how R groups sort them into hydrophobic, hydrophilic, acidic and basic.","summary":"Every amino acid has the same backbone parts; its R group decides whether it hides inside a protein or sits on the surface. Next: three amino acids that bend the rules.","terms":["protein","amino-acid","alpha-carbon","r-group","aa-classes","hydrophobic-aa","hydrophilic-aa","van-der-waals"]},{"id":"special","title":"Three special amino acids","pages":"p. 71","blurb":"Glycine, proline and cysteine, and how each one changes a protein's shape.","summary":"Glycine makes the backbone flexible, proline makes it rigid, and cysteine cross-links it with disulfide bonds. Next: how amino acids join.","terms":["glycine","proline","cysteine","disulfide"]},{"id":"peptide","title":"Peptide bonds","pages":"p. 71","blurb":"Dehydration reactions, the N-terminus and C-terminus, and writing a primary sequence.","summary":"Peptide bonds join the carboxyl group of one amino acid to the amino group of the next, releasing water, and give the chain an N end and a C end. Next: how the chain folds.","terms":["peptide-bond","condensation","n-terminus","polypeptide","residue"]},{"id":"structure","title":"Four levels of structure","pages":"pp. 72–74","blurb":"Primary, secondary, tertiary and quaternary structure, and the bonds that hold each one.","summary":"Sequence (1°) sets the helices and sheets (2°), the whole fold (3°) and how subunits fit (4°). Next: the code that sets the sequence.","terms":["levels","conformation","primary-structure","secondary-structure","alpha-helix","beta-sheet","tertiary-structure","quaternary-structure","subunit","disulfide"]},{"id":"code","title":"Codons and the genetic code","pages":"pp. 75–76, 78","blurb":"The central dogma, codons, reading frames, and reading the code table.","summary":"mRNA is read three bases at a time, 5′ to 3′, starting from AUG; 64 codons code for 20 amino acids plus stop. Next: the machines that read it.","terms":["central-dogma","translation","mrna","codon","reading-frame","genetic-code","redundant","start-codon","stop-codon"]},{"id":"machines","title":"Ribosomes and tRNAs","pages":"pp. 75–77","blurb":"The ribosome's A, P and E sites, the tRNA cloverleaf, anticodons, and charging tRNAs.","summary":"Ribosomes have A, P and E sites; tRNAs carry amino acids on their CCA end and read codons with their anticodons; synthetases charge them. Next: translation itself.","terms":["ribosome","sites","trna","anticodon","synthetase","charged"]},{"id":"translate","title":"Translation","pages":"pp. 78–80","blurb":"Initiation at the 5′ cap, elongation through the A, P and E sites, and termination at a stop codon.","summary":"Initiation finds AUG, elongation adds one amino acid per codon, and termination releases the chain at a stop codon. Next: what's different in prokaryotes.","terms":["initiation","initiation-factors","start-codon","elongation","peptidyl-transferase","termination","release-factor","stop-codon"]},{"id":"prokeuk","title":"Prokaryotes vs eukaryotes","pages":"pp. 79–80","blurb":"Cap scanning vs the Shine-Dalgarno sequence, and polycistronic mRNA.","summary":"Elongation and termination are the same; initiation differs: eukaryotes start at the 5′ cap and scan, prokaryotes start at Shine-Dalgarno sequences, so one prokaryotic mRNA can make several proteins. Next: what happens after translation.","terms":["shine-dalgarno","polycistronic","initiation"]},{"id":"after","title":"Regulation and sorting","pages":"pp. 81–82","blurb":"Levels of gene regulation, post-translational modification, signal sequences and SRP.","summary":"Expression is controlled from DNA access to protein activation, and signal sequences decide where each protein goes. Last: one gene, start to finish.","terms":["gene-regulation","ptm","sorting","signal-sequence","srp","signal-anchor","er-fates"]},{"id":"finale","title":"One gene, start to finish","pages":"pp. 68–84","blurb":"The finale: take one gene through transcription, translation and sorting, one call at a time.","summary":"From gene to finished, delivered protein. Try the codon desk for endless practice, or take the mini exam.","terms":["central-dogma","codon","anticodon","start-codon","stop-codon","srp"]}]},{"id":"m6","n":6,"file":"module-6.html","title":"Making Life Work: Energy and Metabolism","short":"Metabolism","progressKey":"biol130-m6-progress-v1","blurb":"Where cells get energy, how they store it as ATP, the laws that govern it, and how enzymes run and regulate reactions.","topics":["Phototrophs, chemotrophs, autotrophs, heterotrophs","Anabolism, catabolism and ATP","Kinetic and potential energy; laws of thermodynamics","Free energy, ΔG and energetic coupling","Enzymes, activation energy and inhibition"],"ready":true,"outcomes":["Describe energy flow in anabolic and catabolic metabolism","Contrast kinetic and potential forms of energy and relate the structure of ATP to its role in cellular energy interactions","Explain the laws of thermodynamics as they apply to biological systems","Describe the type(s) of energy involved in chemical reactions and the role of enzymes."],"chapters":[{"id":"harvest","title":"Harvesting energy","pages":"p. 90","blurb":"Why cells need energy, and how organisms are classified by their energy source and carbon source.","summary":"Energy comes from the sun (phototrophs) or from chemical compounds (chemotrophs); carbon comes from CO₂ (autotrophs) or from organic compounds (heterotrophs). Next: what cells do with that energy.","terms":["phototroph","chemotroph","autotroph","heterotroph","cellular-work"]},{"id":"metab","title":"Metabolism","pages":"p. 91","blurb":"Metabolic pathways, and the two branches: anabolism builds and uses ATP, catabolism breaks down and makes ATP.","summary":"Metabolism is all the chemical processes that maintain life. Catabolism breaks molecules down and produces ATP; anabolism builds molecules and uses it. Next: the forms energy takes.","terms":["metabolism","pathway","anabolism","catabolism"]},{"id":"energy","title":"Kinetic and potential energy","pages":"pp. 91–92","blurb":"Stored energy vs energy of motion, chemical energy in bonds, and why weak bonds hold more energy.","summary":"Potential energy is stored; kinetic energy is motion; one converts into the other. Chemical energy is potential energy in bonds, and weak bonds hold more of it. Next: the molecule cells use to store it.","terms":["potential-energy","kinetic-energy","chemical-energy","bond-strength"]},{"id":"atp","title":"ATP, the energy currency","pages":"p. 92","blurb":"Adenine, ribose and three phosphates; AMP, ADP and ATP; and where ATP's energy is held.","summary":"ATP is adenine, ribose and three phosphate groups. Its energy is in the weak bonds between the phosphates, which repel each other. Next: the laws that govern every energy transfer.","terms":["atp","ribose","adp-amp","phosphate-bonds","cellular-work"]},{"id":"thermo","title":"Laws of thermodynamics","pages":"p. 93","blurb":"Energy is neither created nor destroyed, and every transformation loses some usable energy as heat.","summary":"First law: total energy is constant. Second law: events run downhill, and each transformation loses usable energy, often as heat, increasing entropy. Next: how this applies to chemical reactions.","terms":["thermodynamics","first-law","second-law","spontaneous","entropy"]},{"id":"freeg","title":"Chemical reactions and free energy","pages":"pp. 94–95","blurb":"Bonds breaking and forming, Gibbs free energy, ΔG, and endergonic vs exergonic reactions.","summary":"ΔG = G(products) − G(reactants). Negative ΔG: exergonic, spontaneous, energy released. Positive ΔG: endergonic, nonspontaneous, energy required. Next: how cells pay for the endergonic ones.","terms":["chemical-reaction","reactants","products","equilibrium","gibbs","delta-g","endergonic","exergonic","enthalpy","gibbs-equation"]},{"id":"couple","title":"ATP hydrolysis and energetic coupling","pages":"pp. 95–96","blurb":"Why ATP hydrolysis is exergonic, and how it drives endergonic reactions.","summary":"ATP + H₂O → ADP + Pᵢ releases 7.3 kcal/mol. Coupled to an endergonic reaction, it makes the net ΔG negative so both run. Next: why reactions need a push to start at all.","terms":["atp-hydrolysis","phosphate-bonds","adp-amp","coupling","equilibrium","endergonic","exergonic"]},{"id":"enzymes","title":"Activation energy and enzymes","pages":"pp. 95–97","blurb":"The activation energy barrier, how enzymes lower it, the active site, and the enzyme cycle.","summary":"Enzymes speed reactions by lowering the activation energy; they don't change ΔG. The active site binds the substrate, stabilizes the transition state, and releases the product. Next: how cells turn enzymes up and down.","terms":["activation-energy","transition-state","enzyme","active-site","substrate","es-complex","lock-key","induced-fit"]},{"id":"regulate","title":"Enzyme regulation","pages":"p. 97","blurb":"Competitive and noncompetitive inhibitors, reversible and irreversible inhibition, activators, and feedback inhibition.","summary":"Inhibitors lower enzyme activity (competitive at the active site, noncompetitive elsewhere; reversible or irreversible) and activators raise it. Feedback inhibition lets a pathway's end product switch off its first enzyme. Last: the whole module in one go.","terms":["inhibitor","competitive","noncompetitive","activator","feedback-inhibition","allosteric","pathway"]},{"id":"finale","title":"Making life work","pages":"pp. 89–98","blurb":"The finale: follow a meal from food to muscle work, one call at a time.","summary":"Energy in, energy stored as ATP, energy spent on work, and enzymes controlling every step. Try the enzyme desk for endless practice, or take the mini exam.","terms":["chemotroph","catabolism","anabolism","atp-hydrolysis","coupling","enzyme","feedback-inhibition"]}]},{"id":"m7","n":7,"file":"module-7.html","title":"Cellular Respiration","short":"Respiration","progressKey":"biol130-m7-progress-v1","blurb":"Glucose to ATP: glycolysis, pyruvate oxidation, the citric acid cycle, oxidative phosphorylation and fermentation.","topics":["The four stages and where they happen","Redox, NAD⁺ and FAD","Glycolysis and pyruvate oxidation","Citric acid cycle, ETC and ATP synthase","Fermentation and other fuels"],"ready":true,"outcomes":["Describe cellular respiration and the catabolic reactions that produce ATP.","Describe how glycolysis oxidizes glucose and in turn produce pyruvate, ATP and reduced electron carriers.","Describe the reduction of pyruvate and the citric acid cycle.","Explain how the electron transport chain produces a large amount of ATP.","Distinguish between aerobic cellular respiration with alternative anaerobic pathways to produce ATP."],"chapters":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview of cellular respiration","pages":"pp. 100–101","blurb":"The four stages, where each one happens, and the two ways cells make ATP.","summary":"Four stages: glycolysis in the cytosol, then pyruvate oxidation, the citric acid cycle and oxidative phosphorylation in the mitochondria. A little ATP comes from substrate-level phosphorylation; most comes from oxidative phosphorylation. Next: the electrons that carry the energy.","terms":["cellular-respiration","atp","four-stages","substrate-level","oxidative-phos","glycolysis","pyruvate-ox","cac"]},{"id":"redox","title":"Redox and electron carriers","pages":"pp. 101–102","blurb":"OIL RIG, NAD⁺ and FAD, and why oxygen is the final electron acceptor.","summary":"Glucose is oxidized to CO₂ and O₂ is reduced to H₂O. In between, NAD⁺ and FAD are reduced to NADH and FADH₂, which hold the energy until the last stage. Next: glycolysis.","terms":["redox","oxidation","reduction","electron-carriers","nad","fad","final-acceptor","free-energy"]},{"id":"glycolysis","title":"Glycolysis","pages":"pp. 103–105","blurb":"Ten reactions in the cytosol: spend 2 ATP, split the sugar, make 4 ATP and 2 NADH.","summary":"Glycolysis spends 2 ATP, splits glucose into two three-carbon molecules, and pays back 4 ATP and 2 NADH, for a net gain of 2 ATP and 2 NADH. Next: pyruvate enters the mitochondria.","terms":["glycolysis","catabolic","anaerobic","prep-phase","cleavage-phase","payoff-phase","f16bp","g3p","pyruvate"]},{"id":"pyruvate","title":"Mitochondria and pyruvate oxidation","pages":"pp. 105–107","blurb":"Two membranes and two spaces, and how pyruvate becomes acetyl-CoA in the matrix.","summary":"With O₂ present, each pyruvate enters the matrix and gives 1 CO₂, 1 NADH and 1 acetyl-CoA, so 2 of each per glucose. Next: the citric acid cycle.","terms":["mitochondria","inner-membrane","intermembrane","matrix","pyruvate-ox","acetyl-coa","coa","pdh","aerobic"]},{"id":"cac","title":"The citric acid cycle","pages":"pp. 107–108","blurb":"Acetyl-CoA in, CO₂ out: counting carbons, NADH, FADH₂ and ATP around the cycle.","summary":"Each turn takes in 2 carbons and gives out 2 CO₂, 3 NADH, 1 FADH₂ and 1 ATP, and regenerates oxaloacetate. Two turns per glucose finish its oxidation. Next: where all those carriers go.","terms":["cac","oxaloacetate","acetyl-coa","cac-intermediates","substrate-level","nad","fad"]},{"id":"oxphos","title":"Oxidative phosphorylation","pages":"pp. 108–110","blurb":"The electron transport chain, the proton gradient and ATP synthase.","summary":"Electrons from NADH and FADH₂ run down the ETC to O₂; complexes I, III and IV pump H⁺ into the intermembrane space; the H⁺ flow back through ATP synthase, which makes ATP. Next: adding up the ATP.","terms":["etc","complexes","proton-gradient","atp-synthase","fo-f1","final-acceptor","inner-membrane","oxidative-phos"]},{"id":"yield","title":"Adding up the ATP","pages":"pp. 110, 114","blurb":"How one glucose gives about 32 ATP, and where each one comes from.","summary":"About 32 ATP per glucose: 2 from glycolysis, 2 from the citric acid cycle and 28 from oxidative phosphorylation. Next: what happens without oxygen.","terms":["atp-yield","substrate-level","oxidative-phos","free-energy","four-stages"]},{"id":"anaerobic","title":"Anaerobic conditions","pages":"p. 111","blurb":"Lactic acid and ethanol fermentation, and why regenerating NAD⁺ matters.","summary":"Without O₂, pyruvate is reduced: to lactic acid in animal cells and bacteria, or to ethanol and CO₂ in plants and fungi. Either way NAD⁺ is regenerated and glycolysis keeps making 2 ATP per glucose. Next: other fuels.","terms":["anaerobic","aerobic","fermentation","lactic","ethanol","nad"]},{"id":"integration","title":"Metabolic integration","pages":"pp. 112–113","blurb":"Storing glucose as glycogen and starch, dietary sugars, fats and proteins as fuel.","summary":"Glucose is stored as glycogen (animals) or starch (plants); other sugars, glycerol, fatty acids and amino acids feed into the same four stages at different points. Last: one glucose, start to finish.","terms":["glycogen","starch","monosaccharide","disaccharide","fatty-acid","beta-ox","fuel-entry"]},{"id":"finale","title":"One glucose, start to finish","pages":"pp. 99–115","blurb":"The finale: follow one glucose through all four stages, then take away the oxygen.","summary":"From glucose to about 32 ATP, CO₂ and water, or to 2 ATP and lactic acid when oxygen runs out. Try the ATP desk for endless practice, or take the mini exam.","terms":["four-stages","glycolysis","pyruvate-ox","cac","etc","atp-synthase","atp-yield","fermentation"]}]},{"id":"m8","n":8,"file":"module-8.html","title":"Photosynthesis","short":"Photosynthesis","progressKey":"biol130-m8-progress-v1","blurb":"Light to sugar: pigments, the two photosystems, the proton gradient, the Calvin cycle and how photosynthesis evolved.","topics":["The two stages and the chloroplast","Light, chlorophyll and accessory pigments","Photosystems and the Z scheme","The proton gradient and ATP synthase","The Calvin cycle and the evolution of photosynthesis"],"ready":true,"outcomes":["Describe the chemical pathways of photosynthesis in capturing sunlight in synthesizing carbohydrates.","Describe the outcomes of the photosynthetic electron transport chain.","Explain the Calvin cycle in carbohydrate synthesis.","Describe the evolution of photosynthesis."],"chapters":[{"id":"overview","title":"What photosynthesis is","pages":"p. 117","blurb":"Light energy to chemical energy, how little sunlight ends up as sugar, and the two linked stages.","summary":"Photosynthesis is a redox reaction: water is oxidized, CO₂ is reduced, and light supplies the energy. Light capture makes ATP and NADPH; carbon fixation spends them. Next: where it all happens.","terms":["photosynthesis","efficiency","two-stages","light-reactions","carbon-fixation","oxidation","reduction","water-split","carbohydrate"]},{"id":"chloro","title":"The chloroplast","pages":"p. 118","blurb":"Outer and inner membranes, thylakoids, grana, lumen and stroma, and the overall equation.","summary":"Light capture happens in the thylakoid membranes; carbon fixation happens in the stroma. Next: how light is absorbed.","terms":["chloroplast","chloro-membranes","thylakoid","lumen","grana","stroma","light-reactions","carbon-fixation"]},{"id":"light","title":"Light and pigments","pages":"pp. 118–119","blurb":"Photons and excited states, why leaves are green, chlorophyll's two parts and accessory pigments.","summary":"Chlorophyll absorbs blue and red light, its porphyrin ring holds Mg and its phytol tail anchors it in the thylakoid membrane; carotenoids fill the gaps and protect. Next: what happens to the absorbed energy.","terms":["photon","excited-state","fluorescence","em-spectrum","pigment","chlorophyll","porphyrin","phytol","accessory"]},{"id":"antenna","title":"Antenna and reaction center","pages":"p. 120","blurb":"Energy passed from chlorophyll to chlorophyll, the reaction center's electron, and refilling it from water.","summary":"Antenna chlorophylls pass energy to the reaction center; the reaction center passes an electron to an acceptor and takes a new one from water. Next: two photosystems working together.","terms":["antenna","reaction-center","excited-state","water-split","oxidation","reduction"]},{"id":"zscheme","title":"Two photosystems: the Z scheme","pages":"p. 121","blurb":"Photosystem II and photosystem I in series, the electron's energy path, and why it looks like a Z.","summary":"Light at PSII lifts electrons from water, they lose energy down the chain, and light at PSI lifts them again to reduce NADP⁺. Next: how that chain makes ATP.","terms":["photosystem","psii","psi","z-scheme","ps-etc","nadph"]},{"id":"etc","title":"Proton gradient and ATP","pages":"p. 122","blurb":"Plastoquinone, cytochrome b6f, plastocyanin, the proton gradient, ATP synthase, and why plants still need mitochondria.","summary":"Electron flow pumps H⁺ into the lumen; H⁺ flowing back through ATP synthase makes ATP that stays in the chloroplast. Next: spending ATP and NADPH in the Calvin cycle.","terms":["ps-etc","plastoquinone","b6f","plastocyanin","ferredoxin","proton-gradient","atp-synthase","photophosphorylation","cyclic","atp"]},{"id":"calvin","title":"The Calvin cycle","pages":"pp. 123–124","blurb":"Carboxylation by rubisco, reduction to triose phosphate, regeneration of RuBP, and storing starch.","summary":"Rubisco fixes CO₂ onto RuBP, ATP and NADPH reduce PGA to triose phosphate, and five of every six triose phosphates rebuild RuBP. Next: where two photosystems came from.","terms":["calvin-cycle","carboxylation","rubisco","rubp","pga","calvin-reduction","triose-phosphate","regeneration","starch"]},{"id":"evolution","title":"Evolution of photosynthesis","pages":"p. 124","blurb":"From a single photosystem to two, and how a cyanobacterium became the chloroplast.","summary":"Two photosystems arose by horizontal gene transfer or gene duplication; cyanobacteria were the first to use water; an engulfed cyanobacterium became the chloroplast. Last: the whole path in one go.","terms":["single-ps","hgt","duplication","cyanobacteria","endosymbiosis","chloroplast"]},{"id":"finale","title":"From sunlight to starch","pages":"pp. 116–125","blurb":"The finale: follow energy from one photon to stored sugar, one call at a time.","summary":"From a photon to stored starch. Try the light desk for endless practice, or take the mini exam.","terms":["photosynthesis","antenna","reaction-center","z-scheme","proton-gradient","calvin-cycle","starch"]}]},{"id":"m9","n":9,"file":"module-9.html","title":"Cell Signalling","short":"Cell signalling","progressKey":"biol130-m9-progress-v1","blurb":"How cells signal: distances, receptors, GPCRs, amplification, termination, receptor kinases and ion channels.","topics":["The four elements and four steps of signalling","Endocrine, paracrine, autocrine and contact-dependent signalling","Intracellular and cell-surface receptors","GPCRs, cAMP, amplification and termination","Receptor kinases, Ras and ligand-gated ion channels"],"ready":true,"outcomes":["Describe process of signal transduction in a target cell from signal molecule to receptor to amplification to termination.","Compare and contrast signalling over short and long distances.","Explain the mechanism of G-protein coupled receptor, receptor kinase, and ligand-gated ion channel signalling."],"chapters":[{"id":"talk","title":"How cells talk","pages":"pp. 127–128","blurb":"What cell signalling is, the four elements every signal needs, and signalling in bacteria and in the body.","summary":"Every signal needs a signalling cell, a signalling molecule, a receptor and a responding cell, in bacteria and in us. Next: the four steps a signal goes through.","terms":["cell-signalling","four-elements","signalling-cell","signalling-molecule","receptor","responding-cell","quorum","epinephrine"]},{"id":"steps","title":"The four steps of signalling","pages":"pp. 128–129","blurb":"Receptor activation, signal transduction, response and termination.","summary":"A signal binds and activates a receptor, is transduced into the cell, produces a response that depends on the cell type, and is then terminated. Next: how far signals travel.","terms":["receptor-activation","ligand-binding-site","signal-transduction","response","signal-termination"]},{"id":"distance","title":"How far signals travel","pages":"p. 130","blurb":"Endocrine, paracrine, autocrine and contact-dependent signalling.","summary":"Endocrine signals travel far through the blood; paracrine signals diffuse to neighbours; autocrine signals act on the cell that made them; contact-dependent signals need touching cells. Next: where the receptors are.","terms":["endocrine","hormone","paracrine","autocrine","contact-dependent"]},{"id":"receptors","title":"Cellular receptors","pages":"p. 131","blurb":"Intracellular and cell-surface receptors, and the three groups of cell-surface receptor.","summary":"Nonpolar signals cross the membrane and bind receptors inside; polar signals bind cell-surface receptors, which are GPCRs, receptor kinases or ion channels. Next: G protein-coupled receptors.","terms":["polar-signal","nonpolar-signal","intracellular-receptor","cell-surface-receptor","ligand-binding-site","gpcr","receptor-kinase","ion-channel"]},{"id":"gpcr","title":"G protein-coupled receptors","pages":"p. 132","blurb":"G proteins, their three subunits, and how GDP and GTP switch them off and on.","summary":"A GPCR is off until its ligand binds; then GTP replaces GDP on the α subunit, which separates from β and γ and activates a target protein. Next: a real example, epinephrine in the heart.","terms":["gpcr","g-protein","alpha-subunit","gdp-gtp"]},{"id":"epi","title":"Epinephrine and second messengers","pages":"p. 133","blurb":"Adenylyl cyclase, cAMP, protein kinase A, and kinases versus phosphatases.","summary":"Epinephrine → GPCR → G protein → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP → PKA → phosphorylated heart proteins: the heart rate rises. Next: why a little epinephrine has such a big effect.","terms":["epinephrine","adenylyl-cyclase","second-messenger","camp","pka","kinase","phosphatase","phosphorylation"]},{"id":"amp","title":"Signal amplification","pages":"p. 134","blurb":"How one molecule of epinephrine changes thousands of proteins.","summary":"The signal is amplified three times: one receptor activates many G proteins, each adenylyl cyclase makes lots of cAMP, and each PKA phosphorylates many targets. Next: how the signal is switched off.","terms":["amplification","gpcr","adenylyl-cyclase","camp","pka"]},{"id":"term","title":"Signal termination","pages":"p. 135","blurb":"Binding affinity, GTP to GDP, breaking down cAMP, and phosphatases.","summary":"The ligand leaves, the G protein turns itself off, cAMP is degraded and phosphatases inactivate the targets. Next: the other cell-surface receptors.","terms":["signal-termination","binding-affinity","gdp-gtp","camp","phosphatase"]},{"id":"other","title":"Receptor kinases and ion channels","pages":"pp. 136–138","blurb":"Dimerization, Ras and the MAP kinase cascade in wound healing, and ligand-gated ion channels.","summary":"Receptor kinases dimerize, phosphorylate each other and turn on Ras and a kinase cascade; ligand-gated ion channels open to let ions in or out. Last: the whole module in one go.","terms":["receptor-kinase","dimerization","pdgf","ras","map-kinase","ion-channel","ligand-gated","voltage-gated","influx-efflux"]},{"id":"finale","title":"From signal to response","pages":"pp. 126–139","blurb":"The finale: compare the three cell-surface receptors, then eight calls across the whole module.","summary":"Signal, receptor, transduction, amplification, response and termination. Try the signal desk for endless practice, or take the mini exam.","terms":["gpcr","receptor-kinase","ligand-gated","amplification","signal-termination"]}]},{"id":"m10","n":10,"file":"module-10.html","title":"Cell and Tissue Form","short":"Cell and tissue form","progressKey":"biol130-m10-progress-v1","blurb":"How cells hold together: the cytoskeleton, cell junctions and the extracellular matrix, using the skin as the example.","topics":["Tissues, organs, and stem, somatic and germ cells","Microtubules, microfilaments and intermediate filaments","Dynamic instability and motor proteins","Cadherins, integrins and the five cell junctions","The plant cell wall, collagen and the basal lamina"],"ready":true,"outcomes":["Describe how cells form communities within tissues and organs to perform specific functions.","Describe the cytoskeletal elements that help the cell maintain its shape.","Explain cell to cell junctions that connect cells to one another.","Explain the role of the extracellular matrix."],"chapters":[{"id":"community","title":"Cells, tissues and skin","pages":"pp. 141–142","blurb":"Tissues and organs, stem, somatic and germ cells, and the skin as the module's working example.","summary":"Cells form tissues, tissues form organs, and the skin shows how shape, junctions and the ECM hold an organ together. Next: the cytoskeleton that gives each cell its shape.","terms":["tissue","organ","stem-cell","somatic-cell","germ-cell","cell-community","epidermis","dermis","keratinocyte","melanocyte","fibroblast","basal-lamina"]},{"id":"filaments","title":"Microtubules and microfilaments","pages":"pp. 143–145","blurb":"The three cytoskeletal elements, then tubulin and the centrosome, and actin in microvilli.","summary":"Microtubules are hollow tubes of tubulin dimers radiating from the centrosome; microfilaments are thin double helices of actin under the membrane and in microvilli. Next: how both grow and shrink.","terms":["cytoskeleton","microtubule","tubulin","centrosome","microfilament","actin","microvilli","mf-band"]},{"id":"dynamic","title":"Dynamic instability","pages":"pp. 145–146","blurb":"Plus and minus ends, growth and catastrophe, and how microtubules search the cell.","summary":"Microtubules and microfilaments add subunits faster at the plus end; microtubules randomly switch between slow growth and rapid shrinkage, which lets them explore the cell. Next: the motors that use them as tracks.","terms":["plus-minus","dynamic-instability","centrosome","microtubule"]},{"id":"motors","title":"Motor proteins, cilia and flagella","pages":"p. 147","blurb":"Kinesin and dynein on microtubule tracks, myosin on actin, and what moves cilia and flagella.","summary":"Kinesin walks to the plus end, dynein to the minus end, both on ATP; dynein also bends cilia and flagella, and myosin pulls on actin. Next: the third filament.","terms":["motor-protein","kinesin-dynein","myosin","cilia-flagella"]},{"id":"ifs","title":"Intermediate filaments","pages":"pp. 147–148","blurb":"The strong, animal-only filament, its types, and Table 10.1 comparing all three.","summary":"Intermediate filaments are rope-like and give mechanical strength; microfilaments and microtubules share shape, transport and division jobs. Next: the proteins that join cells to each other and to the ECM.","terms":["intermediate-filament","if-types","microfilament","microtubule","cytoskeleton"]},{"id":"adhesion","title":"Polarity, cadherins and integrins","pages":"pp. 149–150","blurb":"Apical and basolateral membranes, and the two families of cell adhesion molecules.","summary":"Epithelial cells have an apical and a basolateral side; cadherins join cell to cell and integrins join cell to ECM, both linked to the cytoskeleton inside. Next: the junctions they build.","terms":["polarity","apical","basolateral","cam","cadherin","integrin"]},{"id":"anchoring","title":"Anchoring junctions","pages":"pp. 150–151","blurb":"Adherens junctions, desmosomes and hemidesmosomes: what binds, and which filament each grips.","summary":"Adherens junctions (cadherins and microfilaments) and desmosomes (cadherins and intermediate filaments) join cell to cell; hemidesmosomes (integrins and intermediate filaments) join cell to basal lamina. Next: junctions that seal and connect.","terms":["cell-junctions","anchoring","adherens","desmosome","hemidesmosome","cadherin","integrin"]},{"id":"sealcomm","title":"Tight junctions, gap junctions, plasmodesmata","pages":"pp. 152–153","blurb":"The barrier junction, the two communicating junctions, and Table 10.2. Then build a whole epithelium.","summary":"Tight junctions seal the epithelium; gap junctions pass ions and small molecules between animal cells; plasmodesmata pass RNA and proteins between plant cells. Next: the matrix outside the cells.","terms":["tight-junction","gap-junction","connexon","plasmodesmata","cell-junctions"]},{"id":"ecm","title":"The extracellular matrix","pages":"pp. 154–156","blurb":"Plant cell walls, the animal ECM, collagen from triple helix to fiber, and the basal lamina.","summary":"The ECM is a secreted meshwork of proteins and polysaccharides: a three-layer cell wall in plants; collagen, elastin and laminin in a gel in animals; and the basal lamina under every epithelium. Last: the whole module in one organ.","terms":["ecm","cell-wall","middle-lamella","primary-wall","secondary-wall","animal-ecm","collagen","elastin","laminin","basal-lamina"]},{"id":"finale","title":"Skin under stress","pages":"pp. 140–157","blurb":"The finale: one organ, ten calls, every part of the module.","summary":"Shape from the cytoskeleton, strength from junctions, and support from the ECM: that is cell and tissue form. Try the junction desk for endless practice, or take the mini exam.","terms":["epidermis","cytoskeleton","cell-junctions","ecm","collagen","basal-lamina"]}]},{"id":"m11","n":11,"file":"module-11.html","title":"DNA Replication and Cell Division","short":"Replication and division","progressKey":"biol130-m11-progress-v1","blurb":"Copying DNA and dividing cells: the cell cycle, replication at the fork, telomeres, mitosis and how the cycle is controlled.","topics":["Binary fission and the eukaryotic cell cycle","Semiconservative replication and the enzymes at the fork","Leading and lagging strands, origins and telomeres","Chromosome packaging, mitosis and cytokinesis","Cyclins, CDKs, checkpoints, apoptosis and cancer"],"ready":true,"outcomes":["Describe the process of cell division and DNA replication in both prokaryotes and eukaryotes.","Describe the various phases of the eukaryotic cell cycle.","Explain the processes that occur during mitosis.","Explain why the cell cycle is regulated."],"chapters":[{"id":"div","title":"Why and how cells divide","pages":"pp. 159–161","blurb":"Reasons for division, the two requirements, binary fission in prokaryotes, and how eukaryotic division differs.","summary":"Prokaryotes split by binary fission: copy the circular chromosome, grow, pinch in two. Eukaryotes have a large, linear genome locked in a nucleus, so they need the cell cycle. Next: the cell cycle.","terms":["cell-division","parent-cell","daughter-cell","binary-fission","chromosome"]},{"id":"cycle","title":"The cell cycle","pages":"p. 162","blurb":"M phase and interphase, and what happens in G₁, S, G₂ and G₀.","summary":"Interphase (G₁, S, G₂) prepares the cell; M phase (mitosis, then cytokinesis) divides it. Cells that stop dividing sit in G₀. Next: how DNA is copied in S phase.","terms":["cell-cycle","m-phase","interphase","g1","s-phase","g2","g0","mitosis","cytokinesis"]},{"id":"semi","title":"Semiconservative replication","pages":"p. 163","blurb":"Each parental strand is a template, so every new DNA molecule is one old strand and one new strand.","summary":"Replication is semiconservative: the two parental strands separate at the replication fork and each templates a new daughter strand. Next: the enzymes that do it.","terms":["dna-replication","semiconservative","template-strand","daughter-strand","replication-fork"]},{"id":"enzymes","title":"The replication machinery","pages":"pp. 164–165","blurb":"Helicase, single-strand binding proteins, topoisomerase, primase and DNA polymerase, and what DNA polymerase needs.","summary":"Helicase opens the fork, SSBs hold it open, topoisomerase relieves the twisting ahead, primase lays a primer, and DNA polymerase extends it 5′ to 3′. Next: why the two new strands are made differently.","terms":["helicase","ssb","topoisomerase","primase","dna-pol","rna-primer"]},{"id":"lagging","title":"Leading and lagging strands","pages":"pp. 166–168","blurb":"Continuous and discontinuous synthesis, Okazaki fragments, primers, ligase, the trombone model and proofreading.","summary":"Because DNA grows only 5′ to 3′, one strand is made continuously and the other in Okazaki fragments that are primed, extended, re-filled and sealed by ligase, while proofreading fixes rare errors. Next: where replication starts.","terms":["leading","lagging","okazaki","rna-primer","primase","dna-ligase","trombone","proofreading"]},{"id":"origins","title":"Circular and linear chromosomes","pages":"pp. 169–170","blurb":"One origin on a circular chromosome, many origins and replication bubbles on linear ones.","summary":"Circular chromosomes have one origin and two forks that meet on the far side. Linear eukaryotic chromosomes have many origins; bubbles grow, fuse and are sealed by ligase. Next: the problem at the very ends.","terms":["origin","replication-bubble","replication-fork","chromosome","dna-ligase"]},{"id":"telo","title":"Telomeres and telomerase","pages":"pp. 170–171","blurb":"Why linear chromosomes shorten, telomere repeats, telomerase, and the Hayflick limit.","summary":"The last lagging-strand primer leaves the 3′ end of the template uncopied, so linear chromosomes shorten each round. Telomeres buffer the loss, and telomerase rebuilds them in stem cells and germ cells. Next: how DNA is packed into chromosomes.","terms":["end-problem","telomere","telomerase","hayflick"]},{"id":"pack","title":"Packaging DNA into chromosomes","pages":"p. 172","blurb":"Chromatin, six levels of condensation, haploid and diploid, and sister chromatids.","summary":"DNA wraps histones and coils up to a 1400 nm chromatid. After S phase each chromosome is two sister chromatids joined at the centromere. Next: separating them in mitosis.","terms":["chromatin","nucleosome","condensation","haploid","homologous","sister-chromatids","nonsister","centromere"]},{"id":"mitosis","title":"Mitosis and cytokinesis","pages":"pp. 173–175","blurb":"Prophase, prometaphase, metaphase, anaphase and telophase, then cytokinesis in animal and plant cells.","summary":"Mitosis separates the sister chromatids in five stages you can tell apart by where the chromosomes are; cytokinesis then splits the cytoplasm, by a contractile ring in animal cells or a cell plate in plant cells. Next: how the cycle is controlled.","terms":["mitosis","prophase","prometaphase","metaphase","anaphase","telophase","mitotic-spindle","centrosome","kinetochore","metaphase-plate","cytokinesis","contractile-ring","cell-plate","equational"]},{"id":"reg","title":"Cyclins, CDKs and checkpoints","pages":"pp. 176–178","blurb":"Cyclin-CDK complexes drive the cycle; three checkpoints and p53 stop it when something is wrong.","summary":"Cyclins rise and fall, switching CDKs on at each step; checkpoints test DNA damage, replication and spindle attachment, and p53 holds a damaged cell at G₁/S. Next: what happens when repair fails.","terms":["cyclin","cdk","checkpoint","p53"]},{"id":"death","title":"Apoptosis and cancer","pages":"pp. 179–180","blurb":"Bax and Bcl-2, programmed cell death, proto-oncogenes, tumour suppressors and the multiple mutation model.","summary":"If damage isn't repaired, p53 tips the Bax/Bcl-2 balance and the cell dies by apoptosis. Cancer needs several mutations that switch on oncogenes and switch off tumour suppressors. Last: the whole module in one cell.","terms":["bax","apoptosis","oncogene","proto-oncogene","tumour-suppressor","cancer"]},{"id":"finale","title":"One cell, start to finish","pages":"pp. 158–182","blurb":"The finale: take one skin stem cell through replication, mitosis and the checkpoints, one call at a time.","summary":"From G₁ to two daughter cells. Try the replication desk for endless practice, or take the mini exam.","terms":["cell-cycle","dna-replication","mitosis","checkpoint","telomerase","apoptosis"]}]}],"glossary":[{"k":"cell","mod":"m1","n":"Cell","d":"The fundamental, basic unit of life. Every organism is made of either a single cell or a group of cells.","p":"4","g":"Seeing cells","ch":0},{"k":"hooke","mod":"m1","n":"Robert Hooke (1665)","d":"Looked at thin slices of cork and described its chambers as \"cells\". He was really looking at empty walls, the remains of dead cells.","p":"4","g":"Seeing cells","ch":0},{"k":"leeuwenhoek","mod":"m1","n":"Anton van Leeuwenhoek (1673–1700)","d":"An early light microscopist who improved the magnifying power of microscope lenses. He saw living \"animalcules\" swimming in pond water.","p":"4","g":"Seeing cells","ch":0},{"k":"light-microscopy","mod":"m1","n":"Light microscopy","d":"Uses visible light and glass lenses. Good for whole cells and tissues, including living ones; it cannot show the smallest structures.","p":"4","g":"Seeing cells","ch":0},{"k":"electron-microscopy","mod":"m1","n":"Electron microscopy","d":"Uses a beam of electrons instead of light, so it can show much finer detail, such as the surface of bacteria or structures smaller than a micrometre.","p":"4","g":"Seeing cells","ch":0},{"k":"fluorescence-microscopy","mod":"m1","n":"Fluorescence microscopy","d":"Uses dyes that glow to label specific parts of a cell. In your notes, DAPI stains the nucleus blue and phalloidin stains actin.","p":"4","g":"Seeing cells","ch":0},{"k":"cell-theory","mod":"m1","n":"The cell theory","d":"A pillar of modern biology, with three tenets: 1. All living organisms are composed of one (unicellular) or more (multicellular) cells. 2. Each cell is the structural and functional unit of life. 3. Cells can arise only from pre-existing cells.","p":"5","g":"The cell theory","ch":1},{"k":"schleiden","mod":"m1","n":"Schleiden","d":"A botanist who noted that all plant tissues are composed of cells and that plant embryos arise from a single cell. With Schwann, proposed the first two tenets.","p":"5","g":"The cell theory","ch":1},{"k":"schwann","mod":"m1","n":"Schwann","d":"A zoologist who came to the same conclusion about animals, and saw that plants and animals are similar. With Schleiden, proposed the first two tenets.","p":"5","g":"The cell theory","ch":1},{"k":"virchow","mod":"m1","n":"Virchow","d":"A physician who added the third tenet of the cell theory: cells can arise only from pre-existing cells.","p":"5","g":"The cell theory","ch":1},{"k":"unicellular","mod":"m1","n":"Unicellular and multicellular","d":"A unicellular organism is a single cell (a bacterium, a yeast cell). A multicellular organism is made of many cells (a plant, a mouse).","p":"5","g":"The cell theory","ch":1},{"k":"exception","mod":"m1","n":"Exceptions to the cell theory","d":"Viruses, viroids and prions. They are not made of cells and cannot reproduce on their own, yet they can be copied inside living cells.","p":"5","g":"Not quite cells","ch":2},{"k":"virus","mod":"m1","n":"Virus","d":"A small amount of nucleic acid (DNA or RNA) inside a protein coat, sometimes with an outer envelope. Outside a living cell it is inert; it needs a host cell to reproduce.","p":"5","g":"Not quite cells","ch":2},{"k":"capsid","mod":"m1","n":"Capsid","d":"The protein coat of a virus, which surrounds its genome.","p":"5","g":"Not quite cells","ch":2},{"k":"viral-genome","mod":"m1","n":"Viral genome","d":"The virus's genetic material, DNA or RNA, packed inside the capsid.","p":"5","g":"Not quite cells","ch":2},{"k":"viroid","mod":"m1","n":"Viroid","d":"A small circular RNA with no protein coat. It needs the host's machinery to replicate. Example: potato spindle tuber viroid.","p":"5","g":"Not quite cells","ch":2},{"k":"prion","mod":"m1","n":"Prion","d":"A proteinaceous infectious particle with NO nucleic acid. A misfolded form of the prion protein (PrP^Sc) makes the normal form (PrP^C) misfold too, and the misfolded protein piles up, leaving holes in brain tissue.","p":"5","g":"Not quite cells","ch":2},{"k":"host-cell","mod":"m1","n":"Host cell","d":"A living cell that a virus or viroid uses to copy itself. It supplies the ribosomes, enzymes and energy the virus lacks.","p":"5","g":"Not quite cells","ch":2},{"k":"cell-properties","mod":"m1","n":"Basic properties of cells","d":"Cells are highly complex and organized, enclosed by a plasma membrane; have a genetic program (DNA); can reproduce; acquire and use energy; carry out chemical reactions; do mechanical work; respond to stimuli; and regulate themselves.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":3},{"k":"plasma-membrane","mod":"m1","n":"Cell (plasma) membrane","d":"The physical barrier that encloses every cell and separates it from the outside. It controls what goes in and out.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":3},{"k":"cytoplasm","mod":"m1","n":"Cytoplasm","d":"All the contents found inside the cell membrane. (Module 3 refines this: the contents minus the nucleus.)","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":3},{"k":"cytosol","mod":"m1","n":"Cytosol","d":"The jelly-like internal fluid of a cell: the cytoplasm minus the organelles. It contains water, dissolved ions and molecules.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":3},{"k":"nucleic-acids","mod":"m1","n":"Nucleic acids (DNA, RNA)","d":"The information molecules of the cell. DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) stores the genetic program; RNA (ribonucleic acid) carries it to be used. Covered in detail in Modules 2 and 4.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":null},{"k":"dna","mod":"m1","n":"DNA","d":"Deoxyribonucleic acid, the genetic material of every cell. Its genes are transcribed into RNA.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":null},{"k":"rna","mod":"m1","n":"RNA","d":"Ribonucleic acid. Made from DNA by transcription, then translated into protein.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":null},{"k":"protein","mod":"m1","n":"Protein","d":"The functional product of most genes, made by translation of RNA. Proteins do most of the work in a cell.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":null},{"k":"gene","mod":"m1","n":"Gene","d":"The DNA code for the information needed to produce a functional product, such as a protein.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":3},{"k":"central-dogma","mod":"m1","n":"The central dogma","d":"The flow of genetic information: DNA is transcribed into RNA, and RNA is translated into protein.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":3},{"k":"transcription","mod":"m1","n":"Transcription","d":"Making RNA from a DNA template. The \"language\" stays the same (nucleic acid to nucleic acid).","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":3},{"k":"translation","mod":"m1","n":"Translation","d":"Making a protein from the information in RNA. The language changes from nucleotides to amino acids.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":3},{"k":"bioenergetics","mod":"m1","n":"Bioenergetics","d":"How cells acquire and use energy. Example: plants convert light energy into chemical energy.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":3},{"k":"metabolism","mod":"m1","n":"Metabolism","d":"All the chemical reactions in a cell, organized into metabolic pathways such as glycolysis and the citric acid cycle.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":3},{"k":"atp","mod":"m1","n":"Adenosine triphosphate (ATP)","d":"The cell's energy currency: adenine + ribose + three phosphate groups. The bonds linking the phosphate groups have high potential energy. With one phosphate it is AMP, with two ADP.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":3},{"k":"adenine","mod":"m1","n":"Adenine","d":"The nitrogen-containing base in ATP. Adenine plus ribose is adenosine.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":null},{"k":"ribose","mod":"m1","n":"Ribose","d":"The five-carbon sugar in ATP (and in RNA).","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":null},{"k":"phosphate-group","mod":"m1","n":"Phosphate groups (AMP, ADP, ATP)","d":"Adenosine with one phosphate is AMP, with two is ADP, with three is ATP. The bonds between phosphates carry high potential energy.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":null},{"k":"self-regulation","mod":"m1","n":"Self-regulation and response to stimuli","d":"Cells respond to signals using receptors, and control their own activities. A cell that loses self-regulation can grow uncontrollably.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":3},{"k":"prokaryote","mod":"m1","n":"Prokaryote","d":"\"Before nucleus\". Single-celled organisms with no nucleus and no membrane-bound organelles. The most abundant organisms on Earth. Two domains: Bacteria and Archaea.","p":"7","g":"Prokaryotes","ch":4},{"k":"eukaryote","mod":"m1","n":"Eukaryote","d":"\"True nucleus\". Cells with a membrane-bound nucleus and many organelles. Domain Eukarya: protists, fungi, plants and animals.","p":"7","g":"Prokaryotes","ch":4},{"k":"tree-of-life","mod":"m1","n":"Tree of life","d":"The evolutionary tree of all organisms, with three domains: Bacteria, Archaea and Eukarya. Most of life's diversity is microbial.","p":"7","g":"Prokaryotes","ch":4},{"k":"bacteria","mod":"m1","n":"Domain Bacteria","d":"Prokaryotes with a single circular chromosome. Many have plasmids, and they have a cell wall surrounding the cell membrane.","p":"7","g":"Prokaryotes","ch":4},{"k":"archaea","mod":"m1","n":"Domain Archaea","d":"Prokaryotes with a single circular chromosome and an isoprenoid cell membrane. They often inhabit extreme environments. Eukaryotes are thought to have descended from within the Archaea.","p":"7","g":"Prokaryotes","ch":4},{"k":"eukarya","mod":"m1","n":"Domain Eukarya","d":"All eukaryotes. Plants and animals lie on branches within the Eukarya.","p":"7","g":"Prokaryotes","ch":4},{"k":"nucleoid","mod":"m1","n":"Nucleoid","d":"The region of a prokaryotic cell where its single circular chromosome sits. It is not enclosed by a membrane.","p":"6–7","g":"Prokaryotes","ch":4},{"k":"plasmid","mod":"m1","n":"Plasmid","d":"A small circle of extra DNA found in many bacteria, separate from the main chromosome.","p":"7","g":"Prokaryotes","ch":4},{"k":"cell-wall","mod":"m1","n":"Cell wall","d":"A rigid layer outside the plasma membrane. Bacteria have one; fungal walls contain chitin; plant walls are made of polysaccharides. Animal cells have none.","p":"7, 9","g":"Prokaryotes","ch":4},{"k":"flagellum","mod":"m1","n":"Flagellum (plural flagella)","d":"A long whip-like structure some cells use to move.","p":"6–7","g":"Prokaryotes","ch":4},{"k":"ribosome","mod":"m1","n":"Ribosome","d":"The machine that makes proteins (translation). Found in every cell, prokaryotic and eukaryotic. It has no membrane, so it is not part of the endomembrane system.","p":"7, 9","g":"Prokaryotes","ch":4},{"k":"binary-fission","mod":"m1","n":"Binary fission","d":"How prokaryotes reproduce: one cell copies its DNA and divides into two.","p":"7","g":"Prokaryotes","ch":4},{"k":"bacterial-shapes","mod":"m1","n":"Bacterial shapes","d":"Spheres (Streptococcus, in strings), rods (E. coli), helices (Streptomyces). Sizes range up to Thiomargarita namibiensis, the largest known bacterial cell. Myxobacteria aggregate into fruiting bodies.","p":"7","g":"Prokaryotes","ch":4},{"k":"organelle","mod":"m1","n":"Organelle","d":"A specialized structure inside a eukaryotic cell that carries out a particular job, such as the nucleus, mitochondria or Golgi apparatus.","p":"9","g":"Inside a eukaryotic cell","ch":6},{"k":"protists","mod":"m1","n":"Protists","d":"A diverse group of eukaryotes, mostly single cells but some form colonies. Includes algae (Volvox), water molds (potato blight), slime molds and protozoa (ciliates).","p":"8","g":"Eukaryotes","ch":5},{"k":"fungi","mod":"m1","n":"Fungi","d":"Eukaryotes with cell walls containing chitin. Includes yeasts, rusts, smuts, mildews, molds and mushrooms. They are heterotrophs that absorb food directly from the environment.","p":"8","g":"Eukaryotes","ch":5},{"k":"chitin","mod":"m1","n":"Chitin","d":"A modified polysaccharide found in fungal cell walls.","p":"8","g":"Eukaryotes","ch":5},{"k":"heterotroph","mod":"m1","n":"Heterotroph","d":"An organism that depends on preformed organic molecules for its carbon and energy. Fungi and animals are heterotrophs.","p":"8–9","g":"Eukaryotes","ch":5},{"k":"autotroph","mod":"m1","n":"Autotroph","d":"An organism that produces its own food. Plants use chloroplasts to convert the sun's energy into chemical energy.","p":"9","g":"Eukaryotes","ch":5},{"k":"plants","mod":"m1","n":"Plants","d":"Multicellular autotrophs with cell walls made of polysaccharides, large water-filled vacuoles, chloroplasts, and connections between cells called plasmodesmata.","p":"9","g":"Eukaryotes","ch":5},{"k":"animals","mod":"m1","n":"Animals","d":"Multicellular heterotrophs with no cell walls, which gain energy and carbon from preformed organic molecules and have a distinct mode of early development.","p":"9","g":"Eukaryotes","ch":5},{"k":"vacuole","mod":"m1","n":"Vacuole","d":"A membrane-bound sac. Plant cells have a large water-filled vacuole that gives structural rigidity by contributing to turgor pressure.","p":"9","g":"Eukaryotes","ch":5},{"k":"plasmodesmata","mod":"m1","n":"Plasmodesmata","d":"Channels through the cell wall that connect neighbouring plant cells.","p":"9","g":"Eukaryotes","ch":5},{"k":"chloroplast","mod":"m1","n":"Chloroplast","d":"The organelle where plant cells harness the energy of sunlight to make sugars. Not part of the endomembrane system.","p":"9","g":"Eukaryotes","ch":5},{"k":"nucleus","mod":"m1","n":"Nucleus and nuclear envelope","d":"The nucleus is the membrane-bound storehouse for the cell's chromosomes and the site of RNA synthesis. The nuclear envelope around it is part of the endomembrane system.","p":"9","g":"Inside a eukaryotic cell","ch":6},{"k":"nucleolus","mod":"m1","n":"Nucleolus","d":"A dense region inside the nucleus where ribosome parts are made. Not part of the endomembrane system.","p":"9","g":"Inside a eukaryotic cell","ch":6},{"k":"nuclear-pores","mod":"m1","n":"Nuclear pores","d":"Openings in the nuclear envelope that let molecules such as RNA move between the nucleus and the cytoplasm. (More in Module 3, p. 38.)","p":"9","g":"Inside a eukaryotic cell","ch":null},{"k":"endomembrane","mod":"m1","n":"Endomembrane system (EMS)","d":"Builds, modifies and transports cellular materials. Includes the nuclear envelope, rough and smooth ER, Golgi apparatus, lysosomes, vesicles and the plasma membrane.","p":"9","g":"Inside a eukaryotic cell","ch":6},{"k":"non-endomembrane","mod":"m1","n":"Non-endomembrane structures","d":"Organelles that are not part of the EMS: mitochondria, chloroplasts, peroxisomes, ribosomes, the nucleolus, cytoskeleton, centrosomes and cilia/flagella.","p":"9","g":"Inside a eukaryotic cell","ch":6},{"k":"er","mod":"m1","n":"Endoplasmic reticulum (smooth and rough)","d":"A network of membranes involved in both protein and lipid synthesis. Rough ER has ribosomes attached; smooth ER does not.","p":"9","g":"Inside a eukaryotic cell","ch":6},{"k":"golgi","mod":"m1","n":"Golgi apparatus","d":"Modifies and sorts proteins and lipids as they move to their final destinations inside or outside the cell.","p":"9","g":"Inside a eukaryotic cell","ch":6},{"k":"lysosome","mod":"m1","n":"Lysosome","d":"Contains enzymes that break down macromolecules such as proteins, nucleic acids, lipids and carbohydrates.","p":"9","g":"Inside a eukaryotic cell","ch":6},{"k":"vesicle","mod":"m1","n":"Vesicle","d":"A small membrane sac that carries material between parts of the endomembrane system.","p":"9","g":"Inside a eukaryotic cell","ch":6},{"k":"mitochondria","mod":"m1","n":"Mitochondrion","d":"Produces most of the ATP that serves as the energy currency of the cell. Not part of the endomembrane system.","p":"9","g":"Inside a eukaryotic cell","ch":6},{"k":"peroxisome","mod":"m1","n":"Peroxisome","d":"Breaks down specific organic molecules such as fatty acids, and synthesizes others such as cholesterol and some phospholipids. Not part of the endomembrane system.","p":"9","g":"Inside a eukaryotic cell","ch":6},{"k":"cytoskeleton","mod":"m1","n":"Cytoskeleton","d":"A network of protein filaments and associated proteins that gives the cell an internal structural framework.","p":"9","g":"Inside a eukaryotic cell","ch":6},{"k":"model-organism","mod":"m1","n":"Model organism","d":"A non-human species studied to understand biology in general. Your notes list E. coli, yeast, Arabidopsis, Drosophila, C. elegans and the mouse.","p":"10","g":"Model organisms","ch":7},{"k":"ecoli","mod":"m1","n":"Escherichia coli (bacterium)","d":"Used to study DNA replication, gene transcription and translation.","p":"10","g":"Model organisms","ch":7},{"k":"yeast","mod":"m1","n":"Saccharomyces cerevisiae (yeast)","d":"Used to study the cell cycle: the \"minimal model eukaryote\". Reproduces by budding.","p":"10","g":"Model organisms","ch":7},{"k":"arabidopsis","mod":"m1","n":"Arabidopsis thaliana (plant)","d":"A model for flowering plants, which are closely related to each other.","p":"10","g":"Model organisms","ch":7},{"k":"drosophila","mod":"m1","n":"Drosophila melanogaster (fruit fly)","d":"Used to study genetics and development.","p":"10","g":"Model organisms","ch":7},{"k":"celegans","mod":"m1","n":"Caenorhabditis elegans (worm)","d":"The first animal genome to be sequenced. The location, lineage and fate of every cell in the embryo, larva and adult is known.","p":"10","g":"Model organisms","ch":7},{"k":"mouse","mod":"m1","n":"Mus musculus (mouse)","d":"The \"model mammal\", with well understood genetics.","p":"10","g":"Model organisms","ch":7},{"k":"element","mod":"m2","n":"Element","d":"A substance made of only one type of atom. Around a dozen elements make up living things; carbon, hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen and phosphorus are the most abundant in cells.","p":"13","g":"Atoms","ch":0},{"k":"atom","mod":"m2","n":"Atom","d":"The basic unit of matter: a dense central nucleus of protons and neutrons, with electrons moving around it.","p":"13","g":"Atoms","ch":0},{"k":"nucleus-atom","mod":"m2","n":"Atomic nucleus","d":"The dense centre of an atom, made of positively charged protons and neutral neutrons.","p":"13","g":"Atoms","ch":0},{"k":"proton","mod":"m2","n":"Proton","d":"A positively charged particle in the atomic nucleus. The number of protons decides which element an atom is.","p":"13","g":"Atoms","ch":0},{"k":"neutron","mod":"m2","n":"Neutron","d":"A neutral (uncharged) particle in the atomic nucleus.","p":"13","g":"Atoms","ch":0},{"k":"electron","mod":"m2","n":"Electron","d":"A negatively charged particle that moves around the nucleus in orbitals.","p":"13","g":"Atoms","ch":0},{"k":"orbital","mod":"m2","n":"Orbital","d":"The region of space around the nucleus where electrons move. An orbital holds at most 2 electrons.","p":"13","g":"Atoms","ch":0},{"k":"valence","mod":"m2","n":"Valence electrons","d":"Electrons in the outermost orbitals of an atom. Their number determines how many bonds the atom can make.","p":"13","g":"Atoms","ch":0},{"k":"molecule","mod":"m2","n":"Molecule","d":"Two or more atoms combined by chemical bonds, for example H₂, CH₄ or H₂O.","p":"13","g":"Atoms","ch":0},{"k":"covalent-bond","mod":"m2","n":"Covalent bond","d":"A bond in which atoms share valence electrons in a molecular orbital. Covalent bonds are the strong bonds in biological systems.","p":"14","g":"Bonds","ch":1},{"k":"electronegativity","mod":"m2","n":"Electronegativity","d":"How strongly an atom attracts shared electrons. Oxygen and nitrogen are highly electronegative; hydrogen and carbon much less so.","p":"14","g":"Bonds","ch":1},{"k":"polar-covalent","mod":"m2","n":"Polar covalent bond","d":"Unequal sharing of electrons: the more electronegative atom pulls them closer and gets a slight negative charge (δ⁻), leaving the other slightly positive (δ⁺). The O–H bonds in water are polar.","p":"14","g":"Bonds","ch":1},{"k":"nonpolar-covalent","mod":"m2","n":"Nonpolar covalent bond","d":"Equal sharing of electrons between atoms with the same, or nearly the same, electronegativity, as in H–H or C–H.","p":"14","g":"Bonds","ch":1},{"k":"polar","mod":"m2","n":"Polar","d":"Having an uneven distribution of charge. Water is a polar molecule: the oxygen is slightly negative and the two hydrogens slightly positive, giving it an angular (bent) shape.","p":"14","g":"Bonds","ch":1},{"k":"nonpolar","mod":"m2","n":"Nonpolar","d":"Having an even distribution of charge, so there are no partial charges. Hydrocarbons such as oils and the methyl group are nonpolar.","p":"14","g":"Bonds","ch":1},{"k":"ionic-bond","mod":"m2","n":"Ionic bond","d":"Forms when one atom \"steals\" an electron from another because of a large difference in electronegativity. The resulting ions are held together by opposite charges, as in NaCl.","p":"15","g":"Bonds","ch":1},{"k":"cation","mod":"m2","n":"Cation","d":"A positively charged ion: it has an extra proton relative to its number of electrons. Na⁺ is a cation.","p":"15","g":"Bonds","ch":1},{"k":"anion","mod":"m2","n":"Anion","d":"A negatively charged ion: it has an extra electron relative to its number of protons. Cl⁻ is an anion.","p":"15","g":"Bonds","ch":1},{"k":"chemical-reaction","mod":"m2","n":"Chemical reaction","d":"The breaking and forming of chemical bonds, which transforms starting substances into different molecules.","p":"16","g":"Water","ch":2},{"k":"reactants","mod":"m2","n":"Reactants","d":"The starting substances in a chemical reaction.","p":"16","g":"Water","ch":2},{"k":"products","mod":"m2","n":"Products","d":"The new molecules formed by a chemical reaction.","p":"16","g":"Water","ch":2},{"k":"solvent","mod":"m2","n":"Water as a solvent","d":"Water is the medium of life and the most abundant molecule in organisms. It dissolves more kinds of molecules than any other liquid, thanks to its polarity.","p":"16","g":"Water","ch":2},{"k":"hydrogen-bond","mod":"m2","n":"Hydrogen bond","d":"A weak bond between an electronegative atom (such as O or N) and a hydrogen atom that is already covalently bonded to another electronegative atom. Water molecules hydrogen-bond to each other.","p":"16","g":"Water","ch":2},{"k":"hydrophilic","mod":"m2","n":"Hydrophilic","d":"\"Water loving\": highly attracted to water. Polar and charged molecules, such as NaCl or sugars, are hydrophilic and dissolve.","p":"16","g":"Water","ch":2},{"k":"hydrophobic","mod":"m2","n":"Hydrophobic","d":"\"Water fearing\": not attracted to water. Nonpolar molecules such as oils clump together in water.","p":"16","g":"Water","ch":2},{"k":"amphipathic","mod":"m2","n":"Amphipathic (amphiphilic)","d":"Having both a hydrophilic and a hydrophobic region. Phospholipids are amphipathic, an important feature in biology: in water they form micelles and bilayers.","p":"16, 30","g":"Water","ch":2},{"k":"ph","mod":"m2","n":"pH","d":"A measure of the concentration of protons (H⁺) in a solution, on a scale from 0 to 14. Pure water is pH 7. pH matters for cell function, such as protein structure and enzyme activity.","p":"17","g":"Water","ch":3},{"k":"acid","mod":"m2","n":"Acidic","d":"A solution with pH lower than 7: more H⁺ than pure water. Lemon juice (pH 2) and stomach fluid are acidic.","p":"17","g":"Water","ch":3},{"k":"base","mod":"m2","n":"Basic","d":"A solution with pH higher than 7: less H⁺ than pure water. Baking soda (pH 9) and bleach are basic.","p":"17","g":"Water","ch":3},{"k":"hydrogen-ion","mod":"m2","n":"Hydrogen ion (H⁺)","d":"A proton. A small proportion of water molecules dissociate into H⁺ and OH⁻.","p":"17","g":"Water","ch":3},{"k":"hydroxide","mod":"m2","n":"Hydroxide ion (OH⁻)","d":"The other product when a water molecule dissociates.","p":"17","g":"Water","ch":3},{"k":"organic-molecule","mod":"m2","n":"Organic molecule","d":"A molecule that contains carbon. With water removed, the human body is mostly carbon (52% of dry mass), oxygen, hydrogen and nitrogen.","p":"18","g":"Carbon","ch":4},{"k":"carbon","mod":"m2","n":"Carbon","d":"Forms four covalent bonds, single or double, so it can link into chains and rings with many other elements. In methane its four bonds point to the corners of a tetrahedron.","p":"18","g":"Carbon","ch":4},{"k":"functional-group","mod":"m2","n":"Functional group","d":"A group of atoms attached to a carbon chain that gives it chemical properties of its own. Groups with N, O, P or S are polar; the methyl group is nonpolar. They take part in reactions that build polymers.","p":"20","g":"Carbon","ch":4},{"k":"fg-amino","mod":"m2","n":"Amino group (–NH₂)","d":"Polar, positively charged at the pH of a cell, behaves as a base, hydrophilic. Found in amino acids and proteins.","p":"20","g":"Carbon","ch":4},{"k":"fg-amide","mod":"m2","n":"Amide group","d":"Polar and hydrophilic. Found in proteins.","p":"20","g":"Carbon","ch":4},{"k":"fg-carboxyl","mod":"m2","n":"Carboxyl group (–COOH)","d":"Polar, negatively charged at the pH of a cell, behaves as an acid, hydrophilic. Found in fatty acids, amino acids and proteins.","p":"20","g":"Carbon","ch":4},{"k":"fg-carbonyl","mod":"m2","n":"Carbonyl group (>C=O)","d":"Polar and hydrophilic. Found in carbohydrates and proteins.","p":"20","g":"Carbon","ch":4},{"k":"fg-hydroxyl","mod":"m2","n":"Hydroxyl group (–OH)","d":"Polar and hydrophilic. Found in carbohydrates, proteins and nucleic acids.","p":"20","g":"Carbon","ch":4},{"k":"fg-sulfhydryl","mod":"m2","n":"Sulfhydryl group (–SH)","d":"Polar; two of them can form an S–S disulfide bond. Found in the amino acid cysteine and in proteins.","p":"20","g":"Carbon","ch":4},{"k":"fg-methyl","mod":"m2","n":"Methyl group (–CH₃)","d":"Nonpolar. Found in amino acids, proteins and nucleic acids.","p":"20","g":"Carbon","ch":4},{"k":"fg-phosphate","mod":"m2","n":"Phosphate group","d":"Polar, negatively charged at the pH of a cell, hydrophilic. Found in phospholipids, nucleic acids and ATP.","p":"20","g":"Carbon","ch":4},{"k":"macromolecule","mod":"m2","n":"Macromolecule","d":"A large molecule built from smaller organic molecules. The four types of biological molecules are proteins, nucleic acids, carbohydrates and lipids.","p":"19","g":"Macromolecules","ch":5},{"k":"polymer","mod":"m2","n":"Polymer","d":"A large molecule made of many repeating building blocks (monomers) joined by covalent bonds.","p":"19","g":"Macromolecules","ch":5},{"k":"monomer","mod":"m2","n":"Monomer","d":"A building block of a polymer: amino acids, nucleotides and monosaccharides.","p":"19","g":"Macromolecules","ch":5},{"k":"condensation","mod":"m2","n":"Condensation reaction","d":"Making the chain. Monomers form a covalent bond and a water molecule is released as a byproduct. Requires energy; sped up by enzymes.","p":"19","g":"Macromolecules","ch":5},{"k":"hydrolysis","mod":"m2","n":"Hydrolysis","d":"Breaking the chain. A water molecule is inserted across the bond, splitting the polymer. Releases energy; sped up by enzymes.","p":"19","g":"Macromolecules","ch":5},{"k":"protein","mod":"m2","n":"Proteins","d":"Polymers of amino acids. They provide structural support and act as catalysts, and have more kinds of functions than any other macromolecule.","p":"19, 21","g":"Proteins","ch":5},{"k":"protein-functions","mod":"m2","n":"Functions of proteins","d":"Enzymes (catalysts), transport (across membranes, in circulation), signalling and regulation (hormones, receptors, messengers), movement (contractile proteins, flagella, motor proteins), support (cytoskeleton, extracellular matrix) and defense (antibodies, complement).","p":"21","g":"Proteins","ch":6},{"k":"amino-acid","mod":"m2","n":"Amino acid","d":"The monomer of proteins. A central α carbon bonded to a carboxyl group, an amino group, a hydrogen and an R group (side chain). There are 20 genetically encoded amino acids.","p":"21","g":"Proteins","ch":6},{"k":"alpha-carbon","mod":"m2","n":"α (alpha) carbon","d":"The central carbon of an amino acid, covalently linked to four groups: carboxyl, amino, hydrogen and R group.","p":"21","g":"Proteins","ch":6},{"k":"r-group","mod":"m2","n":"R group (side chain)","d":"The part of an amino acid that distinguishes one amino acid from another.","p":"21","g":"Proteins","ch":6},{"k":"polypeptide","mod":"m2","n":"Polypeptide","d":"A linear polymer of amino acids joined by peptide bonds. Sometimes two or more polypeptide chains combine to form a mature protein.","p":"21","g":"Proteins","ch":6},{"k":"zwitterion","mod":"m2","n":"Zwitterion","d":"A molecule with both positive and negative charges. At physiological pH (7.4) an amino acid's amino group is –NH₃⁺ and its carboxyl group is –COO⁻.","p":"22","g":"Proteins","ch":6},{"k":"peptide-bond","mod":"m2","n":"Peptide bond","d":"The covalent bond between the carboxyl carbon of one amino acid and the amino nitrogen of the next. As it forms, the carboxyl group gives up an oxygen and the nitrogen two hydrogens, making a water molecule.","p":"22","g":"Proteins","ch":6},{"k":"nucleic-acid","mod":"m2","n":"Nucleic acids (DNA and RNA)","d":"Information molecules that encode and transmit genetic information in their sequence of nucleotides. DNA is the genetic material of all cellular organisms; RNA has many roles, especially in protein synthesis and gene regulation.","p":"19, 23","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":5},{"k":"dna","mod":"m2","n":"DNA","d":"Deoxyribonucleic acid: the genetic material in all cellular organisms. Contains the information used to direct protein synthesis. Bases A, G, C, T; sugar deoxyribose.","p":"23","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":7},{"k":"rna","mod":"m2","n":"RNA","d":"Ribonucleic acid. A key player in protein synthesis and the regulation of gene expression. Bases A, G, C, U; sugar ribose.","p":"23","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":7},{"k":"nucleotide","mod":"m2","n":"Nucleotide","d":"The monomer of nucleic acids, with three parts (nucleoTide has Three parts): a nitrogenous base, a five-carbon sugar, and one or more phosphate groups.","p":"23–24","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":7},{"k":"nucleoside","mod":"m2","n":"Nucleoside","d":"Just a base and a sugar, with no phosphate (nucleoSide = base and Sugar).","p":"24","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":7},{"k":"nitrogenous-base","mod":"m2","n":"Nitrogenous base","d":"The nitrogen-containing part of a nucleotide, attached to the 1′ carbon of the sugar. The order of bases carries the information in DNA and RNA.","p":"23","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":7},{"k":"pyrimidine","mod":"m2","n":"Pyrimidines","d":"Bases with a single ring: cytosine (C), thymine (T) and uracil (U).","p":"23","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":7},{"k":"purine","mod":"m2","n":"Purines","d":"Bases with a double ring: adenine (A) and guanine (G).","p":"23","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":7},{"k":"deoxyribose","mod":"m2","n":"Deoxyribose","d":"The five-carbon sugar in DNA. It has just –H on the 2′ carbon.","p":"23–24","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":7},{"k":"ribose","mod":"m2","n":"Ribose","d":"The five-carbon sugar in RNA (and ATP). It has an –OH on the 2′ carbon.","p":"23–24","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":7},{"k":"adenine","mod":"m2","n":"Adenine","d":"A purine base (two rings), found in DNA, RNA and ATP.","p":"23","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":null},{"k":"phosphate-grp","mod":"m2","n":"Phosphate group (in a nucleotide)","d":"One or more phosphates attached to the 5′ carbon of the sugar. In a chain, a phosphate links the 3′ carbon of one sugar to the 5′ carbon of the next.","p":"24","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":null},{"k":"nucleotide-functions","mod":"m2","n":"Functions of nucleotides","d":"i. Monomers of DNA and RNA. ii. Signal molecules, such as cyclic AMP (cAMP). iii. Energy transfer: ATP → ADP + Pᵢ + energy. iv. Coenzymes, such as NAD (made with vitamin B3).","p":"24","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":7},{"k":"atp","mod":"m2","n":"ATP","d":"Adenosine triphosphate, a nucleotide. Cleaving off the terminal phosphate releases stored energy: ATP → ADP + Pᵢ + energy.","p":"24","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":7},{"k":"phosphodiester","mod":"m2","n":"Phosphodiester bond","d":"The covalent link between the phosphate of one nucleotide and the 3′–OH of the sugar of another. Its formation releases a water molecule and gives the strand direction.","p":"25","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":7},{"k":"five-three","mod":"m2","n":"5′ to 3′ direction","d":"The chain begins at the 5′ end (with a phosphate); new nucleotides are added to the free 3′–OH end. Chains are always built 5′ → 3′.","p":"25","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":7},{"k":"carbohydrate","mod":"m2","n":"Carbohydrates","d":"Sugars, made of C, H and O, usually in a 1:2:1 ratio. A major source of energy and also structural molecules, such as cellulose in plants.","p":"19, 26","g":"Carbohydrates","ch":5},{"k":"monosaccharide","mod":"m2","n":"Monosaccharide","d":"A simple sugar (mono = one), with 5 or 6 carbons, linear or (more commonly) cyclic. In cells, virtually all are in cyclic form: for glucose, C1 bonds to the O of the –OH on C5.","p":"26","g":"Carbohydrates","ch":8},{"k":"isomer","mod":"m2","n":"Isomers (6-carbon sugars)","d":"Molecules with the same formula but a different arrangement. Glucose, galactose and fructose are all C₆H₁₂O₆ but work differently. Galactose differs from glucose at carbon 4; fructose has a ketone group instead of an aldehyde.","p":"26","g":"Carbohydrates","ch":8},{"k":"glycosidic","mod":"m2","n":"Glycosidic bond","d":"The covalent link between two monosaccharides, formed between C1 of one and an –OH on a carbon of the other. Forming it releases a water molecule.","p":"27","g":"Carbohydrates","ch":8},{"k":"disaccharide","mod":"m2","n":"Disaccharide","d":"Two linked monosaccharides (di = two). Sucrose (table sugar, C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁) is glucose plus fructose.","p":"28","g":"Carbohydrates","ch":8},{"k":"oligosaccharide","mod":"m2","n":"Oligosaccharide","d":"A few linked monosaccharides (oligo = few). Attached to proteins they form glycoproteins; attached to lipids, glycolipids.","p":"28","g":"Carbohydrates","ch":8},{"k":"polysaccharide","mod":"m2","n":"Polysaccharide","d":"Many linked monosaccharides (poly = many). Long-term energy storage (starch in plants, glycogen) or structural support (cellulose).","p":"28","g":"Carbohydrates","ch":8},{"k":"glycogen-starch","mod":"m2","n":"Starch and glycogen","d":"Storage forms of glucose. Glycogen is a large, branched chain of glucose attached to a central protein. Starch is a large, branched chain of glucose found in plants such as potatoes, wheat and corn.","p":"28","g":"Carbohydrates","ch":8},{"k":"lipid","mod":"m2","n":"Lipids","d":"A chemically diverse group, and the only macromolecule that is NOT a polymer. Grouped together because they are all hydrophobic. They make up membranes, store energy and help in cell communication.","p":"19, 29","g":"Lipids","ch":5},{"k":"triacylglycerol","mod":"m2","n":"Triacylglycerol","d":"Glycerol joined to three fatty acids. The major component of animal fat and vegetable oil; used for energy storage.","p":"29","g":"Lipids","ch":9},{"k":"fatty-acid","mod":"m2","n":"Fatty acid","d":"A long chain of carbons with a carboxyl group (–COOH) at one end.","p":"29","g":"Lipids","ch":9},{"k":"glycerol","mod":"m2","n":"Glycerol","d":"A three-carbon molecule with a hydroxyl (–OH) group on each carbon. The backbone of triacylglycerols and phospholipids.","p":"29","g":"Lipids","ch":9},{"k":"saturated","mod":"m2","n":"Saturated fatty acid","d":"No double bonds between the carbon atoms, so the chain is straight and packs tightly.","p":"29","g":"Lipids","ch":9},{"k":"unsaturated","mod":"m2","n":"Unsaturated fatty acid","d":"Has one or more carbon–carbon double bonds, which put kinks in the chain.","p":"29","g":"Lipids","ch":9},{"k":"steroid","mod":"m2","n":"Steroids","d":"Lipids made of many carbon atoms bonded into four characteristic fused rings.","p":"30","g":"Lipids","ch":9},{"k":"cholesterol","mod":"m2","n":"Cholesterol","d":"A steroid that is a component of animal cell membranes and the precursor for steroid hormones such as cortisol.","p":"30","g":"Lipids","ch":9},{"k":"phospholipid","mod":"m2","n":"Phospholipid","d":"A major component of cell membranes: a glycerol backbone attached to a polar phosphate-containing head group (hydrophilic) and two nonpolar fatty acid tails (hydrophobic). Amphipathic.","p":"30","g":"Lipids","ch":9},{"k":"plasma-membrane","mod":"m3","n":"Plasma membrane","d":"The phospholipid bilayer and its proteins that surround the cell. It decides what gets in and out.","p":"33","g":"Membrane structure","ch":null},{"k":"phospholipid","mod":"m3","n":"Phospholipid","d":"A lipid with a hydrophilic (water-attracting) phosphate head and two hydrophobic fatty acid tails. In water, phospholipids line up into a bilayer with heads facing the water on both sides and tails tucked inside.","p":"34","g":"Membrane structure","ch":2},{"k":"fatty-acid-tails","mod":"m3","n":"Fatty acid tails","d":"The two long hydrocarbon chains of each phospholipid. They are nonpolar, so together they form the oily core of the membrane. That core is what blocks ions and most polar molecules. A kink in an unsaturated tail stops lipids packing tightly and keeps the membrane fluid.","p":"34","g":"Membrane structure","ch":2},{"k":"hydrophilic-head","mod":"m3","n":"Hydrophilic head","d":"The polar phosphate end of a phospholipid. Heads face the watery fluid outside the cell and the cytosol inside.","p":"34","g":"Membrane structure","ch":2},{"k":"bilayer","mod":"m3","n":"Phospholipid bilayer","d":"Two layers of phospholipids, tails pointing inward. The hydrophobic middle is the barrier; proteins set in it are the gates.","p":"34","g":"Membrane structure","ch":2},{"k":"fluid-mosaic","mod":"m3","n":"Fluid mosaic model","d":"The current model of membrane structure. Fluid: lipids and many proteins move sideways within their layer. Mosaic: the membrane is a patchwork of lipids, proteins, cholesterol and carbohydrates.","p":"36","g":"Membrane structure","ch":2},{"k":"selective-permeability","mod":"m3","n":"Selective permeability","d":"The membrane lets some substances through and stops others. Small nonpolar molecules cross the bilayer easily. Water crosses slowly on its own. Ions and large polar molecules need a protein.","p":"43","g":"Membrane structure","ch":2},{"k":"cholesterol","mod":"m3","n":"Cholesterol","d":"A lipid tucked between phospholipids in animal cell membranes. It buffers fluidity: it stops the membrane getting too runny when warm and too stiff when cold.","p":"35","g":"Membrane structure","ch":2},{"k":"integral-protein","mod":"m3","n":"Integral membrane protein","d":"A protein set into the membrane, held there by hydrophobic regions that sit among the fatty acid tails. It can only be removed by breaking the bilayer apart (for example with detergent). Most integral proteins are transmembrane.","p":"36","g":"Membrane structure","ch":2},{"k":"transmembrane-protein","mod":"m3","n":"Transmembrane protein","d":"An integral membrane protein that spans the whole bilayer, with parts on both sides. Every channel and carrier protein is transmembrane, because it has to connect the two sides.","p":"36","g":"Membrane structure","ch":2},{"k":"peripheral-protein","mod":"m3","n":"Peripheral membrane protein","d":"A protein that sits on the surface of the membrane, attached by weak bonds to lipid heads or to integral proteins. It never enters the hydrophobic core, so it can be washed off without breaking the membrane.","p":"36","g":"Membrane structure","ch":2},{"k":"glycoprotein","mod":"m3","n":"Glycoprotein","d":"A membrane protein with a carbohydrate chain attached on the outside surface. Used for cell to cell recognition.","p":"36","g":"Membrane structure","ch":2},{"k":"glycolipid","mod":"m3","n":"Glycolipid","d":"A membrane lipid with a carbohydrate chain attached, facing outside the cell.","p":"36","g":"Membrane structure","ch":3},{"k":"cytoskeleton","mod":"m3","n":"Cytoskeleton","d":"A network of protein filaments (microtubules, actin filaments, intermediate filaments). It gives the cell its shape, anchors organelles and some membrane proteins, and acts as tracks for vesicles.","p":"37","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":0},{"k":"cytoplasm","mod":"m3","n":"Cytoplasm","d":"Everything inside the plasma membrane except the nucleus: the cytosol plus all the organelles in it.","p":"37","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":0},{"k":"cytosol","mod":"m3","n":"Cytosol","d":"The fluid part of the cytoplasm, outside the organelles. Water with dissolved ions, sugars and proteins. Many reactions, including the first stage of breaking down glucose, happen here.","p":"37","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":0},{"k":"nucleus","mod":"m3","n":"Nucleus","d":"Holds the cell's DNA. Surrounded by the nuclear envelope, a double membrane that is continuous with the ER.","p":"38","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":0},{"k":"nucleolus","mod":"m3","n":"Nucleolus","d":"A dense region inside the nucleus where ribosomal RNA is made and ribosome subunits are put together. It has no membrane around it.","p":"38","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":0},{"k":"nuclear-pores","mod":"m3","n":"Nuclear pores","d":"Protein-lined openings through the nuclear envelope. mRNA and ribosome subunits leave through them; proteins the nucleus needs come in through them.","p":"38","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":0},{"k":"ribosome","mod":"m3","n":"Ribosome","d":"Makes proteins. Free ribosomes in the cytosol make proteins that stay in the cytosol. Ribosomes on the rough ER make proteins bound for membranes, for secretion, or for the endomembrane system.","p":"39","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":0},{"k":"er","mod":"m3","n":"Endoplasmic reticulum","d":"A network of membrane sacs and tubes continuous with the nuclear envelope. Rough ER has ribosomes and makes membrane and secreted proteins. Smooth ER has no ribosomes and makes lipids and breaks down toxins.","p":"38–39","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":0},{"k":"golgi","mod":"m3","n":"Golgi apparatus","d":"Stacks of flattened membrane sacs. Receives proteins and lipids from the ER, modifies them (for example by adding sugar chains), sorts them and ships them out in vesicles.","p":"39","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":0},{"k":"lysosome","mod":"m3","n":"Lysosome","d":"A membrane sac full of digestive enzymes that work at acidic pH. Breaks down material brought in by endocytosis and worn-out parts of the cell.","p":"40","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":0},{"k":"vesicle","mod":"m3","n":"Vesicle","d":"A small membrane sac that carries cargo between compartments, or to and from the plasma membrane.","p":"37–38","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":0},{"k":"endomembrane","mod":"m3","n":"Endomembrane system","d":"The connected set of membranes that make, modify and move proteins and lipids: nuclear envelope, ER, Golgi, lysosomes, vesicles and the plasma membrane. Parts are linked directly or by vesicles. Mitochondria and chloroplasts are not part of it.","p":"38","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":0},{"k":"vacuole","mod":"m3","n":"Vacuole","d":"A membrane sac used for storage. Animal cells have small vacuoles. Plant cells usually have one large central vacuole that stores water and presses outward to keep the cell firm.","p":"37","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":0},{"k":"mitochondria","mod":"m3","n":"Mitochondria","d":"Double-membrane organelles that make most of the cell's ATP by cellular respiration. The inner membrane folds into cristae, which adds surface area. They have their own DNA and ribosomes. Found in both animal and plant cells.","p":"41","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":0},{"k":"chloroplast","mod":"m3","n":"Chloroplast","d":"A double-membrane organelle in plants and algae where photosynthesis happens. Inside are stacks of thylakoids (grana). A chloroplast is one type of plastid. Animal cells do not have them.","p":"41","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":1},{"k":"plastid","mod":"m3","n":"Plastid","d":"A family of plant organelles with a double membrane and their own DNA. Chloroplasts do photosynthesis, amyloplasts store starch, chromoplasts hold pigments. Not found in animal cells.","p":"41","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":null},{"k":"cell-wall","mod":"m3","n":"Cell wall","d":"A rigid layer outside the plasma membrane of plant cells, made mostly of cellulose. It resists swelling, so a plant cell in a hypotonic solution becomes firm instead of bursting. Animal cells do not have one.","p":"37","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":4},{"k":"diffusion","mod":"m3","n":"Diffusion","d":"Net movement of a substance from where it is more concentrated to where it is less concentrated, caused by random motion of molecules. It continues until the concentrations even out.","p":"43","g":"Passive transport","ch":5},{"k":"simple-diffusion","mod":"m3","n":"Simple diffusion","d":"Diffusion straight through the phospholipid bilayer, with no protein. Works for small nonpolar molecules such as O₂, CO₂ and steroid hormones.","p":"43","g":"Passive transport","ch":2},{"k":"facilitated-diffusion","mod":"m3","n":"Facilitated diffusion","d":"Diffusion through a membrane protein, either a channel or a carrier. Still passive: the substance moves down its gradient and no energy is spent.","p":"44","g":"Passive transport","ch":5},{"k":"passive-transport","mod":"m3","n":"Passive transport","d":"Movement across a membrane down a concentration or electrochemical gradient, with no energy spent by the cell. Includes simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion and osmosis.","p":"43","g":"Passive transport","ch":5},{"k":"channel","mod":"m3","n":"Channel protein","d":"A transmembrane protein that forms a water-filled pore. Specific ions or water flow through it quickly, down their gradient. Many channels are gated: they open or close in response to a signal.","p":"44","g":"Passive transport","ch":2},{"k":"carrier","mod":"m3","n":"Carrier protein","d":"A transmembrane protein that binds its cargo, changes shape, and releases it on the other side. Slower than a channel, and it can be saturated: once every carrier is busy, adding more cargo does not speed things up.","p":"44","g":"Passive transport","ch":2},{"k":"aquaporin","mod":"m3","n":"Aquaporin","d":"A channel protein for water. Most water that crosses a membrane goes through aquaporins.","p":"44","g":"Passive transport","ch":4},{"k":"concentration-gradient","mod":"m3","n":"Concentration gradient","d":"A difference in concentration of a substance between two places. Substances diffuse down their gradient (high to low) on their own; moving them up the gradient takes energy.","p":"43","g":"Active transport","ch":5},{"k":"osmosis","mod":"m3","n":"Osmosis","d":"Diffusion of water across a selectively permeable membrane. Water moves from the side with lower solute concentration (more free water) to the side with higher solute concentration.","p":"45","g":"Water and tonicity","ch":4},{"k":"osmotic-pressure","mod":"m3","n":"Osmotic pressure","d":"The pressure that would be needed to stop water moving into a solution by osmosis. The more solute a solution has, the higher its osmotic pressure and the harder it pulls water in.","p":"45","g":"Water and tonicity","ch":4},{"k":"isotonic","mod":"m3","n":"Isotonic","d":"The outside solution has the same solute concentration as the cell. Water moves both ways equally, so there is no net change in volume.","p":"45","g":"Water and tonicity","ch":4},{"k":"hypotonic","mod":"m3","n":"Hypotonic","d":"The outside solution has a lower solute concentration than the cell. Water moves in. An animal cell swells and can burst (lysis).","p":"45","g":"Water and tonicity","ch":4},{"k":"hypertonic","mod":"m3","n":"Hypertonic","d":"The outside solution has a higher solute concentration than the cell. Water moves out. An animal cell shrinks and shrivels (crenation).","p":"45","g":"Water and tonicity","ch":4},{"k":"active-transport","mod":"m3","n":"Active transport","d":"Movement across a membrane against a gradient. It needs an energy source: ATP directly (primary) or another gradient (secondary).","p":"46","g":"Active transport","ch":6},{"k":"primary-active","mod":"m3","n":"Primary active transport","d":"Active transport powered directly by ATP. The protein splits ATP and uses that energy to change shape and push cargo against its gradient. The Na⁺/K⁺ pump is the main example.","p":"46","g":"Active transport","ch":6},{"k":"nak-pump","mod":"m3","n":"Na⁺/K⁺ pump (Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase)","d":"A transmembrane protein that uses ATP to move Na⁺ out of the cell and K⁺ into it, both against their gradients. It moves three Na⁺ out for every two K⁺ in, which also helps keep the inside of the cell negative. The Na⁺ gradient it builds powers secondary active transport.","p":"46","g":"Active transport","ch":6},{"k":"membrane-potential","mod":"m3","n":"Membrane potential","d":"The voltage across the plasma membrane. The inside of an animal cell is negative compared with the outside.","p":"47","g":"Active transport","ch":6},{"k":"electrochemical-gradient","mod":"m3","n":"Electrochemical gradient","d":"The total push on an ion across a membrane: its concentration gradient plus the pull from the charge difference across the membrane. For Na⁺, both push it into the cell. For K⁺, concentration pushes it out while the negative inside pulls it back.","p":"47","g":"Active transport","ch":6},{"k":"importer","mod":"m3","n":"Importer","d":"A transporter that moves its cargo into the cell. The Na⁺/K⁺ pump imports K⁺.","p":"46","g":"Active transport","ch":6},{"k":"exporter","mod":"m3","n":"Exporter","d":"A transporter that moves its cargo out of the cell. The Na⁺/K⁺ pump exports Na⁺.","p":"46","g":"Active transport","ch":6},{"k":"secondary-active","mod":"m3","n":"Secondary active transport","d":"Active transport powered by a gradient that a primary pump already built. One substance (usually Na⁺) flows down its gradient and that energy drags a second substance up its gradient. No ATP is used by this protein directly, but if the pump stops, this stops too.","p":"47","g":"Co-transport","ch":7},{"k":"cotransport","mod":"m3","n":"Co-transport","d":"Two substances moved by the same protein at the same time. Symport and antiport are the two kinds.","p":"47–48","g":"Co-transport","ch":7},{"k":"uniporter","mod":"m3","n":"Uniporter","d":"A carrier that moves one kind of substance in one direction. The glucose transporter (GLUT) is a uniporter.","p":"48","g":"Co-transport","ch":7},{"k":"symporter","mod":"m3","n":"Symporter","d":"A carrier that moves two substances in the same direction. The Na⁺/glucose symporter brings Na⁺ and glucose into the cell together.","p":"48","g":"Co-transport","ch":7},{"k":"antiporter","mod":"m3","n":"Antiporter","d":"A carrier that moves two substances in opposite directions. The Na⁺/H⁺ exchanger brings Na⁺ in and sends H⁺ out.","p":"48","g":"Co-transport","ch":7},{"k":"sglt","mod":"m3","n":"Na⁺/glucose symporter (SGLT)","d":"The apical carrier that brings glucose into gut lining cells against its gradient, powered by Na⁺ flowing in down its electrochemical gradient. Secondary active transport.","p":"48","g":"Co-transport","ch":7},{"k":"glut","mod":"m3","n":"Glucose transporter (GLUT)","d":"A uniporter carrier for glucose. On the basolateral side of a gut lining cell it lets glucose leave by facilitated diffusion, down its gradient into the interstitial fluid.","p":"48","g":"Co-transport","ch":5},{"k":"apical","mod":"m3","n":"Apical membrane","d":"The part of an epithelial cell's plasma membrane that faces the lumen (the gut contents). In gut lining cells it is folded into microvilli.","p":"48","g":"The gut lining","ch":0},{"k":"basolateral","mod":"m3","n":"Basolateral membrane","d":"The sides and base of an epithelial cell, facing neighbouring cells and the interstitial fluid, toward the blood. It carries a different set of transport proteins from the apical side.","p":"48","g":"The gut lining","ch":0},{"k":"tight-junction","mod":"m3","n":"Tight junction","d":"A seal between neighbouring epithelial cells near the apical surface. It stops gut contents leaking between cells and keeps apical and basolateral proteins from mixing.","p":"48","g":"The gut lining","ch":0},{"k":"microvilli","mod":"m3","n":"Microvilli","d":"Finger-like folds of the apical membrane. They increase the surface area for absorption.","p":"48","g":"The gut lining","ch":null},{"k":"lumen","mod":"m3","n":"Gut lumen","d":"The space inside the small intestine, where digested food is.","p":"48","g":"The gut lining","ch":0},{"k":"interstitial-fluid","mod":"m3","n":"Interstitial fluid","d":"The fluid that fills the space between cells in a tissue. Glucose leaving the basolateral side of a gut lining cell enters the interstitial fluid first, then diffuses into a capillary.","p":"48","g":"The gut lining","ch":0},{"k":"capillary","mod":"m3","n":"Capillary","d":"The smallest blood vessel. Its thin wall lets glucose, O₂ and CO₂ pass between blood and interstitial fluid.","p":"48","g":"The gut lining","ch":null},{"k":"atp","mod":"m3","n":"ATP","d":"Adenosine triphosphate, the cell's energy currency. Splitting off a phosphate releases energy that pumps use. Most ATP is made in mitochondria.","p":"46","g":"Active transport","ch":6},{"k":"bulk-transport","mod":"m3","n":"Bulk transport","d":"Moving large particles, or a lot of material at once, into or out of the cell inside membrane vesicles. It needs energy.","p":"49","g":"Bulk transport","ch":8},{"k":"exocytosis","mod":"m3","n":"Exocytosis","d":"A vesicle fuses with the plasma membrane and releases its contents outside. It also adds the vesicle's membrane and proteins to the plasma membrane, which is how new transporters arrive.","p":"49","g":"Bulk transport","ch":8},{"k":"endocytosis","mod":"m3","n":"Endocytosis","d":"The plasma membrane folds inward and pinches off a vesicle, bringing material in. Three kinds: phagocytosis, pinocytosis and receptor-mediated endocytosis.","p":"49","g":"Bulk transport","ch":8},{"k":"phagocytosis","mod":"m3","n":"Phagocytosis","d":"Cell eating. The cell reaches around a large particle such as a bacterium with pseudopodia and engulfs it in a phagosome. The phagosome then fuses with a lysosome, which digests it.","p":"49–50","g":"Bulk transport","ch":8},{"k":"pinocytosis","mod":"m3","n":"Pinocytosis","d":"Cell drinking. The cell takes in small droplets of extracellular fluid, with whatever is dissolved in it, in small vesicles. Not selective.","p":"50","g":"Bulk transport","ch":8},{"k":"rme","mod":"m3","n":"Receptor-mediated endocytosis","d":"Selective endocytosis. Specific molecules (ligands) bind receptors clustered in coated pits. The pit, lined with clathrin on the inside, pinches off as a coated vesicle. Cells take in LDL (a cholesterol carrier) this way.","p":"50","g":"Bulk transport","ch":8},{"k":"amphipathic","mod":"m3","n":"Amphipathic","d":"Having both a hydrophilic (polar) region and a hydrophobic (nonpolar) region. Phospholipids are amphipathic: a polar head with glycerol, a phosphate and a polar group, and two nonpolar fatty acid tails.","p":"34","g":"Membrane structure","ch":3},{"k":"micelle","mod":"m3","n":"Micelle","d":"A small sphere of lipids with their hydrophobic tails buried inside and heads facing the water. Lipids with a large, bulky head and one tail form micelles. A micelle has no inner space.","p":"34","g":"Membrane structure","ch":3},{"k":"liposome","mod":"m3","n":"Liposome","d":"A closed, hollow bilayer that encloses an inner watery space. Phospholipids added to water form liposomes on their own, which is why a bilayer makes an effective cell membrane.","p":"34","g":"Membrane structure","ch":3},{"k":"lipid-raft","mod":"m3","n":"Lipid rafts","d":"Specialized microdomains of the membrane where certain lipids and proteins gather. They act as platforms for various cell processes.","p":"35","g":"Membrane structure","ch":3},{"k":"flip-flop","mod":"m3","n":"Lipid flip-flop","d":"A lipid moving from one layer of the bilayer to the other. It is rare (lipids move sideways easily instead), so the two layers can differ in composition.","p":"35","g":"Membrane structure","ch":3},{"k":"membrane-functions","mod":"m3","n":"Functions of membranes","d":"Compartmentalization, a selectively permeable barrier, transporting solutes, a scaffold for biochemical activities, responding to external signals, interaction between neighbouring cells, and energy transduction (converting one form of energy into another).","p":"33","g":"Membrane structure","ch":3},{"k":"membrane-protein-functions","mod":"m3","n":"Membrane protein functions","d":"Transporters move ions and molecules across; receptors let the cell receive signals; enzymes catalyze reactions; anchors attach to other proteins, such as the cytoskeleton.","p":"36","g":"Membrane structure","ch":3},{"k":"endosymbiosis","mod":"m3","n":"Endosymbiotic theory","d":"The idea that mitochondria and chloroplasts descend from free-living bacteria that were engulfed by an ancestral cell and kept. Evidence: two membranes, their own circular DNA and ribosomes, division by splitting, and bacterial size.","p":"42","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":1},{"k":"outer-membrane","mod":"m3","n":"Double membrane (outer and inner)","d":"Mitochondria and chloroplasts are each surrounded by two membranes. In the endosymbiotic theory, the inner one came from the engulfed bacterium and the outer one from the host cell.","p":"41","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":1},{"k":"cristae","mod":"m3","n":"Cristae","d":"The folds of the mitochondrial inner membrane. They increase the surface area where ATP is made.","p":"41","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":1},{"k":"matrix","mod":"m3","n":"Mitochondrial matrix","d":"The space enclosed by the inner mitochondrial membrane. It holds the mitochondrion's DNA, ribosomes and many enzymes of cellular respiration.","p":"41","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":1},{"k":"intermembrane-space","mod":"m3","n":"Intermembrane space","d":"The narrow space between the outer and inner mitochondrial membranes.","p":"41","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":1},{"k":"stroma","mod":"m3","n":"Stroma","d":"The fluid inside the chloroplast's inner membrane, around the thylakoids. It holds the chloroplast's DNA and ribosomes, and is where sugars are built.","p":"42","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":1},{"k":"thylakoid","mod":"m3","n":"Thylakoid","d":"A flattened membrane sac inside a chloroplast where light energy is captured. Thylakoids are stacked into grana.","p":"42","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":1},{"k":"granum","mod":"m3","n":"Granum (plural grana)","d":"A stack of thylakoids in a chloroplast.","p":"42","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":1},{"k":"mito-dna","mod":"m3","n":"Organelle DNA","d":"Mitochondria and chloroplasts carry their own small, circular DNA, like a bacterial chromosome, along with their own ribosomes.","p":"41","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":1},{"k":"crenation","mod":"m3","n":"Crenation","d":"An animal cell in a hypertonic solution loses water by osmosis, shrinks and shrivels. A red blood cell with a wrinkled, spiky outline is crenated.","p":"45","g":"Water and tonicity","ch":4},{"k":"plasmolysis","mod":"m3","n":"Plasmolysis","d":"A plant cell in a hypertonic solution loses water, and its plasma membrane pulls away from the cell wall. The wall itself keeps its shape.","p":"45","g":"Water and tonicity","ch":4},{"k":"lysis","mod":"m3","n":"Lysis (hemolysis)","d":"An animal cell in a hypotonic solution takes in water by osmosis, swells and can burst. When a red blood cell bursts this way it is called hemolysis.","p":"45","g":"Water and tonicity","ch":4},{"k":"turgor","mod":"m3","n":"Turgor pressure","d":"The pressure of the cell contents pushing against the cell wall. A plant cell in a hypotonic solution takes in water and pushes against its wall; it becomes turgid (firm) instead of bursting.","p":"45","g":"Water and tonicity","ch":4},{"k":"endosome","mod":"m3","n":"Endosomes","d":"The network of tubules and vesicles that receives material brought in by receptor-mediated endocytosis. Early endosomes sit near the edge of the cell; late endosomes are deeper inside and also receive material from the Golgi.","p":"50","g":"Bulk transport","ch":8},{"k":"lysozyme","mod":"m3","n":"Lysozyme","d":"A lysosomal enzyme that breaks down bacterial cell walls. Together with the acidic pH of the lysosome and the H₂O₂ made by oxidases in the phagosome, it helps phagocytes such as macrophages and neutrophils kill microorganisms.","p":"50","g":"Bulk transport","ch":8},{"k":"dna","mod":"m4","n":"Deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA)","d":"The molecule that stores genetic information in all organisms. A double-stranded polymer of deoxyribonucleotides. Its structure is tied to its two functions: storing genetic information and copying itself so that information passes from one generation to the next.","p":53,"g":"DNA structure","ch":0},{"k":"griffith","mod":"m4","n":"Griffith's experiment (1928)","d":"Frederick Griffith injected mice with two strains of Streptococcus pneumoniae. Heat-killed virulent bacteria alone did not kill mice, but mixed with live nonvirulent bacteria they did: something in the dead cells' debris turned nonvirulent cells virulent. The molecule was not identified at the time.","p":53,"g":"The genetic material","ch":0},{"k":"transformation","mod":"m4","n":"Transformation","d":"A change in a cell's traits caused by taking up genetic material from outside. In Griffith's experiment, nonvirulent bacteria became virulent after contact with debris from dead virulent cells.","p":54,"g":"The genetic material","ch":0},{"k":"avery","mod":"m4","n":"Avery, MacLeod and McCarty (1944)","d":"Extracted DNA, RNA and protein from heat-killed virulent bacteria and treated the extract with enzymes. Only DNase (which destroys DNA) stopped the extract from transforming nonvirulent bacteria. Key evidence that DNA is the genetic material. Your notes: you don't need to know the researchers' names, just what the experiment showed.","p":54,"g":"The genetic material","ch":0},{"k":"nuclease","mod":"m4","n":"DNase, RNase and protease","d":"Enzymes that break down DNA, RNA and protein respectively. Avery's group used them to find out which molecule carried the transforming information.","p":54,"g":"The genetic material","ch":0},{"k":"nucleotide","mod":"m4","n":"Nucleotide","d":"The subunit of DNA and RNA. It has three parts: a five-carbon sugar, a nitrogenous base attached to the 1′ carbon, and one or more phosphate groups attached to the 5′ carbon. Memory trick: nucleoTide has Three parts.","p":55,"g":"Building blocks","ch":1},{"k":"nucleoside","mod":"m4","n":"Nucleoside","d":"A base plus a sugar, with no phosphate. Memory trick: nucleoSide is just base and Sugar.","p":55,"g":"Building blocks","ch":1},{"k":"deoxyribose","mod":"m4","n":"Deoxyribose","d":"The five-carbon sugar in DNA. It has no oxygen at the 2′ carbon: just a hydrogen (–H) where ribose has a hydroxyl (–OH).","p":55,"g":"Building blocks","ch":1},{"k":"ribose","mod":"m4","n":"Ribose","d":"The five-carbon sugar in RNA. It has a hydroxyl (–OH) at the 2′ carbon. That extra –OH makes RNA less stable than DNA.","p":60,"g":"RNA","ch":4},{"k":"carbons","mod":"m4","n":"1′ to 5′ carbons","d":"The carbons of the sugar are numbered with primes (1′ to 5′) to tell them apart from atoms in the base. The base attaches at 1′, the phosphate at 5′, and the free –OH at 3′ is where the next nucleotide is added.","p":55,"g":"Building blocks","ch":1},{"k":"purines","mod":"m4","n":"Purines","d":"Bases with a double ring: adenine (A) and guanine (G).","p":55,"g":"Building blocks","ch":1},{"k":"pyrimidines","mod":"m4","n":"Pyrimidines","d":"Bases with a single ring: cytosine (C), thymine (T, DNA only) and uracil (U, RNA only).","p":55,"g":"Building blocks","ch":1},{"k":"adenine","mod":"m4","n":"Adenine (A)","d":"A purine. Pairs with thymine in DNA (two hydrogen bonds) and with uracil in RNA.","p":55,"g":"Building blocks","ch":null},{"k":"guanine","mod":"m4","n":"Guanine (G)","d":"A purine. Pairs with cytosine through three hydrogen bonds.","p":55,"g":"Building blocks","ch":null},{"k":"thymine","mod":"m4","n":"Thymine (T)","d":"A pyrimidine found only in DNA. It has a methyl (–CH₃) group where uracil has a hydrogen. Pairs with adenine.","p":55,"g":"Building blocks","ch":null},{"k":"cytosine","mod":"m4","n":"Cytosine (C)","d":"A pyrimidine found in DNA and RNA. Pairs with guanine through three hydrogen bonds.","p":55,"g":"Building blocks","ch":null},{"k":"uracil","mod":"m4","n":"Uracil (U)","d":"The pyrimidine that replaces thymine in RNA. It has a hydrogen where thymine has a methyl group. In RNA, A pairs with U.","p":60,"g":"RNA","ch":4},{"k":"phosphate","mod":"m4","n":"Phosphate group","d":"Attached to the 5′ carbon of the sugar. At cellular pH (about 7) its hydroxyl groups are ionized, giving it negative charges. Sugars and phosphates together form the backbone of a strand.","p":55,"g":"Building blocks","ch":1},{"k":"phosphodiester","mod":"m4","n":"Phosphodiester bond","d":"The link between nucleotides in a strand: a phosphate joins the 3′ carbon of one sugar to the 5′ carbon of the next. The chain of sugars and phosphates is the backbone.","p":55,"g":"Building blocks","ch":1},{"k":"polarity","mod":"m4","n":"Polarity (5′ and 3′ ends)","d":"The two ends of a strand differ. One has a free 5′ phosphate, the other a free 3′ hydroxyl. Sequences are written 5′ to 3′; if the ends are not labelled, the left end is 5′.","p":55,"g":"Building blocks","ch":1},{"k":"five-three","mod":"m4","n":"5′→3′ vs. 3′→5′","d":"Bases can only be added to the 3′–OH of the sugar, so every nucleic acid grows 5′→3′. The template strand is read in the opposite direction, 3′→5′.","p":55,"g":"Building blocks","ch":1},{"k":"double-helix","mod":"m4","n":"Double helix","d":"The 3-D shape of DNA: two strands wrapped around each other in a right-handed helix, sugar-phosphate backbones on the outside and bases pointing in. About 10 base pairs (3.4 nm) per turn and 2 nm across.","p":56,"g":"DNA structure","ch":2},{"k":"watson-crick","mod":"m4","n":"Watson, Crick, Franklin and Wilkins","d":"James Watson and Francis Crick built the 3-D model of DNA using Rosalind Franklin and Maurice Wilkins's X-ray crystallography and Erwin Chargaff's base composition data.","p":56,"g":"DNA structure","ch":2},{"k":"chargaff","mod":"m4","n":"Chargaff's rule","d":"In DNA, %A = %T and %C = %G. This is because A always pairs with T, and C with G.","p":56,"g":"DNA structure","ch":2},{"k":"antiparallel","mod":"m4","n":"Antiparallel","d":"The two strands of a double helix run in opposite directions: one 5′→3′, the other 3′→5′.","p":56,"g":"DNA structure","ch":2},{"k":"complementary","mod":"m4","n":"Complementary","d":"Bases that pair with each other: A with T, and G with C. Knowing one strand tells you the other. (Complementary means completing each other. Complimentary means free.)","p":56,"g":"DNA structure","ch":2},{"k":"grooves","mod":"m4","n":"Major and minor grooves","d":"The uneven pair of grooves on the outside of the helix, formed because the two backbones are not evenly spaced around it.","p":56,"g":"DNA structure","ch":2},{"k":"hydrogen-bonding","mod":"m4","n":"Hydrogen bonding","d":"Weak bonds between paired bases: two between A and T, three between G and C. Weak alone, strong in large numbers. Because they are weak, DNA can unzip down the middle.","p":57,"g":"DNA structure","ch":2},{"k":"base-stacking","mod":"m4","n":"Base stacking","d":"Interactions between neighbouring bases on the same strand, stacked on top of each other. Together with hydrogen bonds, it keeps the double helix stable.","p":57,"g":"DNA structure","ch":2},{"k":"chromatin","mod":"m4","n":"Chromatin and condensation","d":"DNA wraps around histone proteins (H2A, H2B, H3, H4) to form nucleosomes (a 10 nm fibre), which coil into a 30 nm fibre, then 300 nm and 700 nm coils, and finally a condensed chromatid about 1400 nm across.","p":53,"g":"DNA structure","ch":2},{"k":"replication","mod":"m4","n":"Replication","d":"Copying DNA so it can pass to the next generation. This is one of DNA's two functions. In this module it is only mentioned: complementary base pairing lets each strand act as a guide for a new partner strand.","p":53,"g":"DNA structure","ch":0},{"k":"genome","mod":"m4","n":"Genome","d":"The genetic material of an organism, or of a compartment: the bacterial genome, the nuclear genome, the mitochondrial genome.","p":58,"g":"Gene expression","ch":3},{"k":"gene","mod":"m4","n":"Gene","d":"The unit of heredity affecting one or more traits of an organism; the DNA sequence that corresponds to a specific protein.","p":58,"g":"Gene expression","ch":3},{"k":"gene-expression","mod":"m4","n":"Gene expression","d":"The production of a functional gene product, such as a protein. Genes can be expressed (turned on) or not expressed (turned off), and expression is regulated.","p":58,"g":"Gene expression","ch":3},{"k":"central-dogma","mod":"m4","n":"Central dogma","d":"DNA is transcribed into RNA, which is then translated into protein. Exception: reverse transcription makes DNA from an RNA template.","p":58,"g":"Gene expression","ch":3},{"k":"transcription","mod":"m4","n":"Transcription","d":"Making RNA from a DNA template. The name stresses that DNA and RNA use the same language of nucleic acids. Three stages: initiation, elongation and termination.","p":58,"g":"Transcription","ch":3},{"k":"translation","mod":"m4","n":"Translation","d":"Making protein from the code on an mRNA, carried out by ribosomes. The name signals a change of language, from nucleic acids to amino acids. Covered in detail in a later module.","p":58,"g":"Gene expression","ch":3},{"k":"ribosome","mod":"m4","n":"Ribosomes","d":"The machines that translate mRNA into protein. Always in the cytoplasm. In prokaryotes they start on an mRNA before its transcription is even finished.","p":58,"g":"Gene expression","ch":3},{"k":"reverse-transcription","mod":"m4","n":"Reverse transcription","d":"Making DNA from an RNA template, catalyzed by reverse transcriptase (an RNA-dependent DNA polymerase). HIV uses it to convert its RNA genome into DNA.","p":58,"g":"Gene expression","ch":3},{"k":"rna","mod":"m4","n":"Ribonucleic acid (RNA)","d":"A polymer of ribonucleotides. Like DNA it has polarity, but it uses ribose and uracil, has a triphosphate at its 5′ end, is usually shorter and single-stranded, and can fold into complex shapes.","p":60,"g":"RNA","ch":4},{"k":"mrna","mod":"m4","n":"Messenger RNA (mRNA)","d":"The RNA that carries a gene's code to the ribosome for protein synthesis.","p":58,"g":"RNA","ch":4},{"k":"rna-polymerase","mod":"m4","n":"RNA polymerase","d":"The enzyme that carries out transcription. It unwinds DNA, reads the template strand 3′→5′ and builds RNA 5′→3′. Prokaryotes have one type. Eukaryotes have three; RNA Pol II makes mRNA.","p":61,"g":"Transcription","ch":5},{"k":"template","mod":"m4","n":"Template vs nontemplate strand","d":"The template strand is the one RNA polymerase reads; the RNA is complementary to it. The nontemplate strand has the same sequence as the RNA, with T instead of U. Which strand is the template can differ from gene to gene.","p":61,"g":"Transcription","ch":5},{"k":"ntp","mod":"m4","n":"Ribonucleoside triphosphates","d":"The building blocks for RNA: ATP, GTP, CTP and UTP. Their extra phosphates provide the energy for the synthetic reaction. Two phosphates are released as pyrophosphate each time one is added.","p":61,"g":"Transcription","ch":5},{"k":"pyrophosphate","mod":"m4","n":"Pyrophosphate (P–P)","d":"The two phosphates released when a ribonucleotide is added. The 3′–OH of the growing strand attacks the innermost phosphate of the incoming triphosphate, and the other two leave as pyrophosphate.","p":64,"g":"Transcription","ch":6},{"k":"rna-transcript","mod":"m4","n":"RNA transcript","d":"The RNA strand made during transcription. It is complementary to the template strand and grows in the 5′→3′ direction.","p":61,"g":"Transcription","ch":6},{"k":"stages","mod":"m4","n":"Initiation, elongation, termination","d":"The three stages of transcription: RNA polymerase binds at the promoter and opens the DNA (initiation), adds nucleotides one at a time (elongation), and stops and releases the RNA at a terminator (termination).","p":61,"g":"Transcription","ch":5},{"k":"promoter","mod":"m4","n":"Promoter","d":"A conserved double-stranded DNA sequence where transcription factors and RNA polymerase bind, marking where transcription starts.","p":62,"g":"Transcription","ch":5},{"k":"tata","mod":"m4","n":"TATA box","d":"A very common promoter sequence in eukaryotes, 5′-TATAAA-3′. The first nucleotide to be transcribed is usually about 25 base pairs away from it.","p":62,"g":"Transcription","ch":5},{"k":"terminator","mod":"m4","n":"Terminator","d":"The DNA sequence where transcription ends. RNA polymerase transcribes until it reaches one.","p":62,"g":"Transcription","ch":5},{"k":"sigma","mod":"m4","n":"Sigma factor","d":"The bacterial protein that recognizes promoters. It associates with RNA polymerase and directs it to bind specific promoters. Eukaryotes do not use it.","p":62,"g":"Transcription","ch":5},{"k":"gtf","mod":"m4","n":"General transcription factors","d":"In eukaryotes, at least six proteins that work together to start transcription. They bind the promoter region and help position RNA Pol II.","p":62,"g":"Transcription","ch":5},{"k":"activator","mod":"m4","n":"Transcriptional activator protein","d":"A protein that binds an enhancer. Through looping of the DNA, it contacts the proteins at the promoter and helps start transcription.","p":62,"g":"Transcription","ch":5},{"k":"enhancer","mod":"m4","n":"Enhancer","d":"A DNA sequence, often far from the promoter, that transcriptional activator proteins bind.","p":62,"g":"Transcription","ch":5},{"k":"mediator","mod":"m4","n":"Mediator complex","d":"A large protein complex that associates with the general transcription factors and RNA Pol II, and bridges them to activator proteins when the DNA loops.","p":63,"g":"Transcription","ch":5},{"k":"pol2","mod":"m4","n":"RNA polymerase II","d":"The eukaryotic RNA polymerase that makes mRNA. One of three eukaryotic RNA polymerases.","p":62,"g":"Transcription","ch":5},{"k":"bubble","mod":"m4","n":"Transcription bubble","d":"The unwound stretch of DNA inside RNA polymerase, about 14 base pairs long. Inside it, about 8 base pairs of the new RNA are paired with the template strand.","p":63,"g":"Transcription","ch":5},{"k":"channels","mod":"m4","n":"Polymerase channels","d":"RNA polymerase has channels for incoming nucleotides, for the DNA double helix to enter and exit, for the growing RNA to exit, and for releasing the RNA when transcription ends.","p":63,"g":"Transcription","ch":6},{"k":"primary-transcript","mod":"m4","n":"Primary transcript","d":"The RNA that comes straight off the template strand. In prokaryotes it is already the mRNA. In eukaryotes it (pre-mRNA) must be processed first.","p":65,"g":"RNA processing","ch":7},{"k":"polycistronic","mod":"m4","n":"Polycistronic mRNA","d":"A prokaryotic mRNA that carries the information for more than one gene.","p":65,"g":"RNA processing","ch":7},{"k":"coupled","mod":"m4","n":"Coupled transcription and translation","d":"In prokaryotes there is no nuclear envelope, so ribosomes start translating an mRNA before transcription is finished. That makes prokaryotic gene expression faster.","p":65,"g":"RNA processing","ch":7},{"k":"rna-processing","mod":"m4","n":"RNA processing","d":"The chemical changes that turn a eukaryotic primary transcript into mRNA that can leave the nucleus: adding a 5′ cap, removing introns, and adding a poly(A) tail.","p":65,"g":"RNA processing","ch":8},{"k":"cap","mod":"m4","n":"5′ cap","d":"A 7-methylguanosine added to the 5′ end of the primary transcript by a unique 5′–5′ bond through three phosphates. It protects the mRNA from exonucleases and is needed for the ribosome to recognize the mRNA.","p":66,"g":"RNA processing","ch":8},{"k":"polya","mod":"m4","n":"Polyadenylation: poly(A) tail","d":"Adding about 250 adenines to the 3′ end of the mRNA. The tail has roles in transcription termination, export of the mRNA to the cytoplasm, and protection from exonucleases.","p":66,"g":"RNA processing","ch":8},{"k":"exonuclease","mod":"m4","n":"Exonucleases","d":"Enzymes that break down nucleic acids from their ends. The 5′ cap and poly(A) tail protect mRNA from them.","p":66,"g":"RNA processing","ch":8},{"k":"splicing","mod":"m4","n":"RNA splicing","d":"Cutting introns out of the primary transcript and joining the exons together.","p":66,"g":"RNA processing","ch":8},{"k":"introns-exons","mod":"m4","n":"Introns and exons","d":"Introns are sequences removed from the primary transcript. Exons are the sequences left intact and joined together in the mRNA.","p":66,"g":"RNA processing","ch":8},{"k":"alt-splicing","mod":"m4","n":"Alternative splicing","d":"Splicing one gene's primary transcript in different ways, so a single gene can produce different but related proteins in different cells.","p":66,"g":"RNA processing","ch":8},{"k":"protein","mod":"m5","n":"Protein","d":"A linear polymer of a combination of 20 amino acids, like letters in an alphabet. Proteins are ubiquitous, diverse and versatile, and they mediate most cellular activities. Its function depends on its structure.","p":"69","g":"Amino acids","ch":0},{"k":"amino-acid","mod":"m5","n":"Amino acid","d":"The building block of proteins. Four components are bound to the α carbon: a carboxyl group, an amino group and a hydrogen (the same in all amino acids) and an R group (side chain), which makes each one unique.","p":"69","g":"Amino acids","ch":0},{"k":"alpha-carbon","mod":"m5","n":"α (alpha) carbon","d":"The central carbon of an amino acid, bound to the amino group, the carboxyl group, a hydrogen and the R group.","p":"69","g":"Amino acids","ch":0},{"k":"fg-amino","mod":"m5","n":"Amino group","d":"–NH₂, one of the three components shared by all amino acids. At physiological pH it is charged: –NH₃⁺.","p":"69","g":"Amino acids","ch":null},{"k":"fg-carboxyl","mod":"m5","n":"Carboxyl group","d":"–COOH, one of the three components shared by all amino acids. At physiological pH it is charged: –COO⁻.","p":"69","g":"Amino acids","ch":null},{"k":"r-group","mod":"m5","n":"R group (side chain)","d":"The fourth component of an amino acid. R groups make each amino acid unique and are responsible for its chemical and physical properties. They are not involved in the peptide bond.","p":"69","g":"Amino acids","ch":0},{"k":"aa-classes","mod":"m5","n":"Amino acid classification","d":"R groups are grouped by polarity (polar or nonpolar), interaction with water (hydrophilic or hydrophobic) and charge at physiological pH (acidic and negative, basic and positive, or neutral). Your notes: understand the groups, you don't need to memorize all 20 structures.","p":"70","g":"Amino acids","ch":0},{"k":"hydrophobic-aa","mod":"m5","n":"Hydrophobic amino acids","d":"Nonpolar R groups (e.g. alanine, valine, leucine, phenylalanine). They tend to be buried in the interior of folded proteins, aggregating away from water; weak van der Waals forces help hold them together.","p":"70","g":"Amino acids","ch":0},{"k":"hydrophilic-aa","mod":"m5","n":"Hydrophilic amino acids","d":"R groups with electronegative N and/or O, so they can H-bond with each other or water. Includes polar (e.g. serine), basic (positively charged, e.g. lysine) and acidic (negatively charged, e.g. aspartic acid) amino acids. Usually on the outer surface of proteins; charged ones can form ionic bonds.","p":"70","g":"Amino acids","ch":0},{"k":"van-der-waals","mod":"m5","n":"van der Waals forces","d":"Weak attractions that help hydrophobic R groups stick to each other inside a folded protein.","p":"70","g":"Amino acids","ch":0},{"k":"glycine","mod":"m5","n":"Glycine (Gly, G)","d":"Its R group is a hydrogen, so the α carbon carries two H atoms (not asymmetric). Small and nonpolar, it allows free rotation around the C–N bond and makes the backbone more flexible.","p":"71","g":"Special amino acids","ch":1},{"k":"proline","mod":"m5","n":"Proline (Pro, P)","d":"Its R group links back to the amino group. This restricts rotation of the C–N bond and limits how much the protein can fold around proline.","p":"71","g":"Special amino acids","ch":1},{"k":"cysteine","mod":"m5","n":"Cysteine (Cys, C)","d":"Its R group has a sulfhydryl (–SH) group. Two cysteines can form an S–S disulfide bond, a cross-bridge linking different parts of one protein or different proteins.","p":"71","g":"Special amino acids","ch":1},{"k":"disulfide","mod":"m5","n":"Disulfide bond (S–S)","d":"A covalent bond between the –SH groups of two cysteine residues. It cross-links parts of a protein and helps hold tertiary and quaternary structure.","p":"71, 74","g":"Special amino acids","ch":1},{"k":"peptide-bond","mod":"m5","n":"Peptide bond","d":"Joins adjacent amino acids. A dehydration reaction between the carboxyl group of one amino acid and the amino group of the next releases a molecule of H₂O. R groups are not involved.","p":"71","g":"Linking amino acids","ch":2},{"k":"condensation","mod":"m5","n":"Dehydration (condensation) reaction","d":"A reaction that joins two molecules and releases water. Forming each peptide bond releases one H₂O.","p":"71","g":"Linking amino acids","ch":2},{"k":"n-terminus","mod":"m5","n":"N-terminus and C-terminus","d":"The free amino group at the amino end of a polypeptide is the N-terminus; the free carboxyl group at the other end is the C-terminus. Sequences are written N to C.","p":"71","g":"Linking amino acids","ch":2},{"k":"polypeptide","mod":"m5","n":"Polypeptide","d":"A polymer of amino acids connected by peptide bonds, used synonymously with protein. Its backbone repeats N–C–C.","p":"71","g":"Linking amino acids","ch":2},{"k":"residue","mod":"m5","n":"Amino acid residue","d":"What remains of an amino acid once it is part of a polypeptide (after forming peptide bonds).","p":"68","g":"Linking amino acids","ch":2},{"k":"levels","mod":"m5","n":"Levels of protein structure","d":"Primary (1°), secondary (2°), tertiary (3°) and quaternary (4°). The 3-D shape, or conformation, is described by 2°, 3° and 4°.","p":"72","g":"Protein structure","ch":3},{"k":"conformation","mod":"m5","n":"Protein conformation","d":"The three-dimensional structure of a protein, described by its secondary, tertiary and quaternary structure.","p":"72","g":"Protein structure","ch":3},{"k":"primary-structure","mod":"m5","n":"Primary structure","d":"The specific linear sequence of amino acids from the amino end to the carboxyl end, coded for by the genome. Written in three-letter (Val–Gly–Ala–His) or one-letter (V–G–A–H) form. It determines the 2°, 3° and 4° structure.","p":"72","g":"Protein structure","ch":3},{"k":"secondary-structure","mod":"m5","n":"Secondary structure","d":"The conformation of portions of the chain: the α helix and the β sheet. Formed by hydrogen bonds between backbone functional groups of nearby amino acids (C=O and N–H). R groups are not involved.","p":"73","g":"Protein structure","ch":3},{"k":"alpha-helix","mod":"m5","n":"Alpha (α) helix","d":"A very stable right-handed helix. Each carbonyl group in the backbone H-bonds with the amide group of the amino acid four positions away. Drawn as a helical ribbon.","p":"73","g":"Protein structure","ch":3},{"k":"beta-sheet","mod":"m5","n":"Beta (β) sheet","d":"Segments of the chain lying side by side in a pleated conformation, parallel or antiparallel, held by H-bonds between carbonyl groups in one strand and amide groups in the other. Drawn as flat arrows.","p":"73","g":"Protein structure","ch":3},{"k":"tertiary-structure","mod":"m5","n":"Tertiary structure","d":"The 3-D shape of a whole single polypeptide: how its 2° regions are oriented. Set by where hydrophilic and hydrophobic R groups sit, and by bonds between R groups: hydrogen bonds, hydrophobic interactions, ionic bonds and disulfide bonds. Usually the functional form, e.g. with an enzyme's active site.","p":"74","g":"Protein structure","ch":3},{"k":"quaternary-structure","mod":"m5","n":"Quaternary structure","d":"The spatial arrangement of two or more polypeptide subunits, held by the same bonds as tertiary structure.","p":"74","g":"Protein structure","ch":3},{"k":"subunit","mod":"m5","n":"Subunit; homodimer and heterodimer","d":"Each polypeptide chain in a multi-chain protein is a subunit. Two identical subunits make a homodimer (homo = same, di = two); two different ones make a heterodimer.","p":"74","g":"Protein structure","ch":3},{"k":"central-dogma","mod":"m5","n":"The central dogma","d":"DNA is transcribed into mRNA, and mRNA is translated into protein. Translation is the final stage: the order of bases in the mRNA specifies the order of amino acids.","p":"75","g":"Translation basics","ch":4},{"k":"translation","mod":"m5","n":"Translation","d":"Making a polypeptide from the information in mRNA. Needs mRNA, ribosomes, tRNAs, aminoacyl tRNA synthetases, and initiation, elongation and release factors.","p":"75","g":"Translation basics","ch":4},{"k":"mrna","mod":"m5","n":"Messenger RNA (mRNA)","d":"The RNA copy of a gene that carries the code for a protein to the ribosome. It is read 5′ to 3′, one codon at a time.","p":"75","g":"Translation basics","ch":4},{"k":"ribosome","mod":"m5","n":"Ribosome","d":"The protein factory where translation takes place: a complex of RNA and protein made of a small and a large subunit. Eukaryotic ribosomes are larger than prokaryotic ones. It moves along the mRNA 5′ to 3′.","p":"75–76","g":"Translation basics","ch":5},{"k":"sites","mod":"m5","n":"A site, P site, E site","d":"Three tRNA binding sites in the large subunit. A (aminoacyl) site: a charged tRNA enters. P (peptidyl) site: holds the tRNA with the growing chain. E (exit) site: the empty tRNA leaves.","p":"76","g":"Translation basics","ch":5},{"k":"codon","mod":"m5","n":"Codon","d":"A group of three adjacent nucleotides in mRNA that specifies one amino acid (or stop). Codons are read as a nonoverlapping series of triplets.","p":"76","g":"The genetic code","ch":4},{"k":"reading-frame","mod":"m5","n":"Reading frame","d":"Where the ribosome begins reading the sequence, which sets how it is divided into triplets. ZWTHEBIGBOY... only makes sense read as ZW THE BIG BOY.","p":"76","g":"The genetic code","ch":4},{"k":"trna","mod":"m5","n":"Transfer RNA (tRNA)","d":"A small RNA that base pairs with itself into a cloverleaf (an L shape in 3-D). It carries an amino acid on the 3′–OH of its 5′-CCA-3′ end and has an anticodon in its anticodon loop.","p":"76","g":"Translation basics","ch":5},{"k":"anticodon","mod":"m5","n":"Anticodon","d":"Three bases in the tRNA's anticodon loop that pair with the mRNA codon. Pairing is antiparallel: the first (5′) base of the codon pairs with the last (3′) base of the anticodon. AUG pairs with 3′-UAC-5′.","p":"76, 78","g":"The genetic code","ch":5},{"k":"synthetase","mod":"m5","n":"Aminoacyl tRNA synthetase","d":"An enzyme that attaches a specific amino acid to the 3′ end of its matching tRNA. Each one binds one uncharged tRNA and its amino acid. They are very accurate.","p":"77","g":"Translation basics","ch":5},{"k":"charged","mod":"m5","n":"Charged and uncharged tRNA","d":"A tRNA with its amino acid attached is charged; without one it is uncharged.","p":"77","g":"Translation basics","ch":5},{"k":"genetic-code","mod":"m5","n":"The genetic code","d":"64 codons specify 20 amino acids plus stop. Read 5′ → 3′ on the mRNA. Your notes: you don't need to memorize the table.","p":"78","g":"The genetic code","ch":4},{"k":"redundant","mod":"m5","n":"Redundant (degenerate) code","d":"Many amino acids are specified by more than one codon. For example, leucine has six codons.","p":"78","g":"The genetic code","ch":4},{"k":"start-codon","mod":"m5","n":"Start codon (AUG)","d":"AUG codes for methionine and marks where translation begins, so methionine is the first amino acid.","p":"78–79","g":"Translation steps","ch":4},{"k":"stop-codon","mod":"m5","n":"Stop codons","d":"UAA, UAG and UGA. They code for no amino acid; a release factor binds there instead and translation ends.","p":"80","g":"Translation steps","ch":4},{"k":"initiation","mod":"m5","n":"Initiation","d":"Stage 1: the AUG start codon is recognized and methionine becomes the first amino acid. In eukaryotes, initiation factors and the small subunit bind at the 5′ cap and scan for AUG; then the large subunit joins and the initiation factors are released.","p":"78–79","g":"Translation steps","ch":6},{"k":"initiation-factors","mod":"m5","n":"Initiation factors","d":"Proteins that recruit the small ribosomal subunit and the Met tRNA, help scan for AUG, and are released when the large subunit joins.","p":"79","g":"Translation steps","ch":6},{"k":"elongation","mod":"m5","n":"Elongation","d":"Stage 2: a charged tRNA enters the A site, the chain is transferred from the P site tRNA to the A site amino acid (a peptide bond made by the peptidyl transferase centre, which is rRNA), and the ribosome shifts one codon toward 3′. The empty tRNA moves to E and leaves.","p":"79","g":"Translation steps","ch":6},{"k":"peptidyl-transferase","mod":"m5","n":"Peptidyl transferase centre","d":"The part of the large subunit, between the A and P sites and made of rRNA, that catalyzes peptide bond formation.","p":"79","g":"Translation steps","ch":6},{"k":"termination","mod":"m5","n":"Termination","d":"Stage 3: at a stop codon a release factor binds the A site. The bond between the chain and the last tRNA breaks, creating the C-terminus, and the polypeptide is released.","p":"80","g":"Translation steps","ch":6},{"k":"release-factor","mod":"m5","n":"Release factor","d":"A protein that binds the A site at a stop codon and frees the finished polypeptide.","p":"80","g":"Translation steps","ch":6},{"k":"shine-dalgarno","mod":"m5","n":"Shine-Dalgarno sequence","d":"An internal sequence (5′-AGGAGGU-3′) in prokaryotic mRNA where the ribosome binds, since prokaryotic mRNA has no 5′ cap. Translation starts at the next AUG downstream.","p":"80","g":"Translation steps","ch":7},{"k":"polycistronic","mod":"m5","n":"Polycistronic mRNA","d":"A prokaryotic mRNA carrying several genes. It has several Shine-Dalgarno sequences, so one mRNA can make several polypeptides.","p":"80","g":"Translation steps","ch":7},{"k":"gene-regulation","mod":"m5","n":"Gene regulation","d":"Not all genes are expressed all the time. Control points include DNA accessibility, transcription factors, RNA processing and post-translational modification.","p":"81","g":"After translation","ch":8},{"k":"ptm","mod":"m5","n":"Post-translational modification","d":"Changes made to a protein after it is made. Many proteins are made inactive and must be activated, for example by cleavage or by adding a phosphate (phosphorylation).","p":"81","g":"After translation","ch":8},{"k":"sorting","mod":"m5","n":"Protein sorting","d":"Every protein starts on a ribosome in the cytosol. Where it goes depends on its signal sequence: none stays in the cytosol; amino-terminal goes to chloroplasts or mitochondria; internal goes to the nucleus; an ER signal sends the ribosome to the rough ER.","p":"81–82","g":"After translation","ch":8},{"k":"signal-sequence","mod":"m5","n":"Signal sequences","d":"Short stretches of amino acids that act as address labels. Their position in the chain (amino-terminal or internal) and type decide where the protein goes.","p":"81–82","g":"After translation","ch":8},{"k":"srp","mod":"m5","n":"Signal-recognition particle (SRP)","d":"Binds the ER signal sequence at the amino-terminal end of a growing chain and halts translation. It docks on the SRP receptor of the ER, the ribosome is moved to a channel, the SRP leaves, and synthesis resumes into the ER lumen. The signal sequence is cleaved.","p":"82","g":"After translation","ch":8},{"k":"signal-anchor","mod":"m5","n":"Signal-anchor sequence","d":"A sequence that stops a protein being threaded fully through the ER channel; the channel releases it sideways into the membrane, so it becomes a membrane protein.","p":"82","g":"After translation","ch":8},{"k":"er-fates","mod":"m5","n":"Fates of rough ER proteins","d":"Proteins made on the rough ER end up embedded in the ER membrane, inside the lumen of the endomembrane system, or secreted out of the cell.","p":"82","g":"After translation","ch":8},{"k":"phototroph","mod":"m6","n":"Phototroph","d":"An organism that obtains energy from the sun. Plants are the most common: they use the sun's energy to convert CO₂ and water into sugar and O₂.","p":"90","g":"Harvesting energy","ch":0},{"k":"chemotroph","mod":"m6","n":"Chemotroph","d":"An organism that obtains energy from chemical compounds. Animals are the most common chemotrophs: they ingest other organisms and break them down into CO₂ and water.","p":"90","g":"Harvesting energy","ch":0},{"k":"autotroph","mod":"m6","n":"Autotroph","d":"An organism that can convert CO₂ into glucose, so it makes its own organic source of carbon (carbon from inorganic sources such as CO₂). Examples: plants, cyanobacteria, sulfur-oxidizing bacteria.","p":"90","g":"Harvesting energy","ch":0},{"k":"heterotroph","mod":"m6","n":"Heterotroph","d":"An organism that cannot convert CO₂, so it must ingest other organisms or molecules for a source of carbon (carbon from organic compounds). Examples: animals, most bacteria.","p":"90","g":"Harvesting energy","ch":0},{"k":"metabolism","mod":"m6","n":"Metabolism","d":"The chemical processes that occur in a living organism to maintain life. It includes building up and breaking down chemical compounds, which lets energy be harnessed or released. These reactions happen continuously in cells.","p":"91","g":"Metabolism","ch":1},{"k":"pathway","mod":"m6","n":"Metabolic pathway","d":"A series of linked reactions in which the products of one reaction are the reactants of the next.","p":"91","g":"Metabolism","ch":1},{"k":"anabolism","mod":"m6","n":"Anabolic (anabolism)","d":"The branch of metabolism that builds molecules from smaller units (e.g. amino acids into proteins). It requires an input of energy (ATP).","p":"91","g":"Metabolism","ch":1},{"k":"catabolism","mod":"m6","n":"Catabolic (catabolism)","d":"The branch of metabolism that breaks molecules down into smaller units (e.g. macromolecules into sugars, amino acids, fatty acids, nucleotides). It produces energy (ATP).","p":"91","g":"Metabolism","ch":1},{"k":"potential-energy","mod":"m6","n":"Potential energy","d":"Energy that is not associated with movement but is stored, like a ball at the top of a staircase. It can be converted to other forms.","p":"91","g":"Forms of energy","ch":2},{"k":"kinetic-energy","mod":"m6","n":"Kinetic energy","d":"The energy of motion, like a ball bouncing down a staircase. Energy can be converted from one form to another.","p":"91","g":"Forms of energy","ch":2},{"k":"chemical-energy","mod":"m6","n":"Chemical energy","d":"The form of potential energy held in the chemical bonds between pairs of atoms in a molecule.","p":"92","g":"Forms of energy","ch":2},{"k":"bond-strength","mod":"m6","n":"Bond strength and potential energy","d":"Bond strength and potential energy are linked: strong bonds hold less potential energy, weak bonds hold more.","p":"92","g":"Forms of energy","ch":2},{"k":"atp","mod":"m6","n":"Adenosine triphosphate (ATP)","d":"The energy currency of the cell: the most common chemical form of readily accessible energy. Made of adenine, a five-carbon sugar (ribose) and three phosphate groups. Its chemical energy is held in the bonds connecting the phosphate groups.","p":"92","g":"ATP","ch":3},{"k":"adp-amp","mod":"m6","n":"ADP and AMP","d":"With only two phosphate groups the molecule is adenosine diphosphate (ADP); with only one it is adenosine monophosphate (AMP). Adenine plus ribose alone is adenosine.","p":"92","g":"ATP","ch":3},{"k":"ribose","mod":"m6","n":"Ribose","d":"The five-carbon sugar in ATP. Adenine is attached to one carbon and the phosphate chain to another (through its CH₂).","p":"92","g":"ATP","ch":3},{"k":"phosphate-bonds","mod":"m6","n":"Bonds between phosphate groups","d":"The bonds linking ATP's phosphate groups have high potential energy because they are weak. At physiological pH the phosphate groups are negatively charged and resist (repel) one another. Breaking these bonds releases the energy for the cell to do work.","p":"92, 95","g":"ATP","ch":3},{"k":"cellular-work","mod":"m6","n":"Cellular work","d":"What cells use energy for: movement, growth, pumping ions and substances across membranes, vesicle movement, DNA, RNA and protein synthesis, breaking down fats and proteins, and the reactions needed for cellular function.","p":"90, 92","g":"ATP","ch":0},{"k":"atp-hydrolysis","mod":"m6","n":"ATP hydrolysis","d":"ATP + H₂O → ADP + Pᵢ (inorganic phosphate). The reaction of ATP with water is exergonic (spontaneous) and releases free energy (ΔG = −7.3 kcal/mol). ADP has two phosphate groups, so it has lower potential energy than ATP.","p":"95","g":"ATP","ch":6},{"k":"thermodynamics","mod":"m6","n":"Thermodynamics","d":"The study of changes in energy that accompany events in the universe. Its concepts predict the direction events will take and whether an input of energy is required.","p":"93","g":"Thermodynamics","ch":4},{"k":"first-law","mod":"m6","n":"First law of thermodynamics","d":"Energy can be neither created nor destroyed. It can be converted from one form to another, so total energy before a transformation equals total energy after.","p":"93","g":"Thermodynamics","ch":4},{"k":"second-law","mod":"m6","n":"Second law of thermodynamics","d":"Events in the universe have direction: they proceed from a higher energy state to a lower one. Each transformation reduces the amount of usable energy, because energy transformations are not 100% efficient; some is lost, often as heat, increasing entropy.","p":"93","g":"Thermodynamics","ch":4},{"k":"spontaneous","mod":"m6","n":"Spontaneous (thermodynamically favourable)","d":"An event that proceeds from a higher to a lower energy state with no input of external energy.","p":"93","g":"Thermodynamics","ch":4},{"k":"entropy","mod":"m6","n":"Entropy (S)","d":"Energy lost to disorder. Energy released in a transformation, often as heat, increases entropy. Temperature influences the movement of molecules and adds to the degree of disorder.","p":"93, 95","g":"Thermodynamics","ch":4},{"k":"chemical-reaction","mod":"m6","n":"Chemical reaction","d":"A process in which the bonds linking atoms change while the atoms keep their identity. Example: CO₂ + H₂O ⇌ H₂CO₃, where covalent bonds in CO₂ and H₂O break and new ones form.","p":"94","g":"Chemical reactions","ch":5},{"k":"reactants","mod":"m6","n":"Reactants","d":"The starting molecules of a chemical reaction, written on the left of the arrow.","p":"94","g":"Chemical reactions","ch":5},{"k":"products","mod":"m6","n":"Products","d":"The molecules a chemical reaction makes, written on the right of the arrow. In a pathway, the products of one reaction are the reactants of the next.","p":"94","g":"Chemical reactions","ch":5},{"k":"equilibrium","mod":"m6","n":"Chemical equilibrium","d":"The concentrations of products and reactants influence whether a reaction proceeds forward or backward. At equilibrium the forward and backward reactions balance and there is no net change. Cells keep reactant-to-product ratios above that defined by the equilibrium constant, which helps them run +ΔG reactions.","p":"94, 96","g":"Chemical reactions","ch":5},{"k":"gibbs","mod":"m6","n":"Gibbs free energy (G)","d":"The amount of energy in a system available to do work. ΔG is the difference in Gibbs free energy between the reactants and products of a chemical process.","p":"94","g":"Free energy","ch":5},{"k":"delta-g","mod":"m6","n":"ΔG","d":"The change in free energy: Δ = change = final state − initial state, so ΔG = G(products) − G(reactants). Positive ΔG: endergonic. Negative ΔG: exergonic.","p":"94–95","g":"Free energy","ch":5},{"k":"endergonic","mod":"m6","n":"Endergonic","d":"A reaction whose products have more free energy than its reactants (+ΔG). Thermodynamically unfavourable (nonspontaneous): it needs an input of energy. Endergonic reactions require energy.","p":"94","g":"Free energy","ch":5},{"k":"exergonic","mod":"m6","n":"Exergonic","d":"A reaction whose reactants have more free energy than its products (−ΔG). Thermodynamically favourable (spontaneous): energy is available for use in other processes. Exergonic reactions release energy.","p":"94","g":"Free energy","ch":5},{"k":"enthalpy","mod":"m6","n":"Enthalpy (H)","d":"Total energy. Total energy (H) = energy available to do work (G) + energy lost to entropy (S).","p":"95","g":"Free energy","ch":5},{"k":"gibbs-equation","mod":"m6","n":"ΔG = ΔH − TΔS","d":"The total-energy relation rearranged for Gibbs free energy. H = total energy (enthalpy), G = energy available to do work, S = energy lost to entropy or disorder, T = absolute temperature in Kelvin.","p":"95","g":"Free energy","ch":5},{"k":"coupling","mod":"m6","n":"Energetic coupling","d":"Pairing an endergonic reaction with an exergonic one so the net ΔG is negative and both proceed. Example: glucose + Pᵢ → glucose 6-phosphate (+3.3 kcal/mol) coupled with ATP hydrolysis (−7.3 kcal/mol) gives glucose + ATP → glucose 6-phosphate + ADP, ΔG = −4 kcal/mol. Pᵢ is shared between the two reactions.","p":"96","g":"Free energy","ch":6},{"k":"activation-energy","mod":"m6","n":"Activation energy (EA)","d":"The input of energy all reactions need to proceed: reactants must have enough kinetic energy to break certain covalent bonds and get over the activation energy barrier to the transition state.","p":"95","g":"Enzymes","ch":7},{"k":"transition-state","mod":"m6","n":"Transition state","d":"The highest-energy point of a reaction, at the top of the activation energy barrier. An enzyme's active site helps stabilize the transition state, which lowers the activation energy.","p":"95–96","g":"Enzymes","ch":7},{"k":"enzyme","mod":"m6","n":"Enzymes","d":"Proteins that catalyze chemical reactions by decreasing the activation energy barrier, so the reaction can proceed faster. ΔG is the same with and without an enzyme. Both anabolic and catabolic reactions are catalyzed by enzymes.","p":"95–97","g":"Enzymes","ch":7},{"k":"active-site","mod":"m6","n":"Enzyme active site","d":"The region of an enzyme formed by the assembly of certain amino acids in a 3-D structure. These amino acids are often far apart in the linear sequence; folding brings them together. It binds the substrate(s), converts it to product(s), and stabilizes the transition state.","p":"96","g":"Enzymes","ch":7},{"k":"substrate","mod":"m6","n":"Substrate","d":"The reactant an enzyme binds in its active site and converts to product.","p":"96–97","g":"Enzymes","ch":7},{"k":"es-complex","mod":"m6","n":"Enzyme–substrate complex (ES)","d":"The enzyme with its substrate bound. The cycle runs E + S ⇌ ES ⇌ EP ⇌ E + P: the enzyme–product complex (EP) releases the product and the enzyme is free to act again. In a catabolic reaction one substrate is split; in an anabolic reaction two substrates are joined.","p":"97","g":"Enzymes","ch":7},{"k":"lock-key","mod":"m6","n":"Lock-and-key model","d":"A model of enzyme binding in which the substrate fits an active site that already has the matching shape, like a key in a lock (Fig 6.13).","p":"97","g":"Enzymes","ch":7},{"k":"induced-fit","mod":"m6","n":"Induced fit model","d":"A model of enzyme binding in which the enzyme changes shape when the substrate binds, closing more snugly around it (Fig 6.13).","p":"97","g":"Enzymes","ch":7},{"k":"inhibitor","mod":"m6","n":"Enzyme inhibitors","d":"Molecules that reduce enzyme activity. There are two different kinds: reversible and irreversible. A competitive inhibitor binds the active site; a noncompetitive inhibitor binds elsewhere.","p":"97","g":"Enzyme regulation","ch":8},{"k":"competitive","mod":"m6","n":"Competitive inhibition","d":"The inhibitor binds to the active site, competing with the substrate and reducing the rate of the reaction.","p":"97","g":"Enzyme regulation","ch":8},{"k":"noncompetitive","mod":"m6","n":"Noncompetitive inhibition","d":"The inhibitor binds to a site other than the active site, changing the shape of the enzyme and reducing the rate of the reaction.","p":"97","g":"Enzyme regulation","ch":8},{"k":"activator","mod":"m6","n":"Enzyme activators","d":"Molecules that increase enzyme activity.","p":"97","g":"Enzyme regulation","ch":8},{"k":"feedback-inhibition","mod":"m6","n":"Feedback inhibition","d":"Regulation in which the final product of a pathway inhibits the first step. Important for maintaining balance in the cell and helping it conserve energy. Example: isoleucine inhibits threonine dehydratase, the first enzyme of the pathway from threonine.","p":"97","g":"Enzyme regulation","ch":8},{"k":"allosteric","mod":"m6","n":"Allosteric enzyme","d":"An enzyme regulated by a molecule binding at a site other than the active site, which changes its shape and activity. In Fig 6.14, isoleucine is an allosteric inhibitor of threonine dehydratase.","p":"97","g":"Enzyme regulation","ch":8},{"k":"cellular-respiration","mod":"m7","n":"Cellular respiration","d":"The series of catabolic reactions that converts the energy in fuel molecules into ATP, which allows the cell to do work. It can use carbohydrates, lipids and proteins. Four stages: glycolysis, pyruvate oxidation, the citric acid cycle and oxidative phosphorylation.","p":"100","g":"Overview","ch":0},{"k":"atp","mod":"m7","n":"Adenosine triphosphate (ATP)","d":"The molecule cellular respiration makes from the energy in fuel molecules; it lets the cell do work. It is made from ADP + Pi, either by substrate-level phosphorylation (a small amount) or by oxidative phosphorylation (the majority).","p":"100–101","g":"Overview","ch":0},{"k":"four-stages","mod":"m7","n":"The four stages","d":"Stage 1, glycolysis: glucose is partly broken down to pyruvate. Stage 2, pyruvate oxidation: pyruvate becomes acetyl-CoA and CO₂. Stage 3, the citric acid cycle: acetyl-CoA is broken down, releasing CO₂, a little energy and electron carriers. Stage 4, oxidative phosphorylation: the carriers give their electrons to the electron transport chain, which makes ATP.","p":"100","g":"Overview","ch":0},{"k":"substrate-level","mod":"m7","n":"Substrate-level phosphorylation","d":"Making ATP directly in a reaction involving an enzyme/substrate complex: a phosphate is moved onto ADP. Only a small amount of ATP is made this way (in glycolysis and the citric acid cycle).","p":"100–101","g":"Overview","ch":0},{"k":"oxidative-phos","mod":"m7","n":"Oxidative phosphorylation","d":"Stage 4. Electron carriers pass electrons to the electron transport chain; the energy released pumps protons, and the proton gradient drives ATP synthase. The majority of ATP is made this way.","p":"101, 109","g":"Overview","ch":0},{"k":"catabolic","mod":"m7","n":"Catabolic pathway","d":"A pathway that breaks a larger molecule down into smaller ones and releases energy. Glycolysis is catabolic: it breaks glucose into two pyruvates.","p":"103","g":"Overview","ch":2},{"k":"aerobic","mod":"m7","n":"Aerobic","d":"With oxygen. When O₂ is present, pyruvate is oxidized in the mitochondria and O₂ is the final electron acceptor of the electron transport chain.","p":"106, 109","g":"Overview","ch":3},{"k":"anaerobic","mod":"m7","n":"Anaerobic","d":"Without oxygen. Glycolysis is considered anaerobic because it consumes no O₂. If O₂ is not available, the cell is under anaerobic conditions and uses fermentation.","p":"103, 111","g":"Overview","ch":2},{"k":"redox","mod":"m7","n":"Oxidation-reduction (redox) reactions","d":"Reactions in which electrons move from one molecule to another: one is oxidized (loses electrons) and the other is reduced (gains them). In respiration, glucose is oxidized to CO₂ and O₂ is reduced to H₂O.","p":"101–102","g":"Redox and electron carriers","ch":1},{"k":"oxidation","mod":"m7","n":"Oxidation","d":"Loss of electrons (OIL: Oxidation Is Loss; LEO: Loss of Electrons is Oxidation). Glucose is oxidized to CO₂.","p":"101","g":"Redox and electron carriers","ch":1},{"k":"reduction","mod":"m7","n":"Reduction","d":"Gain of electrons (RIG: Reduction Is Gain; GER: Gain of Electrons is Reduction). O₂ is reduced to H₂O; NAD⁺ is reduced to NADH.","p":"101–102","g":"Redox and electron carriers","ch":1},{"k":"electron-carriers","mod":"m7","n":"Electron carriers","d":"Molecules that carry energy, as electrons, from one reaction to another. NAD⁺/NADH and FAD/FADH₂ pick up electrons in glycolysis, pyruvate oxidation and the citric acid cycle and deliver them to the electron transport chain.","p":"101–102","g":"Redox and electron carriers","ch":1},{"k":"nad","mod":"m7","n":"Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide (NAD)","d":"An electron carrier. NAD⁺ is the oxidized form; NADH is the reduced form, which has high potential energy. NADH is made in glycolysis, pyruvate oxidation and the citric acid cycle and gives its electrons to complex I.","p":"102","g":"Redox and electron carriers","ch":1},{"k":"fad","mod":"m7","n":"Flavin adenine dinucleotide (FAD)","d":"An electron carrier. FAD is the oxidized form; FADH₂ is the reduced form. FADH₂ is made in the citric acid cycle (succinate to fumarate) and gives its electrons to complex II.","p":"102","g":"Redox and electron carriers","ch":1},{"k":"final-acceptor","mod":"m7","n":"Final electron acceptor","d":"Oxygen. At the end of the electron transport chain O₂ accepts the electrons, combines with H⁺ and is reduced to water. The original electron donor is glucose.","p":"102, 109","g":"Redox and electron carriers","ch":1},{"k":"free-energy","mod":"m7","n":"Stepwise release of energy","d":"Glucose is not burned in one step. Its free energy is released slowly in a series of reactions (ΔG falls to about −680 kcal/mol), and the energy is stored as ATP and reduced electron carriers along the way.","p":"102, 110","g":"Redox and electron carriers","ch":1},{"k":"glycolysis","mod":"m7","n":"Glycolysis","d":"Stage 1. A catabolic pathway of ten reactions that breaks one six-carbon glucose into two three-carbon pyruvates. It happens in the cytosol, with or without O₂, and is considered anaerobic because no O₂ is consumed. Net yield: 2 ATP and 2 NADH.","p":"103","g":"Glycolysis","ch":0},{"k":"prep-phase","mod":"m7","n":"Phase 1: preparatory phase","d":"Steps 1 to 3. Two phosphate groups are added to glucose, using two ATP, to make fructose 1,6-bisphosphate. This traps glucose in the cell and destabilizes it. Energy consuming: it takes energy to make energy.","p":"104","g":"Glycolysis","ch":2},{"k":"cleavage-phase","mod":"m7","n":"Phase 2: cleavage phase","d":"Steps 4 and 5. Fructose 1,6-bisphosphate is split into glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate and dihydroxyacetone phosphate, which is converted into a second glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate. Phase 2 splits glucose.","p":"104","g":"Glycolysis","ch":2},{"k":"payoff-phase","mod":"m7","n":"Phase 3: payoff phase","d":"Steps 6 to 10, which happen twice per glucose. They make two pyruvates, two NADH and four ATP. Energy producing.","p":"105","g":"Glycolysis","ch":2},{"k":"f16bp","mod":"m7","n":"Fructose 1,6-bisphosphate","d":"The six-carbon sugar with two phosphates made at the end of phase 1 (steps 1 to 3 use two ATP). It is split in two in phase 2.","p":"104","g":"Glycolysis","ch":2},{"k":"g3p","mod":"m7","n":"Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate (G3P)","d":"The three-carbon sugar phosphate made in phase 2. There are two per glucose; each one goes through phase 3 to become pyruvate.","p":"104","g":"Glycolysis","ch":2},{"k":"pyruvate","mod":"m7","n":"Pyruvate","d":"The three-carbon product of glycolysis; two per glucose. With O₂ it enters the mitochondria for pyruvate oxidation. Without O₂ it is reduced by fermentation.","p":"103, 105","g":"Glycolysis","ch":2},{"k":"mitochondria","mod":"m7","n":"Mitochondria","d":"The organelles where stages 2 to 4 happen. A mitochondrion has an outer and an inner membrane, which define two spaces: the intermembrane space and the matrix.","p":"105–106","g":"Mitochondria","ch":3},{"k":"inner-membrane","mod":"m7","n":"Inner mitochondrial membrane","d":"The folded inner membrane. It holds the electron transport chain and ATP synthase, and protons cannot diffuse across it, so it can hold a proton gradient.","p":"106, 109","g":"Mitochondria","ch":3},{"k":"intermembrane","mod":"m7","n":"Intermembrane space","d":"The space between the inner and outer mitochondrial membranes. Complexes I, III and IV pump protons into it, so it has a high H⁺ concentration.","p":"106, 109","g":"Mitochondria","ch":3},{"k":"matrix","mod":"m7","n":"Mitochondrial matrix","d":"The space inside the inner membrane. Pyruvate oxidation and the citric acid cycle happen here, and ATP synthase makes ATP on this side. Low H⁺ concentration.","p":"106","g":"Mitochondria","ch":3},{"k":"pyruvate-ox","mod":"m7","n":"Pyruvate oxidation","d":"Stage 2, in the mitochondrial matrix. Pyruvate is oxidized to CO₂ and an acetyl group, NAD⁺ is reduced to NADH, and the acetyl group is transferred to coenzyme A. One pyruvate gives 1 CO₂, 1 NADH and 1 acetyl-CoA, so one glucose gives two of each.","p":"106–107","g":"Pyruvate oxidation and the citric acid cycle","ch":0},{"k":"acetyl-coa","mod":"m7","n":"Acetyl-CoA","d":"A two-carbon acetyl group attached to coenzyme A. Made by pyruvate oxidation (and by β-oxidation of fatty acids); it carries the acetyl group into the citric acid cycle.","p":"106–107","g":"Pyruvate oxidation and the citric acid cycle","ch":3},{"k":"coa","mod":"m7","n":"Coenzyme A (CoA)","d":"Accepts the acetyl group made from pyruvate and carries it to the citric acid cycle.","p":"106","g":"Pyruvate oxidation and the citric acid cycle","ch":3},{"k":"pdh","mod":"m7","n":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex","d":"The group of enzymes that catalyzes pyruvate oxidation: removing CO₂, reducing NAD⁺ and attaching the acetyl group to coenzyme A.","p":"106","g":"Pyruvate oxidation and the citric acid cycle","ch":3},{"k":"cac","mod":"m7","n":"Citric acid cycle (Krebs cycle, TCA cycle)","d":"Stage 3, in the matrix. Acetyl-CoA (2C) joins oxaloacetate (4C) to make citrate (6C); the cycle releases 2 CO₂ and makes 3 NADH, 1 FADH₂ and 1 ATP (via GTP, by substrate-level phosphorylation) per turn, and regenerates oxaloacetate. It completes the oxidation of glucose. Two turns per glucose.","p":"107–108","g":"Pyruvate oxidation and the citric acid cycle","ch":0},{"k":"oxaloacetate","mod":"m7","n":"Oxaloacetate","d":"The four-carbon molecule that accepts acetyl-CoA to start the citric acid cycle. It is regenerated at the end, which is why it is a cycle.","p":"107","g":"Pyruvate oxidation and the citric acid cycle","ch":4},{"k":"cac-intermediates","mod":"m7","n":"Use of intermediates","d":"Some organisms use molecules from different steps of the citric acid cycle in other pathways. For example, α-ketoglutarate can be modified to form amino acids and purine bases.","p":"107","g":"Pyruvate oxidation and the citric acid cycle","ch":4},{"k":"etc","mod":"m7","n":"Electron transport chain (ETC)","d":"Membrane-associated proteins in the inner mitochondrial membrane. Electrons from NADH and FADH₂ move through a sequence of redox reactions from donor to acceptor until they reach O₂, the final electron acceptor. The energy released pumps protons and forms a proton gradient.","p":"101, 109","g":"Oxidative phosphorylation","ch":5},{"k":"complexes","mod":"m7","n":"Complexes I to IV, CoQ and cytochrome c","d":"The parts of the ETC. NADH gives electrons to complex I and FADH₂ to complex II. Coenzyme Q (CoQ) carries them to complex III, cytochrome c carries them to complex IV, and complex IV passes them to O₂. Complexes I, III and IV pump protons.","p":"109","g":"Oxidative phosphorylation","ch":5},{"k":"proton-gradient","mod":"m7","n":"Proton gradient","d":"The difference in H⁺ concentration across the inner membrane: high in the intermembrane space, low in the matrix. Protons cannot diffuse across the membrane, so the gradient holds high potential energy that powers ATP synthase.","p":"109","g":"Oxidative phosphorylation","ch":5},{"k":"atp-synthase","mod":"m7","n":"ATP synthase","d":"A molecular machine in the inner membrane, made of two subunits, Fo and F1. Protons flow down their gradient through the Fo channel, which rotates; F1 uses the rotational energy to make ATP from ADP + Pi.","p":"110","g":"Oxidative phosphorylation","ch":5},{"k":"fo-f1","mod":"m7","n":"Fo and F1 subunits","d":"Fo sits in the membrane and forms a channel that rotates as protons pass through it. F1 sits in the matrix and uses this rotational energy to catalyze the synthesis of ATP.","p":"110","g":"Oxidative phosphorylation","ch":5},{"k":"atp-yield","mod":"m7","n":"ATP yield","d":"Per glucose: glycolysis 2 ATP (net) and 2 NADH; pyruvate oxidation 2 NADH; citric acid cycle 2 ATP, 6 NADH and 2 FADH₂; oxidative phosphorylation about 28 ATP. Total: about 32 ATP.","p":"110","g":"Oxidative phosphorylation","ch":6},{"k":"fermentation","mod":"m7","n":"Fermentation","d":"What happens to pyruvate when O₂ is not available: pyruvate (or a product of it) is reduced by NADH. This regenerates NAD⁺, so glycolysis can keep going and still make a small amount of ATP.","p":"111","g":"Anaerobic metabolism","ch":7},{"k":"lactic","mod":"m7","n":"Lactic acid fermentation","d":"In bacteria and animal cells, pyruvate is reduced to lactic acid by NADH, regenerating NAD⁺. Glucose + 2 ADP + 2 Pi → 2 lactic acid + 2 ATP + 2 H₂O.","p":"111","g":"Anaerobic metabolism","ch":7},{"k":"ethanol","mod":"m7","n":"Ethanol fermentation","d":"In plants and fungi (e.g. yeast). Pyruvate releases CO₂ to form acetaldehyde; NADH gives its electrons to acetaldehyde, making ethanol and regenerating NAD⁺. Glucose + 2 ADP + 2 Pi → 2 ethanol + 2 CO₂ + 2 ATP + 2 H₂O.","p":"111","g":"Anaerobic metabolism","ch":7},{"k":"glycogen","mod":"m7","n":"Glycogen","d":"How animals store excess glucose: a large, branched chain of glucose molecules attached to a central protein. Stored in muscle cells to power contraction and in the liver for the whole body. Glucose monomers are cleaved off one at a time and enter glycolysis.","p":"112","g":"Metabolic integration","ch":8},{"k":"starch","mod":"m7","n":"Starch","d":"How plants store excess glucose: a large, branched chain of glucose molecules, found in potatoes, wheat and corn.","p":"112","g":"Metabolic integration","ch":8},{"k":"monosaccharide","mod":"m7","n":"Monosaccharide","d":"A single sugar unit, such as glucose, galactose, fructose or mannose. Digested carbohydrates give monosaccharides that become glucose or other glycolysis intermediates.","p":"112","g":"Metabolic integration","ch":8},{"k":"disaccharide","mod":"m7","n":"Disaccharide","d":"Two monosaccharides joined together, such as maltose, lactose and sucrose. They are digested into monosaccharides.","p":"112","g":"Metabolic integration","ch":8},{"k":"fatty-acid","mod":"m7","n":"Fatty acid","d":"A long hydrocarbon chain, rich in C–C and C–H bonds, which makes lipids an excellent energy source. Fatty acids absorbed after a meal, or made from excess glucose, are broken down by β-oxidation.","p":"113","g":"Metabolic integration","ch":8},{"k":"beta-ox","mod":"m7","n":"Beta-oxidation","d":"The pathway that shortens fatty acids, breaking lipids down into glycerol and acetyl-CoA. It makes no ATP directly; it makes NADH and FADH₂, which feed the electron transport chain.","p":"113","g":"Metabolic integration","ch":8},{"k":"fuel-entry","mod":"m7","n":"Other fuel molecules","d":"Other fuels join the same pathway at different points. Other carbohydrates and glycerol enter glycolysis; fatty acids enter as acetyl-CoA; amino acids from proteins enter at pyruvate, acetyl-CoA or the citric acid cycle.","p":"113","g":"Metabolic integration","ch":8},{"k":"photosynthesis","mod":"m8","n":"Photosynthesis","d":"The conversion of light energy to chemical energy. Photosynthetic organisms build carbohydrates using sunlight and CO₂ from the air. It is a major entry point of energy into biological systems, and the biomass it makes supports food webs.","p":"117","g":"Overview","ch":0},{"k":"oxidation","mod":"m8","n":"Oxidation","d":"Loss of electrons. In photosynthesis water is oxidized: it gives up electrons, protons (H⁺) and O₂, which is released as a by-product. Water is the electron donor.","p":"117","g":"Overview","ch":0},{"k":"reduction","mod":"m8","n":"Reduction","d":"Gain of electrons. In photosynthesis CO₂ is reduced to form high-energy carbohydrate molecules. Overall, photosynthesis is a redox reaction: water is oxidized and CO₂ is reduced.","p":"117","g":"Overview","ch":0},{"k":"carbohydrate","mod":"m8","n":"Carbohydrate","d":"The high-energy product of photosynthesis, made by reducing CO₂. The Calvin cycle releases it as a 3-carbon sugar, triose phosphate; the overall equation shows glucose, C₆H₁₂O₆.","p":"117–118","g":"Overview","ch":0},{"k":"two-stages","mod":"m8","n":"Two stages of photosynthesis","d":"Light capture (the light-dependent reactions, in the thylakoid membranes) converts light energy into ATP and NADPH and releases O₂. Carbon fixation (the light-independent reactions, the Calvin cycle, in the stroma) uses that ATP and NADPH to reduce CO₂ to carbohydrate.","p":"117–118","g":"Overview","ch":0},{"k":"light-reactions","mod":"m8","n":"Light-dependent reactions","d":"Stage 1: sunlight energy is converted into chemical energy in the thylakoid membranes. Products: ATP, NADPH and O₂.","p":"118","g":"Overview","ch":0},{"k":"carbon-fixation","mod":"m8","n":"Light-independent reactions (carbon fixation)","d":"Stage 2: ATP and NADPH are used to synthesize carbohydrates from CO₂. This is the Calvin cycle, and it occurs in the stroma.","p":"117–118","g":"Overview","ch":0},{"k":"efficiency","mod":"m8","n":"Efficiency of photosynthesis","d":"Of the sun's output, 60% is outside the visible range and unavailable, 8% is reflected or transmitted, 8% is converted into heat and 20% is lost during carbohydrate synthesis (including photorespiration). The maximum yield is about 4%, as carbohydrate.","p":"117","g":"Overview","ch":0},{"k":"atp","mod":"m8","n":"Adenosine triphosphate (ATP)","d":"The energy carrier made in the light-dependent reactions by ATP synthase in the thylakoid membrane. ATP made in the chloroplast stays in the chloroplast and is used in carbon fixation.","p":"117, 122","g":"Overview","ch":5},{"k":"nadph","mod":"m8","n":"Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate (NADPH)","d":"The electron carrier made when electrons from photosystem I reduce NADP⁺ (NADP⁺ + 2e⁻ + H⁺ → NADPH). It supplies the electrons that reduce PGA in the Calvin cycle.","p":"117, 121","g":"Overview","ch":4},{"k":"chloroplast","mod":"m8","n":"Chloroplast","d":"The organelle where both stages of photosynthesis take place in eukaryotes. Semi-autonomous and self-replicating, bound by 2 membranes (outer and inner) separated by a narrow space. Descended from an engulfed cyanobacterium.","p":"118","g":"The chloroplast","ch":1},{"k":"chloro-membranes","mod":"m8","n":"Outer and inner membranes","d":"The two membranes that bound the chloroplast, separated by a narrow space. The thylakoids inside are a separate, third membrane system.","p":"118","g":"The chloroplast","ch":1},{"k":"thylakoid","mod":"m8","n":"Thylakoid","d":"The highly folded, flattened membrane sacs in the centre of the chloroplast. The thylakoid membrane holds the photosystems, the electron transport chain and ATP synthase, so the light-dependent reactions happen here.","p":"118","g":"The chloroplast","ch":1},{"k":"lumen","mod":"m8","n":"Lumen (thylakoid lumen)","d":"The fluid-filled space inside the thylakoid membrane. H⁺ is pumped into it during electron transport, so its proton concentration rises; plastocyanin diffuses through it.","p":"118, 122","g":"The chloroplast","ch":1},{"k":"grana","mod":"m8","n":"Grana (singular: granum)","d":"Orderly stacks of thylakoids.","p":"118","g":"The chloroplast","ch":1},{"k":"stroma","mod":"m8","n":"Stroma","d":"The fluid space surrounding the thylakoids, inside the inner membrane. Carbon fixation (the Calvin cycle) occurs here, and ATP and NADPH are released into it.","p":"118","g":"The chloroplast","ch":1},{"k":"photon","mod":"m8","n":"Photon","d":"A packet of light energy. Energy from the sun is a form of electromagnetic radiation and travels in photons. Shorter wavelengths carry more energy.","p":"118","g":"Light and pigments","ch":2},{"k":"em-spectrum","mod":"m8","n":"Electromagnetic spectrum and visible light","d":"From high energy (gamma rays, X-rays, ultraviolet) to low energy (infrared, microwaves, radio waves). Visible light, about 400 to 700 nm, is only one part of it, and it is the part used in photosynthesis.","p":"119","g":"Light and pigments","ch":2},{"k":"excited-state","mod":"m8","n":"Excited state","d":"When a photon is absorbed, the compound is converted to a higher-energy state. It returns to the ground state in one of three ways: dissipate the energy as heat, re-emit it at a longer wavelength (fluorescence), or transfer it to another molecule, which is what photosynthetic pigments do.","p":"118","g":"Light and pigments","ch":2},{"k":"fluorescence","mod":"m8","n":"Fluorescence","d":"Re-emitting absorbed energy as light of a longer wavelength. One of the three ways an excited molecule returns to its ground state.","p":"118","g":"Light and pigments","ch":2},{"k":"pigment","mod":"m8","n":"Pigment and chromophore","d":"Pigments are molecules that contain a chromophore, a chemical group capable of absorbing light of specific wavelengths (λ).","p":"119","g":"Light and pigments","ch":2},{"k":"chlorophyll","mod":"m8","n":"Chlorophyll","d":"The major light-capturing molecule. It absorbs blue and red light and is poor at absorbing green, so green light is reflected and leaves look green. Two parts: a porphyrin ring that absorbs light, and a phytol side chain.","p":"119","g":"Light and pigments","ch":2},{"k":"porphyrin","mod":"m8","n":"Porphyrin ring","d":"The light-absorbing part of chlorophyll, with a magnesium atom (Mg) in its centre. Different side groups on the ring give the different types of chlorophyll (for example a and b).","p":"119","g":"Light and pigments","ch":2},{"k":"phytol","mod":"m8","n":"Phytol side chain","d":"The long hydrocarbon tail of chlorophyll. It inserts chlorophyll into the lipid bilayer of the thylakoid membrane.","p":"119","g":"Light and pigments","ch":2},{"k":"accessory","mod":"m8","n":"Accessory pigments (carotenoids)","d":"Pigments in the thylakoid membrane, such as carotenoids, that absorb light from regions of the visible spectrum that chlorophyll absorbs poorly. They increase the efficiency of light absorption and protect photosynthetic components from damage.","p":"119","g":"Light and pigments","ch":2},{"k":"antenna","mod":"m8","n":"Antenna chlorophyll","d":"Chlorophylls that absorb light and pass the energy from chlorophyll to chlorophyll until it reaches the reaction centre. Antenna chlorophylls transfer energy, not electrons.","p":"120","g":"Photosystems","ch":3},{"k":"reaction-center","mod":"m8","n":"Reaction center","d":"A specific pair of chlorophyll molecules that can transfer an excited electron to an electron acceptor. Having lost an electron, it must get one back from an electron donor before it can use more light energy. In photosystem II, that electron comes from water.","p":"120","g":"Photosystems","ch":3},{"k":"photosystem","mod":"m8","n":"Photosystem (PSI and PSII)","d":"A protein-pigment complex that absorbs light and uses it to drive redox reactions. Each has antenna chlorophylls and a reaction-center chlorophyll. Two act in series: photosystem II then photosystem I, linked by the electron transport chain.","p":"121","g":"Photosystems","ch":4},{"k":"psii","mod":"m8","n":"Photosystem II (PSII)","d":"The first photosystem in the chain. Light absorbed by PSII lets electrons pulled from water enter the photosynthetic electron transport chain at a high energy level. Water is split here, releasing O₂ and H⁺ into the lumen.","p":"121","g":"Photosystems","ch":4},{"k":"psi","mod":"m8","n":"Photosystem I (PSI)","d":"The second photosystem. A second input of light energy raises the electron energy high enough to reduce NADP⁺ to NADPH.","p":"121","g":"Photosystems","ch":4},{"k":"water-split","mod":"m8","n":"Oxidation of water","d":"Water is the electron donor. Its oxidation at photosystem II produces electrons (which refill the reaction center), protons and O₂. 2 H₂O → 4 e⁻ + 4 H⁺ + O₂.","p":"117, 120","g":"Photosystems","ch":0},{"k":"z-scheme","mod":"m8","n":"Z scheme","d":"The energy path of electrons through photosynthesis. Light at PSII raises electrons from water to a high energy level, the energy drops along the electron transport chain, then light at PSI raises it again, high enough to reduce NADP⁺. Drawn on an energy axis it looks like a Z.","p":"121","g":"Photosystems","ch":4},{"k":"ps-etc","mod":"m8","n":"Photosynthetic electron transport chain","d":"The chain that connects the two photosystems and links the oxidation of water to the reduction of NADP⁺: H₂O → PSII → plastoquinone → cytochrome b6f complex → plastocyanin → PSI → ferredoxin → NADP⁺ reductase. It drives the formation of ATP and NADPH.","p":"117, 121–122","g":"Electron transport and ATP","ch":4},{"k":"plastoquinone","mod":"m8","n":"Plastoquinone (Pq)","d":"A mobile carrier in the thylakoid membrane that carries electrons from PSII to the cytochrome b6f complex. It also picks up H⁺ from the stroma.","p":"122","g":"Electron transport and ATP","ch":5},{"k":"b6f","mod":"m8","n":"Cytochrome b6f complex","d":"The complex that electrons pass through between PSII and PSI. As electrons pass, H⁺ is moved from the stroma into the thylakoid lumen.","p":"122","g":"Electron transport and ATP","ch":5},{"k":"plastocyanin","mod":"m8","n":"Plastocyanin (Pc)","d":"A small protein that carries electrons from the cytochrome b6f complex to PSI by diffusing through the thylakoid lumen.","p":"122","g":"Electron transport and ATP","ch":5},{"k":"ferredoxin","mod":"m8","n":"Ferredoxin (Fd) and NADP⁺ reductase","d":"On the stroma side of PSI, ferredoxin takes the electrons and NADP⁺ reductase uses them to reduce NADP⁺ + H⁺ to NADPH (Fig 8.8).","p":"122","g":"Electron transport and ATP","ch":5},{"k":"proton-gradient","mod":"m8","n":"Proton gradient","d":"As electrons flow along the Z pathway, H⁺ ions are moved from the stroma into the thylakoid lumen (and water splitting adds H⁺ there), so the proton concentration is higher in the lumen than in the stroma. This gradient drives ATP formation.","p":"122","g":"Electron transport and ATP","ch":5},{"k":"atp-synthase","mod":"m8","n":"ATP synthase","d":"An enzyme embedded in the thylakoid membrane. Protons pass through it from the lumen back to the stroma, and ATP is generated from ADP and Pᵢ on the stroma side.","p":"122","g":"Electron transport and ATP","ch":5},{"k":"photophosphorylation","mod":"m8","n":"Photophosphorylation","d":"Making ATP using light energy: electrons moving through PSII and PSI build a proton gradient, and protons flowing back through ATP synthase drive ATP formation.","p":"122","g":"Electron transport and ATP","ch":5},{"k":"cyclic","mod":"m8","n":"Cyclic electron transport","d":"Listed in your important terms but not described in the notes. Electrons from PSI are passed back to the cytochrome b6f complex instead of to NADP⁺. More H⁺ is pumped, so ATP is made, but no NADPH is made and no water is split (no O₂). It tops up the extra ATP the Calvin cycle needs.","p":"125","g":"Electron transport and ATP","ch":5},{"k":"calvin-cycle","mod":"m8","n":"Calvin cycle","d":"The light-independent reactions in the stroma. It takes a low potential energy carbon compound (CO₂) and converts it into a higher-energy carbon compound, using NADPH and ATP: 15 chemical reactions grouped into three steps, carboxylation, reduction and regeneration.","p":"123","g":"Calvin cycle","ch":6},{"k":"carboxylation","mod":"m8","n":"Carboxylation","d":"Step 1 of the Calvin cycle. CO₂ from the air is combined with RuBP (5 carbons) to form an unstable 6-carbon molecule, catalyzed by rubisco. It breaks down into two 3-carbon molecules of PGA.","p":"123","g":"Calvin cycle","ch":6},{"k":"rubisco","mod":"m8","n":"Rubisco","d":"The enzyme that catalyzes carboxylation: adding CO₂ to the 5-carbon compound RuBP.","p":"123","g":"Calvin cycle","ch":6},{"k":"rubp","mod":"m8","n":"RuBP (ribulose 1,5-bisphosphate)","d":"The 5-carbon molecule that accepts CO₂ in carboxylation. It must be regenerated so the cycle can continue.","p":"123","g":"Calvin cycle","ch":6},{"k":"pga","mod":"m8","n":"PGA (3-phosphoglycerate)","d":"The 3-carbon molecule made when the 6-carbon product of carboxylation breaks in two. Its potential energy is low, so it must be reduced.","p":"123","g":"Calvin cycle","ch":6},{"k":"calvin-reduction","mod":"m8","n":"Reduction (Calvin cycle step 2)","d":"Step 2 of the Calvin cycle, which raises the potential energy of PGA in two steps: PGA is phosphorylated by ATP (to 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate), then reduced by NADPH to triose phosphate.","p":"123","g":"Calvin cycle","ch":6},{"k":"triose-phosphate","mod":"m8","n":"Triose phosphate","d":"The 3-carbon sugar phosphate (G3P) made in the reduction step. For every six made, only one can be withdrawn from the cycle and exported out of the chloroplast; the other five regenerate RuBP.","p":"123","g":"Calvin cycle","ch":6},{"k":"regeneration","mod":"m8","n":"Regeneration","d":"Step 3 of the Calvin cycle. The other five triose phosphates are rearranged into three 5-carbon RuBP molecules for carboxylation. This requires energy in the form of ATP.","p":"123","g":"Calvin cycle","ch":6},{"k":"starch","mod":"m8","n":"Starch","d":"Excess carbohydrates from the Calvin cycle are converted into storage as starch granules during the day. They are a source of carbohydrate used during the night, when there is no sunlight.","p":"124","g":"Calvin cycle","ch":6},{"k":"single-ps","mod":"m8","n":"Single photosystem","d":"Early photosynthetic bacteria had only a single photosystem, which cannot capture enough energy. Two photosystems in series came later.","p":"124","g":"Evolution","ch":7},{"k":"hgt","mod":"m8","n":"Horizontal gene transfer","d":"Hypothesis 1 for the evolution of two photosystems: a bacterium with one type of photosystem received the genes for the other type from a different bacterium.","p":"124","g":"Evolution","ch":7},{"k":"duplication","mod":"m8","n":"Gene duplication and divergence","d":"Hypothesis 2 for the evolution of two photosystems: the photosystem gene was duplicated and one of the two copies diverged into the second photosystem.","p":"124","g":"Evolution","ch":7},{"k":"cyanobacteria","mod":"m8","n":"Cyanobacteria","d":"The first organisms to use water as the electron donor, with both PSI and PSII.","p":"124","g":"Evolution","ch":7},{"k":"endosymbiosis","mod":"m8","n":"Endosymbiotic theory","d":"Proposes that a cyanobacterium was engulfed by a eukaryotic cell. Over time it lost the ability to live outside the host and became the organelle we know as the chloroplast.","p":"124","g":"Evolution","ch":7},{"k":"cell-signalling","mod":"m9","n":"Cell signalling","d":"Transferring information from the outside of the cell to obtain a response inside the cell. Cells communicate by sending and receiving chemical signals over short or long distances.","p":"127","g":"Principles of signalling","ch":0},{"k":"four-elements","mod":"m9","n":"Four elements of cell communication","d":"Communication between cells, prokaryotic or eukaryotic, needs four elements: a signalling cell, a signalling molecule, a receptor molecule and a responding cell. Example in humans: adrenal gland cells release epinephrine, which binds receptors on target organ cells and increases heart rate.","p":"127–128","g":"Principles of signalling","ch":0},{"k":"signalling-cell","mod":"m9","n":"Signalling cell","d":"The cell that makes and releases the signalling molecule. Example: cells of the adrenal glands, which release epinephrine.","p":"127","g":"Principles of signalling","ch":0},{"k":"signalling-molecule","mod":"m9","n":"Signalling molecule (ligand)","d":"The chemical signal released by the signalling cell. A molecule that binds to a receptor is called a ligand. It can be an extracellular ligand secreted by cells, or the signal can come from cell-to-cell contact or contact with the extracellular matrix. Example: epinephrine.","p":"127, 129","g":"Principles of signalling","ch":0},{"k":"responding-cell","mod":"m9","n":"Responding cell (target cell)","d":"The cell that has the receptor for the signal and changes what it does in response. Example: heart muscle cells increase heart rate in response to epinephrine.","p":"127–128","g":"Principles of signalling","ch":0},{"k":"receptor","mod":"m9","n":"Receptor","d":"A protein that recognizes and binds a specific signalling molecule (ligand). Some are cell-surface proteins; others are inside the cell, in the cytosol or nucleus. Binding turns the receptor on.","p":"127, 129","g":"Principles of signalling","ch":0},{"k":"ligand-binding-site","mod":"m9","n":"Ligand-binding site","d":"The part of a receptor where the ligand binds. The ligand fits it much like a substrate fits the active site of an enzyme. Binding causes a conformational change in the receptor that triggers reactions in the cytosol.","p":"129","g":"Principles of signalling","ch":1},{"k":"receptor-activation","mod":"m9","n":"Receptor activation","d":"Step 1 of signalling: the signalling molecule binds to its receptor, which changes conformation and is turned on (activated).","p":"128–129","g":"Four steps","ch":1},{"k":"signal-transduction","mod":"m9","n":"Signal transduction","d":"Step 2 of signalling: the activated receptor transmits the message into the cell through the cytoplasm. A series of distinct proteins is activated or inactivated in a particular sequence (a signal transduction pathway). The message can stay in the cytosol or go to the nucleus.","p":"128–129","g":"Four steps","ch":1},{"k":"response","mod":"m9","n":"Response","d":"Step 3 of signalling: what the cell does. It could activate enzymes, turn on genes, signal other cells, or make the cell divide or change shape. The response depends on the cell type, because it depends on the proteins and pathways in that cell: epinephrine makes heart muscle contract more forcefully and quickly but relaxes the smooth muscle lining the airways.","p":"128–129","g":"Four steps","ch":1},{"k":"signal-termination","mod":"m9","n":"Signal termination","d":"Step 4 of signalling: once the signal has been received and acted on, the response is stopped so that new signals can be received. For a GPCR: the ligand leaves, the G protein converts GTP to GDP, cAMP is degraded, and phosphatases remove phosphates from activated proteins.","p":"128–129, 135","g":"Four steps","ch":1},{"k":"quorum","mod":"m9","n":"Signalling in bacteria","d":"Pneumococcal bacteria continuously make a small peptide and have a surface receptor for it. At low cell density the peptide stays below its critical threshold and DNA-uptake genes stay off. At high density the peptide binds, the signal is relayed to the nucleoid and genes for taking up DNA from the environment turn on.","p":"127","g":"Principles of signalling","ch":0},{"k":"endocrine","mod":"m9","n":"Endocrine signalling","d":"Long-distance signalling. Endocrine cells release chemical signalling molecules called hormones that travel through the bloodstream; only target cells that express the right receptor for the hormone respond.","p":"130","g":"Signal distances","ch":2},{"k":"hormone","mod":"m9","n":"Hormone","d":"A chemical signalling molecule made by endocrine cells and carried through the bloodstream to distant target cells. Epinephrine (adrenaline) is an example.","p":"130","g":"Signal distances","ch":2},{"k":"paracrine","mod":"m9","n":"Paracrine signalling","d":"Short-distance signalling between neighbouring cells. The signalling molecule, typically small and water soluble, moves by diffusion and acts within a range of about 20 cells. Important in embryonic development.","p":"130","g":"Signal distances","ch":2},{"k":"autocrine","mod":"m9","n":"Autocrine signalling","d":"The cell that secretes a signalling molecule is also the target cell: it has receptors for its own signal. Important in embryonic development.","p":"130","g":"Signal distances","ch":2},{"k":"contact-dependent","mod":"m9","n":"Contact-dependent signalling","d":"Signalling through direct contact between neighbouring cells. A transmembrane protein on one cell acts as the signalling molecule and a transmembrane protein on the adjacent cell acts as the receptor. Also called juxtacrine signalling.","p":"130","g":"Signal distances","ch":2},{"k":"polar-signal","mod":"m9","n":"Polar signalling molecules","d":"Signalling molecules that cannot cross the hydrophobic core of the cell membrane. They bind to cell-surface receptors (transmembrane proteins).","p":"131","g":"Receptors","ch":3},{"k":"nonpolar-signal","mod":"m9","n":"Nonpolar signalling molecules","d":"Small, nonpolar signalling molecules pass through the hydrophobic core of the membrane, so they do not need a receptor on the outside of the cell. They bind receptors in the cytosol or nucleus. Steroid hormones are the standard example.","p":"131","g":"Receptors","ch":3},{"k":"intracellular-receptor","mod":"m9","n":"Intracellular receptor","d":"A receptor protein in the cytosol or nucleus, bound by a nonpolar ligand that has crossed the membrane. The activated receptor often acts in the nucleus to change gene expression.","p":"131","g":"Receptors","ch":3},{"k":"cell-surface-receptor","mod":"m9","n":"Cell-surface receptor","d":"A transmembrane protein bound by a polar ligand. It has an extracellular domain with the ligand-binding site, a transmembrane domain and a cytoplasmic domain. Binding changes its conformation. The three main groups: G protein-coupled receptors, receptor kinases and ion channels.","p":"131","g":"Receptors","ch":3},{"k":"gpcr","mod":"m9","n":"G protein-coupled receptor (GPCR)","d":"A cell-surface receptor that associates with a G protein. Found in virtually every eukaryote. It is inactive until a ligand binds; binding activates the G protein by replacing GDP with GTP.","p":"131–132","g":"G protein signalling","ch":3},{"k":"g-protein","mod":"m9","n":"G protein","d":"A protein of three subunits (α, β and γ) on the inside of the membrane. The α subunit binds GDP or GTP. Bound to GDP, the three subunits are joined and the G protein is inactive; bound to GTP, it is active (\"on\") and the α subunit separates to bind and activate a target protein.","p":"132","g":"G protein signalling","ch":4},{"k":"alpha-subunit","mod":"m9","n":"α (alpha) subunit","d":"The part of the G protein that binds GDP or GTP. When GTP replaces GDP, the α subunit separates from β and γ and binds a target protein such as adenylyl cyclase. It turns itself off by converting GTP to GDP.","p":"132–133","g":"G protein signalling","ch":4},{"k":"gdp-gtp","mod":"m9","n":"GDP and GTP","d":"Guanine nucleotides that switch a G protein. GTP bound: on. GDP bound: off. A ligand-bound receptor makes the α subunit swap GDP for GTP; the G protein later converts GTP back to GDP.","p":"132, 135","g":"G protein signalling","ch":4},{"k":"epinephrine","mod":"m9","n":"Epinephrine (adrenaline)","d":"A hormone released by the adrenal glands. It binds a GPCR on heart muscle cells and, through a G protein, adenylyl cyclase, cAMP and PKA, increases heart rate. As long as it stays bound, the heart rate stays high.","p":"128, 133","g":"G protein signalling","ch":0},{"k":"adenylyl-cyclase","mod":"m9","n":"Adenylyl cyclase","d":"An enzyme in the cell membrane. Bound by the GTP-bound α subunit, it converts ATP into the second messenger cyclic AMP (cAMP).","p":"133","g":"G protein signalling","ch":5},{"k":"second-messenger","mod":"m9","n":"Second messenger","d":"A small signalling molecule made inside the cell in response to the signal (the first messenger) binding the receptor. It carries the signal on through the cytosol. cAMP is the example in your notes.","p":"133","g":"G protein signalling","ch":5},{"k":"camp","mod":"m9","n":"Cyclic AMP (cAMP)","d":"A small signalling molecule made from ATP by adenylyl cyclase. It is a second messenger: it binds to and activates protein kinase A. Enzymes in the cytosol degrade it to AMP to end the signal.","p":"133","g":"G protein signalling","ch":5},{"k":"pka","mod":"m9","n":"Protein kinase A (PKA)","d":"An enzyme activated by cAMP. In heart muscle, activated PKA phosphorylates proteins that increase the rate of contraction.","p":"133","g":"G protein signalling","ch":5},{"k":"kinase","mod":"m9","n":"Kinase","d":"An enzyme that phosphorylates target proteins: it transfers a phosphate group from ATP to the protein (ATP becomes ADP). Phosphorylation often switches the target protein on.","p":"133","g":"G protein signalling","ch":5},{"k":"phosphatase","mod":"m9","n":"Phosphatase","d":"An enzyme that dephosphorylates target proteins: it removes phosphate groups (as Pi), which inactivates proteins that were switched on by a kinase.","p":"133, 135","g":"Amplification and termination","ch":5},{"k":"phosphorylation","mod":"m9","n":"Phosphorylation and dephosphorylation","d":"Phosphorylation: a kinase adds a phosphate from ATP to a protein, often activating it. Dephosphorylation: a phosphatase removes it, inactivating the protein. A common on/off switch in signalling.","p":"133","g":"G protein signalling","ch":5},{"k":"amplification","mod":"m9","n":"Signal amplification","d":"A small amount of ligand produces a large response. For epinephrine it happens in three places: each receptor activates multiple G proteins, each adenylyl cyclase makes large amounts of cAMP, and each active PKA activates multiple protein targets.","p":"134","g":"Amplification and termination","ch":6},{"k":"binding-affinity","mod":"m9","n":"Binding affinity","d":"How tightly a receptor holds on to its ligand. It decides how long the ligand stays bound, and so how long the receptor stays active.","p":"135","g":"Amplification and termination","ch":7},{"k":"receptor-kinase","mod":"m9","n":"Receptor kinase","d":"A cell-surface receptor that becomes active when the ligand binds: the receptors pair up (dimerize) and each member attaches phosphate groups to the other. The phosphate groups are binding sites for intracellular signalling proteins. Used in limb bud formation, insulin signalling and wound healing.","p":"131, 136","g":"Receptor kinases","ch":3},{"k":"dimerization","mod":"m9","n":"Dimerization","d":"Two receptor kinase molecules associating into a pair when the ligand binds. Each member then phosphorylates the other (ATP to ADP).","p":"136","g":"Receptor kinases","ch":8},{"k":"pdgf","mod":"m9","n":"Platelet-derived growth factor (PDGF)","d":"A protein released by platelets when blood reaches damaged tissue, such as a paper cut. It binds PDGF receptor kinases, which dimerize and become active, stimulating repair.","p":"137","g":"Receptor kinases","ch":8},{"k":"ras","mod":"m9","n":"Ras","d":"The cytoplasmic signalling protein activated by phosphorylated receptor kinases in the MAP kinase pathway. Like a G protein, it is on when bound to GTP and off when bound to GDP. GTP-bound Ras triggers the kinase cascade.","p":"137","g":"Receptor kinases","ch":8},{"k":"map-kinase","mod":"m9","n":"MAP kinase pathway","d":"A kinase cascade started by GTP-bound Ras: kinase 1 phosphorylates and activates kinase 2, which activates kinase 3. The last kinase enters the nucleus and turns on genes for cell division. The signal is amplified from kinase to kinase. It ends when GDP replaces GTP on Ras.","p":"137","g":"Receptor kinases","ch":8},{"k":"ion-channel","mod":"m9","n":"Ion channels","d":"Membrane proteins that change the flow of ions across the membrane, into the cell (influx) or out of it (efflux). They can be gated: ligand-gated, voltage-gated or mechanically gated. The gate closed prevents ion movement; the gate open allows it.","p":"131, 138","g":"Ion channels","ch":3},{"k":"ligand-gated","mod":"m9","n":"Ligand-gated ion channel","d":"An ion channel that opens in response to binding of a signalling molecule. Inactive (closed) without the ligand, active (open) with it, letting ions flow through.","p":"138","g":"Ion channels","ch":8},{"k":"voltage-gated","mod":"m9","n":"Voltage-gated and mechanically gated channels","d":"Other gated ion channels. Voltage-gated channels respond to changes of voltage in the cell. Mechanically gated channels respond to force applied to the cell.","p":"138","g":"Ion channels","ch":8},{"k":"influx-efflux","mod":"m9","n":"Influx and efflux","d":"Influx: ions flowing into the cell. Efflux: ions flowing out of the cell.","p":"138","g":"Ion channels","ch":8},{"k":"tissue","mod":"m10","n":"Tissue","d":"A collection of cells that work together to perform a specific function. In a multicellular organism, cells come together to form tissues.","p":"141","g":"Cells and tissues","ch":0},{"k":"organ","mod":"m10","n":"Organ","d":"Two or more tissues that combine and function together. The skin is an example: epithelial tissue (epidermis) on top of connective tissue (dermis).","p":"141","g":"Cells and tissues","ch":0},{"k":"stem-cell","mod":"m10","n":"Stem cells","d":"Undifferentiated cells that can undergo an unlimited number of mitotic divisions and differentiate into any of the large number of specialized cells.","p":"141","g":"Cells and tissues","ch":0},{"k":"somatic-cell","mod":"m10","n":"Somatic cells","d":"Nonreproductive cells. The most common type of cell in the body of a multicellular organism.","p":"141","g":"Cells and tissues","ch":0},{"k":"germ-cell","mod":"m10","n":"Germ cells","d":"Reproductive cells that produce gametes (sperm or eggs).","p":"141","g":"Cells and tissues","ch":0},{"k":"cell-community","mod":"m10","n":"Cell communities","d":"The shape of cells and organs reflects their function. Shape is maintained by the cytoskeleton, and a tissue's structural integrity depends on cells adhering to one another (cell junctions) and to the extracellular matrix.","p":"141","g":"Cells and tissues","ch":0},{"k":"epithelial-tissue","mod":"m10","n":"Epithelial tissue","d":"Tissue that covers the outside of the body and lines many internal structures of the body. In skin it forms the epidermis, which sits on a basal lamina.","p":"142","g":"Skin","ch":null},{"k":"connective-tissue","mod":"m10","n":"Connective tissue","d":"Tissue that provides structure and support. It is extensive in ECM, with relatively few cells (mainly fibroblasts). The dermis is mostly connective tissue.","p":"142, 155","g":"Skin","ch":null},{"k":"epidermis","mod":"m10","n":"Epidermis","d":"The outer layer of skin, a water-resistant and protective barrier. Made mainly of epithelial cells called keratinocytes, with melanocytes that make skin pigment. Its bottom layer is attached to the basal lamina.","p":"142","g":"Skin","ch":0},{"k":"dermis","mod":"m10","n":"Dermis","d":"The layer beneath the epidermis. It supports the epidermis and supplies it with nutrients. Mostly connective tissue; its main cell type is the fibroblast. Also contains nerves and blood vessels.","p":"142","g":"Skin","ch":0},{"k":"keratinocyte","mod":"m10","n":"Keratinocytes","d":"The main epithelial cells of the epidermis, specialized to protect underlying tissues and organs. The bottom layer of keratinocytes is attached to the basal lamina.","p":"142","g":"Skin","ch":0},{"k":"melanocyte","mod":"m10","n":"Melanocytes","d":"Cells in the epidermis that produce the pigments of skin.","p":"142","g":"Skin","ch":0},{"k":"fibroblast","mod":"m10","n":"Fibroblast","d":"The main cell type in the dermis and other connective tissue. Fibroblasts produce (secrete) the extracellular matrix, including collagen.","p":"142, 155","g":"Skin","ch":0},{"k":"cytoskeleton","mod":"m10","n":"Cytoskeleton","d":"Protein fibers in the cytoplasm that give internal support to cells, just as bones support the body. Formed from long chains of protein subunits. Gives structural support and enables movement of substances within cells. All eukaryotic cells have microtubules and microfilaments; animal cells also have intermediate filaments.","p":"143","g":"The cytoskeleton","ch":1},{"k":"microtubule","mod":"m10","n":"Microtubules","d":"Hollow tubes made of polymers of tubulin dimers, the widest cytoskeletal element. In animal cells they radiate out from the centrosome, help the cell keep its shape and withstand compression, and position organelles. Also used for vesicle transport, cilia and flagella, and chromosome segregation.","p":"144, 148","g":"Microtubules","ch":1},{"k":"tubulin","mod":"m10","n":"Alpha and beta tubulin","d":"The two tubulin proteins. One α tubulin and one β tubulin combine to form a tubulin dimer, and the dimers assemble to form microtubules.","p":"144","g":"Microtubules","ch":1},{"k":"centrosome","mod":"m10","n":"Centrosome","d":"The microtubule organizing centre of an animal cell, near the nucleus. Minus ends of microtubules sit at the centrosome; plus ends project outward toward the cell membrane.","p":"144, 146","g":"Microtubules","ch":1},{"k":"microfilament","mod":"m10","n":"Microfilaments","d":"Polymers of actin monomers twisted into a double helix. The thinnest cytoskeletal fibers. Short and extensively branched just beneath the cell membrane, where they reinforce it and organize its proteins. Also form the cores of microvilli and take part in transport, muscle contraction and cell division.","p":"144–145","g":"Microfilaments","ch":1},{"k":"actin","mod":"m10","n":"Actin","d":"The protein monomer that polymerizes into microfilaments. Two strands of actin twist into a double helix.","p":"144, 148","g":"Microfilaments","ch":1},{"k":"microvilli","mod":"m10","n":"Microvilli","d":"Finger-like projections on the surface of intestinal epithelial cells. Each contains a bundle of actin microfilaments held together by cross-linking actin-binding proteins.","p":"145","g":"Microfilaments","ch":1},{"k":"mf-band","mod":"m10","n":"Band of microfilaments","d":"A band of longer microfilaments around the circumference of epithelial cells. It supports each cell and the whole layer of cells, and it is attached to a cell junction that connects neighbouring cells (the adherens junction).","p":"145, 151","g":"Microfilaments","ch":1},{"k":"intermediate-filament","mod":"m10","n":"Intermediate filaments","d":"Strong, cable-like polymers of proteins found in animal cells. Their diameter is intermediate between microfilaments and microtubules. They give the cell mechanical strength. More than 100 types, made of different proteins in different cell types.","p":"147–148","g":"Intermediate filaments","ch":4},{"k":"if-types","mod":"m10","n":"Types of intermediate filaments","d":"Epithelial cells: keratins. Fibroblasts: vimentin. Neurons: neurofilaments. Nucleus: lamins. Subunits are diverse, but the function is the same: cell shape and support.","p":"148","g":"Intermediate filaments","ch":4},{"k":"plus-minus","mod":"m10","n":"Plus and minus ends","d":"Microtubules and microfilaments grow faster at one end than the other. The faster-growing end is the plus (+) end; the slower-growing end is the minus (−) end. Subunits can be added or removed at both ends.","p":"145–146","g":"Dynamics and motors","ch":2},{"k":"dynamic-instability","mod":"m10","n":"Dynamic instability","d":"Random cycles of slower growth (polymerization) and rapid shrinkage (depolymerization) in microtubules. The dramatic shrinkage is called a catastrophe. It lets the cell reorganize its cytoskeleton quickly, and lets spindle microtubules find and attach to chromosomes during cell division.","p":"146","g":"Dynamics and motors","ch":2},{"k":"motor-protein","mod":"m10","n":"Motor proteins","d":"Small accessory proteins that join microtubules and microfilaments and move along them. Movement comes from conformational changes in the motor protein, powered by ATP.","p":"147","g":"Dynamics and motors","ch":3},{"k":"kinesin-dynein","mod":"m10","n":"Kinesin and dynein","d":"Motor proteins that walk along microtubule tracks. Kinesin carries cargo, such as vesicles, toward the plus end (out to the cell periphery). Dynein carries cargo toward the minus end (in toward the centrosome). Dynein also moves cilia and flagella.","p":"147","g":"Dynamics and motors","ch":3},{"k":"myosin","mod":"m10","n":"Myosin","d":"The motor protein that moves along actin microfilaments, powered by ATP. Myosin pulling on actin shortens muscle cells during contraction and pinches daughter cells apart in cytokinesis. (Named in the terms list; the standard textbook definition.)","p":"145, 148","g":"Dynamics and motors","ch":3},{"k":"cilia-flagella","mod":"m10","n":"Cilia and flagella","d":"Structures built from microtubules that propel cells (for example a sperm's flagellum) or move substances over the cell surface (for example cilia sweeping mucus in the airway). Dynein, bound to the microtubules, causes the movement.","p":"147","g":"Dynamics and motors","ch":3},{"k":"polarity","mod":"m10","n":"Cell polarity","d":"Spatial differences in shape, structure and function within a cell. In epithelial cells, the apical membrane faces the outside of the body or the lumen and the basolateral membrane is attached to the basal lamina.","p":"149","g":"Cell adhesion","ch":5},{"k":"apical","mod":"m10","n":"Apical membrane","d":"The surface of an epithelial cell that faces the outside of the body or the lumen. In intestinal cells it carries the microvilli.","p":"149","g":"Cell adhesion","ch":5},{"k":"basolateral","mod":"m10","n":"Basolateral membrane","d":"The sides and base of an epithelial cell. The base is attached to the basal lamina.","p":"149","g":"Cell adhesion","ch":5},{"k":"cam","mod":"m10","n":"Cell adhesion molecules","d":"Integral transmembrane glycoproteins that attach cells to each other or to the ECM and link to the cytoskeleton inside. Cadherins join cell to cell; integrins join cell to ECM.","p":"149–151","g":"Cell adhesion","ch":5},{"k":"cadherin","mod":"m10","n":"Cadherins","d":"Integral transmembrane glycoproteins for cell–cell adhesion. The extracellular domain binds a cadherin of the same type on an adjacent cell; the cytoplasmic part links to the cytoskeleton. This gives structural continuity from one cell's cytoskeleton to the next and strengthens tissues. Found in adherens junctions and desmosomes.","p":"149","g":"Cell adhesion","ch":5},{"k":"integrin","mod":"m10","n":"Integrins","d":"Integral transmembrane glycoproteins for cell–ECM adhesion. The extracellular domain binds ECM proteins; the cytoplasmic domain interacts with the cytoskeleton, which keeps tissues intact under physical stress. The main adhesion molecule of hemidesmosomes.","p":"150","g":"Cell adhesion","ch":5},{"k":"cell-junctions","mod":"m10","n":"Cell junctions","d":"Structures that physically connect a cell to another cell or to the ECM (basal lamina), reinforced by the cytoskeleton. Five types: adherens junctions, desmosomes, hemidesmosomes, tight junctions and gap junctions. Plants use plasmodesmata.","p":"149–150","g":"Cell junctions","ch":6},{"k":"anchoring","mod":"m10","n":"Anchoring junctions","d":"Junctions that hold cells in place and link to the cytoskeleton: adherens junctions and desmosomes (cell–cell, cadherins) and hemidesmosomes (cell–ECM, integrins).","p":"153","g":"Cell junctions","ch":6},{"k":"adherens","mod":"m10","n":"Adherens junction","d":"A belt-like complex of cadherins around the circumference of the cell. Inside, the cadherins attach to a band of actin microfilaments; outside, they bind cadherins of the adjacent cell. Function: cell–cell adhesion.","p":"151","g":"Cell junctions","ch":6},{"k":"desmosome","mod":"m10","n":"Desmosomes","d":"Button-like points of adhesion that hold the membranes of adjacent cells together. Cadherins in one cell's desmosome bind those of the adjacent cell, and their cytoplasmic domains link to intermediate filaments. Function: cell–cell adhesion.","p":"151","g":"Cell junctions","ch":6},{"k":"hemidesmosome","mod":"m10","n":"Hemidesmosome","d":"A desmosome-like structure that firmly anchors epithelial cells to the basal lamina. Integrins bind ECM proteins of the basal lamina outside and intermediate filaments inside. Function: cell–ECM adhesion.","p":"151","g":"Cell junctions","ch":6},{"k":"tight-junction","mod":"m10","n":"Tight junction","d":"A barrier junction made of claudins and occludins that seals the extracellular space between cells, which adherens junctions and desmosomes can't do. A substance can only cross a sheet of epithelial cells by moving through the cells by a transport mechanism. Function: epithelial boundary.","p":"152–153","g":"Cell junctions","ch":7},{"k":"gap-junction","mod":"m10","n":"Gap junction","d":"A communicating junction in animal cells. Tiny channels (connexons, made of connexins) span a 2–4 nm gap and connect the cytoplasm of adjacent cells. Only low molecular weight material, such as ions, can pass. They integrate the activities of cells in a tissue.","p":"152–153","g":"Cell junctions","ch":7},{"k":"connexon","mod":"m10","n":"Connexons","d":"The extremely small channels of a gap junction, made of connexin proteins. They span the 2–4 nm gap and give cytoplasmic continuity between adjacent cells.","p":"152–153","g":"Cell junctions","ch":7},{"k":"plasmodesmata","mod":"m10","n":"Plasmodesmata","d":"Channels between plant cells, lined by the cell membrane, which is continuous from one cell to the next. Much larger than gap junctions: they can pass RNA molecules and proteins, so plant cells can signal despite their rigid walls. Function: communication between plant cells.","p":"152–153","g":"Cell junctions","ch":7},{"k":"ecm","mod":"m10","n":"Extracellular matrix (ECM)","d":"The layer of material secreted beyond the cell membrane: an insoluble meshwork of proteins and polysaccharides. It acts as a supportive and protective material and allows the expression of different cell functions. In plants it is the cell wall.","p":"141, 154","g":"The ECM","ch":8},{"k":"cell-wall","mod":"m10","n":"Plant cell wall","d":"The ECM of plant cells. Three layers: the middle lamella (carbohydrates; glues neighbouring cells together), the primary cell wall (cellulose, pectin and proteins) and the secondary cell wall (cellulose and lignin).","p":"154","g":"The ECM","ch":8},{"k":"middle-lamella","mod":"m10","n":"Middle lamella","d":"The outermost layer between two plant cells, made of carbohydrates. The main mechanism by which plant cells adhere to one another.","p":"154","g":"The ECM","ch":8},{"k":"primary-wall","mod":"m10","n":"Primary cell wall","d":"Made of cellulose fibers, pectin and several other proteins. Laid down first, while the cell is still growing, so it is thin and flexible.","p":"154","g":"The ECM","ch":8},{"k":"secondary-wall","mod":"m10","n":"Secondary cell wall","d":"Made of cellulose and lignin. Laid down inside the primary wall once the cell stops growing. Lignin hardens the wall and makes it water resistant.","p":"154","g":"The ECM","ch":8},{"k":"animal-ecm","mod":"m10","n":"Animal ECM","d":"Extensive in connective tissue. A mixture of proteins and polysaccharides secreted by cells: large fibrous proteins (collagen, elastin and laminin) in a gel-like polysaccharide matrix.","p":"155","g":"The ECM","ch":8},{"k":"collagen","mod":"m10","n":"Collagen","d":"The most abundant protein in the animal ECM and in animals (about 1/4 of the body's protein). More than 20 forms; type I, found in the dermis, is the most abundant. Three polypeptides wind into a triple helix; bundles of these molecules form fibrils, and fibrils form fibers, stronger than a single fiber of the same diameter.","p":"155","g":"The ECM","ch":8},{"k":"elastin","mod":"m10","n":"Elastin","d":"A large fibrous protein of the animal ECM, seen as elastin fibers among the collagen fibers of the dermis.","p":"155","g":"The ECM","ch":8},{"k":"laminin","mod":"m10","n":"Laminin","d":"A large fibrous protein of the animal ECM. It is a main protein of the basal lamina, where integrins bind it. (Named in your notes; the basal lamina role is the standard textbook answer.)","p":"155","g":"The ECM","ch":8},{"k":"basal-lamina","mod":"m10","n":"Basal lamina","d":"A specialized layer of ECM beneath all epithelial tissues, such as below the bottom layer of the epidermis. Made of several proteins, including a type of collagen. It gives a structural foundation and flexible support, and acts as a scaffold for other proteins to assemble on.","p":"142, 156","g":"The ECM","ch":0},{"k":"cell-division","mod":"m11","n":"Cell division","d":"How a cell makes more cells. It happens for cell growth, cell replacement, cell healing and cell reproduction. Each daughter cell must receive all of the parent's genetic material, and the parent must be big enough that each daughter gets enough cytoplasm.","p":"159","g":"Cell division","ch":0},{"k":"parent-cell","mod":"m11","n":"Parent cell","d":"The single cell that divides. It must copy all of its genetic material and be big enough to divide in two, so each daughter cell gets adequate cytoplasmic components.","p":"159","g":"Cell division","ch":0},{"k":"daughter-cell","mod":"m11","n":"Daughter cell","d":"One of the two cells produced when a parent cell divides. Each receives a full copy of the parent's genetic material and is identical to the parent cell.","p":"159–160","g":"Cell division","ch":0},{"k":"binary-fission","mod":"m11","n":"Binary fission","d":"Cell division in prokaryotes: DNA replication, increase in cell size, then division into two daughter cells that each get one copy of the replicated DNA. The circular genome is attached to the plasma membrane; the attachment sites separate as the cell elongates, and a new membrane and cell wall form at a constriction in the middle.","p":"159–160","g":"Cell division","ch":0},{"k":"chromosome","mod":"m11","n":"Chromosomes (circular and linear)","d":"Prokaryotes have a small, circular chromosome in the cytoplasm, attached to the cell membrane. Eukaryotes have a large genome of linear chromosomes inside the nucleus, made of DNA packaged with histones and other proteins (chromatin).","p":"161, 169–170","g":"Cell division","ch":0},{"k":"cell-cycle","mod":"m11","n":"Cell cycle","d":"The series of stages by which a eukaryotic cell divides. Two distinct stages: M phase (the parent cell divides) and interphase (the time between two successive M phases: G1, S and G2).","p":"162","g":"The cell cycle","ch":1},{"k":"m-phase","mod":"m11","n":"M phase","d":"The part of the cell cycle when the parent cell divides into two daughter cells. It consists of mitosis (separation of the replicated chromosomes) and cytokinesis (division of the cytoplasm).","p":"162","g":"The cell cycle","ch":1},{"k":"interphase","mod":"m11","n":"Interphase","d":"The time between two successive M phases, about 10 to 14 hours. The cell prepares for division: DNA replication in the nucleus and an increase in cell size. Made up of G1, S and G2 (the notes also list G0 here).","p":"162","g":"The cell cycle","ch":1},{"k":"g1","mod":"m11","n":"G₁ phase","d":"The first \"gap\" phase. The cell increases in size and protein content, prepares for S phase, and makes and activates regulatory proteins.","p":"162","g":"The cell cycle","ch":1},{"k":"s-phase","mod":"m11","n":"S phase","d":"The \"synthesis\" phase: the DNA is replicated, so every chromosome becomes two sister chromatids.","p":"162","g":"The cell cycle","ch":1},{"k":"g2","mod":"m11","n":"G₂ phase","d":"The second \"gap\" phase. The cell prepares for M phase.","p":"162","g":"The cell cycle","ch":1},{"k":"g0","mod":"m11","n":"G₀ phase","d":"A state separate from G1 with no active preparation for cell division. It occurs in cells that do not actively divide, for example liver cells.","p":"162","g":"The cell cycle","ch":1},{"k":"dna-replication","mod":"m11","n":"DNA replication","d":"Copying the DNA before a cell divides. The two parental strands unwind and each serves as a template for a new daughter strand. It is similar in prokaryotes and eukaryotes.","p":"163","g":"Replication basics","ch":2},{"k":"semiconservative","mod":"m11","n":"Semiconservative replication","d":"The way DNA is copied: each new DNA molecule consists of one original parental strand and one newly made daughter strand.","p":"163","g":"Replication basics","ch":2},{"k":"template-strand","mod":"m11","n":"Template strand","d":"A parental DNA strand that is read to make a complementary daughter strand. After the parent molecule unwinds, each of its two strands is a template.","p":"163","g":"Replication basics","ch":2},{"k":"daughter-strand","mod":"m11","n":"Daughter strand","d":"The newly synthesized DNA strand, complementary and antiparallel to its template strand.","p":"163","g":"Replication basics","ch":2},{"k":"replication-fork","mod":"m11","n":"Replication fork","d":"The Y-shaped point where the parental double helix is being unwound and the two strands separate, so each can be copied.","p":"163–164","g":"Replication basics","ch":2},{"k":"helicase","mod":"m11","n":"Helicase","d":"Unwinds the parental double helix at the replication fork, so a single strand is available for DNA polymerase to add complementary bases.","p":"164","g":"Replication enzymes","ch":3},{"k":"ssb","mod":"m11","n":"Single-strand binding protein (SSB)","d":"Binds to the single-stranded regions of the parental strands and prevents them from coming back together.","p":"164","g":"Replication enzymes","ch":3},{"k":"topoisomerase","mod":"m11","n":"Topoisomerase","d":"Works upstream (ahead) of the replication fork. It changes the supercoiled state of the DNA caused by unwinding of the double helix at the fork, relieving the stress of unwinding.","p":"164","g":"Replication enzymes","ch":3},{"k":"dna-pol","mod":"m11","n":"DNA polymerase","d":"Adds nucleotides to the growing strand. It needs the 4 deoxyribonucleotides (dATP, dTTP, dGTP, dCTP), a DNA template and a primer with a 3′-OH end. It can only synthesize DNA 5′ to 3′, and most DNA polymerases can correct their own mistakes.","p":"165","g":"Replication enzymes","ch":3},{"k":"primase","mod":"m11","n":"RNA primase","d":"An RNA polymerase that synthesizes a short piece of RNA complementary to the parental DNA strand. This RNA primer is needed so DNA polymerase can add DNA bases.","p":"165, 167","g":"Replication enzymes","ch":3},{"k":"rna-primer","mod":"m11","n":"RNA primer","d":"A short piece of RNA laid down by primase. It provides the 3′-OH end that DNA polymerase extends. A different DNA polymerase later removes it and replaces it with DNA.","p":"165, 167","g":"Replication enzymes","ch":3},{"k":"dna-ligase","mod":"m11","n":"DNA ligase","d":"Joins DNA fragments once the RNA primer has been replaced with DNA, completing the sugar-phosphate backbone. It also seals the gap when two replication bubbles meet.","p":"167, 170","g":"Replication enzymes","ch":4},{"k":"proofreading","mod":"m11","n":"Proofreading","d":"How most DNA polymerases correct their own errors. Hydrogen bonds briefly hold the new nucleotide against the template base; if the polymerase detects mispairing, it activates a cleavage function, removes the incorrect nucleotide and inserts the correct one.","p":"168","g":"Replication enzymes","ch":4},{"k":"leading","mod":"m11","n":"Leading strand","d":"The daughter strand whose 3′ end points toward the replication fork. It grows toward the fork and is synthesized as one long, continuous polymer as the parental strand unwinds.","p":"166","g":"Leading and lagging strands","ch":4},{"k":"lagging","mod":"m11","n":"Lagging strand","d":"The daughter strand whose 3′ end points away from the replication fork. It grows away from the fork and is made discontinuously in short pieces (Okazaki fragments), each started with a new RNA primer.","p":"166","g":"Leading and lagging strands","ch":4},{"k":"okazaki","mod":"m11","n":"Okazaki fragments","d":"The short, discontinuous pieces of the lagging strand. Each needs an RNA primer, extension by DNA polymerase, then replacement of the primer with DNA and joining by ligase. They are needed because DNA is made only 5′ to 3′.","p":"166","g":"Leading and lagging strands","ch":4},{"k":"trombone","mod":"m11","n":"Trombone model","d":"How the leading and lagging strands are made at the same time: the lagging strand template loops out, and the loop persists until the new Okazaki fragment reaches the previous one.","p":"167","g":"Leading and lagging strands","ch":4},{"k":"origin","mod":"m11","n":"Origin of replication","d":"The spot where replication starts. Circular chromosomes (most bacteria, mitochondria, chloroplasts) have a single origin; linear eukaryotic chromosomes have many.","p":"169–170","g":"Origins and ends","ch":5},{"k":"replication-bubble","mod":"m11","n":"Replication bubble","d":"The opened region around an origin. Each bubble has two replication forks moving in opposite directions. When two bubbles meet they fuse into one larger bubble and ligase seals the backbone.","p":"170","g":"Origins and ends","ch":5},{"k":"end-problem","mod":"m11","n":"Shortening of linear DNA","d":"The leading strand copies to the end of its template, but the last lagging-strand primer is laid about 100 nucleotides from the 3′ end. When it is removed, that section of template stays unreplicated, so one strand gets shorter each round.","p":"170","g":"Origins and ends","ch":6},{"k":"telomere","mod":"m11","n":"Telomere","d":"The end of a linear chromosome: a repeated sequence whose sequence and number of repeats vary between species. In humans, 5′-TTAGGG-3′ is repeated 1500 to 3000 times at each telomere.","p":"171","g":"Origins and ends","ch":6},{"k":"telomerase","mod":"m11","n":"Telomerase","d":"A ribonucleoprotein (protein-RNA complex) with reverse transcriptase activity (RNA → DNA). It carries its own RNA template, complementary to the telomere repeat, and adds repeats to the 3′-OH end of the template strand so it doesn't shorten. Fully active in stem cells and germ cells; inactive in adult somatic cells.","p":"171","g":"Origins and ends","ch":6},{"k":"hayflick","mod":"m11","n":"Hayflick limit","d":"In adult somatic cells, with telomerase inactive, mitotic division can occur only about 50 times before the telomeres become so short that the cells stop dividing.","p":"171","g":"Origins and ends","ch":6},{"k":"chromatin","mod":"m11","n":"Chromatin","d":"DNA organized with histones and other proteins. Chromatin can be looped and packaged to form chromosomes.","p":"172","g":"Chromosomes","ch":7},{"k":"condensation","mod":"m11","n":"Chromosome condensation","d":"Packaging of chromatin into compact chromosomes: DNA (2 nm) wraps histones into a nucleosome fiber (10 nm), then a 30 nm chromatin fiber, a 300 nm coiled fiber, a 700 nm coiled coil, and a 1400 nm condensed chromatid. Chromosomes are visible only in cells about to divide.","p":"172","g":"Chromosomes","ch":7},{"k":"nucleosome","mod":"m11","n":"Nucleosome and histones","d":"DNA wrapped around histone proteins (H2A, H2B, H3 and H4) forms nucleosomes, giving the 10 nm nucleosome fiber.","p":"172","g":"Chromosomes","ch":7},{"k":"haploid","mod":"m11","n":"Haploid and diploid","d":"A cell with one copy of each chromosome is haploid; a cell with two copies of each chromosome is diploid.","p":"172","g":"Chromosomes","ch":7},{"k":"homologous","mod":"m11","n":"Homologous chromosomes","d":"The two copies of a chromosome in a diploid cell (shown as a dark and a light pair in the notes' figure). They carry the same genes and are each copied in S phase.","p":"172","g":"Chromosomes","ch":7},{"k":"sister-chromatids","mod":"m11","n":"Sister chromatids","d":"The two identical copies of a chromosome made by DNA replication in S phase. They stay side by side, held together at the centromere, until anaphase.","p":"172","g":"Chromosomes","ch":7},{"k":"nonsister","mod":"m11","n":"Nonsister chromatids","d":"Chromatids that belong to different members of a homologous pair (one from each homolog), as opposed to sister chromatids, which are copies of the same chromosome.","p":"172","g":"Chromosomes","ch":7},{"k":"centromere","mod":"m11","n":"Centromere","d":"The region where sister chromatids are held together. Spindle microtubules attach there through kinetochores. In anaphase the centromere divides.","p":"172, 174","g":"Chromosomes","ch":7},{"k":"mitosis","mod":"m11","n":"Mitosis (all phases)","d":"Separation of the replicated chromosomes, the nuclear division part of M phase. Five stages, told apart by the position of the chromosomes: prophase, prometaphase, metaphase, anaphase and telophase.","p":"162, 173–174","g":"Mitosis and cytokinesis","ch":1},{"k":"prophase","mod":"m11","n":"Prophase","d":"Stage 1: chromosomes condense and become visible. Outside the nucleus the mitotic spindle assembles, and the duplicated centrosomes migrate to opposite poles.","p":"173","g":"Mitosis and cytokinesis","ch":8},{"k":"prometaphase","mod":"m11","n":"Prometaphase","d":"Stage 2: the nuclear membrane breaks down. Spindle microtubules grow and shrink to explore the old nuclear region and attach to the kinetochores at each centromere.","p":"174","g":"Mitosis and cytokinesis","ch":8},{"k":"metaphase","mod":"m11","n":"Metaphase","d":"Stage 3: spindle microtubules lengthen and shorten, pushing the chromosomes to the metaphase plate, a single plane roughly equidistant from both spindle poles.","p":"174","g":"Mitosis and cytokinesis","ch":8},{"k":"anaphase","mod":"m11","n":"Anaphase","d":"Stage 4: the centromere divides and kinetochore microtubules shorten, pulling sister chromatids apart toward opposite poles. Each chromatid is now an individual chromosome.","p":"174","g":"Mitosis and cytokinesis","ch":8},{"k":"telophase","mod":"m11","n":"Telophase","d":"Stage 5: a complete set of chromosomes arrives at each pole, the spindle breaks down, nuclear envelopes re-form around each set, and the chromosomes decondense. Telophase marks the end of mitosis.","p":"174","g":"Mitosis and cytokinesis","ch":8},{"k":"mitotic-spindle","mod":"m11","n":"Mitotic spindle","d":"A structure of microtubules assembled outside the nucleus in prophase. It attaches to the chromosomes and pulls them apart into the daughter cells.","p":"173","g":"Mitosis and cytokinesis","ch":8},{"k":"centrosome","mod":"m11","n":"Centrosome","d":"The microtubule-organizing centre of animal cells. Centrosomes are duplicated and migrate to opposite poles in prophase. Plant cells have microtubule spindles but lack centrosomes.","p":"173","g":"Mitosis and cytokinesis","ch":8},{"k":"kinetochore","mod":"m11","n":"Kinetochore","d":"One of two protein complexes at each centromere, one on each side. Each is associated with one sister chromatid and is the attachment site for a microtubule, which is key for separating sister chromatids.","p":"174","g":"Mitosis and cytokinesis","ch":8},{"k":"metaphase-plate","mod":"m11","n":"Metaphase plate","d":"The single plane, roughly equidistant from both spindle poles, where chromosomes line up in metaphase.","p":"174","g":"Mitosis and cytokinesis","ch":8},{"k":"equational","mod":"m11","n":"Equational division","d":"A division in which the daughter cells keep the same number of chromosomes as the parent. Mitotic cell division is equational: it produces two cells genetically identical to the parent cell, except for rare mutations.","p":"181–182","g":"Mitosis and cytokinesis","ch":8},{"k":"cytokinesis","mod":"m11","n":"Cytokinesis","d":"Division of the cytoplasm into two daughter cells, the second part of M phase. Animal cells use a contractile ring of actin; plant cells build a cell plate.","p":"162, 175","g":"Mitosis and cytokinesis","ch":1},{"k":"contractile-ring","mod":"m11","n":"Contractile ring","d":"A ring of actin filaments on the inner face of the animal cell membrane, at the equator, perpendicular to the old spindle axis. It contracts and pinches the cytoplasm into two daughter cells.","p":"175","g":"Mitosis and cytokinesis","ch":8},{"k":"cell-plate","mod":"m11","n":"Cell plate and phragmoplast","d":"In plant cells the phragmoplast (overlapping microtubules) forms in the middle of the cell in telophase and guides vesicles of cell wall components there. In late anaphase and telophase they fuse into the cell plate, which fuses with the original wall to complete cytokinesis.","p":"175","g":"Mitosis and cytokinesis","ch":8},{"k":"cyclin","mod":"m11","n":"Cyclin","d":"Regulatory protein subunits of specific protein kinases (CDKs). Their levels rise and fall with each turn of the cell cycle. Cyclin D and E rise in G1, cyclin A in S, cyclin B in G2.","p":"176–177","g":"Cell cycle regulation","ch":9},{"k":"cdk","mod":"m11","n":"Cyclin-dependent kinases (CDKs)","d":"Kinases that phosphorylate other proteins needed for the cell cycle to progress. Always present in the cell, but active only when bound to the appropriate cyclin.","p":"176","g":"Cell cycle regulation","ch":9},{"k":"checkpoint","mod":"m11","n":"Checkpoint","d":"A control point that makes sure the cell is ready to move on. Three major ones: the DNA damage checkpoint (before S), the DNA replication checkpoint (end of G2) and the spindle assembly checkpoint (before anaphase).","p":"177","g":"Cell cycle regulation","ch":9},{"k":"p53","mod":"m11","n":"p53","d":"A nuclear protein, often called the guardian of the genome. DNA damage activates a kinase that phosphorylates p53; it then turns on genes including an inhibitor of the G1/S cyclin–CDK, arresting the cell so the DNA can be repaired. It is a tumour suppressor.","p":"178–180","g":"Cell cycle regulation","ch":9},{"k":"apoptosis","mod":"m11","n":"Apoptosis","d":"Programmed cell death: a controlled, orderly disintegration of the cell, triggered when Bax/Bax dimers increase. It remodels tissues in the embryo, maintains tissue size, removes specific cells and removes genetically damaged cells.","p":"179","g":"Cell death and cancer","ch":10},{"k":"bax","mod":"m11","n":"Bax and Bcl-2","d":"Phosphorylated p53 increases transcription of Bax (promotes cell death) and represses Bcl-2 (blocks cell death). A healthy cell has balanced Bax/Bcl-2 dimers; excess Bax forms Bax/Bax dimers, which trigger apoptosis.","p":"179","g":"Cell death and cancer","ch":10},{"k":"oncogene","mod":"m11","n":"Oncogene","d":"A cancer-causing gene. First discovered in viruses; also altered versions of proto-oncogenes.","p":"180","g":"Cell death and cancer","ch":10},{"k":"proto-oncogene","mod":"m11","n":"Proto-oncogene","d":"A normal gene important in cell division that promotes division. It has the potential to be mutated into an oncogene.","p":"180","g":"Cell death and cancer","ch":10},{"k":"tumour-suppressor","mod":"m11","n":"Tumour suppressor","d":"A gene whose product inhibits cell division, for example p53. Proto-oncogenes and tumour suppressors are a counterbalance system that must agree for division to take place.","p":"180","g":"Cell death and cancer","ch":10},{"k":"cancer","mod":"m11","n":"Cancer","d":"A group of diseases characterized by uncontrolled cell division. Cancer cells divide without growth signals, resist inhibitory and death signals, invade tissues (metastasis) and promote new blood vessel formation (angiogenesis). It builds up through several mutations.","p":"180","g":"Cell death and cancer","ch":10}],"exam":{"m1":[{"ch":0,"p":"4","q":"Who first described and named \"cells\", and what was he looking at?","opts":[["Robert Hooke, looking at cork",1,"In 1665 he saw the empty walls of dead cork cells."],["Anton van Leeuwenhoek, looking at pond water",0,"Leeuwenhoek saw living animalcules, but Hooke named cells first."],["Schleiden, looking at plant embryos",0,"Schleiden came much later, with the cell theory."],["Virchow, looking at blood",0,"Virchow added the third tenet of the cell theory."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"4","q":"What did Leeuwenhoek see that Hooke did not?","opts":[["Living, moving single-celled organisms",1,"He called them animalcules."],["Cell walls",0,"Hooke saw walls; Leeuwenhoek saw living cells."],["Viruses",0,"Viruses are far too small for a light microscope."],["Mitochondria",0,"Early lenses could not show organelles."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"4","q":"Which kind of microscopy uses dyes such as DAPI to make specific structures glow?","opts":[["Fluorescence microscopy",1,"DAPI labels the nucleus; phalloidin labels actin."],["Light microscopy with no stain",0,"Plain light microscopy does not make structures glow."],["Electron microscopy",0,"Electron microscopes use electron beams, not glowing dyes."],["Hooke's microscope",0,"Hooke used simple light microscopy."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"5","q":"Which list gives the three tenets of the cell theory?","opts":[["All organisms are made of one or more cells; the cell is the structural and functional unit of life; cells arise only from pre-existing cells",1,""],["All cells have a nucleus; all cells have mitochondria; all cells have a cell wall",0,"Prokaryotes have no nucleus, and animal cells have no wall."],["Cells come from nonliving matter; cells are alive; cells divide",0,"The cell theory rejects spontaneous generation."],["DNA makes RNA; RNA makes protein; protein makes cells",0,"That's closer to the central dogma."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"5","q":"Who proposed the first two tenets of the cell theory?","opts":[["Schleiden and Schwann",1,"A botanist and a zoologist."],["Hooke and Leeuwenhoek",0,"They made the first observations, not the theory."],["Virchow alone",0,"Virchow added the third tenet."],["Watson and Crick",0,"They described DNA's structure."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"5","q":"Virchow was a ___ who added the idea that ___.","opts":[["physician; cells arise only from pre-existing cells",1,""],["botanist; plant tissues are made of cells",0,"That was Schleiden."],["zoologist; animals are made of cells",0,"That was Schwann."],["microscopist; cork is made of cells",0,"That was Hooke."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"5","q":"Which is true of viruses?","opts":[["They are inert outside a living cell and need a host cell to reproduce",1,""],["They have their own ribosomes",0,"Viruses use the host's ribosomes."],["They have no nucleic acid",0,"That describes prions."],["They reproduce by binary fission",0,"That is how prokaryotes reproduce."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"5","q":"A small circular RNA with no protein coat that infects plants is a:","opts":[["Viroid",1,"Example: potato spindle tuber viroid."],["Virus",0,"Viruses have a protein coat."],["Prion",0,"Prions are protein with no nucleic acid."],["Plasmid",0,"Plasmids are DNA circles inside bacteria."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"5","q":"How does a prion cause disease?","opts":[["A misfolded protein makes normal copies of the protein misfold, and the misfolded form accumulates",1,"PrP^Sc converts PrP^C."],["It inserts its DNA into the host genome",0,"Prions have no DNA."],["It reproduces using the host's ribosomes",0,"No nucleic acid, no genes to translate."],["It digests the cell wall",0,"Not how prions work."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"6","q":"What is the difference between cytoplasm and cytosol?","opts":[["Cytoplasm is all the contents inside the membrane; cytosol is the fluid part, cytoplasm minus organelles",1,""],["They mean exactly the same thing",0,"Cytosol excludes the organelles."],["Cytosol includes the organelles; cytoplasm doesn't",0,"It's the other way around."],["Cytoplasm is only found in prokaryotes",0,"All cells have cytoplasm."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"6","q":"What is a gene?","opts":[["The DNA code for the information needed to produce a functional product, such as a protein",1,""],["A protein that copies DNA",0,"That's an enzyme, not a gene."],["All the DNA in a cell",0,"That would be the genome."],["A molecule of ATP",0,"ATP is an energy carrier."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"6","q":"In the central dogma, making RNA from DNA is called ___ and making protein from RNA is called ___.","opts":[["transcription; translation",1,""],["translation; transcription",0,"Reversed."],["replication; transcription",0,"Replication copies DNA into DNA."],["transcription; replication",0,"Protein is made by translation."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"6","q":"ATP is made of:","opts":[["Adenine, ribose and three phosphate groups",1,"With two phosphates it's ADP; with one, AMP."],["Adenine, deoxyribose and one phosphate",0,"ATP has ribose and three phosphates."],["Three amino acids",0,"ATP is a nucleotide, not a peptide."],["Glucose and three phosphates",0,"The sugar is ribose and there is a base, adenine."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"6","q":"A cell loses the ability to control its own division and grows uncontrollably. Which basic property has failed?","opts":[["Self-regulation",1,""],["Acquiring energy",0,"Energy use isn't the problem here."],["Having a membrane",0,"The membrane is still there."],["Producing more of themselves",0,"It's producing too many, because regulation failed."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"7","q":"Which describes prokaryotes?","opts":[["No nucleus and no membrane-bound organelles; a single circular chromosome",1,""],["A nucleus but no mitochondria",0,"Prokaryotes have no nucleus."],["Always multicellular",0,"Prokaryotes are single cells."],["Found only in extreme environments",0,"Some archaea are, but prokaryotes are everywhere: the most abundant organisms on Earth."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"7","q":"What are the three domains of life?","opts":[["Bacteria, Archaea and Eukarya",1,""],["Plants, animals and fungi",0,"Those are groups within Eukarya."],["Prokaryotes, viruses and eukaryotes",0,"Viruses aren't on the tree of cellular life."],["Protists, bacteria and archaea",0,"Protists are eukaryotes."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"7","q":"Which feature does your notes give for Archaea but not Bacteria?","opts":[["An isoprenoid cell membrane",1,"They often live in extreme environments too."],["A single circular chromosome",0,"Both have one."],["No nucleus",0,"Both lack a nucleus."],["Ribosomes",0,"All cells have ribosomes."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"7","q":"How do prokaryotes reproduce?","opts":[["Binary fission",1,""],["Budding",0,"Yeast buds."],["Mitosis inside a nucleus",0,"There is no nucleus."],["Only inside a host cell",0,"That describes viruses."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"8","q":"Fungal cell walls contain:","opts":[["Chitin, a modified polysaccharide",1,""],["Cellulose and chloroplasts",0,"Fungi have no chloroplasts."],["Cholesterol",0,"Cholesterol is in animal membranes."],["Nothing; fungi have no cell walls",0,"They do have walls."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"9","q":"Which set of features belongs to plant cells but not animal cells?","opts":[["Cell wall, large water-filled vacuole, chloroplasts, plasmodesmata",1,""],["Nucleus, mitochondria, ER",0,"Animal cells have these too."],["No cell wall and a distinct mode of early development",0,"Those describe animals."],["Nucleoid and plasmids",0,"Those are prokaryote features."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"8–9","q":"An organism that depends on preformed organic molecules for its carbon and energy is a:","opts":[["Heterotroph",1,"Fungi and animals are heterotrophs."],["Autotroph",0,"Autotrophs make their own food."],["Prokaryote",0,"That describes cell type, not how it feeds."],["Viroid",0,"Viroids don't feed at all."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"8","q":"Volvox, slime molds, water molds and ciliates are all:","opts":[["Protists",1,"A diverse group, mostly single cells."],["Fungi",0,"Slime and water molds are protists, despite the names."],["Bacteria",0,"They are eukaryotes."],["Plants",0,"Only Volvox does photosynthesis, and it's still classed as a protist (alga)."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"9","q":"Which of these is NOT part of the endomembrane system?","opts":[["Mitochondria",1,"Mitochondria, chloroplasts, peroxisomes and ribosomes are outside it."],["Golgi apparatus",0,"Part of the EMS."],["Nuclear envelope",0,"Part of the EMS."],["Lysosomes",0,"Part of the EMS."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"9","q":"Which organelle modifies and sorts proteins and lipids on their way to their destinations?","opts":[["Golgi apparatus",1,""],["Lysosome",0,"Lysosomes break things down."],["Nucleolus",0,"The nucleolus makes ribosome parts."],["Peroxisome",0,"Peroxisomes break down fatty acids."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"9","q":"What does the cytoskeleton do?","opts":[["Provides an internal structural framework of protein filaments",1,""],["Makes ATP",0,"That's mitochondria."],["Stores DNA",0,"That's the nucleus."],["Breaks down macromolecules",0,"That's lysosomes."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"10","q":"Which model organism is the \"minimal model eukaryote\" used to study the cell cycle?","opts":[["Saccharomyces cerevisiae (yeast)",1,""],["Escherichia coli",0,"E. coli is a prokaryote."],["Mus musculus",0,"The mouse is the model mammal."],["Arabidopsis thaliana",0,"Arabidopsis is the plant model."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"10","q":"E. coli is used as a model organism mainly to study:","opts":[["DNA replication, transcription and translation",1,""],["Flowering",0,"That's Arabidopsis."],["Embryo development in insects",0,"That's Drosophila."],["The fate of every cell in an animal",0,"That's C. elegans."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"7–9","q":"You find a cell with a cell wall, a nucleus, mitochondria and no chloroplasts. Its wall contains chitin. What is it most likely?","opts":[["A fungal cell",1,"Chitin walls and no chloroplasts: a fungus such as yeast."],["A plant cell",0,"Plant walls are polysaccharide without chitin, and leaf cells have chloroplasts."],["A bacterium",0,"Bacteria have no nucleus or mitochondria."],["An animal cell",0,"Animal cells have no wall."]]}],"m2":[{"ch":0,"p":"13","q":"What is in the nucleus of an atom?","opts":[["Protons and neutrons",1,"Electrons move around the nucleus."],["Protons and electrons",0,"Electrons are outside the nucleus."],["Only electrons",0,"Electrons orbit; they aren't in the nucleus."],["Neutrons and electrons",0,"Protons are in the nucleus too, and electrons are outside."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"13","q":"Valence electrons are:","opts":[["The electrons in the outermost orbitals, which decide how many bonds an atom makes",1,""],["The electrons closest to the nucleus",0,"Those are inner electrons."],["Electrons that have left the atom",0,"An atom that loses electrons becomes an ion."],["Another name for protons",0,"Protons are in the nucleus."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"13","q":"How many electrons can one orbital hold?","opts":[["2",1,""],["8",0,"Eight fills a whole shell of several orbitals."],["4",0,"Too many for one orbital."],["1",0,"An orbital can hold a pair."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"14","q":"In a water molecule, oxygen pulls the shared electrons closer than hydrogen does. This makes the O–H bond:","opts":[["Polar covalent",1,"Oxygen is δ⁻ and each hydrogen δ⁺."],["Nonpolar covalent",0,"That needs equal sharing."],["Ionic",0,"No electron is fully transferred."],["A hydrogen bond",0,"Hydrogen bonds form between molecules."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"14","q":"Electronegativity is:","opts":[["How strongly an atom attracts shared electrons",1,""],["The number of electrons an atom has",0,"That's set by the protons in a neutral atom."],["The charge of an ion",0,"Charge comes from gaining or losing electrons."],["The mass of the nucleus",0,"Unrelated."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"15","q":"An atom that has one more proton than electrons is a:","opts":[["Cation",1,"It is positively charged, like Na⁺."],["Anion",0,"An anion has an extra electron."],["Neutral atom",0,"Neutral atoms have equal numbers."],["Isotope",0,"Not a term in this module."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"14–15","q":"Which bond involves one atom taking an electron from another?","opts":[["Ionic bond",1,"As in NaCl: Na⁺ and Cl⁻."],["Nonpolar covalent bond",0,"Electrons are shared equally."],["Polar covalent bond",0,"Electrons are shared unequally, not taken."],["Hydrogen bond",0,"A weak attraction between molecules."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"15","q":"When NaCl dissolves in water, the δ⁺ hydrogens of water face:","opts":[["The Cl⁻ ions",1,"Opposite charges attract. The δ⁻ oxygens face Na⁺."],["The Na⁺ ions",0,"Like charges would repel."],["Each other",0,"They face the ions."],["Nothing; water ignores ions",0,"Water surrounds the ions, which is why salt dissolves."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"16","q":"A hydrogen bond is:","opts":[["A weak bond between an electronegative atom and a hydrogen already covalently bonded to another atom",1,""],["A strong covalent bond between two hydrogens",0,"That's the bond in H₂."],["An ionic bond involving H⁺",0,"Not how the notes define it."],["The bond inside a water molecule",0,"That's polar covalent."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"16","q":"Oil and water don't mix because oil is:","opts":[["Nonpolar, so it is hydrophobic",1,"Water hydrogen-bonds to itself and pushes oil together."],["Polar, so it is hydrophilic",0,"Hydrophilic things dissolve in water."],["Ionic",0,"Oils are hydrocarbons."],["Too acidic",0,"pH isn't the reason."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"16","q":"In 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, what are H₂ and O₂?","opts":[["Reactants",1,"The starting substances."],["Products",0,"Water is the product."],["Catalysts",0,"They are used up."],["Isomers",0,"Different molecules, not isomers."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"17","q":"A solution with a pH of 3 is:","opts":[["Acidic, with more H⁺ than pure water",1,""],["Basic, with fewer H⁺",0,"Basic is above 7."],["Neutral",0,"Neutral is 7."],["Basic, with more H⁺",0,"More H⁺ means acidic."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"17","q":"Solution A is pH 4 and solution B is pH 6. Compared with B, A has:","opts":[["100 times more H⁺",1,"Two pH units: 10 × 10."],["2 times more H⁺",0,"pH is a log scale."],["100 times less H⁺",0,"Lower pH means more H⁺."],["The same amount of H⁺",0,"Different pH means different H⁺."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"18","q":"Why is carbon the backbone of organic molecules?","opts":[["It forms four covalent bonds and can build chains and rings",1,""],["It forms ionic bonds with water",0,"Carbon shares electrons."],["It has one valence electron",0,"It has four."],["It is the lightest element",0,"Hydrogen is."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"20","q":"Which functional group is nonpolar?","opts":[["Methyl, –CH₃",1,""],["Hydroxyl, –OH",0,"Polar."],["Carboxyl, –COOH",0,"Polar and acidic."],["Amino, –NH₂",0,"Polar and basic."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"20","q":"Which group behaves as an acid and is negatively charged at the pH of a cell?","opts":[["Carboxyl",1,""],["Amino",0,"Amino is positive and acts as a base."],["Methyl",0,"Methyl is nonpolar and uncharged."],["Hydroxyl",0,"Hydroxyl is polar but uncharged."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"19","q":"A condensation reaction:","opts":[["Links monomers with a covalent bond and releases water",1,"It requires energy."],["Uses water to break a polymer apart",0,"That's hydrolysis."],["Only happens in lipids",0,"It builds proteins, nucleic acids and polysaccharides."],["Releases energy as it breaks bonds",0,"Hydrolysis releases energy."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"19","q":"How many water molecules are used to completely hydrolyze a chain of 8 glucose units?","opts":[["7",1,"One per bond, and 8 units have 7 bonds."],["8",0,"Count the bonds."],["9",0,"More than the bonds."],["16",0,"One water per bond."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"19, 29","q":"Which macromolecule is not a polymer?","opts":[["Lipids",1,"They are grouped together because they are hydrophobic."],["Proteins",0,"Polymers of amino acids."],["Nucleic acids",0,"Polymers of nucleotides."],["Polysaccharides",0,"Polymers of monosaccharides."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"21","q":"What distinguishes one amino acid from another?","opts":[["Its R group (side chain)",1,""],["Its α carbon",0,"All amino acids have one."],["Its carboxyl group",0,"Shared by all."],["Its amino group",0,"Shared by all."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"22","q":"A peptide bond forms between:","opts":[["The carboxyl carbon of one amino acid and the amino nitrogen of the next",1,"Water is released."],["Two R groups",0,"R groups are side chains, not the backbone link."],["Two amino groups",0,"One carboxyl and one amino group are needed."],["A sugar and a phosphate",0,"That's in nucleic acids."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"21","q":"Antibodies are an example of which protein function?","opts":[["Defense",1,"Along with complement proteins."],["Transport",0,"Transport proteins move molecules."],["Support",0,"Support proteins include the cytoskeleton."],["Movement",0,"Motor and contractile proteins do that."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"23–24","q":"A nucleotide is made of:","opts":[["A nitrogenous base, a five-carbon sugar and one or more phosphate groups",1,"NucleoTide has Three parts."],["A base and a sugar only",0,"That's a nucleoside."],["An amino acid and a sugar",0,"Amino acids build proteins."],["Glycerol and fatty acids",0,"That's a lipid."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"23","q":"Which bases are purines?","opts":[["Adenine and guanine (two rings)",1,""],["Cytosine, thymine and uracil",0,"Those are pyrimidines, with one ring."],["Thymine and uracil only",0,"Pyrimidines."],["All five bases",0,"Only A and G are purines."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"23–24","q":"How does RNA differ from DNA?","opts":[["RNA has ribose (–OH on the 2′ carbon) and uracil instead of thymine",1,""],["RNA has deoxyribose and thymine",0,"That's DNA."],["RNA has no phosphate groups",0,"Both have phosphates."],["RNA has no bases",0,"Both have bases."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"25","q":"New nucleotides are added to a growing strand at its:","opts":[["3′ end (the free 3′–OH)",1,"Chains are built 5′ → 3′."],["5′ end",0,"The chain begins at the 5′ end."],["Middle",0,"Only at the 3′ end."],["Either end",0,"Only the 3′ end."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"24","q":"cAMP, ATP and NAD show that nucleotides can also act as:","opts":[["Signal molecules, energy carriers and coenzymes",1,""],["Cell wall components",0,"Not a nucleotide job."],["Hormones only",0,"Too narrow."],["Membrane lipids",0,"That's phospholipids."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"26","q":"Glucose, galactose and fructose all have the formula C₆H₁₂O₆. They are:","opts":[["Isomers: same formula, different arrangement",1,""],["Identical molecules",0,"They differ in configuration and function."],["Polysaccharides",0,"They are monosaccharides."],["Lipids",0,"They are carbohydrates."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"27–28","q":"Sucrose is:","opts":[["A disaccharide of glucose and fructose, joined by a glycosidic bond",1,"Table sugar, C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁."],["A polysaccharide of many glucose units",0,"That's starch or glycogen."],["A monosaccharide",0,"It is two sugars."],["A disaccharide of two glucose units",0,"One of them is fructose."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"28","q":"Which polysaccharide gives structural support?","opts":[["Cellulose",1,""],["Starch",0,"Energy storage in plants."],["Glycogen",0,"Energy storage."],["Sucrose",0,"A disaccharide."]]},{"ch":9,"p":"29","q":"A triacylglycerol is made of:","opts":[["Glycerol and three fatty acids",1,"Fats and oils, for energy storage."],["Glycerol, a phosphate and two fatty acids",0,"That's a phospholipid."],["Four fused rings",0,"That's a steroid."],["Three glucose units",0,"That's a carbohydrate."]]},{"ch":9,"p":"29","q":"An unsaturated fatty acid has:","opts":[["One or more C=C double bonds, which kink the chain",1,""],["No double bonds",0,"That's saturated."],["A phosphate head",0,"That's a phospholipid."],["Four rings",0,"That's a steroid."]]},{"ch":9,"p":"30","q":"Why are phospholipids described as amphipathic?","opts":[["They have a hydrophilic phosphate head and two hydrophobic fatty acid tails",1,""],["They are entirely hydrophobic",0,"The head is polar."],["They are polymers",0,"Lipids aren't polymers."],["They carry genetic information",0,"That's nucleic acids."]]},{"ch":9,"p":"30","q":"Cholesterol is:","opts":[["A steroid found in animal cell membranes and a precursor of steroid hormones",1,"For example cortisol."],["A fatty acid",0,"It has four fused rings."],["A polysaccharide",0,"It is a lipid."],["A protein",0,"It is a lipid."]]}],"m3":[{"ch":0,"p":"37–40","q":"Which of these is part of the endomembrane system?","opts":[["Golgi apparatus",1,"The endomembrane system is the nuclear envelope, ER, Golgi, lysosomes, vesicles and plasma membrane."],["Mitochondrion",0,"Mitochondria are not connected to the endomembrane system."],["Chloroplast",0,"Chloroplasts are separate, like mitochondria."],["Ribosome",0,"Ribosomes have no membrane."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"37–40","q":"A protein will be secreted from the cell. Where is it made?","opts":[["On ribosomes attached to the rough ER",1,"Membrane and secreted proteins start on the rough ER, then go to the Golgi."],["On free ribosomes in the cytosol",0,"Those make proteins that stay in the cytosol."],["In the nucleolus",0,"The nucleolus makes ribosome parts."],["In the smooth ER",0,"Smooth ER makes lipids and breaks down toxins."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"37–40","q":"What is the main job of the smooth ER?","opts":[["Making lipids and breaking down toxins",1,""],["Making proteins for secretion",0,"That's the rough ER."],["Digesting worn-out organelles",0,"That's lysosomes."],["Making ATP",0,"That's mitochondria."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"37–40","q":"What passes through nuclear pores?","opts":[["mRNA and ribosome subunits going out, and proteins the nucleus needs coming in",1,""],["Only water",0,"Pores move large molecules too."],["Vesicles from the Golgi",0,"Vesicles don't pass through nuclear pores."],["Nothing; the nuclear envelope is sealed",0,"The pores connect the nucleus and cytoplasm."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"41–42","q":"Which observation supports the endosymbiotic theory?","opts":[["Mitochondria have their own circular DNA and ribosomes, like bacteria",1,"They also have a double membrane and divide by splitting, like bacteria."],["Mitochondria are part of the endomembrane system",0,"They aren't; that's part of why they're thought to have a separate origin."],["Mitochondria are found only in plants",0,"They're in nearly all eukaryotes."],["Mitochondria are made by the Golgi",0,"Mitochondria come only from other mitochondria."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"41–42","q":"Why do mitochondria and chloroplasts have two membranes, according to the endosymbiotic theory?","opts":[["The inner one came from the engulfed bacterium and the outer one from the host cell that engulfed it",1,""],["One membrane is for lipids and one for proteins",0,"Not the explanation."],["They were made by fusing two vesicles",0,"Not the explanation."],["All organelles have two membranes",0,"Most organelles have one."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"41–42","q":"Where in the mitochondrion is the inner membrane folded to add surface area?","opts":[["The cristae",1,"The folds raise the surface area for making ATP."],["The stroma",0,"The stroma is the fluid inside a chloroplast."],["The thylakoids",0,"Those are in chloroplasts."],["The nucleolus",0,"That's in the nucleus."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"41–42","q":"In a chloroplast, stacks of thylakoids are called:","opts":[["Grana",1,"The fluid around them is the stroma."],["Cristae",0,"Cristae are folds in mitochondria."],["Cisternae",0,"Those are Golgi sacs."],["Microvilli",0,"Those are on intestinal cells."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"33–34, 36","q":"An integral membrane protein differs from a peripheral one because it:","opts":[["Is held in the bilayer by hydrophobic regions and can't be removed without breaking the membrane",1,""],["Only sits on the outer surface",0,"Peripheral proteins sit on surfaces."],["Has no hydrophobic parts",0,"Integral proteins have hydrophobic parts among the tails."],["Is always a glycoprotein",0,"Not always."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"33–34, 36","q":"Which molecule crosses a pure phospholipid bilayer most easily?","opts":[["O₂",1,"Small and nonpolar."],["Na⁺",0,"Charged ions can't enter the hydrophobic core."],["Glucose",0,"Too big and polar."],["A protein",0,"Far too big."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"33–34, 36","q":"In the fluid mosaic model, the 'mosaic' refers to:","opts":[["The mix of lipids, proteins and carbohydrates in the membrane",1,""],["Lipids moving sideways",0,"That's the 'fluid' part."],["The pattern of microvilli",0,"Not related."],["The two layers of the bilayer",0,"Not what mosaic means."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"33–36","q":"Phospholipids with small heads and two hydrophobic tails, added to water, spontaneously form:","opts":[["A bilayer that closes into a liposome",1,"The liposome encloses an inner watery space, like a cell."],["Micelles",0,"Micelles form from lipids with a large head and one tail."],["A single layer on the bottom of the tube",0,"They arrange so the tails avoid water."],["Nothing; they need ATP",0,"Assembly is spontaneous."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"33–36","q":"Which change makes a membrane more fluid?","opts":[["More C=C double bonds in the fatty acid tails",1,"Kinks stop tight packing."],["Longer fatty acid tails",0,"Longer tails pack more tightly: less fluid."],["Lower temperature",0,"Cooling reduces fluidity."],["Fewer double bonds",0,"Fewer double bonds: straighter tails, tighter packing."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"33–36","q":"How does cholesterol affect membrane fluidity?","opts":[["It buffers it: it can increase or decrease fluidity depending on temperature",1,""],["It always makes the membrane stiffer",0,"At low temperature it keeps the membrane from packing solid."],["It always makes the membrane more fluid",0,"At high temperature it holds the membrane together."],["It has no effect",0,"It's a fluidity buffer."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"33–36","q":"Lipid flip-flop (moving from one layer of the bilayer to the other) is:","opts":[["Rare, which lets the two layers differ in composition",1,""],["Constant, so both layers are identical",0,"It's rare."],["How lipids move sideways",0,"Sideways movement is lateral movement."],["Driven by the Na⁺/K⁺ pump",0,"Not related."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"33–36","q":"What are lipid rafts?","opts":[["Specialized microdomains of lipids and proteins that act as platforms for cell processes",1,""],["Vesicles that carry lipids to the Golgi",0,"Not what rafts are."],["Fat droplets in the cytoplasm",0,"Not what rafts are."],["Micelles inside the membrane",0,"Not what rafts are."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"45","q":"A red blood cell is placed in a hypertonic solution. What happens?","opts":[["Water leaves the cell and it shrinks",1,""],["Water enters and it bursts",0,"That's a hypotonic solution."],["Nothing changes",0,"That's isotonic."],["Salt enters and it swells",0,"Water moves, not the salt."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"45","q":"Osmosis is:","opts":[["Diffusion of water across a selectively permeable membrane",1,"Water moves toward the side with more solute."],["Active transport of water",0,"Water always moves passively."],["Diffusion of solute toward lower water concentration",0,"Osmosis is about water moving."],["Endocytosis of droplets",0,"That's pinocytosis."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"43–44","q":"How does a carrier protein differ from a channel protein?","opts":[["A carrier binds its cargo and changes shape; a channel forms a pore",1,"Carriers are slower and can be saturated."],["Carriers always use ATP",0,"Carriers can work passively (facilitated diffusion)."],["Channels only move glucose",0,"Channels mostly move ions and water."],["There is no difference",0,"They work differently."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"43–44","q":"Which is true of all passive transport?","opts":[["Substances move down their gradient with no energy spent by the cell",1,""],["It always needs a protein",0,"Simple diffusion doesn't."],["It can move substances against a gradient",0,"Only active transport does that."],["It only moves water",0,"It moves many substances."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"46–47","q":"The Na⁺/K⁺ pump moves:","opts":[["Na⁺ out of the cell and K⁺ into it, using ATP",1,"Both against their gradients: primary active transport."],["Na⁺ into the cell and K⁺ out of it",0,"Backwards."],["Na⁺ and K⁺ down their gradients",0,"It moves them up their gradients."],["Glucose into the cell",0,"That's the symporter."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"46–47","q":"The electrochemical gradient for an ion is made of:","opts":[["Its concentration gradient plus the charge difference across the membrane",1,""],["Only its concentration gradient",0,"Charge matters too."],["The pH of the cytosol",0,"Not the definition."],["The number of pumps",0,"Not the definition."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"47–48","q":"Glucose enters an intestinal cell through the Na⁺/glucose symporter. What directly powers this?","opts":[["Na⁺ moving into the cell down its electrochemical gradient",1,"That's secondary active transport."],["ATP split by the symporter",0,"The symporter never uses ATP directly."],["Glucose moving down its gradient",0,"Glucose goes up its gradient here."],["The cell wall",0,"Animal cells don't have one."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"47–48","q":"How does glucose leave the intestinal cell on the side facing the blood?","opts":[["Through GLUT, by facilitated diffusion",1,"Downhill, on the basolateral side."],["Through the Na⁺/glucose symporter",0,"That's on the apical (gut) side."],["By exocytosis",0,"Single glucose molecules aren't packaged in vesicles."],["Through the Na⁺/K⁺ pump",0,"The pump doesn't carry glucose."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"47–48","q":"A Na⁺/H⁺ exchanger moves Na⁺ in and H⁺ out. It is:","opts":[["An antiporter (secondary active transport)",1,"Opposite directions, powered by the Na⁺ gradient."],["A symporter",0,"A symporter moves both the same way."],["A uniporter",0,"A uniporter moves one substance."],["A channel",0,"Channels don't couple two substances."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"49–50","q":"Which process brings in specific molecules like LDL using receptors in coated pits?","opts":[["Receptor-mediated endocytosis",1,""],["Pinocytosis",0,"Pinocytosis is nonselective."],["Phagocytosis",0,"That engulfs large particles."],["Exocytosis",0,"That exports material."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"49–50","q":"How does exocytosis relate to secretion?","opts":[["Vesicles fuse with the plasma membrane and release their contents outside",1,"Secreted proteins leave the cell this way."],["It brings secreted molecules back in",0,"That's endocytosis."],["It's a type of passive transport",0,"Bulk transport needs energy."],["It only happens in plant cells",0,"All eukaryotic cells use it."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"49–50","q":"Phagocytosis ends with the engulfed particle:","opts":[["In a phagosome that fuses with a lysosome to be digested",1,""],["Being released into the cytosol",0,"It stays inside a membrane."],["Exported again by exocytosis",0,"It's digested."],["Entering the nucleus",0,"Not where it goes."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"45","q":"A plant cell is placed in a hypertonic solution. What do your notes call what happens?","opts":[["Plasmolysis: the cell loses water and its membrane pulls away from the cell wall",1,"Water leaves by osmosis; the wall keeps its shape while the membrane shrinks away from it."],["Crenation: the cell shrivels into a spiky shape",0,"Crenation is the animal-cell (red blood cell) version."],["Lysis: the cell bursts",0,"Bursting happens in a hypotonic solution, and the wall prevents it in plants."],["Turgor: the cell presses against its wall",0,"That happens in a hypotonic solution."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"50","q":"After receptor-mediated endocytosis, where is the material delivered first?","opts":[["Early endosomes near the edge of the cell",1,"Then on to late endosomes deeper inside, which also receive material from the Golgi."],["Straight into the nucleus",0,"Endocytosed material stays in the endomembrane system."],["The rough ER",0,"Material moves from the ER outward, not the other way here."],["The cytosol, freely",0,"It stays inside vesicles and endosomes."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"50","q":"True or false: receptor-mediated endocytosis imports any molecule present in the extracellular fluid, without specificity. (From your notes, p. 52.)","opts":[["False: receptors bind specific molecules, concentrated in coated pits",1,"Taking in fluid without recognition is pinocytosis."],["True: that is what receptor-mediated endocytosis means",0,"That describes pinocytosis."],["True, but only for large particles",0,"Large particles are taken in by phagocytosis."],["False: receptor-mediated endocytosis exports molecules",0,"Exporting is exocytosis."]]}],"m4":[{"ch":0,"p":"53","q":"Griffith studied two strains of which bacterium?","opts":[["Streptococcus pneumoniae",1,"A virulent strain caused pneumonia and killed mice; a mutant nonvirulent strain did not."],["Escherichia coli",0,"Not the organism in Griffith's experiment."],["Bacillus subtilis",0,"Not the organism in Griffith's experiment."],["Staphylococcus aureus",0,"Not the organism in Griffith's experiment."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"54","q":"A mouse is injected with heat-killed virulent bacteria mixed with live nonvirulent bacteria. What happens, and why?","opts":[["It dies, because something in the dead virulent cells transformed the live nonvirulent cells",1,"That was Griffith's key result: transformation by an unknown molecule."],["It lives, because both types of bacteria are harmless on their own",0,"Each is harmless alone, but together the mouse dies."],["It dies, because heat-killed bacteria come back to life",0,"Heat-killed virulent bacteria alone do not kill the mouse."],["It lives, because the immune system destroys the dead cells first",0,"The mouse dies of pneumonia."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"54","q":"In the experiment by Avery, MacLeod and McCarty, which enzyme stopped the extract from transforming nonvirulent bacteria?","opts":[["DNase",1,"Destroying DNA was the only treatment that stopped transformation, so DNA carries the genetic information."],["RNase",0,"The RNase-treated extract still transformed the bacteria."],["Protease",0,"The protease-treated extract still transformed the bacteria."],["None of them; all tubes transformed",0,"The DNase tube did not transform."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"55","q":"Which carbon of deoxyribose is the base attached to?","opts":[["1′",1,"The base is on 1′, the phosphate on 5′, and the free –OH on 3′."],["2′",0,"In DNA the 2′ carbon has just –H."],["3′",0,"The 3′ carbon carries the –OH where the next nucleotide joins."],["5′",0,"The 5′ carbon carries the phosphate."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"55","q":"What is the difference between a nucleoside and a nucleotide?","opts":[["A nucleoside is base + sugar; a nucleotide also has one or more phosphates",1,"NucleoTide has Three parts; nucleoSide has just base and Sugar."],["A nucleoside has a phosphate; a nucleotide does not",0,"It's the other way round."],["A nucleoside is found in RNA; a nucleotide in DNA",0,"Both terms apply to both."],["They are two names for the same molecule",0,"They differ by the phosphate."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"55","q":"Which bases are purines?","opts":[["Adenine and guanine",1,"Purines have two rings."],["Cytosine and thymine",0,"Those are pyrimidines (one ring)."],["Adenine and thymine",0,"Thymine is a pyrimidine."],["Guanine and uracil",0,"Uracil is a pyrimidine."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"55","q":"Where can a new nucleotide be added to a growing DNA strand?","opts":[["Only to the free 3′–OH, so strands grow 5′→3′",1,"Bases can only be added to the 3′–OH on the sugar."],["Only to the 5′ phosphate end",0,"The 5′ end has a phosphate, not a free –OH to attach to."],["To either end",0,"Nucleic acids only grow at the 3′ end."],["To the 2′ carbon",0,"The 2′ carbon is not part of the chain."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"55","q":"Why is the DNA backbone negatively charged?","opts":[["The phosphate groups' hydroxyls are ionized at cellular pH (about 7)",1,"Each phosphate carries negative charge."],["The bases are negatively charged",0,"The charge comes from the phosphates."],["Deoxyribose lacks an oxygen",0,"That does not create a charge."],["Hydrogen bonds carry charge",0,"Hydrogen bonds are weak attractions, not charges on the backbone."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"56","q":"What did Erwin Chargaff contribute to the DNA model?","opts":[["Base composition data showing %A = %T and %C = %G",1,"That pointed to A–T and C–G pairing."],["X-ray crystallography images of DNA",0,"That was Rosalind Franklin and Maurice Wilkins."],["The 3-D model of the double helix",0,"That was Watson and Crick."],["Proof that DNA is the genetic material",0,"That was Avery, MacLeod and McCarty."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"56","q":"A DNA sample is 20% thymine. What percent is cytosine?","opts":[["30%",1,"%T = %A = 20%, so A + T = 40%. The remaining 60% is split between C and G: 30% each."],["20%",0,"That is %A, not %C."],["40%",0,"40% is A + T together."],["60%",0,"60% is C + G together."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"57","q":"How many hydrogen bonds hold a G–C pair together?","opts":[["3",1,"G–C has three; A–T has two."],["2",0,"Two is A–T."],["1",0,"Every pair has more than one."],["4",0,"No base pair in DNA has four."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"57","q":"Which two factors keep the double helix stable?","opts":[["Hydrogen bonds between paired bases, and base stacking along each strand",1,"Many weak hydrogen bonds plus stacking interactions."],["Covalent bonds between the two strands, and histones",0,"The strands are not covalently joined to each other."],["Phosphodiester bonds between paired bases",0,"Phosphodiester bonds link nucleotides within a strand."],["Ionic bonds between phosphates",0,"The phosphates repel each other."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"56","q":"What does it mean that the two strands of DNA are antiparallel?","opts":[["They run in opposite directions: one 5′→3′, the other 3′→5′",1,""],["They never touch each other",0,"They are held together by base pairs."],["They have opposite charges",0,"Both backbones are negative."],["One is DNA and the other is RNA",0,"Both strands of a double helix are DNA."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"53","q":"What is a nucleosome?","opts":[["DNA wrapped around a core of histone proteins (H2A, H2B, H3, H4)",1,"Nucleosomes make up the 10 nm fibre."],["The region of the nucleus where ribosomes are made",0,"That is the nucleolus."],["A single nucleotide inside the nucleus",0,"A nucleosome is DNA plus histones."],["The fully condensed chromatid",0,"That is the last level of condensation (1400 nm)."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"58","q":"Which enzyme makes DNA using an RNA template?","opts":[["Reverse transcriptase",1,"An RNA-dependent DNA polymerase, used by HIV."],["RNA polymerase II",0,"That makes RNA from DNA."],["DNase",0,"DNase breaks DNA down."],["Ribosomes",0,"Ribosomes make protein."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"58","q":"What is gene expression?","opts":[["The production of a functional gene product, such as a protein",1,"Genes can be turned on (expressed) or off."],["Copying DNA before cell division",0,"That is replication."],["The full set of genetic material in an organism",0,"That is the genome."],["Removing introns from mRNA",0,"That is splicing."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"59","q":"Where do transcription and translation happen in a eukaryotic cell?","opts":[["Transcription in the nucleus, translation in the cytoplasm",1,"The nuclear envelope separates them."],["Both in the cytoplasm",0,"That is the prokaryotic arrangement."],["Both in the nucleus",0,"Ribosomes translate in the cytoplasm."],["Transcription in the cytoplasm, translation in the nucleus",0,"Backwards."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"60","q":"Which feature is typical of RNA but not DNA?","opts":[["A triphosphate at its 5′ end",1,"DNA's 5′ end is typically a monophosphate."],["Thymine",0,"Thymine is in DNA; RNA has uracil."],["Deoxyribose",0,"RNA has ribose."],["Two strands wound into a helix",0,"Most RNA is single-stranded."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"60","q":"Why is RNA less stable than DNA?","opts":[["Ribose has a hydroxyl (–OH) on its 2′ carbon",1,"The notes link the 2′ –OH to RNA's lower stability."],["Uracil has a methyl group",0,"Uracil has –H where thymine has –CH₃."],["RNA has no phosphates",0,"RNA has a sugar-phosphate backbone too."],["RNA is longer than DNA",0,"RNA is usually shorter."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"62","q":"The TATA box is:","opts":[["A common eukaryotic promoter sequence, 5′-TATAAA-3′",1,"The first transcribed nucleotide is usually about 25 bp away."],["The sequence where transcription ends",0,"That's a terminator."],["A sequence in mRNA recognized by ribosomes",0,"It is in DNA, in the promoter."],["The sequence enhancers bind to",0,"Activators bind enhancers; general transcription factors bind the promoter."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"62","q":"In bacteria, what directs RNA polymerase to the promoter?","opts":[["Sigma factor",1,"It associates with RNA polymerase and directs it to specific promoters."],["General transcription factors",0,"Those are eukaryotic."],["The mediator complex",0,"Mediator is eukaryotic."],["The 5′ cap",0,"The cap is added to eukaryotic mRNA."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"63","q":"How do activator proteins bound to a distant enhancer help start transcription?","opts":[["The DNA loops so the activators contact the mediator complex and the proteins at the promoter",1,""],["They travel along the DNA until they reach the promoter",0,"The notes describe looping."],["They become part of the RNA transcript",0,"They are proteins bound to DNA."],["They cut the DNA so Pol II can enter",0,"Nothing is cut."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"61","q":"In which direction does RNA polymerase read the template strand?","opts":[["3′→5′, so the RNA grows 5′→3′",1,""],["5′→3′, so the RNA grows 5′→3′",0,"The template and RNA are antiparallel."],["5′→3′, so the RNA grows 3′→5′",0,"RNA never grows 3′→5′."],["It depends on the gene",0,"Which strand is the template can change, but it's always read 3′→5′."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"64","q":"What is released each time a ribonucleotide is added to the growing RNA?","opts":[["Pyrophosphate (two phosphates)",1,"The 3′–OH attacks the innermost phosphate, and the outer two leave."],["ATP",0,"The incoming nucleotide supplies the energy itself."],["A water molecule only",0,"Pyrophosphate is released."],["The 5′ cap",0,"The cap is added in processing."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"63","q":"About how long is the transcription bubble, and how much of it is RNA–DNA duplex?","opts":[["About 14 bp, with about 8 bp of RNA–DNA duplex",1,""],["About 8 bp, with about 14 bp of duplex",0,"The duplex sits inside the bubble."],["The whole gene unwinds at once",0,"Only a short stretch is open."],["About 25 bp, with no duplex",0,"25 bp is the TATA box to start-site distance."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"65","q":"Polycistronic mRNA is:","opts":[["A prokaryotic mRNA carrying information for more than one gene",1,""],["A eukaryotic mRNA with many introns",0,"Introns are removed before translation."],["An mRNA with several 5′ caps",0,"No mRNA has several caps."],["An mRNA made by reverse transcriptase",0,"Reverse transcriptase makes DNA."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"65","q":"Why can translation start before transcription is finished in bacteria?","opts":[["There's no nuclear envelope, and the primary transcript is already the mRNA",1,"Transcription and translation are coupled."],["Bacterial ribosomes are inside the nucleus",0,"Bacteria have no nucleus."],["Bacteria splice mRNA faster",0,"Bacterial primary transcripts aren't processed."],["Bacterial RNA polymerase also makes protein",0,"Ribosomes make protein."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"66","q":"What is the 5′ cap?","opts":[["A 7-methylguanosine joined to the 5′ end by a 5′–5′ triphosphate bridge",1,"It protects the mRNA and lets the ribosome recognize it."],["About 250 adenines added to the 5′ end",0,"That's the poly(A) tail, on the 3′ end."],["The first exon of the gene",0,"The cap is added after transcription."],["A protein that binds the TATA box",0,"That's a transcription factor."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"66","q":"Which is a role of the poly(A) tail?","opts":[["Export of the mRNA to the cytoplasm",1,"It also helps transcription termination and protects against exonucleases."],["Letting the ribosome recognize the 5′ end",0,"That's the cap."],["Binding sigma factor",0,"Sigma factor is bacterial and binds promoters."],["Removing introns",0,"Splicing removes introns."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"66","q":"A gene's primary transcript has exons 1 to 4. One cell makes mRNA with exons 1, 2 and 4; another with exons 1, 2 and 3. What is this?","opts":[["Alternative splicing",1,"One gene, different related proteins in different cells."],["Polyadenylation",0,"That adds the poly(A) tail."],["Reverse transcription",0,"That makes DNA from RNA."],["Polycistronic mRNA",0,"That's a prokaryotic mRNA with several genes."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"55–57","q":"Which of the following is NOT correct about DNA? (From your notes, p. 52.)","opts":[["A pairs with G, and C pairs with T",1,"A pairs with T, and C pairs with G."],["Its sugar is deoxyribose",0,"That is correct."],["It has a sugar-phosphate backbone",0,"That is correct."],["It is double stranded",0,"That is correct."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"61","q":"In which direction does a strand of RNA grow during transcription? (From your notes, p. 52.)","opts":[["5′ to 3′",1,"Nucleotides are only added to the 3′ end, so the template is read 3′ to 5′."],["3′ to 5′",0,"That's the direction the template strand is read."],["Both directions",0,"Nucleic acids only grow at the 3′ end."],["C terminus to N terminus",0,"Termini describe proteins, not RNA."]]}],"m5":[{"ch":0,"p":"69","q":"Which three components are identical in every amino acid?","opts":[["A carboxyl group, an amino group and a hydrogen",1,"All bound to the α carbon. The fourth, the R group, differs."],["An R group, a hydroxyl group and a hydrogen",0,"The R group is the part that differs."],["A phosphate, a sugar and a base",0,"That's a nucleotide."],["A sulfhydryl group, an amino group and a carboxyl group",0,"Only cysteine has –SH."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"70","q":"In a folded protein in water, where are hydrophobic amino acids usually found?","opts":[["Buried in the interior, away from water",1,"Held together by weak van der Waals forces."],["On the outer surface",0,"That's where hydrophilic R groups go."],["Only at the N-terminus",0,"Position in the fold depends on R group, not on the end of the chain."],["Bonded to water by H-bonds",0,"Hydrophobic R groups can't H-bond with water."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"70","q":"At physiological pH, basic amino acids such as lysine have R groups that are:","opts":[["Positively charged",1,"Acidic ones (Asp, Glu) are negatively charged."],["Negatively charged",0,"That's acidic amino acids."],["Nonpolar",0,"Charged R groups are hydrophilic."],["Always bonded to cysteine",0,"Only cysteines form disulfide bonds."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"71","q":"Which amino acid has a hydrogen as its R group and makes the backbone more flexible?","opts":[["Glycine",1,""],["Proline",0,"Proline restricts rotation."],["Cysteine",0,"Cysteine has –SH."],["Alanine",0,"Alanine's R group is –CH₃."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"71","q":"Why does proline limit how a protein can fold around it?","opts":[["Its R group links back to the amino group, restricting rotation of the C–N bond",1,""],["It forms disulfide bonds",0,"That's cysteine."],["It is negatively charged",0,"Proline is not charged."],["It has no α carbon",0,"Every amino acid has an α carbon."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"71, 74","q":"A disulfide bond is a covalent bond between:","opts":[["The –SH groups of two cysteines",1,"A cross-bridge within one protein or between proteins."],["Two glycines",0,"Glycine's R group is H."],["The amino and carboxyl groups of neighbouring amino acids",0,"That's a peptide bond."],["A tRNA and its amino acid",0,"That link is made by a synthetase."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"71","q":"When a peptide bond forms between two amino acids:","opts":[["The carboxyl group of one reacts with the amino group of the other and water is released",1,"A dehydration reaction."],["Two R groups join",0,"R groups are not involved."],["Water is added to break the bond",0,"That's hydrolysis."],["ATP is attached to each amino acid",0,"Not part of the peptide bond reaction."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"71","q":"The free amino group at one end of a polypeptide is called the:","opts":[["N-terminus",1,"The other end, with the free carboxyl group, is the C-terminus."],["C-terminus",0,"That's the carboxyl end."],["R group",0,"R groups are side chains."],["Anticodon",0,"That's on a tRNA."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"72","q":"Which level of protein structure determines all the others?","opts":[["Primary",1,"The sequence governs 2°, 3° and 4° structure."],["Secondary",0,"Secondary depends on the sequence."],["Tertiary",0,"Tertiary depends on the sequence."],["Quaternary",0,"Quaternary depends on the subunits' folds."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"73","q":"α helices and β sheets are held together by:","opts":[["Hydrogen bonds between backbone C=O and N–H groups",1,"R groups are not involved in secondary structure."],["Disulfide bonds",0,"Those help hold tertiary structure."],["Ionic bonds between R groups",0,"That's tertiary."],["Peptide bonds between R groups",0,"Peptide bonds join the backbone, not R groups."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"74","q":"Which bonds and interactions determine tertiary structure?","opts":[["Hydrogen bonds, hydrophobic interactions, ionic bonds and disulfide bonds between R groups",1,""],["Only peptide bonds",0,"Those make the primary structure."],["Only H-bonds between backbone groups",0,"That's secondary."],["Phosphodiester bonds",0,"Those are in nucleic acids."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"74","q":"A protein made of two identical polypeptide subunits is a:","opts":[["Homodimer",1,"Homo = same, di = two."],["Heterodimer",0,"Hetero = different."],["β sheet",0,"That's secondary structure."],["Polycistronic protein",0,"Polycistronic describes mRNA."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"78","q":"The genetic code has 64 codons for 20 amino acids, so many amino acids have more than one codon. The code is therefore:","opts":[["Redundant (degenerate)",1,""],["Overlapping",0,"Codons are a nonoverlapping series of triplets."],["Ambiguous: one codon codes for several amino acids",0,"Each codon has one meaning."],["Read 3′ to 5′",0,"mRNA is read 5′ to 3′."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"76","q":"What sets which triplets the ribosome reads on an mRNA?","opts":[["The reading frame, set where translation starts (AUG)",1,"Shift it and every codon after changes."],["The 5′ cap alone",0,"The cap is where scanning starts, but the AUG sets the frame."],["The anticodon of the first tRNA",0,"The anticodon pairs with a codon; it doesn't choose the frame."],["The poly(A) tail",0,"That's at the 3′ end."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"78","q":"Using the code, what does 5′-AUG UUU GGC UAA-3′ encode?","opts":[["Met–Phe–Gly",1,"UAA is a stop codon."],["Met–Phe–Gly–Stop as four amino acids",0,"A stop codon adds no amino acid."],["Met–Leu–Gly",0,"UUU is Phe."],["Phe–Gly",0,"AUG codes for Met, the first amino acid."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"76, 78","q":"Which anticodon pairs with the codon 5′-GUA-3′?","opts":[["3′-CAU-5′",1,"Complementary and antiparallel."],["5′-CAU-3′",0,"The anticodon runs the opposite direction."],["3′-GUA-5′",0,"Not complementary."],["3′-CTA-5′",0,"RNA has U, not T."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"77","q":"What do aminoacyl tRNA synthetases do?","opts":[["Attach the correct amino acid to the 3′ end of its tRNA",1,"Making a charged tRNA."],["Form peptide bonds in the ribosome",0,"That's the peptidyl transferase centre."],["Bind stop codons",0,"Release factors do."],["Add the 5′ cap",0,"That's RNA processing."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"76","q":"In the ribosome, which site holds the tRNA carrying the growing polypeptide?","opts":[["P site",1,"Peptidyl site."],["A site",0,"New charged tRNAs enter at A."],["E site",0,"Empty tRNAs leave from E."],["The small subunit",0,"The sites are in the large subunit."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"78","q":"What are the three stages of translation, in order?","opts":[["Initiation, elongation, termination",1,""],["Elongation, initiation, termination",0,"It starts with initiation."],["Transcription, processing, translation",0,"Those are steps of gene expression."],["Initiation, termination, elongation",0,"Termination is last."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"79","q":"What catalyzes peptide bond formation during elongation?","opts":[["The peptidyl transferase centre of the large subunit, made of rRNA",1,""],["Aminoacyl tRNA synthetase",0,"That charges tRNAs."],["RNA polymerase",0,"That makes RNA."],["The release factor",0,"That ends translation."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"80","q":"What happens when a stop codon reaches the A site?","opts":[["A release factor binds, the chain is freed from the last tRNA, and the C-terminus is formed",1,""],["A tRNA with no amino acid enters",0,"No tRNA pairs with a stop codon."],["The ribosome jumps to the next AUG",0,"Translation of that polypeptide ends."],["Methionine is added",0,"Met is added at AUG."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"80","q":"How does a prokaryotic ribosome find where to start translating?","opts":[["It binds a Shine-Dalgarno sequence and starts at the next AUG downstream",1,"Prokaryotic mRNA has no 5′ cap."],["It binds the 5′ cap and scans",0,"That's eukaryotes."],["It starts at the poly(A) tail",0,"Prokaryotes don't add poly(A) tails in this course."],["It starts at the first U",0,"Translation starts at AUG."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"80","q":"Why can one prokaryotic mRNA make several different polypeptides?","opts":[["It is polycistronic: several Shine-Dalgarno sequences, each followed by an AUG",1,""],["It is spliced in different ways",0,"That's alternative splicing in eukaryotes."],["Its codons overlap",0,"Codons don't overlap."],["Each ribosome reads a different reading frame",0,"Each gene has its own start."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"81","q":"Which is NOT one of the levels of gene regulation in your notes?","opts":[["Changing the genetic code",1,"The code is the same; regulation controls whether and how much a gene is expressed."],["DNA accessibility",0,"It is listed."],["RNA processing",0,"It is listed."],["Post-translational modification",0,"It is listed, e.g. phosphorylation."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"81","q":"A protein has an internal signal sequence. Where does it go?","opts":[["The nucleus",1,""],["The mitochondrion",0,"That needs an amino-terminal signal."],["It stays in the cytosol",0,"That's no signal."],["The ER lumen",0,"That needs an amino-terminal ER signal recognized by SRP."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"82","q":"What does the signal-recognition particle (SRP) do?","opts":[["Binds the ER signal sequence, halts translation, and brings the ribosome to the SRP receptor on the rough ER",1,""],["Adds a phosphate to activate a protein",0,"That's a post-translational modification."],["Carries amino acids to the ribosome",0,"That's tRNA."],["Recognizes the stop codon",0,"That's a release factor."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"82","q":"A protein made on the rough ER could end up:","opts":[["In the ER membrane, inside the endomembrane system, or secreted out of the cell",1,""],["Only in the nucleus",0,"Nuclear proteins have an internal signal and are made on free ribosomes."],["Only in the cytosol",0,"Proteins with no signal stay in the cytosol."],["In the chloroplast stroma",0,"Chloroplast proteins have an amino-terminal chloroplast signal."]]},{"ch":9,"p":"78","q":"A mutation changes the codon GGA to GGU. What happens to the protein?","opts":[["Nothing: both code for glycine",1,"The code is redundant."],["It is cut short",0,"GGU is not a stop codon."],["Glycine becomes proline",0,"Proline is CCN."],["The reading frame shifts",0,"A substitution doesn't shift the frame."]]}],"m6":[{"ch":0,"p":"90","q":"An organism obtains energy from chemical compounds and builds its sugars from CO₂. It is a:","opts":[["Chemoautotroph",1,"Chemotroph for energy, autotroph for carbon. Example: sulfur-oxidizing bacteria."],["Photoautotroph",0,"Phototrophs get energy from the sun."],["Chemoheterotroph",0,"Heterotrophs can't convert CO₂; they ingest organic carbon."],["Photoheterotroph",0,"Wrong on both energy and carbon source."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"90","q":"Which statement about heterotrophs is correct?","opts":[["They can't convert CO₂ into glucose, so they ingest other organisms or molecules for carbon",1,"Animals and most bacteria are heterotrophs."],["They obtain energy from sunlight",0,"That defines phototrophs; the trophs for carbon and energy are separate questions."],["They make their own organic carbon from CO₂",0,"That's autotrophs."],["They never need energy",0,"All cells need energy for work."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"90","q":"Which is NOT one of the things your notes say cells need?","opts":[["A cell wall to keep their shape",1,"The three needs: a membrane, a way to encode and transmit information, and energy to do work."],["A membrane separating inside from outside",0,"That's the first need."],["Nucleic acids and proteins to encode and transmit information",0,"That's the second need."],["Energy for movement, growth, pumping ions and reactions",0,"That's the third need."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"91","q":"Metabolism is best defined as:","opts":[["The chemical processes that occur in a living organism to maintain life",1,"It includes building up and breaking down compounds, so energy is harnessed or released."],["Only the breakdown of food",0,"That's catabolism, one branch."],["The study of energy changes in the universe",0,"That's thermodynamics."],["The synthesis of ATP only",0,"ATP synthesis is one part of it."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"91","q":"Which describes anabolism?","opts":[["Building molecules from smaller units; requires an input of energy (ATP)",1,"For example, joining amino acids into a protein."],["Breaking molecules into smaller units; produces ATP",0,"That's catabolism."],["A reaction with no change in free energy",0,"Anabolic reactions have +ΔG."],["Any reaction catalyzed by an enzyme",0,"Both branches are catalyzed by enzymes."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"91","q":"Digesting starch into glucose is an example of:","opts":[["Catabolism",1,"A macromolecule is broken into subunits."],["Anabolism",0,"Anabolism builds macromolecules from subunits."],["Energetic coupling",0,"Coupling pairs an exergonic and an endergonic reaction."],["Feedback inhibition",0,"That's pathway regulation."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"91","q":"A ball sitting at the top of a staircase has mostly:","opts":[["Potential energy",1,"Stored energy, not associated with movement."],["Kinetic energy",0,"Kinetic energy is the energy of motion; it isn't moving."],["Heat",0,"Heat is released as it bounces down."],["No energy",0,"It has high potential energy."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"92","q":"Which statement about bonds is correct?","opts":[["Weak bonds hold more potential energy than strong bonds",1,"Strong bonds = less potential energy; weak bonds = more."],["Strong bonds hold more potential energy",0,"It's the other way around."],["Bond strength has no link to potential energy",0,"Your notes say they are linked."],["Bonds store kinetic energy",0,"Chemical energy is a form of potential energy."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"92","q":"ATP is composed of:","opts":[["Adenine, ribose and three phosphate groups",1,"Adenosine (adenine + ribose) plus three phosphates."],["Adenine, deoxyribose and three phosphate groups",0,"The sugar in ATP is ribose."],["Adenine, ribose and two phosphate groups",0,"That's ADP."],["Adenosine and one phosphate group",0,"That's AMP."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"92","q":"Where is the chemical energy of ATP held?","opts":[["In the bonds connecting the phosphate groups",1,"Your notes mark this as important."],["In the adenine ring",0,"The energy is in the phosphate bonds."],["In the ribose",0,"Ribose links adenine and the phosphates."],["In the hydrogen bonds to water",0,"The energy is in covalent bonds between phosphates."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"92, 95","q":"Why do the bonds between ATP's phosphate groups have high potential energy?","opts":[["The negatively charged phosphate groups repel one another, so the bonds are weak",1,"At physiological pH they resist one another."],["They are the strongest bonds in the cell",0,"Strong bonds hold less potential energy."],["Adenine donates electrons to them",0,"The repulsion is between the phosphates."],["They are ionic bonds with water",0,"They are covalent bonds."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"92","q":"Which is NOT listed in your notes as cellular work that ATP powers?","opts":[["Absorbing light energy in a chloroplast",1,"Light absorption captures energy; it isn't ATP-powered work in the notes' list."],["Pumping substances across membranes",0,"Listed."],["DNA, RNA and protein synthesis",0,"Listed."],["Vesicle movement in a cell",0,"Listed."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"93","q":"The first law of thermodynamics states that:","opts":[["Energy can be neither created nor destroyed",1,"It can only be converted from one form to another."],["Every transformation increases entropy",0,"That's the second law."],["Events proceed from lower to higher energy",0,"They proceed from higher to lower (second law)."],["ΔG = ΔH − TΔS",0,"That's the Gibbs free energy relation."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"93","q":"According to the second law, why can't an energy transformation be 100% efficient?","opts":[["Some energy is released, often as heat, increasing entropy, so less is available to do work",1,"The total is unchanged (first law), but usable energy drops."],["Energy is destroyed in every transformation",0,"Energy can't be destroyed."],["Cells create extra energy as heat",0,"Energy can't be created."],["Only enzymes can transform energy",0,"Not related to efficiency."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"93","q":"An event that proceeds from a higher to a lower energy state with no input of external energy is:","opts":[["Spontaneous (thermodynamically favourable)",1,"Your notes' wording for the second law."],["Endergonic",0,"Endergonic reactions need an input of energy."],["At equilibrium",0,"At equilibrium there's no net change."],["Anabolic",0,"Anabolic reactions require energy."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"94","q":"During a chemical reaction:","opts":[["Atoms keep their identity, but the bonds linking them change",1,"For example, CO₂ + H₂O ⇌ H₂CO₃."],["Atoms change into different elements",0,"Atoms keep their identity."],["No bonds are broken",0,"Bonds break and new ones form."],["Energy is always released",0,"Some reactions require energy (endergonic)."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"94","q":"If the products of a reaction have more free energy than the reactants, the reaction is:","opts":[["Endergonic, +ΔG, nonspontaneous",1,"It needs an input of energy."],["Exergonic, −ΔG, spontaneous",0,"That's when the reactants have more free energy."],["Endergonic, −ΔG, spontaneous",0,"Endergonic means +ΔG."],["Exergonic, +ΔG, nonspontaneous",0,"Exergonic means −ΔG."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"94–95","q":"The reactants of a reaction have G = 20 kcal/mol and the products have G = 8 kcal/mol. What is ΔG?","opts":[["−12 kcal/mol; exergonic",1,"ΔG = final − initial = 8 − 20 = −12."],["+12 kcal/mol; endergonic",0,"Subtract initial from final, not the other way."],["+28 kcal/mol",0,"ΔG is a difference, not a sum."],["0; the reaction is at equilibrium",0,"The free energies differ."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"95","q":"In ΔG = ΔH − TΔS, what does S represent?","opts":[["Energy lost to entropy or disorder",1,"H is total energy (enthalpy), G is energy available to do work, T is absolute temperature in Kelvin."],["Total energy (enthalpy)",0,"That's H."],["Energy available to do work",0,"That's G."],["Absolute temperature",0,"That's T."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"95","q":"ΔH = −15 kcal/mol and TΔS = −5 kcal/mol. What is ΔG?","opts":[["−10 kcal/mol",1,"ΔG = ΔH − TΔS = −15 − (−5) = −10."],["−20 kcal/mol",0,"Subtracting a negative adds: −15 + 5."],["+10 kcal/mol",0,"Check the signs."],["−15 kcal/mol",0,"TΔS changes the answer."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"95","q":"What happens when ATP is hydrolyzed?","opts":[["ATP + H₂O → ADP + Pᵢ; the reaction is exergonic and releases free energy",1,"ΔG = −7.3 kcal/mol."],["ADP + Pᵢ → ATP; energy is stored",0,"That's ATP synthesis, the reverse."],["ATP + H₂O → AMP; energy is required",0,"Hydrolysis releases energy and gives ADP + Pᵢ."],["ATP splits adenine from ribose",0,"Hydrolysis removes the terminal phosphate."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"96","q":"Glucose + Pᵢ → glucose 6-phosphate has ΔG = +3.3 kcal/mol. Coupled with ATP hydrolysis (−7.3 kcal/mol), the net ΔG is:","opts":[["−4.0 kcal/mol, so the coupled reaction proceeds",1,"+3.3 + (−7.3) = −4.0. Pᵢ is shared between the two reactions."],["+10.6 kcal/mol",0,"Add the two ΔG values with their signs."],["+4.0 kcal/mol",0,"The sign is negative."],["−7.3 kcal/mol",0,"Both reactions count."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"95–96","q":"Which is one of the two reasons your notes give for why cells can carry out reactions with a +ΔG?","opts":[["Relative concentrations of reactants to products are kept above that defined by the equilibrium constant",1,"The other reason is coupling endergonic and exergonic reactions."],["Enzymes make ΔG negative",0,"Enzymes don't change ΔG."],["Cells raise their temperature until ΔG is negative",0,"Not one of the two reasons."],["The first law creates the extra energy",0,"Energy can't be created."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"96","q":"An endergonic reaction has ΔG = +9.0 kcal/mol. What is the smallest number of ATP hydrolyses (−7.3 each) that makes the coupled net ΔG negative?","opts":[["2",1,"9.0 − 14.6 = −5.6. With one, the net is +1.7."],["1",0,"9.0 − 7.3 = +1.7, still positive."],["3",0,"Two is enough."],["0",0,"Alone it is endergonic."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"95","q":"What is activation energy?","opts":[["The input of energy all reactions need to reach the transition state and proceed",1,"Reactants need enough kinetic energy to get over the barrier."],["The energy released by an exergonic reaction",0,"That's −ΔG."],["The energy stored in ATP",0,"Not the same thing."],["The energy lost as heat",0,"That's entropy."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"95","q":"An enzyme is added to a reaction. Which is true?","opts":[["The activation energy decreases and ΔG stays the same",1,"Enzymes speed up reactions, not change where they start and end."],["ΔG becomes more negative",0,"ΔG is the same with and without an enzyme."],["An endergonic reaction becomes exergonic",0,"Enzymes don't change ΔG."],["The enzyme is used up",0,"The enzyme is released unchanged."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"96","q":"How is an enzyme's active site formed?","opts":[["Folding brings together amino acids that are often far apart in the linear sequence",1,"The active site is an assembly of certain amino acids in a 3-D structure."],["It is a single amino acid at the N-terminus",0,"Several amino acids form it."],["It is coded by its own gene",0,"It is part of the folded enzyme."],["It forms only after the substrate is converted",0,"It must exist to bind the substrate."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"97","q":"In the induced fit model:","opts":[["The enzyme changes shape when the substrate binds",1,"In the lock-and-key model the site already fits."],["The substrate changes shape to fit a rigid enzyme",0,"It's the enzyme that changes."],["The enzyme is permanently changed after each reaction",0,"It returns to its original shape."],["No active site is involved",0,"Binding is at the active site."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"97","q":"Which is the correct order of an enzyme-catalyzed reaction?","opts":[["E + S ⇌ ES ⇌ EP ⇌ E + P",1,"Enzyme-substrate complex, then enzyme-product complex, then release."],["E + P ⇌ EP ⇌ ES ⇌ E + S",0,"That's backwards."],["E + S ⇌ EP ⇌ ES ⇌ E + P",0,"The substrate binds (ES) before it becomes product (EP)."],["ES ⇌ E + S ⇌ E + P",0,"Missing the enzyme-product complex."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"97","q":"A competitive inhibitor:","opts":[["Binds the active site, competing with the substrate",1,"It reduces the rate of the reaction."],["Binds a site other than the active site and changes the enzyme's shape",0,"That's a noncompetitive inhibitor."],["Increases enzyme activity",0,"That's an activator."],["Changes the ΔG of the reaction",0,"Inhibitors change activity, not ΔG."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"97","q":"Which statement about noncompetitive inhibition is correct?","opts":[["The inhibitor binds a site other than the active site and changes the enzyme's shape",1,"That reduces the rate of the reaction."],["The inhibitor must look like the substrate",0,"It doesn't bind the active site."],["It always binds irreversibly",0,"Inhibitors can be reversible or irreversible."],["It speeds the reaction up",0,"Inhibitors reduce activity."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"97","q":"In the pathway threonine → ... → isoleucine, isoleucine inhibits threonine dehydratase. This is an example of:","opts":[["Feedback inhibition by an allosteric inhibitor",1,"The final product inhibits the first step, which keeps balance and conserves energy."],["Competitive inhibition by the substrate",0,"Isoleucine is the end product, binding away from the active site."],["Energetic coupling",0,"Coupling is about ΔG, not regulation."],["Enzyme activation",0,"Isoleucine reduces activity."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"97","q":"Why are many metabolic pathways regulated?","opts":[["Because energy is needed for chemical reactions; regulation keeps balance and conserves energy",1,"Making more product than needed wastes energy."],["Because enzymes would otherwise change ΔG",0,"Enzymes never change ΔG."],["To stop the first law from applying",0,"The laws always apply."],["Because pathways can only run backwards",0,"Not the reason."]]},{"ch":9,"p":"91, 94, 96","q":"A muscle cell builds a protein from amino acids. Which description is correct?","opts":[["Anabolic and endergonic, driven by coupling to ATP hydrolysis",1,"Building up needs an input of energy; coupling to ATP hydrolysis makes the net ΔG negative."],["Catabolic and exergonic, producing ATP",0,"Building a protein is anabolic."],["Anabolic and exergonic, needing no ATP",0,"Anabolism requires an input of energy (ATP)."],["Catabolic, with an enzyme making ΔG negative",0,"Enzymes don't change ΔG, and this is anabolic."]]}],"m7":[{"ch":0,"p":"100","q":"Which list gives the four stages of cellular respiration in order?","opts":[["Glycolysis, pyruvate oxidation, citric acid cycle, oxidative phosphorylation",1,"Stage 1 to stage 4, starting from glucose."],["Pyruvate oxidation, glycolysis, citric acid cycle, oxidative phosphorylation",0,"Glycolysis makes the pyruvate, so it comes first."],["Glycolysis, citric acid cycle, pyruvate oxidation, oxidative phosphorylation",0,"Pyruvate must become acetyl-CoA before the cycle."],["Glycolysis, fermentation, citric acid cycle, oxidative phosphorylation",0,"Fermentation is the anaerobic alternative, not a stage."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"100–101","q":"Which statement about substrate-level phosphorylation is correct?","opts":[["It makes a small amount of ATP directly, in a reaction involving an enzyme/substrate complex",1,"It happens in glycolysis and the citric acid cycle."],["It makes the majority of the cell's ATP",0,"Oxidative phosphorylation makes the majority."],["It needs the electron transport chain",0,"That's oxidative phosphorylation."],["It only happens when O₂ is absent",0,"It happens in glycolysis whether or not O₂ is present."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"105","q":"Which stage of cellular respiration does NOT take place in the mitochondria?","opts":[["Glycolysis",1,"Glycolysis is in the cytoplasm (cytosol); stages 2 to 4 are in the mitochondria."],["Pyruvate oxidation",0,"It happens in the matrix."],["The citric acid cycle",0,"It happens in the matrix."],["Oxidative phosphorylation",0,"It happens at the inner membrane."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"101","q":"In the breakdown of glucose, which is correct?","opts":[["Glucose is oxidized to CO₂ and O₂ is reduced to H₂O",1,"OIL RIG: glucose loses electrons, O₂ gains them."],["Glucose is reduced to CO₂ and O₂ is oxidized to H₂O",0,"Reversed."],["Glucose and O₂ are both oxidized",0,"Electrons lost by one are gained by the other."],["CO₂ is reduced to glucose",0,"That's the reverse process."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"102","q":"Which pair lists the REDUCED forms of the two electron carriers?","opts":[["NADH and FADH₂",1,"NAD⁺ and FAD are the oxidized forms."],["NAD⁺ and FAD",0,"These are the oxidized forms."],["NADH and FAD",0,"FAD is oxidized."],["ATP and ADP",0,"These are not electron carriers."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"102","q":"What is the final electron acceptor in aerobic cellular respiration?","opts":[["Oxygen",1,"O₂ accepts the electrons and is reduced to water."],["Glucose",0,"Glucose is the original electron donor."],["NAD⁺",0,"NAD⁺ is a carrier in the middle of the process."],["Pyruvate",0,"Pyruvate is only reduced in fermentation, when O₂ is absent."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"102","q":"Why do NADH and FADH₂ matter for making ATP?","opts":[["Their reduced form holds high potential energy that is used to make ATP in the final stage",1,"They carry energy from stages 1 to 3 to the electron transport chain."],["They are converted directly into ATP",0,"Their electrons power the ETC; they are not turned into ATP."],["They pump protons themselves",0,"The ETC complexes pump protons."],["They store carbon for the citric acid cycle",0,"They carry electrons, not carbon."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"103","q":"Where does glycolysis occur, and why is it considered anaerobic?","opts":[["In the cytosol; no oxygen is consumed",1,"It runs with or without O₂."],["In the matrix; it only runs without O₂",0,"Glycolysis is in the cytosol and runs in both conditions."],["In the cytosol; it only runs without O₂",0,"It runs with O₂ too; it just doesn't use it."],["On the inner membrane; it produces no ATP",0,"It makes a net 2 ATP."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"104","q":"What happens in phase 1 (the preparatory phase) of glycolysis?","opts":[["Two ATP are used to add two phosphates, making fructose 1,6-bisphosphate",1,"This traps glucose in the cell and destabilizes it."],["Fructose 1,6-bisphosphate is split into two three-carbon molecules",0,"That's phase 2."],["Four ATP and two NADH are made",0,"That's phase 3."],["Pyruvate is converted to acetyl-CoA",0,"That's pyruvate oxidation."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"104","q":"At the end of phase 2 of glycolysis, what is present?","opts":[["Two molecules of glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate",1,"Dihydroxyacetone phosphate is converted into a second G3P."],["Two molecules of pyruvate",0,"Pyruvate is made at the end of phase 3."],["One molecule of fructose 1,6-bisphosphate",0,"It has been split in phase 2."],["Two acetyl-CoA",0,"That's after pyruvate oxidation."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"105","q":"What is the net gain from glycolysis of one glucose?","opts":[["2 ATP and 2 NADH",1,"4 ATP are made in phase 3, but 2 were used in phase 1."],["4 ATP and 2 NADH",0,"That's the total made; subtract the 2 used."],["2 ATP and 2 FADH₂",0,"Glycolysis makes NADH, not FADH₂."],["32 ATP",0,"That's the whole of aerobic respiration."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"103","q":"Which of the following is NOT a product of glycolysis?","opts":[["CO₂",1,"No CO₂ is released in glycolysis; the first CO₂ comes from pyruvate oxidation."],["Pyruvate",0,"Two pyruvates are made."],["NADH",0,"Two NADH are made."],["ATP",0,"Four are made, net two."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"106","q":"What is the space inside the inner mitochondrial membrane called?","opts":[["The mitochondrial matrix",1,"The space between the two membranes is the intermembrane space."],["The intermembrane space",0,"That's between the outer and inner membranes."],["The cytosol",0,"The cytosol is outside the mitochondrion."],["The lumen",0,"That term is used for the ER."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"106","q":"During pyruvate oxidation, what happens to pyruvate?","opts":[["It is oxidized to CO₂ and an acetyl group, which is transferred to coenzyme A, and NADH is made",1,"Catalyzed by the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex in the matrix."],["It is reduced to lactic acid",0,"That's fermentation."],["It is split into two G3P",0,"That's glycolysis."],["It joins oxaloacetate to form citrate",0,"Acetyl-CoA does that, in the citric acid cycle."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"107","q":"For each glucose, pyruvate oxidation produces:","opts":[["2 CO₂, 2 NADH and 2 acetyl-CoA",1,"One of each per pyruvate, and there are two pyruvates."],["1 CO₂, 1 NADH and 1 acetyl-CoA",0,"That's per pyruvate; glycolysis made two."],["2 ATP, 2 NADH and 2 acetyl-CoA",0,"No ATP is made in pyruvate oxidation."],["6 CO₂",0,"Only 2 CO₂ come from pyruvate oxidation."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"106","q":"Which enzyme group catalyzes the conversion of pyruvate to acetyl-CoA?","opts":[["The pyruvate dehydrogenase complex",1,"In the mitochondrial matrix."],["ATP synthase",0,"ATP synthase makes ATP."],["Aminoacyl tRNA synthetase",0,"That charges tRNAs (Module 5)."],["Complex I",0,"Complex I accepts electrons from NADH."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"107","q":"Why is the citric acid cycle a cycle?","opts":[["Its first reactant, oxaloacetate, is regenerated at the end",1,"It can then accept the next acetyl-CoA."],["It runs twice per glucose",0,"True, but that's not why it's a cycle."],["It recycles NAD⁺",0,"Fermentation and the ETC regenerate NAD⁺."],["Its carbons go back to glucose",0,"They leave as CO₂."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"107","q":"Acetyl-CoA (2C) combines with oxaloacetate (4C). What is formed?","opts":[["Citrate, with 6 carbons",1,"2 + 4 = 6."],["Isocitrate, with 4 carbons",0,"Citrate (6C) is formed first."],["α-Ketoglutarate, with 5 carbons",0,"That's after one CO₂ leaves."],["Pyruvate, with 3 carbons",0,"Pyruvate comes from glycolysis."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"107–108","q":"Per glucose (two turns), the citric acid cycle produces:","opts":[["4 CO₂, 6 NADH, 2 FADH₂ and 2 ATP",1,"Per turn: 2 CO₂, 3 NADH, 1 FADH₂, 1 ATP."],["2 CO₂, 3 NADH, 1 FADH₂ and 1 ATP",0,"That's one turn."],["6 CO₂, 10 NADH, 2 FADH₂ and 4 ATP",0,"That mixes in other stages."],["4 CO₂, 6 NADH, 2 FADH₂ and 28 ATP",0,"The 28 ATP come from oxidative phosphorylation."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"108","q":"Where does the CO₂ you exhale come from?","opts":[["The oxidation of acetyl-CoA (and pyruvate) in the mitochondria",1,"The carbons of glucose leave as CO₂."],["The oxygen you inhale",0,"O₂ becomes water."],["Glycolysis",0,"Glycolysis releases no CO₂."],["The electron transport chain",0,"The ETC produces water, not CO₂."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"109","q":"Which complexes of the electron transport chain pump protons?","opts":[["Complexes I, III and IV",1,"Complex II passes electrons on but does not pump."],["Complexes I, II, III and IV",0,"Complex II does not pump protons."],["Only complex IV",0,"I and III pump too."],["ATP synthase only",0,"ATP synthase lets protons flow back."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"109","q":"Protons are pumped across the inner membrane in which direction?","opts":[["From the matrix into the intermembrane space",1,"So the intermembrane space has a high H⁺ concentration."],["From the intermembrane space into the matrix",0,"That's the direction they flow back through ATP synthase."],["From the cytosol into the matrix",0,"The gradient is across the inner membrane."],["Out of the cell",0,"They stay in the mitochondrion."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"109","q":"Why does the proton gradient contain high potential energy?","opts":[["Protons cannot diffuse back across the inner membrane, so they build up in the intermembrane space",1,"They can only return through ATP synthase."],["Protons are bonded to ATP",0,"Protons are free ions in solution."],["The matrix has more protons than the intermembrane space",0,"The intermembrane space has more."],["Protons are reduced by O₂",0,"O₂ is reduced, combining with H⁺ to make water."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"110","q":"How does ATP synthase make ATP?","opts":[["Protons flow through the Fo channel, which rotates; F1 uses the rotational energy to make ATP from ADP + Pi",1,"A molecular machine with two subunits, Fo and F1."],["F1 pumps protons using ATP",0,"In respiration protons flow down their gradient to make ATP."],["It transfers electrons from NADH to O₂",0,"That's the ETC."],["It moves a phosphate from a substrate to ADP",0,"That's substrate-level phosphorylation."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"109","q":"If O₂ were unavailable, what would happen to the electron transport chain?","opts":[["Electrons would have no final acceptor, so the chain would stop and so would ATP synthesis there",1,"The carriers stay reduced and proton pumping stops."],["It would run faster",0,"Without an acceptor electrons back up."],["FADH₂ would replace O₂ as the final acceptor",0,"FADH₂ is an electron donor."],["Nothing would change",0,"O₂ is essential as the final acceptor."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"110","q":"According to your notes, the complete oxidation of one glucose yields about how much ATP?","opts":[["32 ATP",1,"2 (glycolysis) + 2 (citric acid cycle) + 28 (oxidative phosphorylation)."],["2 ATP",0,"That's glycolysis or fermentation alone."],["4 ATP",0,"That's the substrate-level ATP only."],["28 ATP",0,"That's oxidative phosphorylation alone."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"110","q":"Which statement about where the ATP comes from is NOT true?","opts":[["Most of it is made by substrate-level phosphorylation in the citric acid cycle",1,"The cycle makes only 2 ATP per glucose; oxidative phosphorylation makes about 28."],["Glycolysis gives a net 2 ATP",0,"True."],["Oxidative phosphorylation gives about 28 ATP",0,"True."],["NADH and FADH₂ power oxidative phosphorylation",0,"True."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"111","q":"In animal cells under anaerobic conditions, pyruvate is:","opts":[["Reduced to lactic acid, regenerating NAD⁺",1,"Lactic acid fermentation, also in bacteria."],["Oxidized to acetyl-CoA",0,"That needs O₂."],["Converted to ethanol and CO₂",0,"That's plants and fungi."],["Stored as glycogen",0,"Glycogen is a glucose polymer."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"111","q":"In ethanol fermentation, what is the correct order of events?","opts":[["Pyruvate releases CO₂ to form acetaldehyde, then NADH reduces acetaldehyde to ethanol",1,"NAD⁺ is regenerated at the last step."],["Pyruvate is reduced to ethanol, then CO₂ is released from ethanol",0,"CO₂ leaves first, forming acetaldehyde."],["Acetaldehyde is oxidized to pyruvate",0,"Reversed."],["Ethanol is converted to lactic acid",0,"These are two different pathways."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"111","q":"What is the main purpose of fermentation?","opts":[["To regenerate NAD⁺ so glycolysis can keep making ATP",1,"Glycolysis needs NAD⁺; fermentation oxidizes NADH back to it."],["To make more ATP than aerobic respiration",0,"It makes far less: 2 ATP per glucose."],["To produce O₂",0,"It happens when O₂ is absent."],["To build a proton gradient",0,"That's the ETC."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"112","q":"How do animals store excess glucose, and where?","opts":[["As glycogen, in muscle cells and the liver",1,"Muscle glycogen powers contraction; liver glycogen serves the whole body."],["As starch, in the liver",0,"Starch is the plant storage form."],["As fatty acids, only in muscle",0,"Glucose is stored as a branched glucose polymer."],["As sucrose, in the blood",0,"Sucrose is a dietary disaccharide."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"113","q":"Which statement about β-oxidation is correct?","opts":[["It breaks lipids down into glycerol and acetyl-CoA, producing NADH and FADH₂ but no ATP directly",1,"The carriers then feed the ETC."],["It produces ATP directly by substrate-level phosphorylation",0,"Your notes: ATP is not produced directly."],["It converts fatty acids into glucose",0,"It converts them into acetyl-CoA."],["It happens in the cytosol as part of glycolysis",0,"It is a separate pathway that feeds acetyl-CoA into respiration."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"112","q":"Which of these is a disaccharide?","opts":[["Lactose",1,"Lactose, maltose and sucrose are disaccharides."],["Fructose",0,"A monosaccharide."],["Galactose",0,"A monosaccharide."],["Mannose",0,"A monosaccharide."]]},{"ch":9,"p":"111","q":"A runner's muscles run short of O₂. How much ATP does each glucose now give, and what builds up?","opts":[["About 2 ATP; lactic acid",1,"Only glycolysis makes ATP; pyruvate is reduced to lactic acid."],["About 32 ATP; lactic acid",0,"Without O₂ there is no oxidative phosphorylation."],["About 2 ATP; ethanol",0,"Animal cells make lactic acid."],["None; pyruvate",0,"Fermentation keeps glycolysis going, so 2 ATP are still made."]]}],"m8":[{"ch":0,"p":"117","q":"What is photosynthesis?","opts":[["The conversion of light energy to chemical energy",1,"Light energy is stored in carbohydrates made from CO₂."],["The conversion of chemical energy to light energy",0,"Reversed."],["The breakdown of glucose to make ATP",0,"That's cellular respiration."],["The fixation of O₂ into carbohydrates",0,"CO₂ is fixed; O₂ is released."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"117","q":"About what share of the sun's energy reaching a leaf can, at most, end up as carbohydrate?","opts":[["About 4%",1,"60% is outside the visible range, 8% reflected or transmitted, 8% heat, 20% lost during carbohydrate synthesis."],["About 20%",0,"20% is the share lost during carbohydrate synthesis."],["About 40%",0,"That's roughly all of visible light, most of which is lost."],["About 60%",0,"60% is outside the visible range and unavailable."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"117","q":"In photosynthesis, which molecule is the electron donor?","opts":[["Water",1,"Its oxidation produces electrons, protons and O₂."],["CO₂",0,"CO₂ is reduced: it accepts electrons."],["NADPH",0,"NADPH donates electrons in the Calvin cycle, but the original donor is water."],["Glucose",0,"Glucose is a product."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"117","q":"Which statement about photosynthesis as a redox reaction is correct?","opts":[["Water is oxidized and CO₂ is reduced",1,"The two are linked by the photosynthetic electron transport chain."],["Water is reduced and CO₂ is oxidized",0,"Reversed."],["Both water and CO₂ are oxidized",0,"CO₂ gains electrons to become carbohydrate."],["O₂ is reduced to water",0,"That happens in respiration."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"117","q":"Which is NOT a product of the oxidation of water in photosynthesis?","opts":[["CO₂",1,"CO₂ is a reactant of the Calvin cycle, not a product of splitting water."],["Electrons",0,"These enter photosystem II."],["Protons (H⁺)",0,"Released into the thylakoid lumen."],["O₂",0,"Released as a by-product."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"118","q":"The space surrounding the thylakoids, where carbon fixation occurs, is the:","opts":[["Stroma",1,""],["Thylakoid lumen",0,"The lumen is inside the thylakoid membrane."],["Granum",0,"A granum is a stack of thylakoids."],["Space between the outer and inner membranes",0,"That narrow space isn't where the Calvin cycle runs."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"118","q":"Orderly stacks of thylakoids are called:","opts":[["Grana",1,"Singular: granum."],["Stroma",0,"The stroma surrounds them."],["Lumens",0,"The lumen is inside a thylakoid."],["Cristae",0,"Cristae are folds of the inner mitochondrial membrane."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"118","q":"What are the products of the light-dependent reactions, and where do they occur?","opts":[["ATP, NADPH and O₂; in the thylakoid membranes",1,""],["Carbohydrate; in the stroma",0,"That's the light-independent reactions."],["ATP, NADPH and O₂; in the stroma",0,"They are made at the thylakoid membranes (ATP and NADPH are released on the stroma side)."],["CO₂ and water; in the thylakoid lumen",0,"Those are reactants of the overall equation."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"118","q":"Which is NOT true of chloroplasts?","opts":[["They are bound by a single membrane",1,"They have 2 membranes separated by a narrow space."],["They are semi-autonomous and self-replicating",0,"True, from the notes."],["Both stages of photosynthesis happen inside them",0,"True in photosynthetic eukaryotes."],["They contain thylakoids",0,"True."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"118","q":"An excited pigment molecule re-emits energy as light of a longer wavelength. This is:","opts":[["Fluorescence",1,"One of three ways back to the ground state."],["Energy transfer",0,"Transfer passes energy to another molecule, not as light."],["Heat dissipation",0,"Heat is not re-emitted light."],["Photophosphorylation",0,"That's ATP synthesis driven by light."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"119","q":"Why do leaves look green?","opts":[["Chlorophyll is poor at absorbing green light, so it is reflected or transmitted",1,"Chlorophyll absorbs blue and red light."],["Chlorophyll absorbs mostly green light",0,"Green is the light it absorbs least."],["Carotenoids reflect only green light",0,"Carotenoids look yellow-orange."],["Green light carries the most energy",0,"Shorter wavelengths (blue) carry more energy."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"119","q":"Which atom is at the centre of chlorophyll's porphyrin ring?","opts":[["Magnesium (Mg)",1,""],["Iron (Fe)",0,"Iron is at the centre of heme, a different porphyrin."],["Manganese (Mn)",0,"Not in the notes for chlorophyll."],["Nitrogen (N)",0,"Four N atoms surround the central atom."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"119","q":"What does chlorophyll's phytol side chain do?","opts":[["Inserts chlorophyll into the lipid bilayer of the thylakoid membrane",1,"It is a long hydrocarbon tail."],["Absorbs blue and red light",0,"That's the porphyrin ring."],["Gives the different types of chlorophyll",0,"Different side groups on the ring do that."],["Transfers electrons to plastoquinone",0,"That's the reaction center's job."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"119","q":"Which is NOT a role of accessory pigments such as carotenoids?","opts":[["Transferring electrons from water to NADP⁺",1,"That's the electron transport chain."],["Absorbing light that chlorophyll absorbs poorly",0,"True."],["Increasing the efficiency of light absorption",0,"True."],["Protecting photosynthetic components from damage",0,"True."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"120","q":"What do antenna chlorophylls pass to the reaction center?","opts":[["Energy",1,"Antenna chlorophylls transfer energy, not electrons."],["Electrons",0,"Only the reaction center transfers an electron, to an acceptor."],["Protons",0,"Protons are moved by the electron transport chain."],["NADPH",0,"NADPH is made at the end of the chain."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"120","q":"After the reaction center of photosystem II gives an electron to an acceptor, what must happen before it can use more light energy?","opts":[["It must get an electron from an electron donor, water",1,""],["It must make one ATP",0,"ATP is made by ATP synthase."],["It must pass energy back to the antenna",0,"Energy flows toward the reaction center."],["Nothing; it can absorb again immediately",0,"It has lost an electron and must be refilled."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"120","q":"The reaction center is best described as:","opts":[["A specific pair of chlorophylls that can transfer an excited electron to an electron acceptor",1,""],["Any chlorophyll that absorbs a photon",0,"Most chlorophylls are antenna chlorophylls."],["The enzyme that splits water into O₂",0,"Water is split at PSII, but the reaction center is chlorophyll."],["The site where CO₂ is fixed",0,"That's rubisco in the stroma."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"121","q":"In the Z scheme, what is the order of electron flow?","opts":[["H₂O → PSII → electron transport chain → PSI → NADP⁺",1,""],["H₂O → PSI → PSII → NADP⁺",0,"PSII acts first."],["NADPH → PSI → PSII → H₂O",0,"Reversed."],["CO₂ → PSII → PSI → O₂",0,"CO₂ isn't part of the chain."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"121","q":"Why does the electron need a second input of light at photosystem I?","opts":[["Its energy drops along the chain and must be raised high enough to reduce NADP⁺",1,""],["To split a second water molecule",0,"Water is split at PSII."],["To pump H⁺ out of the lumen",0,"H⁺ is pumped into the lumen by the b6f complex."],["To make CO₂",0,"No CO₂ is made in the light reactions."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"121","q":"The 'Z' in the Z scheme describes:","opts":[["The energy trajectory of electrons: up at PSII, down the chain, up at PSI",1,""],["The shape of the thylakoid membrane",0,"It's an energy diagram."],["The route of CO₂ through the Calvin cycle",0,"Unrelated."],["The folding of chlorophyll's phytol tail",0,"Unrelated."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"122","q":"Which carrier moves electrons from the cytochrome b6f complex to photosystem I by diffusing through the thylakoid lumen?","opts":[["Plastocyanin",1,""],["Plastoquinone",0,"It carries electrons from PSII to b6f."],["Ferredoxin",0,"It is on the stroma side of PSI."],["NADP⁺ reductase",0,"It makes NADPH."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"122","q":"As electrons flow along the Z pathway, H⁺ ions are moved:","opts":[["From the stroma into the thylakoid lumen",1,"So the lumen has the higher H⁺ concentration."],["From the lumen into the stroma",0,"That's the direction of flow through ATP synthase."],["From the cytosol into the stroma",0,"The gradient is across the thylakoid membrane."],["Out of the chloroplast",0,"The gradient is across the thylakoid membrane."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"122","q":"What directly drives ATP synthesis in the chloroplast?","opts":[["Protons flowing from the lumen through ATP synthase to the stroma",1,"Photophosphorylation."],["Electrons passing through ATP synthase",0,"ATP synthase moves protons."],["Light hitting ATP synthase",0,"Light drives electron flow, which builds the gradient."],["Rubisco",0,"Rubisco fixes CO₂."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"122","q":"Why do plant cells still need mitochondria?","opts":[["ATP made in the chloroplast stays there for carbon fixation, so the rest of the cell needs ATP from mitochondria",1,""],["Chloroplasts cannot make ATP",0,"They can, with ATP synthase."],["Mitochondria make the O₂ for photosynthesis",0,"O₂ comes from water in the chloroplast."],["Mitochondria run the Calvin cycle",0,"The Calvin cycle is in the stroma."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"125","q":"In cyclic electron transport, electrons from PSI return to the cytochrome b6f complex. What is produced?","opts":[["ATP only, with no NADPH and no O₂",1,"H⁺ is still pumped, so ATP synthase works."],["NADPH only",0,"Electrons never reach NADP⁺."],["ATP, NADPH and O₂",0,"That's linear electron flow."],["Triose phosphate",0,"That's the Calvin cycle."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"123","q":"Which enzyme catalyzes carboxylation in the Calvin cycle?","opts":[["Rubisco",1,"It adds CO₂ to RuBP."],["ATP synthase",0,"It makes ATP."],["NADP⁺ reductase",0,"It makes NADPH."],["Cytochrome b6f",0,"Part of the electron transport chain."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"123","q":"In carboxylation, CO₂ combines with RuBP. What forms?","opts":[["An unstable 6-carbon molecule that breaks into two 3-carbon PGA",1,""],["Two 5-carbon molecules",0,"5 + 1 = 6 carbons, split into 3 + 3."],["Glucose directly",0,"Carbohydrate leaves as 3-carbon triose phosphate."],["Triose phosphate and O₂",0,"No O₂ is made in the Calvin cycle."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"123","q":"During the reduction step of the Calvin cycle:","opts":[["PGA is phosphorylated by ATP, then reduced by NADPH to triose phosphate",1,""],["RuBP is reduced by water",0,"Water is not used in the Calvin cycle."],["CO₂ is reduced by ATP alone",0,"NADPH supplies the electrons."],["Triose phosphate is oxidized to PGA",0,"Reversed."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"123","q":"For every six triose phosphates produced in the Calvin cycle, how many can be withdrawn?","opts":[["One; the other five regenerate three RuBP",1,"5 × 3 C = 15 C = 3 × 5 C."],["Six",0,"Then RuBP couldn't be regenerated."],["Three",0,"Count the carbons."],["Five",0,"Only one."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"123","q":"How much ATP and NADPH does it take to fix 6 CO₂ (enough carbon for one glucose)?","opts":[["18 ATP and 12 NADPH",1,"Per 3 CO₂: 9 ATP (6 in reduction, 3 in regeneration) and 6 NADPH."],["9 ATP and 6 NADPH",0,"That fixes only 3 CO₂."],["6 ATP and 6 NADPH",0,"Regeneration also needs ATP."],["12 ATP and 18 NADPH",0,"Reversed."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"124","q":"What happens to excess carbohydrate made during the day?","opts":[["It is converted into starch granules, used at night",1,""],["It is broken down to CO₂ right away",0,"It is stored."],["It is stored as ATP",0,"ATP is not a storage molecule."],["It is converted to RuBP",0,"Only enough is recycled to keep the cycle going."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"124","q":"Which were the first organisms to use water as the electron donor?","opts":[["Cyanobacteria",1,""],["Early bacteria with a single photosystem",0,"One photosystem cannot capture enough energy."],["Green algae",0,"They are eukaryotes that got chloroplasts later."],["Plants",0,"Much later."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"124","q":"Which is NOT one of the two possibilities in your notes for how two photosystems evolved?","opts":[["Endosymbiosis of a mitochondrion",1,"Endosymbiosis explains the chloroplast, not the origin of two photosystems."],["Horizontal gene transfer",0,"Possibility 1."],["Gene duplication and divergence of one of the genes",0,"Possibility 2."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"124","q":"According to the endosymbiotic theory, the chloroplast came from:","opts":[["A cyanobacterium engulfed by a eukaryotic cell, which lost the ability to live outside the host",1,""],["An infolding of the plasma membrane",0,"Not what the notes describe."],["A mitochondrion that gained chlorophyll",0,"Mitochondria and chloroplasts have separate origins."],["A virus carrying photosystem genes",0,"Not in the notes."]]}],"m9":[{"ch":0,"p":"127","q":"Which definition of cell signalling matches your notes?","opts":[["Transferring information from the outside of the cell to obtain a response inside the cell",1,"Cells send and receive chemical signals over short or long distances."],["Moving ions across a membrane against their gradient",0,"That is active transport."],["Copying a gene into mRNA",0,"That is transcription."],["Any chemical reaction in the cytosol",0,"Signalling is specifically about passing information into the cell."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"127","q":"Which is NOT one of the four essential elements of cell communication?","opts":[["A second messenger",1,"Second messengers are used by some pathways, but they aren't one of the four elements."],["A signalling cell",0,"Element 1."],["A receptor molecule",0,"Element 3."],["A responding cell",0,"Element 4. The other is the signalling molecule."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"127","q":"Pneumococcal bacteria at low cell density do not take up DNA. Why?","opts":[["The signalling peptide stays below its critical threshold, so DNA-uptake genes stay off",1,"At high density the peptide binds the receptors and the genes turn on."],["They have no receptor for the peptide",0,"They express the receptor on their surface."],["They stop making the peptide",0,"The peptide is made continuously."],["Bacteria cannot signal; only eukaryotes can",0,"Prokaryotes use the same four elements."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"128","q":"In the epinephrine example, which cells are the signalling cells?","opts":[["Cells of the adrenal glands",1,"They release epinephrine into the blood."],["Heart muscle cells",0,"Those are responding cells."],["Red blood cells",0,"Blood carries the hormone; it doesn't make it."],["Airway smooth muscle cells",0,"Those respond by relaxing."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"128","q":"What are the four steps of cell signalling, in order?","opts":[["Receptor activation, signal transduction, cellular response, signal termination",1,""],["Signal transduction, receptor activation, termination, response",0,"The receptor has to be activated first."],["Response, receptor activation, transduction, termination",0,"The response comes after transduction."],["Amplification, activation, response, transduction",0,"Amplification happens during transduction; it isn't one of the four named steps."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"129","q":"A ligand binding its receptor's ligand-binding site is most similar to:","opts":[["A substrate binding an enzyme's active site",1,"Both are specific fits that change the protein's conformation."],["A peptide bond forming",0,"Ligand binding is not covalent bond formation."],["Water crossing by osmosis",0,"That doesn't involve a binding site."],["DNA being replicated",0,"Not the comparison in your notes."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"129","q":"Epinephrine makes heart muscle contract faster but relaxes airway smooth muscle. This shows that:","opts":[["The response depends on the proteins and signalling pathways in each cell type",1,""],["Epinephrine is a different molecule in each tissue",0,"It is the same hormone."],["Only one tissue has receptors",0,"Both respond, so both have receptors."],["Signals are terminated faster in the airways",0,"Termination doesn't explain opposite responses."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"129","q":"What happens when a ligand binds the ligand-binding site of its receptor?","opts":[["The receptor changes conformation, which triggers chemical reactions in the cytosol",1,""],["The ligand is broken down immediately",0,"It stays bound for a time set by binding affinity."],["The receptor moves to the nucleus",0,"Cell-surface receptors stay in the membrane."],["Nothing until a second ligand binds",0,"One ligand activates the receptor."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"130","q":"Which type of signalling uses hormones carried through the bloodstream?","opts":[["Endocrine",1,"Long distance; target cells express the right receptor."],["Paracrine",0,"Paracrine signals diffuse to neighbours."],["Autocrine",0,"Autocrine signals act on the cell that made them."],["Contact-dependent",0,"That needs touching cells."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"130","q":"A small, water-soluble signal diffuses to cells within about 20 cell-widths. This is:","opts":[["Paracrine signalling",1,""],["Endocrine signalling",0,"That uses the blood."],["Contact-dependent signalling",0,"No molecule is released in contact-dependent signalling."],["Autocrine signalling",0,"In autocrine signalling the sender is the target."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"130","q":"In contact-dependent (juxtacrine) signalling, what acts as the signalling molecule?","opts":[["A transmembrane protein on the surface of the signalling cell",1,"It binds a transmembrane receptor on the adjacent cell."],["A hormone in the blood",0,"That is endocrine."],["cAMP",0,"cAMP is a second messenger inside the cell."],["A steroid that diffuses into the neighbour",0,"Contact-dependent signals don't diffuse."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"130","q":"Which pair of signalling types is especially important during embryonic development?","opts":[["Paracrine and autocrine",1,""],["Endocrine and contact-dependent",0,"Your notes single out paracrine and autocrine."],["Endocrine and paracrine",0,"Endocrine isn't the one named."],["Contact-dependent only",0,"Not the pair in your notes."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"131","q":"Why can small nonpolar signalling molecules bind receptors inside the cell?","opts":[["They can pass through the hydrophobic core of the cell membrane",1,"So they don't need a receptor on the outside."],["They are carried in by ion channels",0,"They diffuse straight through the lipid bilayer."],["They bind GPCRs that pull them inside",0,"GPCR ligands stay outside."],["They are too large for surface receptors",0,"They are small."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"131","q":"Which is NOT one of the three main groups of cell-surface receptor?","opts":[["Cytosolic steroid receptors",1,"Those are intracellular receptors for nonpolar signals."],["G protein-coupled receptors",0,"Group 1."],["Receptor kinases",0,"Group 2."],["Ion channels",0,"Group 3."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"131","q":"A cell-surface receptor has three domains. Where is the ligand-binding site?","opts":[["In the extracellular domain",1,"The polar ligand stays outside the cell."],["In the transmembrane domain",0,"That domain crosses the bilayer."],["In the cytoplasmic domain",0,"That domain passes the signal inside."],["In the nucleus",0,"Surface receptors are in the plasma membrane."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"132","q":"Which subunit of a G protein binds GDP or GTP?","opts":[["α (alpha)",1,""],["β (beta)",0,"β stays with γ."],["γ (gamma)",0,"γ stays with β."],["All three equally",0,"Only α binds the guanine nucleotide."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"132","q":"When the α subunit of a G protein is bound to GDP:","opts":[["The three subunits are joined and the G protein is inactive",1,""],["The α subunit separates and activates its target",0,"That happens with GTP bound."],["The receptor is bound to its ligand",0,"GDP-bound is the resting state, before the ligand binds."],["cAMP is being made",0,"Adenylyl cyclase is off."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"132","q":"What does ligand binding to a GPCR do to the G protein?","opts":[["Activates it by replacing GDP with GTP on the α subunit",1,"The α subunit then separates from β and γ."],["Phosphorylates the β subunit",0,"G proteins are switched by GDP/GTP, not phosphorylation."],["Moves it into the nucleus",0,"It stays at the membrane."],["Replaces GTP with GDP",0,"That turns it off."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"133","q":"In heart muscle, what does the GTP-bound α subunit bind and activate?","opts":[["Adenylyl cyclase",1,"An enzyme in the cell membrane."],["Protein kinase A",0,"cAMP activates PKA."],["Ras",0,"Ras is in the receptor kinase pathway."],["Phosphatase",0,"Phosphatases help end the signal."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"133","q":"Adenylyl cyclase converts:","opts":[["ATP into cyclic AMP (cAMP)",1,""],["GTP into GDP",0,"The G protein does that."],["cAMP into AMP",0,"Enzymes in the cytosol do that to end the signal."],["ADP into ATP",0,"That's ATP synthesis."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"133","q":"Why is cAMP called a second messenger?","opts":[["It relays the signal inside the cell after the first messenger binds the receptor outside",1,"Epinephrine is the first messenger."],["It is the second hormone made by the adrenal gland",0,"cAMP is made inside the responding cell."],["It is always made second, after ATP",0,"That isn't what the term means."],["It carries signals between two cells",0,"It acts inside one cell."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"133","q":"Kinases are enzymes that:","opts":[["Phosphorylate target proteins",1,"They transfer a phosphate from ATP."],["Dephosphorylate target proteins",0,"That's phosphatases."],["Make cAMP",0,"That's adenylyl cyclase."],["Bind GDP or GTP",0,"That's the G protein α subunit."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"134","q":"Which is NOT one of the three places the epinephrine signal is amplified?","opts":[["Each epinephrine molecule binds many receptors at once",1,"One ligand binds one receptor; amplification happens downstream."],["Each epinephrine-bound receptor activates multiple G proteins",0,"Place 1."],["Each adenylyl cyclase produces large amounts of cAMP",0,"Place 2."],["Each active PKA activates multiple protein targets",0,"Place 3."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"134","q":"One receptor activates 4 G proteins; each adenylyl cyclase makes 50 cAMP; each cAMP activates one PKA; each PKA phosphorylates 10 targets. How many targets are phosphorylated?","opts":[["2,000",1,"1 × 4 × 50 × 10."],["64",0,"Multiply the layers, don't add them."],["200",0,"Include the PKA step."],["500",0,"Include the G protein step."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"135","q":"How long a ligand stays bound to its receptor depends on:","opts":[["Binding affinity: how tightly the receptor holds it",1,""],["How many G proteins the cell has",0,"That affects amplification, not binding time."],["The amount of cAMP",0,"cAMP is downstream of the receptor."],["Whether the cell has phosphatases",0,"Phosphatases act on target proteins."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"135","q":"Which event does NOT help terminate the epinephrine signal?","opts":[["Adenylyl cyclase converting more ATP into cAMP",1,"Making cAMP keeps the signal going."],["The G protein converting GTP to GDP",0,"It turns itself off."],["Enzymes in the cytosol degrading cAMP",0,"Stops activation of more PKA."],["Phosphatases removing phosphates from activated proteins",0,"Inactivates the targets."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"135","q":"A G protein α subunit is mutated so it cannot convert GTP to GDP. What is the likely result?","opts":[["The G protein stays on, so cAMP keeps being made and the response continues",1,"Converting GTP to GDP is the G protein's own off-switch."],["The G protein can never be activated",0,"It can still bind GTP; it just can't switch off."],["The receptor can't bind epinephrine",0,"The receptor is unaffected."],["Phosphatases stop working",0,"Phosphatases are separate enzymes."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"136","q":"What happens when a ligand binds a receptor kinase?","opts":[["The receptors pair into dimers and each phosphorylates the other",1,"The phosphate groups are binding sites for signalling proteins."],["A G protein swaps GDP for GTP",0,"That's GPCR signalling."],["A gate opens and ions flow through",0,"That's a ligand-gated ion channel."],["The receptor enters the nucleus",0,"It stays in the membrane."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"137","q":"In wound healing, which protein do the phosphorylated PDGF receptors activate to start the MAP kinase cascade?","opts":[["Ras",1,"GTP-bound Ras triggers the kinase cascade."],["Protein kinase A",0,"PKA is activated by cAMP in the GPCR pathway."],["Adenylyl cyclase",0,"That's in the GPCR pathway."],["Platelet-derived growth factor",0,"PDGF is the ligand."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"137","q":"How is the MAP kinase pathway terminated?","opts":[["GDP replaces GTP on Ras",1,""],["cAMP is degraded",0,"That ends GPCR signalling."],["The last kinase leaves the nucleus",0,"Not the off-switch in your notes."],["The receptors dimerize",0,"Dimerization activates the pathway."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"138","q":"A channel opens when a signalling molecule binds it. Which type of gated channel is it?","opts":[["Ligand-gated",1,""],["Voltage-gated",0,"Responds to voltage changes."],["Mechanically gated",0,"Responds to force on the cell."],["Ungated",0,"It has a gate that the ligand opens."]]},{"ch":9,"p":"131","q":"Which receptor is matched to its mechanism correctly?","opts":[["Receptor kinase: dimerizes and phosphorylates its partner, activating Ras",1,""],["GPCR: opens a gate so ions can flow",0,"That's an ion channel."],["Ligand-gated ion channel: activates adenylyl cyclase",0,"That's a GPCR via its G protein."],["GPCR: binds steroids in the nucleus",0,"Steroids bind intracellular receptors."]]}],"m10":[{"ch":0,"p":"141","q":"Two or more tissues that combine and function together make up:","opts":[["An organ",1,"For example, skin: epithelial tissue on connective tissue."],["A tissue",0,"A tissue is a collection of cells working on one function."],["A cell community of one cell type only",0,"An organ has two or more tissues."],["The extracellular matrix",0,"The ECM is secreted material outside cells."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"141","q":"Which cell is undifferentiated and can undergo an unlimited number of mitotic divisions?","opts":[["A stem cell",1,"It can differentiate into many specialized cells."],["A somatic cell",0,"Somatic cells are the specialized, nonreproductive cells."],["A germ cell",0,"Germ cells produce gametes."],["A fibroblast",0,"A fibroblast is a specialized cell that makes the ECM."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"141","q":"Which is the most common type of cell in the body of a multicellular organism?","opts":[["Somatic cells",1,"Nonreproductive cells."],["Germ cells",0,"They are reproductive cells."],["Stem cells",0,"They are undifferentiated and relatively few."],["Gametes",0,"Gametes are made by germ cells."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"142","q":"Which statement about the skin is correct?","opts":[["The dermis is mostly connective tissue, and its main cell, the fibroblast, produces the ECM",1,""],["The epidermis is mostly connective tissue",0,"The epidermis is epithelial tissue."],["Melanocytes are found in the dermis and make collagen",0,"Melanocytes are in the epidermis and make pigment."],["The basal lamina lies on top of the epidermis",0,"It lies below the bottom layer of the epidermis."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"143","q":"Which cytoskeletal elements do all eukaryotic cells have?","opts":[["Microtubules and microfilaments",1,"Animal cells also have intermediate filaments."],["Intermediate filaments and microtubules",0,"Intermediate filaments are listed for animal cells."],["Only microfilaments",0,"They have at least two elements."],["Microfilaments and intermediate filaments",0,"Intermediate filaments are not in all eukaryotes."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"144","q":"A microtubule is built from:","opts":[["Dimers of α tubulin and β tubulin, assembled into a hollow tube",1,""],["Actin monomers twisted into a double helix",0,"That is a microfilament."],["Keratin subunits wound into a rope",0,"That is an intermediate filament."],["Cadherins joined end to end",0,"Cadherins are adhesion molecules."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"144–145","q":"Which describes microfilaments?","opts":[["The thinnest cytoskeletal fibers; actin double helices that are branched just beneath the cell membrane",1,""],["The widest cytoskeletal fibers; hollow tubes",0,"That is microtubules."],["Cable-like fibers found only in animal cells",0,"That is intermediate filaments."],["Fibrils of collagen",0,"Collagen is ECM, outside the cell."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"145","q":"Inside each microvillus of an intestinal epithelial cell there is:","opts":[["A bundle of actin microfilaments held by cross-linking actin-binding proteins",1,""],["A single microtubule",0,"Microvilli are supported by microfilaments."],["A desmosome",0,"Desmosomes join neighbouring cells."],["A bundle of intermediate filaments",0,"Not in your notes; microvilli contain microfilaments."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"145","q":"Which statement about the plus and minus ends of microtubules and microfilaments is correct?","opts":[["Subunits can be added or removed at both ends, but the plus end grows faster",1,"Your notes highlight this."],["Subunits can only be added at the plus end",0,"Both ends can gain or lose subunits."],["The minus end grows faster",0,"The minus end assembles slowly."],["Only microtubules have plus and minus ends",0,"Microfilaments do too."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"146","q":"Random cycles of slow growth and rapid shrinkage in microtubules are called:","opts":[["Dynamic instability",1,"The dramatic shrinkage is a catastrophe."],["Polarity",0,"Polarity is spatial differences within a cell."],["Cytokinesis",0,"That is division of the cytoplasm."],["Treadmilling of collagen",0,"Collagen is an ECM protein."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"146","q":"How do spindle microtubules find chromosomes during cell division?","opts":[["They grow into new areas and shrink back (dynamic instability) until they hit and attach to chromosomes",1,""],["Motor proteins drag chromosomes to fixed microtubules",0,"Your notes describe microtubules exploring the cell."],["Chromosomes secrete ECM that pulls them",0,"Not in your notes."],["Microtubules grow from the chromosomes toward the centrosome",0,"Minus ends sit at the centrosome; plus ends grow outward."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"147","q":"Kinesin and dynein differ in that:","opts":[["Kinesin carries cargo toward the plus end, and dynein toward the minus end",1,"Both walk on microtubules using ATP."],["Kinesin walks on actin and dynein on microtubules",0,"Both walk on microtubules; myosin walks on actin."],["Only dynein uses ATP",0,"Both are powered by ATP."],["Kinesin moves cargo toward the centrosome",0,"That is dynein (minus end)."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"147","q":"The energy for motor protein movement comes from:","opts":[["Conformational changes in the motor protein, powered by ATP",1,""],["Diffusion",0,"Motor transport is directed."],["Light",0,"Not for motor proteins."],["Growth of the track",0,"The motor walks along the track."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"147","q":"Which motor protein causes the movement of cilia and flagella?","opts":[["Dynein",1,"Associated with the microtubules inside them."],["Kinesin",0,"Kinesin carries cargo to plus ends."],["Myosin",0,"Myosin moves along actin."],["Integrin",0,"Integrin is an adhesion molecule."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"147","q":"Intermediate filaments:","opts":[["Are found in animal cells, have a diameter between microfilaments and microtubules, and give mechanical strength",1,""],["Are found in all eukaryotic cells",0,"Your notes list them for animal cells."],["Are hollow tubes of tubulin",0,"That is microtubules."],["Carry vesicles toward the plus end",0,"That is kinesin on microtubules."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"148","q":"Which pairing of cell type and intermediate filament is correct?","opts":[["Fibroblasts: vimentin",1,""],["Neurons: keratins",0,"Neurons have neurofilaments."],["Epithelial cells: lamins",0,"Epithelial cells have keratins; lamins are in the nucleus."],["Nucleus: neurofilaments",0,"The nucleus has lamins."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"148","q":"According to Table 10.1, which function is listed for microtubules but not microfilaments?","opts":[["Chromosome segregation in cell division",1,"Microfilaments handle cytokinesis instead."],["Vesicle transport",0,"Listed for both."],["Cell shape and support",0,"Listed for all three."],["Muscle contraction",0,"That is microfilaments."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"148","q":"Which is NOT listed as a function of microfilaments in Table 10.1?","opts":[["Organelle arrangement",1,"That is a microtubule function."],["Cell movement by crawling",0,"Listed for microfilaments."],["Cytokinesis",0,"Listed for microfilaments."],["Muscle contraction",0,"Listed for microfilaments."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"149","q":"In an epithelial cell, the membrane that faces the lumen is the:","opts":[["Apical membrane",1,"The basolateral membrane is attached to the basal lamina."],["Basolateral membrane",0,"That sits on the basal lamina."],["Basal lamina",0,"That is ECM, not a membrane."],["Middle lamella",0,"That is in plant cell walls."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"149","q":"The extracellular domain of a cadherin binds:","opts":[["The extracellular domain of a cadherin of the same type on an adjacent cell",1,""],["ECM proteins in the basal lamina",0,"That is integrins."],["Any adhesion molecule on any cell",0,"Same type to same type."],["Microtubules",0,"The cytoplasmic part links to the cytoskeleton."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"150","q":"Integrins are important for tissues under physical stress because:","opts":[["Their cytoplasmic domain interacts with the cytoskeleton while their extracellular domain binds the ECM",1,""],["They seal the extracellular space",0,"That is tight junctions."],["They form channels between cells",0,"That is gap junctions."],["They are made of lignin",0,"Integrins are glycoproteins."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"150","q":"Cadherins are found in which junctions?","opts":[["Adherens junctions and desmosomes",1,""],["Hemidesmosomes and tight junctions",0,"Hemidesmosomes use integrins; tight junctions use claudins and occludins."],["Gap junctions only",0,"Gap junctions use connexins."],["All five junctions",0,"Only two of them."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"151","q":"A belt-like complex of cadherins around the cell's circumference, linked inside to a band of actin microfilaments, is:","opts":[["An adherens junction",1,""],["A desmosome",0,"Desmosomes are button-like and linked to intermediate filaments."],["A hemidesmosome",0,"That uses integrins and binds the basal lamina."],["A tight junction",0,"Tight junctions seal; they are not linked to actin in Table 10.2."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"151","q":"Which is true of desmosomes?","opts":[["Button-like adhesion points where cadherins link to intermediate filaments",1,""],["They attach cells to the basal lamina",0,"That is hemidesmosomes."],["They prevent the passage of materials between cells",0,"They don't; tight junctions do."],["They are made of connexins",0,"That is gap junctions."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"151","q":"Hemidesmosomes anchor epithelial cells to the basal lamina using:","opts":[["Integrins linked to intermediate filaments",1,""],["Cadherins linked to microfilaments",0,"That is the adherens junction."],["Connexons",0,"That is gap junctions."],["Claudins and occludins",0,"That is tight junctions."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"152","q":"With tight junctions in place, how does a substance cross a sheet of epithelial cells?","opts":[["By moving through the cells by a cellular transport mechanism",1,"The space between cells is sealed."],["By leaking between the cells",0,"Tight junctions seal that route."],["Through desmosomes",0,"Desmosomes are not channels."],["Through plasmodesmata",0,"Those are in plant cells."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"152","q":"Which is NOT true of gap junctions?","opts":[["They can pass RNA molecules and proteins",1,"That is plasmodesmata; gap junctions pass only low molecular weight material."],["They are made of connexons spanning a 2–4 nm gap",0,"True."],["They allow cytoplasmic continuity between adjacent cells",0,"True."],["They integrate the activities of cells in a tissue",0,"True."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"152","q":"Plasmodesmata differ from gap junctions because plasmodesmata:","opts":[["Are much larger, are lined by continuous cell membrane, and pass RNA and proteins between plant cells",1,""],["Are found in animal cells",0,"They connect plant cells."],["Only pass ions",0,"That is gap junctions."],["Seal the space between cells",0,"That is tight junctions."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"153","q":"According to Table 10.2, which junction is a barrier junction?","opts":[["Tight junction",1,"Function: epithelial boundary."],["Gap junction",0,"Communicating."],["Desmosome",0,"Anchoring."],["Plasmodesma",0,"Communicating."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"154","q":"Which layer of the plant cell wall is the main way plant cells adhere to one another?","opts":[["The middle lamella, made of carbohydrates",1,""],["The secondary wall, made of cellulose and lignin",0,"That hardens and waterproofs the wall."],["The primary wall, made of cellulose and pectin",0,"Not the adhesion layer."],["The cell membrane",0,"Not part of the wall."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"154","q":"What makes the secondary cell wall hard and water resistant?","opts":[["Lignin",1,"Together with cellulose."],["Pectin",0,"Pectin is in the primary wall."],["Collagen",0,"Collagen is animal ECM."],["Laminin",0,"Laminin is animal ECM."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"155","q":"Which statement about collagen is correct?","opts":[["It is the most abundant animal protein, and each molecule is three polypeptides wound into a triple helix",1,""],["It is a polysaccharide that forms a gel",0,"Collagen is a fibrous protein in the gel-like polysaccharide matrix."],["There is only one form of collagen",0,"More than 20 forms; type I is most abundant."],["It is made by keratinocytes in the epidermis",0,"Fibroblasts in the dermis make the ECM."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"156","q":"The basal lamina:","opts":[["Is a specialized ECM beneath all epithelial tissues that includes a type of collagen and acts as a scaffold",1,""],["Is a layer of epithelial cells",0,"It is ECM, not cells."],["Lies on the apical side of epithelial cells",0,"It lies beneath them."],["Is made of cellulose",0,"That is plant ECM."]]},{"ch":9,"p":"141","q":"The structural integrity of a tissue depends on cells adhering to one another and to:","opts":[["The extracellular matrix, a meshwork of proteins and polysaccharides outside the cell",1,""],["The nucleus",0,"Inside the cell."],["Ribosomes",0,"Not a structural link between cells."],["Gametes",0,"Not relevant to tissue structure."]]}],"m11":[{"ch":0,"p":"159","q":"Which is one of the two requirements your notes give for a successful cell division?","opts":[["Each daughter cell must receive all of the genetic material found in the parent cell",1,"The other: the parent must be big enough that each daughter gets adequate cytoplasm."],["Each daughter cell must receive half of the parent's chromosomes",0,"Each daughter gets a full copy."],["The parent cell must first enter G₀",0,"G₀ cells are not dividing."],["The nuclear envelope must stay intact",0,"In eukaryotes it breaks down and re-forms; prokaryotes have none."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"160","q":"In binary fission, what separates the two copies of the circular chromosome?","opts":[["Each copy is attached to the plasma membrane, and the attachment sites move apart as the cell elongates",1,"Then a constriction forms at the midpoint."],["A mitotic spindle pulls them to opposite poles",0,"Prokaryotes don't use a mitotic spindle."],["The nuclear envelope pinches in two",0,"Prokaryotes have no nucleus."],["Kinetochore microtubules shorten",0,"That's anaphase in eukaryotes."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"161","q":"Which feature belongs to eukaryotic, not prokaryotic, cell division (Table 11.1)?","opts":[["The nuclear membrane must be broken down and then restored",1,"Eukaryotic DNA is in the nucleus."],["The genome is small and circular",0,"That's prokaryotes."],["The DNA is found in the cytoplasm",0,"That's prokaryotes."],["Cell growth separates the DNA attached to the membrane",0,"That's binary fission."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"162","q":"M phase consists of which two processes?","opts":[["Mitosis and cytokinesis",1,"Mitosis separates the replicated chromosomes; cytokinesis divides the cytoplasm."],["G₁ and G₂",0,"Those are gap phases of interphase."],["DNA replication and mitosis",0,"DNA replication is in S phase."],["Interphase and cytokinesis",0,"Interphase is the time between M phases."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"162","q":"A cell has just finished S phase. Which phase comes next, and what does the cell do there?","opts":[["G₂: it prepares for M phase",1,"The second gap phase."],["G₁: it grows and makes regulatory proteins",0,"G₁ comes before S."],["G₀: it stops preparing for division",0,"G₀ branches off from G₁."],["M phase: it replicates its DNA",0,"DNA is replicated in S."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"163","q":"Why is DNA replication called semiconservative?","opts":[["Each new DNA molecule has one original parental strand and one newly made strand",1,"Each parental strand serves as a template."],["Half of the genes are copied in each cycle",0,"The whole genome is copied."],["Only the leading strand is kept",0,"Both parental strands are kept, one in each new molecule."],["The new strands are made half from RNA",0,"Primers are replaced by DNA."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"163","q":"One DNA molecule replicates three times, giving 8 molecules. How many of them contain one of the two original strands?","opts":[["2",1,"Only two original strands exist; each stays in one molecule."],["4",0,"There are only two original strands."],["8",0,"Most are new/new."],["1",0,"Both original strands are kept."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"164","q":"Which enzyme works upstream (ahead) of the replication fork to relieve supercoiling?","opts":[["Topoisomerase",1,"Unwinding at the fork overwinds the DNA ahead."],["Helicase",0,"Helicase unwinds at the fork itself."],["Single-strand binding protein",0,"SSBs coat single strands behind the fork."],["DNA ligase",0,"Ligase joins fragments."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"165","q":"Which is NOT required by DNA polymerase to make DNA?","opts":[["An RNA template carried inside the enzyme",1,"That is telomerase. DNA polymerase reads a DNA template."],["A DNA template",0,"Required."],["A primer with a 3′-OH end",0,"Required; primase supplies it."],["dATP, dTTP, dGTP and dCTP",0,"Required: the 4 deoxyribonucleotides."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"165","q":"In which direction does DNA polymerase synthesize new DNA?","opts":[["5′ to 3′, adding nucleotides to the 3′-OH end",1,"The same direction as transcription."],["3′ to 5′, adding nucleotides to the 5′ end",0,"It can only add to a 3′ end."],["Either direction, depending on the strand",0,"Only 5′ to 3′; that's why there is a lagging strand."],["Toward the origin on both strands",0,"Direction depends on the 5′ → 3′ rule, not the origin."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"166","q":"The lagging strand is made discontinuously because:","opts":[["DNA can be elongated only at the 3′ end, and the lagging strand's 3′ end points away from the fork",1,"So it is made in pieces as more template is exposed."],["Its template is RNA",0,"Both templates are DNA."],["Helicase only unwinds one strand",0,"Helicase separates both strands."],["It has no origin of replication",0,"Both strands start at origins."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"166–167","q":"Put these lagging-strand events in order: (1) ligase seals, (2) primase lays a primer, (3) DNA polymerase extends the primer, (4) a different DNA polymerase replaces the primer with DNA.","opts":[["2, 3, 4, 1",1,"Prime, extend, replace the primer, seal."],["2, 4, 3, 1",0,"The primer is replaced after the next fragment reaches it."],["3, 2, 4, 1",0,"DNA polymerase needs the primer first."],["2, 3, 1, 4",0,"Ligase joins DNA to DNA, so the RNA primer must be replaced first."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"167","q":"What does the trombone model explain?","opts":[["How the leading and lagging strands are made at the same time, by looping the lagging strand",1,"The loop lasts until the new fragment meets the previous one."],["How telomeres are extended",0,"That's telomerase."],["How circular chromosomes finish replication",0,"Forks meet and fuse on the far side."],["How chromatin condenses into chromosomes",0,"That's packaging, p. 172."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"168","q":"During proofreading, how does DNA polymerase recognize an incorrect nucleotide?","opts":[["It detects mispairing in hydrogen bond formation with the template base",1,"Then it cleaves out the wrong nucleotide and inserts the correct one."],["Ligase refuses to seal next to it",0,"Proofreading is done by DNA polymerase."],["The incorrect nucleotide is RNA",0,"It's a wrong DNA base."],["It re-reads the strand after replication is finished",0,"It checks as each nucleotide is added."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"169","q":"Which describes replication of a bacterial chromosome?","opts":[["A single origin; forks move in both directions and meet and fuse on the opposite side",1,""],["Many origins and many replication bubbles",0,"That's linear eukaryotic chromosomes."],["Replication from both telomeres inward",0,"Circular DNA has no ends."],["One fork moving in a single direction around the circle",0,"Replication is bidirectional."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"170","q":"What happens when two replication bubbles on a linear chromosome meet?","opts":[["They fuse into one larger bubble and DNA ligase seals the gap in the backbone",1,""],["Replication stops and a telomere forms",0,"Telomeres are at chromosome ends."],["Topoisomerase joins the strands",0,"Ligase seals the backbone."],["The two bubbles become separate chromosomes",0,"They merge into one larger bubble."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"170","q":"Why does a linear chromosome shorten with each round of replication?","opts":[["The final lagging-strand primer sits about 100 nucleotides from the 3′ end, and when it's removed that template stays unreplicated",1,""],["The leading strand cannot reach the end of its template",0,"The leading strand can be copied all the way to the end."],["Helicase removes the telomere each time",0,"Helicase only unwinds."],["Proofreading cuts off the chromosome ends",0,"Proofreading removes single wrong nucleotides."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"171","q":"Which statement about telomerase is FALSE?","opts":[["It is fully active in adult somatic cells",1,"It is inactive in adult somatic cells; fully active in stem cells and germ cells."],["It is a ribonucleoprotein",0,"True: a protein-RNA complex."],["It has reverse transcriptase activity",0,"True: RNA → DNA."],["Its RNA template is complementary to the telomere repeats",0,"True."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"171","q":"Why do adult somatic cells stop dividing after about 50 divisions?","opts":[["Their telomeres become too short because telomerase is inactive (the Hayflick limit)",1,""],["They run out of DNA polymerase",0,"Cyclin D and E-CDK lead to DNA polymerase being made each cycle."],["Their origins of replication are used up",0,"Origins are reused each cycle."],["They become haploid",0,"Mitosis keeps the chromosome number the same."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"172","q":"In what order does DNA condense, from smallest to largest?","opts":[["DNA molecule, nucleosome fiber, chromatin fiber, coiled chromatin fiber, coiled coil, condensed chromatid",1,"2, 10, 30, 300, 700 and 1400 nm."],["Nucleosome fiber, DNA molecule, chromatin fiber, coiled coil, coiled chromatin fiber, chromatid",0,"The DNA molecule (2 nm) is smallest."],["DNA molecule, chromatin fiber, nucleosome fiber, coiled coil, chromatid, coiled chromatin fiber",0,"Nucleosomes (10 nm) come before the 30 nm fiber."],["Chromatid, coiled coil, coiled chromatin fiber, chromatin fiber, nucleosome fiber, DNA molecule",0,"That's largest to smallest."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"172","q":"What are sister chromatids?","opts":[["The two identical copies of a chromosome made in S phase, held together at the centromere",1,""],["The two homologous chromosomes from each parent",0,"Those are homologs."],["One chromatid from each of two homologs",0,"Those are nonsister chromatids."],["The two strands of one DNA double helix",0,"Each chromatid contains a whole double helix."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"172","q":"A diploid cell has 8 chromosomes in G₁. How many chromatids does it have in G₂?","opts":[["16",1,"Each chromosome becomes two sister chromatids in S phase."],["8",0,"That's before S phase."],["4",0,"That's the number of homologous pairs."],["32",0,"DNA is copied only once per cycle."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"173–174","q":"In which stage of mitosis does the nuclear membrane break down and spindle microtubules attach to kinetochores?","opts":[["Prometaphase",1,""],["Prophase",0,"Prophase: chromosomes condense; spindle forms outside the nucleus."],["Metaphase",0,"Chromosomes are already attached and line up."],["Anaphase",0,"Chromatids separate."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"174","q":"What happens in anaphase?","opts":[["The centromeres divide and kinetochore microtubules shorten, pulling sister chromatids to opposite poles",1,""],["Chromosomes line up at the metaphase plate",0,"That's metaphase."],["Nuclear envelopes re-form",0,"That's telophase."],["Chromosomes condense and become visible",0,"That's prophase."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"173","q":"Which statement about centrosomes is correct?","opts":[["They are the microtubule-organizing centres of animal cells and migrate to opposite poles in prophase",1,"Plant cells have spindles but lack centrosomes."],["They hold sister chromatids together",0,"That's the centromere."],["They form the cell plate",0,"The phragmoplast guides vesicles into the cell plate."],["They are found in plant cells but not animal cells",0,"The reverse."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"175","q":"Why is cytokinesis in plant cells different from animal cells?","opts":[["Plant cells have a cell wall, so they must build a new wall: vesicles fuse into a cell plate",1,"Animal cells pinch with a contractile ring of actin."],["Plant cells have no mitotic spindle",0,"They have microtubule-based spindles."],["Plant cells skip telophase",0,"Mitosis is mostly the same in both."],["Plant cells use a contractile ring of microtubules",0,"The contractile ring (actin) is in animal cells."]]},{"ch":9,"p":"176","q":"Which statement about CDKs is correct?","opts":[["They are always present but active only when bound to the appropriate cyclin",1,""],["Their levels rise and fall each cycle",0,"That's cyclins."],["They are only made in M phase",0,"They are always present."],["They inhibit cell cycle progression",0,"They phosphorylate proteins needed for progression."]]},{"ch":9,"p":"177","q":"A drug blocks cyclin A from rising. Which event is most directly prevented?","opts":[["The initiation of DNA synthesis in S phase",1,"Cyclin A-CDK initiates DNA synthesis."],["Breakdown of the nuclear envelope in prometaphase",0,"That's cyclin B-CDK."],["Expression of DNA polymerase in G₁",0,"That's cyclin D and E-CDK."],["Cytokinesis",0,"Not linked to cyclin A in the notes."]]},{"ch":9,"p":"177","q":"A cell reaches the end of G₂ with part of a chromosome not yet copied. Which checkpoint stops it?","opts":[["The DNA replication checkpoint",1,"It checks for unreplicated DNA at the end of G₂."],["The DNA damage checkpoint",0,"That acts before S phase."],["The spindle assembly checkpoint",0,"That acts before anaphase."],["None; it enters mitosis anyway",0,"Checkpoints prevent this."]]},{"ch":9,"p":"178","q":"How does p53 arrest a cell with radiation-damaged DNA?","opts":[["A kinase phosphorylates p53, which activates transcription of an inhibitor of the G₁/S cyclin-CDK",1,"This gives the cell time to repair the DNA."],["p53 cuts out the damaged nucleotides",0,"p53 controls transcription; it isn't a repair enzyme here."],["p53 binds the spindle and blocks anaphase",0,"That's the spindle assembly checkpoint."],["p53 degrades all cyclins",0,"It blocks the G₁/S cyclin-CDK through an inhibitor."]]},{"ch":10,"p":"179","q":"If DNA damage is not repaired in time, phosphorylated p53:","opts":[["Stimulates Bax transcription and represses Bcl-2, leading to Bax/Bax dimers and apoptosis",1,""],["Represses Bax and stimulates Bcl-2",0,"Reversed: that would protect the cell."],["Activates telomerase",0,"Not in the notes."],["Turns on cyclin B to force mitosis",0,"p53 stops the cycle."]]},{"ch":10,"p":"179","q":"Which is NOT a function of apoptosis in the adult, according to your notes?","opts":[["Increasing cell numbers in tissues that need to grow",1,"Apoptosis removes cells; it balances proliferation."],["Maintaining tissue size by eliminating excess cells",0,"Listed."],["Removing activated T lymphocytes that are no longer needed",0,"Listed."],["Eliminating genetically damaged cells that can't be repaired",0,"Listed."]]},{"ch":10,"p":"180","q":"A mutation inactivates both copies of a tumour suppressor gene. What is the likely effect?","opts":[["A brake on cell division is lost, a step toward cancer",1,"Tumour suppressors such as p53 inhibit division."],["The cell divides more slowly",0,"Losing a brake speeds division."],["The gene becomes an oncogene",0,"Oncogenes come from altered proto-oncogenes."],["The cell's telomeres lengthen",0,"Unrelated."]]},{"ch":11,"p":"162, 177–178","q":"UV light damages the DNA of a skin stem cell late in G₁. The damage is repaired and the cell goes on to divide. Which sequence is right?","opts":[["Arrest at the DNA damage checkpoint (p53), repair, S phase, replication checkpoint at the end of G₂, mitosis, cytokinesis",1,"Damaged DNA is caught before S; unreplicated DNA is caught at the end of G₂."],["S phase, then the DNA damage checkpoint, then repair in G₂, then mitosis",0,"The DNA damage checkpoint acts before S phase."],["Apoptosis, then S phase, then mitosis",0,"A cell that undergoes apoptosis doesn't divide; apoptosis follows only if repair fails."],["Mitosis first, then the damage is repaired in the daughter cells",0,"Checkpoints stop damaged DNA from being copied and passed on."]]}]},"review":[{"mod":"m1","ch":0,"p":"4","q":"Who was the first person to observe and name \"cells,\" and what material was he examining?","a":"Robert Hooke, in 1665. He looked at thin slices of cork and described its chambers as \"cells\". He was actually seeing empty walls, the remains of dead cells."},{"mod":"m1","ch":0,"p":"4","q":"What was Antonie van Leeuwenhoek's contribution to cell biology, and how did his observations differ from those of Robert Hooke?","a":"Leeuwenhoek (1673–1700) was an early light microscopist who improved the magnifying power of microscope lenses. He saw living \"animalcules\" in pond water. Hooke saw the empty walls of dead cork cells; Leeuwenhoek saw living, moving cells."},{"mod":"m1","ch":1,"p":"5","q":"What are the three main tenets of the cell theory? Who proposed the first two tenets? Who proposed the third tenet?","a":"1. All living organisms are composed of one (unicellular) or more (multicellular) cells. 2. Each cell is the structural and functional unit of life. 3. Cells can arise only from pre-existing cells. Schleiden and Schwann proposed the first two; Virchow proposed the third."},{"mod":"m1","ch":1,"p":"4–5","q":"Who contributed to the development of the cell theory?","a":"Hooke (named cells in cork, 1665) and Leeuwenhoek (saw living cells) made the first observations. Schleiden, a botanist, noted that all plant tissues are made of cells and plant embryos arise from a single cell. Schwann, a zoologist, reached the same conclusion for animals and saw that plants and animals are similar. Virchow, a physician, added the third tenet."},{"mod":"m1","ch":3,"p":"6","q":"What features are shared by all cells?","a":"All cells are highly complex and organized and enclosed by a plasma membrane; have a genetic program (DNA, read through the central dogma); can produce more of themselves (duplicating their DNA before division); acquire and use energy; carry out chemical reactions (metabolism); engage in mechanical activities; respond to stimuli (receptors); and regulate themselves."},{"mod":"m1","ch":2,"p":"5","q":"What are exceptions to the cell theory?","a":"Viruses, viroids and prions. None is made of cells or can reproduce on its own."},{"mod":"m1","ch":2,"p":"5","q":"How do viruses differ from living cells in terms of structure, replication, and dependence on host cells?","a":"Structure: a virus is just a small amount of nucleic acid (DNA or RNA) in a protein coat (capsid), sometimes with an envelope; it has no membrane-bound cytoplasm, ribosomes or metabolism. Replication: it cannot divide; it injects its genome into a host cell, which copies it and builds new viruses. Dependence: outside a living cell a virus is inert; it needs a host cell's machinery to reproduce."},{"mod":"m1","ch":2,"p":"5","q":"What are viroids? What are prions?","a":"Viroids are small circular RNAs with no protein coat that need host machinery to replicate (e.g. potato spindle tuber viroid). Prions are proteinaceous infectious particles with no nucleic acid: a misfolded prion protein (PrP^Sc) converts normal protein (PrP^C) into the misfolded form, which accumulates."},{"mod":"m1","ch":3,"p":"6","q":"What is the difference between the cytoplasm and the cytosol, and what roles do they play in cellular function?","a":"The cytoplasm is all the contents inside the cell membrane. The cytosol is the jelly-like internal fluid: the cytoplasm minus the organelles, containing water, dissolved ions and molecules. The cytosol is where many of the cell's chemical reactions happen and where organelles sit and materials move."},{"mod":"m1","ch":3,"p":"6","q":"How do cells maintain a boundary between their internal environment and the outside world?","a":"Every cell is enclosed by a physical barrier, the cell (plasma) membrane. Inside it is the cytoplasm. Bacteria, plants and fungi also have a cell wall outside the membrane for support."},{"mod":"m1","ch":3,"p":"6","q":"How do cells store, express, and transmit genetic information?","a":"Information is stored in DNA. A gene is the DNA code for a functional product. It is expressed through the central dogma: DNA is transcribed into RNA, and RNA is translated into protein. Cells transmit it by duplicating their genetic material before they divide."},{"mod":"m1","ch":4,"p":"6–9","q":"How do prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells differ in structure and complexity?","a":"Prokaryotes (Bacteria and Archaea) are single cells with no nucleus and no membrane-bound organelles: a single circular chromosome in a nucleoid, often plasmids, ribosomes, a membrane and (bacteria) a cell wall. They are small and divide by binary fission. Eukaryotes have a membrane-bound nucleus holding their chromosomes and many specialized organelles (endomembrane system, mitochondria, chloroplasts in plants), and are usually much larger and more complex."},{"mod":"m1","ch":3,"p":"6","q":"How is genetic information stored, transmitted, and expressed in cells?","a":"Stored as DNA; transmitted by copying the DNA before cell division so each new cell gets a full set; expressed by transcription of genes into RNA and translation of RNA into protein (the central dogma)."},{"mod":"m1","ch":3,"p":"3, 6–9","q":"What is the relationship between structure and function in biological systems?","a":"A structure's shape and makeup fit the job it does. Examples from Module 1: the plasma membrane forms a barrier around every cell; the cell wall gives bacteria and plants rigid support; the plant vacuole holds water to make turgor pressure; chloroplasts capture sunlight; mitochondria make ATP; the cytoskeleton gives an internal framework."},{"mod":"m1","ch":5,"p":"8","q":"What are protists? What are fungi?","a":"Protists are a diverse group of eukaryotes, mostly single cells though some form colonies: algae (Volvox), water molds (potato blight), slime molds and protozoa (ciliates). Fungi are eukaryotes with cell walls containing chitin (a modified polysaccharide): yeasts, rusts, smuts, mildews, molds and mushrooms. They are heterotrophs that absorb preformed organic molecules directly from the environment."},{"mod":"m1","ch":5,"p":"9","q":"How do plant cells differ structurally from animal cells, and what is the function of these differences (e.g., cell wall, chloroplasts, large central vacuole)?","a":"Plant cells have a cell wall of polysaccharides (a rigid barrier for support), a large water-filled vacuole (structural rigidity through turgor pressure), chloroplasts (capture sunlight to make sugars: plants are autotrophs) and plasmodesmata (connect neighbouring cells). Animal cells have no cell wall, are heterotrophs and have a distinct mode of early development."},{"mod":"m1","ch":6,"p":"9","q":"What is the cytoskeleton, and what are its main functions in eukaryotic cells?","a":"A network of protein filaments and associated proteins that gives the cell an internal structural framework. It supports the cell's shape and helps with mechanical activities such as moving materials within the cell. It is not part of the endomembrane system."},{"mod":"m1","ch":6,"p":"9","q":"What is the nuclear envelope, and how does it separate the nucleus from the cytoplasm?","a":"The nuclear envelope is the membrane around the nucleus, part of the endomembrane system. It encloses the chromosomes, separating the genetic material and RNA synthesis from the cytoplasm. Pores in it let molecules such as RNA move in and out (covered in more detail in Module 3)."},{"mod":"m1","ch":6,"p":"9","q":"How do ribosomes, the endoplasmic reticulum, and the Golgi apparatus work together to synthesize and process proteins?","a":"Ribosomes make proteins. Ribosomes on the rough ER make proteins that enter the ER, which is involved in protein (and lipid) synthesis. Vesicles carry them to the Golgi apparatus, which modifies and sorts them and sends them on in vesicles to their final destination, inside or outside the cell."},{"mod":"m1","ch":6,"p":"9","q":"What is the primary function of mitochondria? How about lysosomes? How about peroxisomes?","a":"Mitochondria produce most of the ATP, the cell's energy currency. Lysosomes contain enzymes that break down macromolecules (proteins, nucleic acids, lipids, carbohydrates). Peroxisomes break down specific organic molecules such as fatty acids and synthesize others, such as cholesterol and some phospholipids."},{"mod":"m1","ch":7,"p":"10","q":"How do scientists investigate biological questions using model organisms? What are some examples of model organisms?","a":"Model organisms are non-human species studied to understand biology in general, because all cells share basic properties. Examples: E. coli (DNA replication, transcription, translation), Saccharomyces cerevisiae (cell cycle; minimal model eukaryote), Arabidopsis thaliana (flowering plants), Drosophila melanogaster (genetics, development), Caenorhabditis elegans (first animal genome sequenced; fate of every cell known), Mus musculus (model mammal)."},{"mod":"m2","ch":0,"p":"13","q":"What are atoms, and what particles are they made of?","a":"An atom is the basic unit of matter; each element contains only one type of atom. Every atom has a dense central nucleus of positively charged protons and neutral neutrons, with negatively charged electrons moving around the nucleus in orbitals (at most 2 electrons per orbital)."},{"mod":"m2","ch":0,"p":"13","q":"What are valence electrons, and why are they important for the formation of chemical bonds in biological molecules?","a":"Valence electrons are the electrons in an atom's outermost orbitals. Their number determines how many bonds the atom can make (carbon 4, nitrogen 3, oxygen 2, hydrogen 1). Atoms share or transfer valence electrons to combine into molecules."},{"mod":"m2","ch":1,"p":"14–16","q":"How do covalent, ionic, and hydrogen bonds differ in strength and behavior?","a":"Covalent bonds share valence electrons in a molecular orbital; they are the strong bonds of biological systems. Ionic bonds form when one atom takes an electron from another, leaving oppositely charged ions held together by attraction (they come apart in water, as when NaCl dissolves). Hydrogen bonds are weak attractions between an electronegative atom and a hydrogen already covalently bonded to another atom; they constantly break and re-form, as between water molecules."},{"mod":"m2","ch":1,"p":"14","q":"What is electronegativity, and how does it influence bond polarity?","a":"Electronegativity is how strongly an atom attracts shared electrons. If two atoms have the same or nearly the same electronegativity, they share equally: a nonpolar covalent bond. If one is more electronegative (like O in water), it pulls the electrons closer, gaining a slight negative charge and leaving the other slightly positive: a polar covalent bond. A very large difference leads to an ionic bond."},{"mod":"m2","ch":2,"p":"16","q":"How do polar and nonpolar molecules interact differently with water?","a":"Polar and charged molecules are attracted to water's partial charges and dissolve: they are hydrophilic. Nonpolar molecules can't form these attractions, so water molecules hydrogen-bond to each other and push the nonpolar molecules together: they are hydrophobic. Amphipathic molecules have both kinds of region and form structures like micelles and bilayers."},{"mod":"m2","ch":1,"p":"15","q":"What is the difference between a cation and an anion? What happens to sodium chloride (NaCl) when it dissolves in water, and how do the properties of water molecules facilitate this process?","a":"A cation is positively charged (an extra proton relative to its electrons, e.g. Na⁺); an anion is negatively charged (an extra electron, e.g. Cl⁻). When NaCl is added to water, Na⁺ and Cl⁻ are pulled apart and each becomes surrounded by water molecules. Water is polar: the partial negative oxygens face Na⁺ and the partial positive hydrogens face Cl⁻, so NaCl dissolves."},{"mod":"m2","ch":2,"p":"14, 16","q":"What is the structure of a water molecule, and how does it lead to hydrogen bonding?","a":"Water is one oxygen covalently bonded to two hydrogens in an angular (bent) shape. Oxygen is more electronegative, so it carries a slight negative charge and each hydrogen a slight positive charge. The δ⁺ hydrogen of one molecule is attracted to the δ⁻ oxygen of a neighbour: a hydrogen bond."},{"mod":"m2","ch":2,"p":"16","q":"How do the properties of water support life?","a":"Water is the medium of life and the most abundant molecule in organisms. Because it is polar, it dissolves more kinds of molecules than any other liquid, letting reactions happen in solution. Its interactions with hydrophilic, hydrophobic and amphipathic molecules shape structures such as membranes. Its slight dissociation into H⁺ and OH⁻ sets pH, which affects proteins and enzymes."},{"mod":"m2","ch":2,"p":"16","q":"What is the difference between hydrophilic and hydrophobic molecules?","a":"Hydrophilic (\"water loving\") molecules are highly attracted to water; they are polar or charged, like salts and sugars. Hydrophobic (\"water fearing\") molecules are not attracted to water; they are nonpolar, like oils and hydrocarbon chains."},{"mod":"m2","ch":3,"p":"17","q":"What is pH? What type of solution has a pH lower than 7? How about higher than 7?","a":"pH measures the concentration of protons (H⁺) in a solution, on a scale from 0 to 14; pure water is 7. A pH lower than 7 is acidic (more H⁺). Higher than 7 is basic (less H⁺). Each step of 1 is a tenfold change. pH matters for cell function, such as protein structure and enzyme activity."},{"mod":"m2","ch":4,"p":"18","q":"Why is carbon uniquely suited to form the backbone of biological molecules?","a":"Carbon can form four covalent bonds, in many combinations of single and double bonds, with many other elements. It can link into chains or rings, giving a huge variety of molecules. In methane its four bonds point to the corners of a tetrahedron."},{"mod":"m2","ch":5,"p":"19","q":"How are macromolecules assembled from monomers?","a":"Macromolecules are large molecules built from smaller organic molecules. Many are polymers of repeating monomers: amino acids make proteins, nucleotides make nucleic acids, monosaccharides make polysaccharides. Monomers are joined by covalent bonds in condensation reactions, each releasing one water. Specific enzymes speed this up."},{"mod":"m2","ch":5,"p":"19","q":"What is condensation reaction, and how does it differ from hydrolysis?","a":"Condensation (making the chain) joins two monomers with a covalent bond and releases a water molecule; it requires energy. Hydrolysis (breaking the chain) inserts a water molecule across the bond to split it; it releases energy. Both are sped up by enzymes."},{"mod":"m2","ch":4,"p":"20","q":"What are functional groups, and what are some common types found in biological molecules?","a":"Functional groups are groups of atoms attached to carbon chains that give them their own chemical properties. Groups with N, O, P or S are polar: amino (–NH₂, basic, +), amide, carboxyl (–COOH, acidic, –), carbonyl (C=O), hydroxyl (–OH), sulfhydryl (–SH, forms disulfide bonds) and phosphate (–). The methyl group (–CH₃) is nonpolar. They take part in the reactions that build polymers."},{"mod":"m2","ch":8,"p":"26–28","q":"What are the monomers and functions of carbohydrates? How do simple sugars differ from complex carbohydrates?","a":"The monomers are monosaccharides (simple sugars, 5 or 6 carbons, C:H:O about 1:2:1, mostly in ring form; e.g. glucose, galactose, fructose). Carbohydrates are a major energy source and also structural (cellulose). Simple sugars are single units; complex carbohydrates are many units linked by glycosidic bonds: disaccharides (2, e.g. sucrose), oligosaccharides (a few, often on proteins or lipids) and polysaccharides (many: starch and glycogen for storage, cellulose for structure)."},{"mod":"m2","ch":9,"p":"29–30","q":"What are lipids, and how do their structures affect their functions? How do phospholipids contribute to membrane formation? What is the difference between saturated and unsaturated fatty acids?","a":"Lipids are a diverse group grouped by being hydrophobic; they are not polymers. Triacylglycerols (glycerol + three fatty acids) store energy. Steroids (four fused rings, e.g. cholesterol) sit in animal membranes and make hormones. Phospholipids have a polar phosphate head and two nonpolar tails; being amphipathic, they arrange with heads to water and tails inside, forming membranes. Saturated fatty acids have no C=C double bonds (straight); unsaturated ones have one or more (kinked)."},{"mod":"m2","ch":6,"p":"21–22","q":"How do proteins differ in structure and function? What roles do proteins play in catalysis, structure, transport, and signaling?","a":"Proteins are polymers of amino acids (20 kinds, differing in their R groups) joined by peptide bonds; the order of amino acids determines the protein. They have more functions than any other macromolecule: enzymes catalyze reactions; transport proteins move substances across membranes and in circulation; signalling proteins include hormones, receptors and messengers; others give movement (motor proteins, flagella), support (cytoskeleton, extracellular matrix) and defense (antibodies)."},{"mod":"m2","ch":7,"p":"23–24","q":"What are nucleotides, and what are their components? What is the difference between DNA and RNA?","a":"Nucleotides are the monomers of nucleic acids, with three parts: a nitrogenous base, a five-carbon sugar and one or more phosphate groups. DNA uses deoxyribose (–H on the 2′ carbon) and bases A, G, C, T; it is the genetic material. RNA uses ribose (–OH on the 2′ carbon) and bases A, G, C, U; it works in protein synthesis and gene regulation."},{"mod":"m2","ch":7,"p":"23","q":"How do nitrogenous bases pair in DNA and RNA?","a":"Module 2 introduces the bases; pairing is covered in Module 4. A purine always pairs with a pyrimidine: in DNA, A pairs with T and G pairs with C. In RNA, U takes the place of T, so A pairs with U."},{"mod":"m2","ch":7,"p":"25","q":"How is a DNA strand oriented, and what does 5′ to 3′ mean?","a":"Phosphodiester bonds link the phosphate on the 5′ carbon of one nucleotide to the 3′–OH of the next, giving the strand direction. The chain begins at the 5′ end (with a free phosphate) and new nucleotides are added to the free 3′–OH end, so chains are always built 5′ → 3′."},{"mod":"m2","ch":7,"p":"23","q":"What role do nucleic acids play in storing and transmitting genetic information?","a":"Nucleic acids are information molecules: the order of their nitrogenous bases encodes genetic information. DNA is the genetic material of all cellular organisms and directs protein synthesis; RNA carries out protein synthesis and regulates gene expression."},{"mod":"m2","ch":7,"p":"23","q":"What are the structural differences between purines and pyrimidines?","a":"Purines (adenine and guanine) have a double ring. Pyrimidines (cytosine, thymine and uracil) have a single ring."},{"mod":"m2","ch":7,"p":"24","q":"What is ATP, and why is it important in cellular processes?","a":"ATP (adenosine triphosphate) is a nucleotide: adenine, ribose and three phosphate groups. Cleaving off the terminal phosphate releases stored energy (ATP → ADP + Pᵢ + energy), so ATP transfers energy in metabolism and powers cell work."},{"mod":"m3","ch":3,"p":"33","q":"How does the structure of cellular membranes allow them to fuse together without losing their overall continuity?","a":"Membranes are continuous, unbroken sheets of phospholipid bilayer that enclose compartments. Because the bilayer is held together by hydrophobic interactions rather than fixed bonds, and the lipids can move, two bilayers can merge into one and phospholipids spontaneously rearrange to close any gap. That is why a vesicle can fuse with the plasma membrane (or bud from the ER) without leaving a hole, and why membranes are self-healing."},{"mod":"m3","ch":3,"p":"33","q":"What are the various functions of membranes?","a":"1. Compartmentalization (compartments have different contents and activities). 2. A selectively permeable barrier that prevents unrestricted exchange of molecules. 3. Transporting solutes across the membrane. 4. A scaffold for biochemical activities. 5. Responding to external signals (signal transduction). 6. Intercellular interaction with neighbouring cells. 7. Energy transduction, converting one form of energy into another."},{"mod":"m3","ch":3,"p":"34","q":"What is the structure of a phospholipid? How do phospholipids arrange themselves?","a":"A phospholipid has a glycerol backbone attached to a polar phosphate-containing head group and two nonpolar fatty acid tails. In water, the hydrophilic heads face the water and the hydrophobic tails hide from it. Depending on their shape they form micelles (single layer sphere) or bilayers. Phospholipids added to water spontaneously form closed bilayers called liposomes, and the cell membrane is a phospholipid bilayer with tails inside and heads on both surfaces."},{"mod":"m3","ch":3,"p":"34","q":"How does the amphipathic nature of phospholipids contribute to membrane formation?","a":"Amphipathic means having both a hydrophilic and a hydrophobic region. The polar heads are attracted to water while the nonpolar tails are pushed together away from it. So phospholipids arrange themselves spontaneously into a bilayer with the tails in the middle and the heads facing the watery cytosol and extracellular fluid. No energy is needed, and the bilayer reseals itself if disturbed."},{"mod":"m3","ch":3,"p":"35","q":"How can membrane fluidity be affected? What are lipid rafts?","a":"Lipids move laterally within the membrane, and how freely depends on the tails. Longer fatty acid tails make the membrane less fluid. Fewer carbon–carbon double bonds (more saturated, straighter tails that pack tightly) make it less fluid; more double bonds (kinks) make it more fluid. Cholesterol in animal membranes can increase or decrease fluidity depending on temperature. Lipid rafts are discrete regions of the membrane where certain lipids and proteins gather together."},{"mod":"m3","ch":2,"p":"36","q":"What is the fluid mosaic model of membrane structure?","a":"The core of the membrane is a lipid bilayer that is fluid: its molecules can move laterally. Embedded in and attached to it is a mosaic (mixture) of different molecules: lipids, proteins and carbohydrates. Membranes are dynamic structures whose components are mobile and can come together in transient interactions."},{"mod":"m3","ch":2,"p":"36","q":"What types of proteins are found in cell membranes, and what are their functions? How about membrane carbohydrates?","a":"Integral membrane proteins are permanently associated with the membrane and can span the whole bilayer (transmembrane proteins). Peripheral membrane proteins are temporarily attached to the bilayer surface or to integral proteins by noncovalent bonds, on the cytoplasmic or extracellular side. Their functions: transporters that move ions and molecules, receptors that receive signals, enzymes that catalyze reactions, and anchors that attach to other proteins. Membrane carbohydrates all face outward and are covalently linked to proteins (glycoproteins) or lipids (glycolipids)."},{"mod":"m3","ch":5,"p":"43–46","q":"What are the differences between passive and active transport?","a":"Passive transport moves substances down their concentration gradient (high to low) by diffusion, either directly through the bilayer or through a protein, and needs no energy input. Active transport moves substances against their concentration gradient (low to high) using a carrier protein and a source of energy: ATP directly (primary) or an existing electrochemical gradient (secondary)."},{"mod":"m3","ch":5,"p":"43","q":"What is diffusion, and how does it move substances across membranes?","a":"Diffusion is the spontaneous net movement of molecules from an area of high concentration to an area of low concentration. Net movement stops when there is no concentration gradient, though random motion continues both ways. Small nonpolar molecules (O₂, CO₂, lipids) and small uncharged polar molecules like water can diffuse directly across the bilayer: simple diffusion."},{"mod":"m3","ch":5,"p":"44","q":"What is facilitated diffusion, and what role do channel and carrier proteins play?","a":"Facilitated diffusion is movement down the concentration gradient through a protein transporter. Channel proteins are integral proteins that surround an aqueous pore, are selective for specific molecules, and can be gated open or closed. Carrier proteins bind a specific molecule on one side, change conformation, and release it on the other side, where it diffuses away down its gradient."},{"mod":"m3","ch":4,"p":"45","q":"How does osmosis differ from simple diffusion? What happens to cells in hypotonic, isotonic, and hypertonic solutions?","a":"Osmosis is the diffusion of water across a semi-permeable membrane, from higher water concentration (lower solute) to lower water concentration (higher solute). Isotonic: equal on both sides, no net water movement. Hypertonic (more solute outside): water leaves, the cell shrinks; animal cells shrivel (crenation), plant cells pull away from the wall (plasmolysis). Hypotonic (less solute outside): water enters, the cell swells; animal cells can burst (lysis; hemolysis in red blood cells), plant cells push against the wall (turgor)."},{"mod":"m3","ch":6,"p":"46","q":"What is active transport, and how does it use energy? How does the sodium-potassium pump work?","a":"Active transport moves a substance against its gradient using a carrier that changes conformation, so movement is one-way. The Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase (in animal cells) is primary active transport. Na⁺ ions bind on the inside; ATP is hydrolyzed and the pump is phosphorylated; the pump changes shape and releases Na⁺ outside; K⁺ binds on the outside; the phosphate is removed; the pump snaps back and releases K⁺ inside. Your notes give the ratio as 3 Na⁺ out and 2 K⁺ in per cycle, both against their gradients."},{"mod":"m3","ch":7,"p":"47–48","q":"What is secondary active transport, and how is it different from primary active transport?","a":"Primary active transport uses the chemical energy of ATP directly (e.g. the Na⁺/K⁺ pump or a proton pump). Secondary active transport (co-transport) uses an electrochemical gradient that a primary pump built: an ion moving down its gradient drives another molecule against its gradient. Symporters move both in the same direction (Na⁺ and glucose); antiporters move them in opposite directions (Na⁺ and H⁺). In intestinal cells, Na⁺ entering at the apical membrane drives glucose in; glucose then leaves at the basal membrane by facilitated diffusion."},{"mod":"m3","ch":6,"p":"47","q":"What is an electrochemical gradient?","a":"A gradient across a membrane with both a chemical part (a difference in concentration) and an electrical part (a difference in charge). Pumping ions such as Na⁺ or H⁺ builds one; the inside of the cell is negative relative to the outside. Its stored energy can drive secondary active transport."},{"mod":"m3","ch":0,"p":"37–38","q":"What is the endomembrane system? Which are found in a typical animal cell? How about a plant cell?","a":"The endomembrane system is the set of interconnected membranes and organelles, linked physically or by vesicles: nuclear envelope, ER, Golgi apparatus, lysosomes, vesicles and the plasma membrane. A typical animal cell has ER, Golgi, lysosomes, mitochondria and a cytoskeleton. A plant cell has the same plus a cell wall for support, vacuoles that maintain turgor pressure, and chloroplasts that capture light energy."},{"mod":"m3","ch":8,"p":"49–50","q":"What is endocytosis, and how does it allow cells to internalize substances? What is the difference between phagocytosis, pinocytosis, and receptor-mediated endocytosis? What are the steps for each process?","a":"In endocytosis the membrane folds inward around material, the pocket pinches off, and the material ends up inside a new vesicle. Phagocytosis: a large particle binds the surface, the membrane surrounds it to form a phagosome, which fuses with a lysosome; enzymes digest the contents (nutrition or defense, e.g. macrophages and neutrophils). Pinocytosis (cell drinking): extracellular fluid is taken in with no recognition, forming small vesicles that don't need a lysosome. Receptor-mediated endocytosis: specific molecules bind receptors concentrated in a coated pit, clathrin on the cytoplasmic side pulls it in as a coated vesicle, and the material goes to early then late endosomes."},{"mod":"m3","ch":8,"p":"49","q":"What is exocytosis, and how does it relate to secretion?","a":"Exocytosis is when a vesicle fuses with the plasma membrane and releases its contents outside the cell. Vesicles that bud off the endomembrane system (from the Golgi) carry secreted proteins this way, so exocytosis is how cells secrete substances. It also adds the vesicle's membrane and proteins to the plasma membrane."},{"mod":"m3","ch":0,"p":"38","q":"What is the structure and function of the nucleus? What is the nuclear envelope, and what is the role of nuclear pores?","a":"The nucleus holds the cell's DNA (chromosomes) and is where RNA is made; the nucleolus inside makes ribosome parts. The nuclear envelope is a double membrane (inner and outer) that separates the nucleus from the cytoplasm and is continuous with the ER. Nuclear pores are protein openings in the envelope that let molecules such as RNA and proteins move in and out."},{"mod":"m3","ch":0,"p":"38–39","q":"What are the roles of the rough and smooth endoplasmic reticulum?","a":"The rough ER has ribosomes on its cytosolic face and makes proteins that go into membranes, are secreted, or go to the Golgi and lysosomes. The smooth ER lacks ribosomes; it makes lipids, and parts of it bud off as vesicles that carry material onward to the Golgi."},{"mod":"m3","ch":0,"p":"39","q":"What is the Golgi apparatus, and what is its role in the cell?","a":"The Golgi is a stack of flattened membrane sacs (cisternae), not attached to the ER, that receives vesicles from the ER. It modifies proteins and lipids (for example adding sugars), then sorts and ships them in vesicles to the plasma membrane, to lysosomes, or out of the cell. It is the cell's sorting station."},{"mod":"m3","ch":0,"p":"40","q":"What are lysosomes and what is their function?","a":"Lysosomes are specialized vesicles derived from the Golgi. They contain enzymes (made in the RER, sorted in the Golgi) that degrade damaged or unneeded proteins, nucleic acids, lipids and complex carbohydrates. A proton pump in their membrane keeps the inside at about pH 5."},{"mod":"m3","ch":1,"p":"41","q":"What organelles are not part of the endomembrane system?","a":"Mitochondria and chloroplasts are membranous organelles that are not part of the endomembrane system. They are semi-autonomous: they grow and divide on their own and have their own circular genomes. (Peroxisomes, ribosomes and the cytoskeleton are also outside it.)"},{"mod":"m3","ch":1,"p":"41","q":"How does the structure of mitochondria relate to their function?","a":"Mitochondria convert the energy in sugars into ATP. They have an outer membrane and a highly folded inner membrane; the folds (cristae) give a large surface area for the reactions that make ATP. The inner membrane encloses the matrix, which holds enzymes, ribosomes and the mitochondrion's own DNA."},{"mod":"m3","ch":1,"p":"41–42","q":"What is the role of chloroplasts in plant cells? What is their structure?","a":"Chloroplasts capture light energy and convert it into chemical energy (sugars) by photosynthesis. They have a double membrane (outer and inner) and an internal membrane system of flattened sacs called thylakoids, which hold pigments such as chlorophyll. Thylakoids are stacked into grana; the space inside a thylakoid is the lumen, and the fluid around them is the stroma."},{"mod":"m3","ch":1,"p":"42","q":"How do mitochondria and chloroplasts support the endosymbiotic theory?","a":"Both resemble bacteria in organization and biochemistry: they have a double membrane, their own circular DNA and ribosomes, and they grow and divide on their own. Chloroplasts resemble cyanobacteria (photosynthetic bacteria) and mitochondria resemble proteobacteria. The theory says each began as a bacterium engulfed by a host cell that kept it as an endosymbiont."},{"mod":"m4","ch":0,"p":"53","q":"How is the structure of DNA linked to its function?","a":"DNA has two jobs: storing genetic information and copying itself to pass that information on. Its sequence of bases stores the information, and complementary base pairing (A–T, G–C) means each strand can act as a guide for building a new partner strand, so the information can be copied."},{"mod":"m4","ch":0,"p":"53–54","q":"How do we know DNA is the genetic material? Describe the experiments conducted by Griffith and Avery, MacLeod & McCarty.","a":"Griffith (1928) used two strains of Streptococcus pneumoniae. Live virulent bacteria killed mice; nonvirulent ones did not; heat-killed virulent bacteria did not; but heat-killed virulent mixed with live nonvirulent bacteria killed the mouse. Something in the debris transformed the nonvirulent cells, but the molecule wasn't identified. Avery, MacLeod and McCarty (1944) took an extract of DNA, RNA and protein from heat-killed virulent bacteria and treated it with DNase, RNase or protease. Only DNase stopped transformation, showing DNA is the transforming molecule."},{"mod":"m4","ch":1,"p":"55, 60","q":"What are the building blocks of DNA and RNA? What is a nucleotide vs. nucleoside? What are the bases in DNA? RNA?","a":"Both are polymers of nucleotides. A nucleotide has three parts: a five-carbon sugar (deoxyribose in DNA, ribose in RNA), a base attached to the 1′ carbon, and one or more phosphates. A nucleoside is just base plus sugar. DNA bases: A, T, C, G. RNA bases: A, U, C, G (uracil replaces thymine)."},{"mod":"m4","ch":1,"p":"55","q":"What is a phosphodiester bond? How does one end of a strand of DNA differ from the other? Where are bases added to a growing chain?","a":"A phosphodiester bond links the 3′ carbon of one sugar to the 5′ carbon of the next through a phosphate, forming the sugar-phosphate backbone. One end of the strand has a free 5′ phosphate, the other a free 3′ hydroxyl (polarity). New nucleotides can only be added to the 3′–OH, so strands grow 5′→3′."},{"mod":"m4","ch":4,"p":"60","q":"How do DNA and RNA differ in structure and function?","a":"DNA uses deoxyribose and thymine, has a 5′ monophosphate, is very large and double-stranded, and stores genetic information. RNA uses ribose (with a 2′ –OH, making it less stable) and uracil, has a 5′ triphosphate, is smaller and usually single-stranded, can fold into complex shapes, and carries out the information (mRNA carries a gene's code to the ribosome)."},{"mod":"m4","ch":2,"p":"56","q":"What is the significance of Watson & Crick? How about Franklin & Wilkins? How about Chargaff?","a":"Watson and Crick built the 3-D double helix model. They based it on Rosalind Franklin and Maurice Wilkins's X-ray crystallography of DNA and on Erwin Chargaff's finding that %A = %T and %C = %G."},{"mod":"m4","ch":2,"p":"56–57","q":"Why is there specificity of base pairing? What are complementary base pairs?","a":"Specificity comes from hydrogen bonding: A and T form two hydrogen bonds, C and G form three, and a purine always pairs with a pyrimidine. A–T and G–C are complementary pairs: knowing one strand tells you the sequence of the other."},{"mod":"m4","ch":2,"p":"57","q":"What is the role of hydrogen bonding in stabilizing the DNA double helix? How about base stacking?","a":"Hydrogen bonds are weak individually but together hold the two strands together, and because they are weak the DNA can unzip down the middle. Base stacking is the interaction between neighbouring bases on the same strand; it also stabilizes the helix."},{"mod":"m4","ch":3,"p":"58","q":"What is the central dogma of molecular biology? What is a genome? What is gene expression?","a":"Central dogma: DNA is transcribed into RNA, which is translated into protein (reverse transcription is an exception). A genome is the genetic material of an organism (or compartment, like the mitochondrial genome). Gene expression is the production of a functional gene product, such as a protein; genes can be turned on or off, and expression is regulated."},{"mod":"m4","ch":3,"p":"58–59","q":"What is transcription, and what is its overall purpose? Where does it occur in prokaryotes? In eukaryotes?","a":"Transcription makes an RNA copy of a DNA sequence, using DNA as the template, so the gene's information can be used (for example, mRNA directing protein synthesis). In prokaryotes it happens in the cytoplasm; in eukaryotes, in the nucleus."},{"mod":"m4","ch":4,"p":"60","q":"What is RNA? What are the similarity and differences between RNA & DNA?","a":"RNA is a polymer of ribonucleotides. Like DNA it has a sugar-phosphate backbone and polarity (5′ phosphate end, 3′ hydroxyl end). Differences: ribose vs deoxyribose, U vs T (A pairs with U), 5′ triphosphate vs monophosphate, shorter, mostly single-stranded, and can fold into complex shapes."},{"mod":"m4","ch":5,"p":"61","q":"What is needed for transcription to occur? What is the template strand of DNA? What are ribonucleotide triphosphates and why are they needed for transcription?","a":"A DNA template, RNA polymerase, and ribonucleotide triphosphates. The template strand is the DNA strand that RNA polymerase reads; the RNA is complementary to it. Ribonucleotide triphosphates (ATP, GTP, CTP, UTP) are the building blocks, and their phosphates provide the energy for the synthesis reaction."},{"mod":"m4","ch":5,"p":"62","q":"What enzyme is responsible for synthesizing RNA from a DNA template in prokaryotes? How about in eukaryotes?","a":"RNA polymerase. Prokaryotes use a single type of RNA polymerase for all transcription. Eukaryotes have three; RNA polymerase II makes mRNA."},{"mod":"m4","ch":5,"p":"61–62","q":"How does transcription take place? What is a promoter, and how does it initiate transcription? How does RNA polymerase identify the template strand?","a":"A region of DNA unwinds and one strand is used as the template; RNA grows 5′→3′ as the template is read 3′→5′, in three stages (initiation, elongation, termination). The promoter is a conserved double-stranded DNA sequence where transcription factors and RNA polymerase bind, which sets where and in which direction transcription starts (in eukaryotes, often a TATA box about 25 bp upstream of the start). The promoter's orientation decides which strand is the template, so it can differ from gene to gene."},{"mod":"m4","ch":5,"p":"62–63","q":"What are the differences in transcription initiation between prokaryotes and eukaryotes? What are general transcription factors? What are enhancers? What is the mediator complex? What role do sigma factors play in bacterial transcription?","a":"In bacteria, a sigma factor associates with RNA polymerase and directs it to bind specific promoters. In eukaryotes, at least six general transcription factors bind the promoter; transcriptional activator proteins bind enhancer sequences (often far away); the mediator complex associates with the general transcription factors and RNA Pol II, and DNA looping brings the activators into contact with it, recruiting Pol II and starting transcription."},{"mod":"m4","ch":6,"p":"63–64","q":"What is the process for transcription elongation? What are the steps of the polymerization reaction? How is transcription terminated?","a":"RNA polymerase unwinds the DNA and adds complementary ribonucleotides, which enter through a channel, to the growing RNA. Each step: the polymerase accepts an incoming ribonucleoside triphosphate only if it base-pairs correctly; the 3′–OH of the growing strand attacks the innermost phosphate of the triphosphate; pyrophosphate is released and the nucleotide is attached. Transcription continues until RNA polymerase reaches a terminator sequence, and the RNA is released (in eukaryotes, polyadenylation also plays a role in termination)."},{"mod":"m4","ch":7,"p":"65","q":"What is polycistronic mRNA? What type of cell produces primary transcripts of this type?","a":"An mRNA that carries the information for more than one gene. Prokaryotes make them."},{"mod":"m4","ch":8,"p":"65","q":"What modifications occur to pre-mRNA in eukaryotes before it exits the nucleus?","a":"RNA processing: a 5′ cap is added, introns are removed by RNA splicing, and a poly(A) tail is added to the 3′ end."},{"mod":"m4","ch":8,"p":"66","q":"What are introns and exons, and where are they found?","a":"Both are found in eukaryotic genes and their primary transcripts. Introns are sequences cut out of the primary transcript during splicing; exons are the sequences left intact and joined together in the mature mRNA."},{"mod":"m4","ch":8,"p":"66","q":"What is the function of the 5′ cap and the 3′ poly-A tail?","a":"The 5′ cap (7-methylguanosine joined by a 5′–5′ bond) protects the mRNA from exonucleases, adds stability, and is needed for the ribosome to recognize the mRNA. The poly(A) tail (about 250 adenines) helps with transcription termination, export of the mRNA to the cytoplasm, and protection from exonucleases."},{"mod":"m4","ch":8,"p":"66","q":"What is RNA splicing, and what is the end result? What is alternative splicing?","a":"RNA splicing cuts the introns out of the primary transcript and joins the exons, giving a mature mRNA. Alternative splicing is when one gene's primary transcript is spliced in different ways, so a single gene can make different but related proteins in different cells."},{"mod":"m5","ch":4,"p":"75","q":"What is the central dogma of molecular biology?","a":"DNA is transcribed into mRNA (DNA is the template), and mRNA is translated into protein: the order of bases in the mRNA specifies the order of amino acids. Translation is the final stage."},{"mod":"m5","ch":2,"p":"71","q":"What defines a polypeptide chain?","a":"A polymer of amino acids connected by peptide bonds, used synonymously with protein. It has direction: a free amino group at one end (the N-terminus) and a free carboxyl group at the other (the C-terminus), with an N–C–C repeating backbone."},{"mod":"m5","ch":3,"p":"72–74","q":"What are the four levels of protein structure and how does each contribute to a protein's overall shape?","a":"Primary (1°): the linear sequence of amino acids, N to C; it determines all the other levels. Secondary (2°): α helices and β sheets formed by H-bonds between backbone C=O and N–H groups. Tertiary (3°): the 3-D fold of one whole polypeptide, set by where hydrophobic and hydrophilic R groups sit and by H-bonds, hydrophobic interactions, ionic bonds and disulfide bonds between R groups; usually the functional form. Quaternary (4°): how two or more subunits fit together (homodimer or heterodimer), held by the same bonds as 3°."},{"mod":"m5","ch":3,"p":"72, 74","q":"How does the unique sequence of amino acids determine the final shape of a protein?","a":"The sequence fixes the order of R groups, which alternate sides of the chain. Their properties decide which parts form helices and sheets, which R groups bury themselves away from water, which pair up as ionic or disulfide bonds, and so the final fold. The primary structure governs the secondary and tertiary structure."},{"mod":"m5","ch":2,"p":"71","q":"How do peptide bonds form between amino acids?","a":"By a dehydration reaction: the carboxyl group of one amino acid reacts with the amino group of the next, forming a C–N peptide bond and releasing one molecule of water. R groups are not involved."},{"mod":"m5","ch":3,"p":"72–73","q":"How do primary and secondary structures differ in proteins?","a":"Primary structure is just the sequence of amino acids joined by peptide bonds. Secondary structure is the local shape of parts of the backbone (α helix or β sheet), held by hydrogen bonds between backbone functional groups of nearby amino acids, without involving R groups."},{"mod":"m5","ch":3,"p":"73","q":"What types of chemical bonds stabilize secondary structures like α-helices and β-sheets?","a":"Hydrogen bonds between backbone groups. In an α helix, each carbonyl (C=O) bonds to the amide (N–H) of the amino acid four positions along. In a β sheet, carbonyl groups in one strand bond to amide groups in a neighbouring strand of the same polypeptide (parallel or antiparallel)."},{"mod":"m5","ch":3,"p":"74","q":"Why is tertiary structure important for protein function?","a":"Tertiary structure is usually the protein's functional form. The overall 3-D shape creates features such as an enzyme's active site, and places R groups on the outside that decide how the protein interacts with other molecules or proteins. Structure determines function."},{"mod":"m5","ch":3,"p":"74","q":"How does quaternary structure arise and what is its biological significance?","a":"Many proteins are made of more than one polypeptide chain (subunit). Their arrangement is the quaternary structure, held by the same bonds as tertiary structure (H-bonds, hydrophobic interactions, ionic bonds, disulfide bonds). Subunits can be identical (homodimer) or different (heterodimer); the assembled complex is what works."},{"mod":"m5","ch":0,"p":"70, 74","q":"What roles do hydrophobic and ionic interactions play in maintaining protein conformation?","a":"Hydrophobic R groups cluster in the interior away from water, held by weak van der Waals forces, forming a core that holds the fold. Charged R groups (basic +, acidic −) form ionic bonds with each other or other charged molecules, and stay on the surface. Both help hold tertiary and quaternary structure."},{"mod":"m5","ch":3,"p":"72, 74","q":"How can a single amino acid substitution drastically change a protein's function?","a":"Because primary structure determines the higher levels. A new R group with different properties (for example a hydrophobic one where a charged one was, or losing a cysteine cross-link or a glycine's flexibility) can change how the chain folds, and the changed shape can disrupt an active site or how the protein interacts with others."},{"mod":"m5","ch":0,"p":"69–71","q":"How does the amino acid sequence determine protein function? What are the major groupings of amino acids?","a":"The sequence sets the structure, and structure sets function. Amino acids are grouped by their R groups: hydrophobic (nonpolar, e.g. Ala, Val, Leu, Phe), hydrophilic polar (e.g. Ser, Thr, Asn, Gln), basic (+, Lys, Arg, His), acidic (−, Asp, Glu), and the special three: glycine (flexible), proline (rigid), cysteine (disulfide bonds)."},{"mod":"m5","ch":3,"p":"70, 74","q":"How do hydrophobic and hydrophilic interactions influence protein folding?","a":"As the chain folds in water, hydrophobic R groups are pushed together into the interior, and hydrophilic R groups stay on the outer surface where they can H-bond with water or form ionic bonds. This spatial distribution is one of the main things that determines tertiary structure."},{"mod":"m5","ch":4,"p":"76, 78","q":"What is the genetic code? What is a codon? What is an anti-codon? Describe directionality of each?","a":"The genetic code is the set of 64 codons that specify 20 amino acids plus stop; it is redundant (degenerate). A codon is three adjacent nucleotides in mRNA, read 5′ → 3′ as nonoverlapping triplets from the reading frame set by AUG. An anticodon is three bases in the tRNA's anticodon loop that pair with the codon antiparallel: the codon's first (5′) base pairs with the anticodon's last (3′) base, so 5′-AUG-3′ pairs with 3′-UAC-5′."},{"mod":"m5","ch":5,"p":"77","q":"How do tRNAs become charged with amino acids?","a":"Enzymes called aminoacyl tRNA synthetases each bind one uncharged tRNA and its matching amino acid and attach the amino acid to the 3′ end (the 3′–OH of the CCA end). A tRNA with its amino acid is charged; without it, uncharged. The synthetases are very accurate."},{"mod":"m5","ch":5,"p":"75–76","q":"What role do ribosomes play in protein synthesis?","a":"Ribosomes are the protein factories where translation happens: complexes of rRNA and protein with a small and a large subunit. The mRNA runs through them; the large subunit has the A, P and E sites for tRNAs and the peptidyl transferase centre (rRNA) that forms peptide bonds. They move along the mRNA 5′ to 3′, reading one codon at a time."},{"mod":"m5","ch":6,"p":"78","q":"How does mRNA direct the sequence of amino acids during translation?","a":"The mRNA is read codon by codon, 5′ to 3′, starting at AUG. Each codon is matched by the complementary anticodon of a charged tRNA, so each tRNA delivers the amino acid that codon specifies. The order of codons becomes the order of amino acids."},{"mod":"m5","ch":4,"p":"58, 75","q":"What are the main differences between transcription and translation?","a":"Transcription makes RNA from a DNA template, stays in the language of nucleic acids, uses RNA polymerase, and in eukaryotes happens in the nucleus. Translation makes a polypeptide from mRNA, changes the language from nucleotides to amino acids, uses ribosomes, tRNAs and synthetases, and happens in the cytosol."},{"mod":"m5","ch":7,"p":"79–80","q":"How is translation initiated in prokaryotes and eukaryotes?","a":"Eukaryotes: initiation factors and the small subunit bind at the 5′ cap, scan the mRNA for the first AUG, and the Met-tRNA pairs with it; then the large subunit joins and initiation factors are released. Prokaryotes: mRNA has no cap; the ribosome binds internal Shine-Dalgarno sequences (5′-AGGAGGU-3′) and starts at the next AUG, so one polycistronic mRNA can make several polypeptides."},{"mod":"m5","ch":6,"p":"79","q":"What are the steps of translation elongation?","a":"A charged tRNA enters the A site and pairs with the next codon. The growing chain is transferred from the P-site tRNA to the amino acid on the A-site tRNA, forming a peptide bond (catalyzed by the peptidyl transferase centre, made of rRNA). The ribosome shifts one codon toward 3′: the empty tRNA moves to the E site and leaves, the chain-bearing tRNA moves to P, and the A site is free for the next tRNA."},{"mod":"m5","ch":6,"p":"80","q":"How is translation terminated?","a":"When a stop codon (UAA, UAG or UGA) reaches the A site, a release factor binds there instead of a tRNA. The bond between the polypeptide and the last tRNA breaks, creating the C-terminus, and the polypeptide is released from the ribosome."},{"mod":"m5","ch":3,"p":"74","q":"How does a protein's shape relate to its biological activity?","a":"Structure determines function. The 3-D shape positions the residues that form an active site and the exterior R groups that decide what the protein can bind or interact with. If the shape is lost or altered, the activity is lost or altered."},{"mod":"m5","ch":9,"p":"78, 72","q":"How would a DNA mutation alter protein synthesis and protein function?","a":"A changed base changes the mRNA codon. Because the code is redundant, the codon may still code for the same amino acid (no change). It may code for a different amino acid, which can change folding and function. Or it may become a stop codon, so translation ends early and the protein is cut short."},{"mod":"m5","ch":5,"p":"76–77","q":"What is the role of tRNA in translation?","a":"tRNAs are adaptors. Each carries a specific amino acid on its 3′ CCA end (attached by its synthetase) and has an anticodon that pairs with a codon on the mRNA, bringing the right amino acid to the ribosome's A site."},{"mod":"m5","ch":6,"p":"77–79","q":"How does the ribosome ensure that amino acids are added in the correct order during translation?","a":"Two matching steps. Aminoacyl tRNA synthetases accurately attach the right amino acid to each tRNA. Then in the A site, only a tRNA whose anticodon base pairs correctly with the codon is accepted. The ribosome moves exactly one codon (three nucleotides) each time, keeping the reading frame."},{"mod":"m5","ch":8,"p":"81–82","q":"Why are some proteins secreted while others remain in the cytoplasm?","a":"All translation starts on ribosomes in the cytosol. A protein with no signal sequence stays in the cytosol. A protein with an amino-terminal ER signal sequence is recognized by SRP, which halts translation and docks the ribosome on the rough ER; the protein is made into the ER lumen and can then be secreted (or stay in the endomembrane system, or in the membrane if it has a signal-anchor sequence)."},{"mod":"m5","ch":8,"p":"81–82","q":"How do signal sequences direct proteins to specific organelles?","a":"Signal sequences are address labels within the amino acid sequence. No signal: stays in the cytosol. Amino-terminal signal: to chloroplasts or mitochondria. Internal signal: to the nucleus. Amino-terminal ER signal: bound by SRP, which brings the ribosome to the SRP receptor on the rough ER; a signal-anchor sequence leaves the protein in the membrane."},{"mod":"m5","ch":8,"p":"66 (Module 4), 81","q":"How does alternative splicing influence the diversity of proteins produced by a single gene?","a":"One gene's primary transcript can be spliced in different ways, keeping different combinations of exons, so one gene can produce different but related mRNAs and proteins in different cells. RNA processing is also one of the levels where gene expression is regulated."},{"mod":"m6","ch":1,"p":"90–91","q":"Give an overview of metabolism: what it is, how organisms obtain energy, and its two branches.","a":"Metabolism is the chemical processes that occur in a living organism to maintain life, including building up and breaking down chemical compounds so energy can be harnessed or released. Organisms get energy from the sun (phototrophs) or from chemical compounds (chemotrophs), and carbon from CO₂ (autotrophs) or from organic compounds (heterotrophs). Many reactions are linked into metabolic pathways, where the products of one reaction are the reactants of the next. The two branches are anabolism, which builds molecules from smaller units and requires ATP, and catabolism, which breaks molecules down and produces ATP."},{"mod":"m6","ch":2,"p":"91–92","q":"Contrast kinetic and potential energy, and explain how chemical energy fits in.","a":"Potential energy is stored energy not associated with movement, like a ball at the top of a staircase; kinetic energy is the energy of motion, like the ball bouncing down. Energy can be converted from one form to the other. Chemical energy is a form of potential energy held in the bonds between atoms in a molecule. Bond strength and potential energy are linked: strong bonds hold less potential energy and weak bonds hold more."},{"mod":"m6","ch":4,"p":"93","q":"State the first and second laws of thermodynamics and explain what they mean for living systems.","a":"The first law: energy can be neither created nor destroyed, only converted from one form to another, so total energy before a transformation equals total energy after. The second law: events proceed from a higher to a lower energy state, and these spontaneous events need no external energy. Each transformation reduces usable energy because it is not 100% efficient; some is released, often as heat, increasing entropy. So cells must keep taking in energy, and part of every transfer, for example from food to movement, is lost as heat."},{"mod":"m6","ch":5,"p":"94–95","q":"What happens in a chemical reaction, and how does ΔG tell you whether it releases or requires energy?","a":"In a chemical reaction atoms keep their identity but the bonds linking them change, as in CO₂ + H₂O ⇌ H₂CO₃, and the concentrations of reactants and products influence whether it proceeds forward or backward. Gibbs free energy (G) is the energy available to do work, and ΔG = G(products) − G(reactants). If the products have more free energy (+ΔG) the reaction is endergonic, nonspontaneous and needs an input of energy. If the reactants have more free energy (−ΔG) it is exergonic and spontaneous, and energy is available for other processes. ΔG = ΔH − TΔS, where H is total energy (enthalpy), S is energy lost to entropy and T is absolute temperature in Kelvin."},{"mod":"m6","ch":6,"p":"95–96","q":"Describe the hydrolysis of ATP and how cells use it to drive reactions.","a":"ATP + H₂O → ADP + Pᵢ is exergonic (spontaneous) and releases free energy, ΔG = −7.3 kcal/mol. At physiological pH ATP's phosphate groups are negatively charged and resist one another, and ADP has only two phosphates, so it has lower potential energy than ATP. Cells couple ATP hydrolysis to endergonic reactions so the net ΔG is negative. For example, glucose + Pᵢ → glucose 6-phosphate (+3.3 kcal/mol) coupled with ATP hydrolysis gives glucose + ATP → glucose 6-phosphate + ADP, ΔG = −4 kcal/mol, with Pᵢ shared between the two reactions."},{"mod":"m6","ch":7,"p":"95–97","q":"How do enzymes catalyze reactions?","a":"All reactions need activation energy: reactants must have enough kinetic energy to break certain bonds and reach the transition state. Enzymes are proteins that catalyze reactions by lowering this activation energy barrier, so the reaction proceeds faster; ΔG is the same with and without the enzyme. The active site is formed by amino acids, often far apart in the sequence, that folding brings together. It binds the substrate, stabilizes the transition state and converts the substrate to product: E + S ⇌ ES ⇌ EP ⇌ E + P. Both anabolic and catabolic reactions are catalyzed this way."},{"mod":"m6","ch":8,"p":"97","q":"How is enzyme activity regulated?","a":"Enzyme activity can be reduced by inhibitors, which are reversible or irreversible, and increased by activators. A competitive inhibitor binds the active site and competes with the substrate; a noncompetitive inhibitor binds another site and changes the enzyme's shape. Both reduce the reaction rate. Because energy is needed for reactions, many pathways are regulated by feedback inhibition, where the final product inhibits the first step. For example, isoleucine is an allosteric inhibitor of threonine dehydratase, which keeps balance in the cell and conserves energy."},{"mod":"m6","ch":1,"p":"89, 91","q":"Learning outcome: describe energy flow in anabolic and catabolic metabolism.","a":"Catabolism breaks macromolecules such as carbohydrates, proteins, lipids and nucleic acids into subunits such as sugars, amino acids, fatty acids and nucleotides, and the energy released is captured as ATP (ADP + Pᵢ → ATP). Anabolism builds those macromolecules from subunits, which requires an input of energy, supplied by ATP hydrolysis (ATP + H₂O → ADP + Pᵢ). So energy flows from catabolism to anabolism through ATP. Catabolic reactions are exergonic and anabolic reactions are endergonic."},{"mod":"m6","ch":3,"p":"89, 91–92","q":"Learning outcome: contrast kinetic and potential energy and relate the structure of ATP to its role in cellular energy interactions.","a":"Kinetic energy is energy of motion; potential energy is stored, including chemical energy in bonds. ATP is adenine, ribose and three phosphate groups. Its energy is held in the bonds connecting the phosphates: the phosphates are negatively charged and repel one another, so these bonds are weak and hold a lot of potential energy. Breaking the last bond (ATP → ADP + Pᵢ) releases that energy for cellular work such as synthesis, transport and movement, which makes ATP a readily usable energy currency."},{"mod":"m6","ch":4,"p":"89, 93, 95","q":"Learning outcome: explain the laws of thermodynamics as they apply to biological systems.","a":"Cells cannot create energy (first law), so they must take it in, from sunlight or chemical compounds, and convert it into forms like ATP and movement. Every conversion loses some usable energy, often as heat, increasing entropy (second law), so energy flows through living things rather than cycling. Spontaneous processes run from higher to lower free energy, and ΔG = ΔH − TΔS describes how much energy is available to do work."},{"mod":"m6","ch":7,"p":"89, 94–97","q":"Learning outcome: describe the types of energy involved in chemical reactions and the role of enzymes.","a":"Chemical reactions involve chemical (potential) energy in bonds and kinetic energy of the reactants. The free energy difference (ΔG) decides whether a reaction is exergonic (releases energy) or endergonic (requires energy). Reactants also need enough kinetic energy to overcome the activation energy barrier. Enzymes lower that barrier by binding substrates in their active site and stabilizing the transition state, which speeds the reaction without changing ΔG, and their activity is controlled by inhibitors, activators and feedback inhibition."},{"mod":"m7","ch":0,"p":"100–101","q":"Give an overview of cellular respiration: its four stages and where each happens.","a":"Cellular respiration converts the energy in fuel molecules (carbohydrates, lipids and proteins) into ATP, which lets the cell do work. Stage 1, glycolysis, breaks glucose down to pyruvate in the cytosol. Stage 2, pyruvate oxidation, converts pyruvate to acetyl-CoA and CO₂. Stage 3, the citric acid cycle, breaks acetyl-CoA down, releasing CO₂, a little energy and electron carriers. Stage 4, oxidative phosphorylation, uses the electrons from those carriers in the electron transport chain to make ATP. Stages 2 to 4 happen in the mitochondria."},{"mod":"m7","ch":2,"p":"103–105","q":"Describe glycolysis and its three phases.","a":"Glycolysis is a catabolic pathway of ten reactions that turns one six-carbon glucose into two three-carbon pyruvates, in the cytosol, with or without O₂ (it is considered anaerobic because no O₂ is consumed). Phase 1 (preparatory) uses 2 ATP to add two phosphates, making fructose 1,6-bisphosphate; this traps glucose in the cell and destabilizes it. Phase 2 (cleavage) splits it into glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate and dihydroxyacetone phosphate, which becomes a second G3P. Phase 3 (payoff) makes 2 pyruvate, 2 NADH and 4 ATP, for a net gain of 2 ATP and 2 NADH."},{"mod":"m7","ch":3,"p":"106–107","q":"Describe pyruvate oxidation.","a":"When O₂ is present, pyruvate enters the mitochondrial matrix. It is oxidized to CO₂ and an acetyl group, and NAD⁺ is reduced to NADH. The acetyl group is transferred to coenzyme A, which carries it to the citric acid cycle as acetyl-CoA. The pyruvate dehydrogenase complex catalyzes these reactions. One pyruvate gives 1 CO₂, 1 NADH and 1 acetyl-CoA, so one glucose gives 2 of each."},{"mod":"m7","ch":4,"p":"107–108","q":"Describe the citric acid cycle and what it produces.","a":"The citric acid cycle (Krebs or TCA cycle) runs in the matrix. Acetyl-CoA (2C) joins oxaloacetate (4C) to form citrate (6C); two oxidation steps each release a CO₂ and make NADH, leaving 4 carbons; later steps make ATP (via GTP, by substrate-level phosphorylation), FADH₂ and another NADH, and regenerate oxaloacetate, which is why it is a cycle. Each turn gives 2 CO₂, 3 NADH, 1 FADH₂ and 1 ATP; one glucose drives two turns. The cycle completes the oxidation of glucose, and its CO₂ is the CO₂ we exhale. Some intermediates, such as α-ketoglutarate, can be used to make amino acids and purine bases."},{"mod":"m7","ch":5,"p":"109–110","q":"Explain oxidative phosphorylation.","a":"NADH and FADH₂ give electrons to the electron transport chain in the inner mitochondrial membrane (NADH to complex I, FADH₂ to complex II). The electrons pass by redox reactions through CoQ, complex III, cytochrome c and complex IV to O₂, the final electron acceptor, which combines with H⁺ to form water. The energy released lets complexes I, III and IV pump protons from the matrix into the intermembrane space, forming a proton gradient that cannot dissipate by diffusion. Protons flow back through ATP synthase: the Fo channel rotates and F1 uses the rotation to make ATP from ADP + Pi. This produces the majority of the cell's ATP."},{"mod":"m7","ch":7,"p":"111","q":"What happens to glucose metabolism under anaerobic conditions?","a":"Without O₂ the electron transport chain has no final acceptor, so the cell relies on glycolysis plus fermentation. In bacteria and animal cells, pyruvate is reduced to lactic acid by NADH (glucose + 2 ADP + 2 Pi → 2 lactic acid + 2 ATP + 2 H₂O). In plants and fungi, pyruvate releases CO₂ to form acetaldehyde, which NADH reduces to ethanol (glucose + 2 ADP + 2 Pi → 2 ethanol + 2 CO₂ + 2 ATP + 2 H₂O). Either way NAD⁺ is regenerated so glycolysis can continue and make a small amount of ATP: 2 per glucose."},{"mod":"m7","ch":8,"p":"112–113","q":"How are other fuels and stored sugars integrated into cellular respiration?","a":"Excess glucose is stored as branched glucose polymers: glycogen in animals (in muscle for contraction and in the liver for the whole body) and starch in plants. Glucose monomers are cleaved off one at a time and enter glycolysis. Digested disaccharides and monosaccharides become glucose or other glycolysis intermediates. Glycerol enters glycolysis, fatty acids are shortened by β-oxidation into acetyl-CoA (making NADH and FADH₂ but no ATP directly), and amino acids enter at pyruvate, acetyl-CoA or the citric acid cycle."},{"mod":"m7","ch":0,"p":"99–101","q":"Learning outcome: Describe cellular respiration and the catabolic reactions that produce ATP.","a":"Cellular respiration is a series of catabolic reactions that break fuel molecules down and capture their energy as ATP. Some ATP is made directly by substrate-level phosphorylation in glycolysis and the citric acid cycle (a small amount). Most of the energy is first transferred to the electron carriers NADH and FADH₂ by redox reactions; they deliver electrons to the electron transport chain, and the energy released is used to make most of the ATP by oxidative phosphorylation. Overall, glucose is oxidized to CO₂ and O₂ is reduced to H₂O."},{"mod":"m7","ch":2,"p":"99, 103–105","q":"Learning outcome: Describe how glycolysis oxidizes glucose and in turn produces pyruvate, ATP and reduced electron carriers.","a":"In phase 1, two ATP phosphorylate glucose to fructose 1,6-bisphosphate. In phase 2 it is split into two glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate. In phase 3 each G3P is oxidized: NAD⁺ is reduced to NADH (2 in all), and phosphates are transferred to ADP by substrate-level phosphorylation (4 ATP in all), ending with 2 pyruvate. The net result per glucose is 2 pyruvate, 2 ATP and 2 NADH, in the cytosol."},{"mod":"m7","ch":4,"p":"99, 106–108","q":"Learning outcome: Describe the reduction of pyruvate and the citric acid cycle. (The notes' wording; with O₂ present pyruvate is oxidized.)","a":"With O₂ present, each pyruvate is oxidized in the matrix by the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex to CO₂ and an acetyl group, reducing NAD⁺ to NADH; coenzyme A carries the acetyl group into the citric acid cycle. There, acetyl-CoA joins oxaloacetate to form citrate, two CO₂ leave, and 3 NADH, 1 FADH₂ and 1 ATP are made per turn as oxaloacetate is regenerated. (Pyruvate is reduced, not oxidized, only in fermentation, when O₂ is absent.)"},{"mod":"m7","ch":5,"p":"99, 109–110","q":"Learning outcome: Explain how the electron transport chain produces a large amount of ATP.","a":"The NADH and FADH₂ from the first three stages (10 NADH and 2 FADH₂ per glucose) feed electrons into the chain. As electrons pass to O₂, complexes I, III and IV pump protons into the intermembrane space, building a proton gradient with high potential energy. Protons can only return through ATP synthase, which turns their flow into rotation and the rotation into ATP. This makes about 28 of the roughly 32 ATP per glucose, far more than substrate-level phosphorylation."},{"mod":"m7","ch":7,"p":"99, 111","q":"Learning outcome: Distinguish between aerobic cellular respiration and alternative anaerobic pathways to produce ATP.","a":"Aerobic respiration uses O₂ as the final electron acceptor and runs all four stages, oxidizing glucose completely to CO₂ and H₂O for about 32 ATP. Anaerobic pathways run only glycolysis, then reduce pyruvate to regenerate NAD⁺: lactic acid fermentation in bacteria and animal cells, or ethanol fermentation (releasing CO₂) in plants and fungi. They yield only 2 ATP per glucose, and much of glucose's energy stays in the lactic acid or ethanol."},{"mod":"m8","ch":0,"p":"117–118","q":"Give an overview of photosynthesis.","a":"Photosynthesis is the conversion of light energy to chemical energy: organisms build carbohydrates from CO₂ and water using sunlight (6CO₂ + 12H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ + 6H₂O). Overall it is a redox reaction: water is oxidized, releasing electrons, protons and O₂, and CO₂ is reduced to carbohydrate. It has two stages in the chloroplast: the light-dependent reactions in the thylakoid membranes make ATP, NADPH and O₂, and the light-independent reactions (Calvin cycle) in the stroma use the ATP and NADPH to make carbohydrate. It is a major entry point of energy into biological systems."},{"mod":"m8","ch":2,"p":"118–120","q":"How is sunlight captured into chemical forms?","a":"Light travels as photons. When chlorophyll absorbs a photon (mainly blue and red light), one of its electrons is raised to an excited state. In a photosystem, antenna chlorophylls pass that energy, not electrons, from chlorophyll to chlorophyll until it reaches the reaction-center pair, which transfers an excited electron to an electron acceptor. The reaction center is refilled with an electron from water. The electrons then flow through the electron transport chain, building a proton gradient that drives ATP synthase, and finally reduce NADP⁺ to NADPH, so light energy ends up as ATP and NADPH."},{"mod":"m8","ch":6,"p":"123–124","q":"Explain the Calvin cycle.","a":"The Calvin cycle, in the stroma, converts low-energy CO₂ into higher-energy carbohydrate using ATP and NADPH: 15 reactions in three steps. In carboxylation, rubisco adds CO₂ to the 5-carbon RuBP, making an unstable 6-carbon molecule that splits into two 3-carbon PGA. In reduction, PGA is phosphorylated by ATP and reduced by NADPH to triose phosphate. In regeneration, five of every six triose phosphates are rearranged into three RuBP using ATP, so only one can be withdrawn and exported. Excess carbohydrate is stored as starch for use at night."},{"mod":"m8","ch":7,"p":"124","q":"Describe the evolution of photosynthesis.","a":"Early photosynthetic bacteria had only a single photosystem, which cannot capture enough energy. Two photosystems may have arisen by horizontal gene transfer (genes for the second photosystem came from another bacterium) or by gene duplication and divergence of one of the genes. Cyanobacteria, with both photosystems, were the first organisms to use water as the electron donor. The endosymbiotic theory proposes that a eukaryotic cell engulfed a cyanobacterium, which over time lost the ability to live outside the host and became the chloroplast."},{"mod":"m8","ch":0,"p":"116–118","q":"Describe the chemical pathways of photosynthesis in capturing sunlight in synthesizing carbohydrates.","a":"In the light-dependent reactions in the thylakoid membranes, light absorbed by photosystems II and I drives electrons from water to NADP⁺, releasing O₂ and producing NADPH, while the resulting proton gradient drives ATP synthase to make ATP. In the light-independent reactions (Calvin cycle) in the stroma, that ATP and NADPH are used to fix CO₂ onto RuBP and reduce the product to triose phosphate. So sunlight is captured as ATP and NADPH, which then power the reduction of CO₂ to carbohydrate."},{"mod":"m8","ch":5,"p":"121–122","q":"Describe the outcomes of the photosynthetic electron transport chain.","a":"The chain links the oxidation of water to the reduction of NADP⁺. Electrons pass from PSII via plastoquinone to the cytochrome b6f complex, then via plastocyanin through the lumen to PSI, then via ferredoxin and NADP⁺ reductase to NADP⁺. The outcomes are: O₂ released from water, NADPH formed, and a proton gradient (H⁺ moved from the stroma into the lumen) that drives ATP synthase to make ATP, which is photophosphorylation. The ATP stays in the chloroplast for carbon fixation."},{"mod":"m8","ch":6,"p":"123","q":"Explain the Calvin cycle in carbohydrate synthesis.","a":"The Calvin cycle builds carbohydrate from CO₂ in three steps. Carboxylation: rubisco joins CO₂ to RuBP, giving two PGA. Reduction: ATP phosphorylates PGA and NADPH reduces it, giving triose phosphate, the 3-carbon sugar that leaves the cycle. Regeneration: the remaining triose phosphates are rearranged into RuBP using ATP. Per 3 CO₂ fixed, the cycle uses 9 ATP and 6 NADPH and releases one triose phosphate, which is exported from the chloroplast or, in excess, stored as starch."},{"mod":"m8","ch":7,"p":"124","q":"Describe the evolution of photosynthesis (learning outcome).","a":"Photosynthesis began in bacteria with one photosystem. A second photosystem evolved, either by horizontal gene transfer or by duplication and divergence of a photosystem gene, so two photosystems could act in series and capture enough energy to use water as the electron donor, first in cyanobacteria. A eukaryotic cell then engulfed a cyanobacterium; by endosymbiosis it became the chloroplast, which still carries PSI and PSII in its thylakoid membranes."},{"mod":"m9","ch":0,"p":"127–129","q":"What are the principles of cell signalling?","a":"Cell signalling is transferring information from the outside of the cell to obtain a response inside the cell. It needs four elements: a signalling cell, a signalling molecule (ligand), a receptor and a responding cell, in prokaryotes and eukaryotes alike. Signalling happens in four steps: receptor activation, signal transduction, the cellular response and signal termination. The response depends on the cell type, because it depends on the proteins and pathways in that cell."},{"mod":"m9","ch":2,"p":"130","q":"How do short-distance and long-distance communication between cells differ?","a":"Endocrine signalling is long distance: endocrine cells release hormones that travel through the bloodstream, and only target cells with the right receptor respond. Paracrine signalling is short distance: a small, water-soluble signal diffuses to neighbouring cells within about 20 cells. In autocrine signalling the secreting cell is also the target. Contact-dependent (juxtacrine) signalling needs direct contact: a transmembrane protein on one cell binds a transmembrane receptor on the next."},{"mod":"m9","ch":3,"p":"129, 131","q":"What kinds of signalling receptors do cells have?","a":"Receptors bind specific ligands at a ligand-binding site, much like an enzyme binds its substrate, and binding changes their conformation. Small nonpolar signals cross the hydrophobic core of the membrane and bind intracellular receptors in the cytosol or nucleus. Polar signals cannot cross, so they bind cell-surface receptors, transmembrane proteins with extracellular, transmembrane and cytoplasmic domains. The three main groups of cell-surface receptor are G protein-coupled receptors, receptor kinases and ion channels."},{"mod":"m9","ch":6,"p":"129, 134","q":"What are signal transduction and signal amplification?","a":"Signal transduction is how an activated receptor transmits the message through the cytoplasm: a series of distinct proteins activated or inactivated in a particular sequence, with the message staying in the cytosol or going to the nucleus. Amplification means a small amount of ligand produces a large response. In the epinephrine pathway the signal is amplified in three places: each receptor activates multiple G proteins, each adenylyl cyclase makes large amounts of cAMP, and each active PKA activates multiple protein targets. In the MAP kinase pathway the signal is amplified from kinase to kinase."},{"mod":"m9","ch":4,"p":"132–133","q":"How do G protein-coupled receptors work?","a":"GPCRs are found in virtually every eukaryote and associate with G proteins made of α, β and γ subunits. With GDP on the α subunit, the subunits are joined and the G protein is inactive. When a ligand binds the GPCR, GTP replaces GDP, the G protein turns on, and the α subunit separates from β and γ and binds a target protein to activate it. In heart muscle, epinephrine works this way: α activates adenylyl cyclase, which converts ATP to cAMP; cAMP activates PKA, which phosphorylates proteins that increase the rate of contraction."},{"mod":"m9","ch":8,"p":"136–137","q":"How do receptor kinases work?","a":"A receptor kinase becomes active when its ligand binds: the receptors pair into dimers and each phosphorylates the other, and the phosphate groups give binding sites for intracellular signalling proteins. Examples are limb bud formation, insulin signalling and wound healing. After a paper cut, platelets release PDGF, which binds PDGF receptor kinases. The phosphorylated receptors activate Ras; GTP-bound Ras triggers the MAP kinase cascade, and the last kinase enters the nucleus to turn on genes for cell division. The pathway ends when GDP replaces GTP on Ras."},{"mod":"m9","ch":8,"p":"138","q":"How do ligand-gated ion channels work?","a":"Ion channels change the flow of ions across the membrane, into the cell (influx) or out of it (efflux). They can be ligand-gated (respond to binding of a signalling molecule), voltage-gated (respond to voltage changes) or mechanically gated (respond to force on the cell). In a ligand-gated channel, the gate is closed without the ligand, preventing ion movement; when the ligand binds the gate opens and ions move through."},{"mod":"m9","ch":7,"p":"128–135","q":"Learning outcome: describe signal transduction in a target cell from signal molecule to receptor to amplification to termination.","a":"A signal molecule such as epinephrine binds its receptor (a GPCR) and changes its conformation. The receptor activates G proteins by swapping GDP for GTP; the α subunit activates adenylyl cyclase, which makes the second messenger cAMP; cAMP activates PKA, which phosphorylates target proteins. The signal is amplified at the G protein, cAMP and PKA steps. It is terminated when the ligand leaves (set by binding affinity), the G protein converts GTP to GDP, adenylyl cyclase stops, cytosolic enzymes degrade cAMP and phosphatases dephosphorylate the targets."},{"mod":"m9","ch":2,"p":"130","q":"Learning outcome: compare and contrast signalling over short and long distances.","a":"All forms use a signalling cell, a signal, a receptor and a responding cell, and target cells respond only if they have the matching receptor. Long-distance endocrine signals are hormones carried through the bloodstream to distant targets. Short-distance signals act locally: paracrine signals diffuse to neighbours within about 20 cells, autocrine signals act on the cell that released them, and contact-dependent signals need the two cells to touch. Paracrine and autocrine signalling are especially important in embryonic development."},{"mod":"m9","ch":9,"p":"131–138","q":"Learning outcome: explain the mechanism of G protein-coupled receptor, receptor kinase and ligand-gated ion channel signalling.","a":"A GPCR activates a G protein: GTP replaces GDP on the α subunit, which separates and activates a target such as adenylyl cyclase, leading to cAMP and PKA. A receptor kinase dimerizes when its ligand binds and each member phosphorylates the other; the phosphates recruit signalling proteins such as Ras, which starts the MAP kinase cascade. A ligand-gated ion channel opens its gate when the ligand binds, letting ions flow into or out of the cell."},{"mod":"m10","ch":0,"p":"141–142","q":"Tissues and organs: what are they, and how does the skin show the difference?","a":"A tissue is a collection of cells that work together to perform a specific function. Two or more tissues combine and function together to make up an organ. The skin is an organ: its epidermis is epithelial tissue (mostly keratinocytes, plus melanocytes) that forms a water-resistant, protective barrier, and its dermis is mostly connective tissue (fibroblasts making ECM, plus nerves and blood vessels) that supports the epidermis and supplies it with nutrients. A basal lamina of specialized ECM lies between them."},{"mod":"m10","ch":4,"p":"143–148","q":"The cytoskeleton: what are its three elements, and what does each do?","a":"The cytoskeleton is made of protein fibers, long chains of subunits, that give the cell internal support and move substances within it. Microtubules are hollow tubes of α and β tubulin dimers that radiate from the centrosome; they let the cell withstand compression, arrange organelles, carry vesicles (with kinesin and dynein), move cilia and flagella, and segregate chromosomes. Microfilaments are the thinnest fibers, double helices of actin under the membrane and in microvilli; they reinforce the membrane and handle crawling, cytokinesis, vesicle transport and muscle contraction. Intermediate filaments, found in animal cells, are strong cable-like polymers of diverse proteins (keratins, vimentin, neurofilaments, lamins) that give mechanical strength. All eukaryotic cells have microtubules and microfilaments; animal cells also have intermediate filaments."},{"mod":"m10","ch":7,"p":"149–153","q":"Cell junctions: what are the types, and what does each do?","a":"Cell junctions connect cells to other cells or to the basal lamina and are reinforced by the cytoskeleton. Anchoring junctions: adherens junctions (a belt of cadherins linked to actin microfilaments) and desmosomes (button-like spots of cadherins linked to intermediate filaments) give cell–cell adhesion, and hemidesmosomes (integrins linked to intermediate filaments) give cell–ECM adhesion to the basal lamina. The barrier junction, the tight junction (claudins and occludins), seals the extracellular space so substances must cross an epithelium by moving through the cells. Communicating junctions are gap junctions (connexons of connexins across a 2–4 nm gap, passing only low molecular weight material like ions) in animal cells, and plasmodesmata (membrane-lined channels large enough for RNA and proteins) in plant cells."},{"mod":"m10","ch":8,"p":"154–156","q":"The extracellular matrix: what is it made of in plants and animals, and what does it do?","a":"The ECM is a layer of material secreted beyond the cell membrane: an insoluble meshwork of proteins and polysaccharides. It acts as a supportive and protective material and allows the expression of different cell functions. In plants it is the cell wall: a carbohydrate middle lamella that glues cells together, a primary wall of cellulose, pectin and proteins, and a secondary wall of cellulose and lignin that is hard and water resistant. In animals it is extensive in connective tissue: large fibrous proteins (collagen, elastin and laminin) in a gel-like polysaccharide matrix, made by fibroblasts. Collagen, the most abundant animal protein, is a triple helix of three polypeptides bundled into fibrils and fibers. The basal lamina is a specialized ECM beneath all epithelia that gives flexible support and a scaffold."},{"mod":"m10","ch":0,"p":"140–142","q":"Learning outcome: describe how cells form communities within tissues and organs to perform specific functions.","a":"In a multicellular organism, cells come together into tissues that work together on a specific function, and two or more tissues form an organ. The shape of cells and organs reflects their function, and it is set and maintained by the cytoskeleton. A tissue's structural integrity depends on cells adhering to one another through cell junctions and to the extracellular matrix. In the skin, keratinocytes of the epidermis are joined to each other and anchored to the basal lamina, while fibroblasts in the dermis secrete the ECM that supports the tissue."},{"mod":"m10","ch":4,"p":"143–148","q":"Learning outcome: describe the cytoskeletal elements that help the cell maintain its shape.","a":"Microtubules, hollow tubes of α and β tubulin dimers, radiate from the centrosome (minus ends) toward the membrane (plus ends) and let the cell withstand compression while holding organelles in place. Microfilaments, actin double helices, are branched beneath the membrane where they reinforce it, form bundles in microvilli and a band around epithelial cells. Intermediate filaments, rope-like polymers of cell-type-specific proteins in animal cells, give mechanical strength against stretching. Microtubules and microfilaments are dynamic: they add and lose subunits, faster at the plus end, and microtubules show dynamic instability so the cell can rapidly reorganize its shape."},{"mod":"m10","ch":6,"p":"149–153","q":"Learning outcome: explain cell to cell junctions that connect cells to one another.","a":"Cells use cell adhesion molecules linked to the cytoskeleton. Cadherins bind cadherins of the same type on the adjacent cell, giving structural continuity from one cell's cytoskeleton to the next. In adherens junctions the cadherins form a belt attached to actin microfilaments; in desmosomes they form button-like spots attached to intermediate filaments. Tight junctions seal the space between epithelial cells so material must pass through the cells. Gap junctions connect the cytoplasm of adjacent animal cells through connexons that pass ions and small molecules, and plasmodesmata connect plant cells through membrane-lined channels that pass RNA and proteins."},{"mod":"m10","ch":8,"p":"141, 154–156","q":"Learning outcome: explain the role of the extracellular matrix.","a":"The ECM is secreted material outside the cell membrane, a meshwork of proteins and polysaccharides. It supports and protects cells and tissues and allows the expression of different cell functions. Cells adhere to it through integrins, whose cytoplasmic domains interact with the cytoskeleton, which keeps tissues intact under physical stress; in epithelia, hemidesmosomes anchor cells to the basal lamina. In animals, collagen fibers give strength, and the basal lamina gives epithelia a flexible foundation and a scaffold. In plants, the cell wall is the ECM: its middle lamella glues cells together and its secondary wall, with lignin, makes the wall hard and water resistant."},{"mod":"m11","ch":1,"p":"162","q":"What is the cell cycle, and what happens in each of its phases?","a":"The cell cycle is the series of stages by which a eukaryotic cell divides. It has two distinct stages: M phase, when the parent cell divides (mitosis separates the replicated chromosomes, cytokinesis divides the cytoplasm), and interphase, the 10 to 14 hours between two M phases. Interphase is G₁ (the cell grows in size and protein content, prepares for S and makes regulatory proteins), S (the DNA is replicated) and G₂ (the cell prepares for M phase). Cells that do not actively divide, such as liver cells, sit in G₀, separate from G₁, with no preparation for division."},{"mod":"m11","ch":3,"p":"163–168","q":"How is DNA replicated?","a":"Replication is semiconservative: the two parental strands unwind and each is a template for a new daughter strand, so each new molecule has one old and one new strand. At the replication fork, helicase unwinds the double helix, single-strand binding proteins keep the strands apart, and topoisomerase relieves supercoiling ahead of the fork. Primase lays down a short RNA primer, and DNA polymerase extends it 5′ to 3′ using dATP, dTTP, dGTP and dCTP, a DNA template and the primer's 3′-OH. The leading strand is made continuously toward the fork; the lagging strand is made away from the fork as Okazaki fragments, whose primers are replaced with DNA by a different DNA polymerase and joined by DNA ligase. Most DNA polymerases proofread, removing an incorrect nucleotide and inserting the right one."},{"mod":"m11","ch":5,"p":"169–172","q":"How are chromosomes replicated in prokaryotes and in eukaryotes?","a":"Circular chromosomes (most bacteria, and mitochondrial and chloroplast DNA) have a single origin of replication; two forks move in opposite directions until they meet and fuse on the far side. Linear eukaryotic chromosomes have many origins, each opening a replication bubble with two forks moving apart; when bubbles meet they fuse and DNA ligase seals the backbone. At the ends, the last lagging-strand primer sits about 100 nucleotides from the 3′ end, so a piece of template stays unreplicated and one strand shortens each round. Telomeres (TTAGGG repeats in humans) buffer this loss, and telomerase, a ribonucleoprotein with its own RNA template and reverse transcriptase activity, restores them in stem cells and germ cells. After S phase each chromosome is two sister chromatids held together at the centromere."},{"mod":"m11","ch":8,"p":"173–175","q":"What happens during mitotic cell division?","a":"Chromosomes condense as the cell moves from G₂ into mitosis. Prophase: chromosomes become visible, the mitotic spindle assembles, and the duplicated centrosomes move to opposite poles. Prometaphase: the nuclear membrane breaks down and microtubules attach to the two kinetochores at each centromere. Metaphase: chromosomes line up on the metaphase plate. Anaphase: centromeres divide and kinetochore microtubules shorten, pulling sister chromatids to opposite poles. Telophase: the spindle breaks down, nuclear envelopes re-form and chromosomes decondense. Cytokinesis then divides the cytoplasm, by a contractile ring of actin in animal cells or by a cell plate built from vesicles guided by the phragmoplast in plant cells, giving two daughter cells genetically identical to the parent."},{"mod":"m11","ch":9,"p":"176–180","q":"How is the cell cycle regulated?","a":"Cyclins, whose levels rise and fall each cycle, bind and activate cyclin-dependent kinases (CDKs), which are always present but active only with their cyclin; the complexes phosphorylate proteins that move the cycle on. Cyclin D and E-CDK act in G₁ (e.g. leading to DNA polymerase being made), cyclin A-CDK starts DNA synthesis in S, and cyclin B-CDK triggers mitosis (nuclear envelope breakdown, spindle formation). Three checkpoints check for DNA damage before S, unreplicated DNA at the end of G₂, and unattached chromosomes before anaphase. If DNA is damaged, phosphorylated p53 turns on an inhibitor of the G₁/S cyclin-CDK so the DNA can be repaired; if repair fails, p53 raises Bax and lowers Bcl-2, Bax/Bax dimers form and the cell dies by apoptosis."},{"mod":"m11","ch":0,"p":"159–161, 169–170","q":"Learning outcome: describe the process of cell division and DNA replication in both prokaryotes and eukaryotes.","a":"Prokaryotes divide by binary fission: the circular chromosome, attached to the plasma membrane, is replicated from a single origin in both directions; the two copies stay attached and separate as the cell elongates; at about twice its size a constriction forms and new membrane and cell wall make two identical daughter cells. Eukaryotes have a large genome of linear chromosomes in a nucleus, so they divide by mitotic cell division in the cell cycle, and the nuclear envelope must break down and re-form. The process of DNA replication is similar in both (semiconservative, with helicase, primase, DNA polymerase, leading and lagging strands, ligase), but eukaryotic chromosomes use many origins and have telomeres at their ends."},{"mod":"m11","ch":1,"p":"162","q":"Learning outcome: describe the various phases of the eukaryotic cell cycle.","a":"Interphase (about 10 to 14 hours) prepares the cell for division. In G₁ the cell grows in size and protein content and makes and activates regulatory proteins. In S the DNA is replicated, so each chromosome becomes two sister chromatids. In G₂ the cell prepares for M phase. In M phase, mitosis separates the replicated chromosomes and cytokinesis divides the cytoplasm into two daughter cells. G₀ is a separate state, out of the cycle, for cells that do not actively divide, such as liver cells."},{"mod":"m11","ch":8,"p":"173–175","q":"Learning outcome: explain the processes that occur during mitosis.","a":"Mitosis has five stages, told apart by the position of the chromosomes. In prophase the chromosomes condense and the spindle forms as centrosomes move to opposite poles. In prometaphase the nuclear membrane breaks down and spindle microtubules attach to kinetochores, one for each sister chromatid. In metaphase the chromosomes are pushed to the metaphase plate. In anaphase the centromeres divide and shortening kinetochore microtubules pull the sister chromatids to opposite poles. In telophase the spindle disappears, nuclear envelopes re-form and the chromosomes decondense, ending mitosis; cytokinesis follows."},{"mod":"m11","ch":9,"p":"176–180","q":"Learning outcome: explain why the cell cycle is regulated.","a":"Two events are critical, the start of DNA replication and the start of mitosis, and the cell must be ready for each. Without regulation a cell may die or divide uncontrollably, which leads to cancer. Cyclin-CDK complexes drive each step, and checkpoints stop the cell if its DNA is damaged, not fully replicated, or not attached to the spindle, giving time for repair; cells that cannot be repaired are removed by apoptosis. Division is a counterbalance between proto-oncogenes that promote it and tumour suppressors such as p53 that inhibit it; mutations that create oncogenes and inactivate tumour suppressors build up toward cancer."}],"built":"2026-11-03"};
